window.NOTES = [{"slug": "README", "label": "About these notes", "tag": "", "group": "start", "title": "Agentic AI Applications in Operations & SCM — study notes index", "md": "# Agentic AI Applications in Operations & SCM — study notes index\nCourse **Agentic AI in Ops & SCM** (TM2335), **MBA (OM)**, semester-end preparation. Built from Samba's uploads (Oct 2026):\n- the **20 session decks** (Sessions 1–20, including the Chain Reaction round briefs and the season debrief);\n- the **syllabus** (9 units);\n- the two **past papers**: **Trishul Consumer Products** and **Kaveri Motors / Chain Reaction**.\n\n**What the faculty said about the exam 📘:**\n- It is case-based applications plus direct numerical problems, with **no standalone theory**.\n- The numericals are straightforward, with no extra formulas.\n- **Basic concepts of SCM** are required.\n- Expect a comprehensive application case covering all concepts, frameworks, projects, class tests, case studies and **the Chain Reaction simulation**, with **particular emphasis on the strategies, decisions and management approaches used in Chain Reaction**.\n\n**Source key used in every file:**\n- 📘 = taken from your session decks, syllabus or past papers (exam-safe wording).\n- 📗 = standard SCM and AI knowledge added to give complete answers.\n- **Exam markers:** ⭐⭐⭐ = very likely to carry marks; ⭐⭐ = likely; ⭐ = good to know.\n\n## ⭐⭐⭐ Start here — the exam pages\n| # | File | What it covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | ⭐⭐⭐ [Exam Playbook](#/exam-playbook) | The two printed rules (**numbered assumptions**, **units and arithmetic**), the A-N-V-C-D-T answer skeleton, data-triage defect patterns, the framework picker, the mandate card, the autonomy rule, time plan, phrases and mistakes |\n| E2 | ⭐⭐⭐ [Past Paper 1 — Trishul](#/pyq-trishul) | The full paper typed out plus model answers for A1–A4 and B1 |\n| E3 | ⭐⭐⭐ [Past Paper 2 — Kaveri Motors](#/pyq-kaveri) | The full Chain Reaction paper plus the arithmetic table and model answers for A1, A2 and B1 |\n| E4 | ⭐⭐⭐ [Chain Reaction Playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook) | Game rules, round-by-round decisions, the four archetypes and 20 teams, the four lessons, the strategy rulebook, practice questions |\n| E5 | ⭐⭐⭐ [Course Frameworks](#/course-frameworks) | All **37** frameworks from the 20 sessions, with steps and applied examples |\n| E6 | ⭐⭐⭐ [Numericals](#/numericals) | 50 worked numericals: case patterns (pools, ceilings, break-evens, weighted fill, contribution forgone, tokens) and basic SCM (EOQ, safety stock, newsvendor, turns, bullwhip) |\n| E7 | ⭐⭐⭐ [Mock Papers](#/practice-cases) | Three new full papers (Meru Pharma, Brahmaputra Electronics, a Chain Reaction mid-season case) with model answers |\n| E8 | ⭐⭐ [Case Library](#/case-library) | Every session case resolved, plus named deployments and failures to cite |\n\n## Chapters (syllabus units → sessions)\n| Ch | Syllabus unit 📘 | Sessions | Opening case |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [0](#/ch00-scm-fundamentals) | Basic SCM concepts (faculty note) | all | — |\n| [1](#/ch01-ai-in-operations-and-scm) | 1. Introduction to AI in Ops & SCM (Kiva; Azure AI, AWS AI) | S1 | NorthStar Foods |\n| [2](#/ch02-ai-readiness-data-technology) | readiness: data & technology | S2 | Meridian Apparel |\n| [3](#/ch03-ai-readiness-process-organisation) | readiness: process & organisation | S3 | Continental Parts |\n| [4](#/ch04-agentic-ai-systems) | agentic systems, autonomy, governance | S4 | Vantage Logistics |\n| [5](#/ch05-ai-platforms) | 1. platforms (Azure AI, AWS AI) | S5 | Solstice Industrials |\n| [6](#/ch06-machine-learning-for-operations) | 2. ML for Operations (SVM, k-Means; scikit-learn, RapidMiner) | S6–S7 | SuryaMart, Meghna Foods |\n| [7](#/ch07-demand-forecasting) | 3. Demand Forecasting using Agentic AI (IoT, RL; Excel vs Prophet vs SageMaker) | S8, S9, S11, S12 | Nandini Beverages, Trident |\n| [8](#/ch08-inventory-optimisation) | 4. Inventory Optimisation (EOQ, RNN/LSTM, real-time reordering) | S10 | Krishna AgroMachinery |\n| [9](#/ch09-predictive-maintenance-digital-twins) | 5. Self-adaptive Maintenance (TensorFlow, digital twins) | S13 | the bearing question |\n| [10](#/ch10-logistics-routing-optimisation) | 6. AI in Logistics (OR-Tools, OptaPlanner, Q-learning routing) | S14–S15 | capacity shock, the LP mandate |\n| [11](#/ch11-nlp-procurement-token-economics) | 7. NLP for Procurement (HuggingFace, OpenAI API, auto-RFx, compliance) + token economics | S16–S17 | clause budget, repriced agent |\n| [12](#/ch12-genai-design-multi-agent) | 8. GenAI in SC Design & Planning (scenarios, design-space, multi-agent simulators) | S18–S19 | paint-colour scenario, Command Your Agent |\n| [13](#/ch13-responsible-ai-ethics) | 9. Ethical, Legal & Social Implications (case law, vendor-selection bias, fairness audits) | S20 | bias before the board; the season debrief |\n\n## Practice and reference\n| File | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| [Exam Q&A Bank](#/exam-questions-qna) | 45 application questions with model answers, by unit and for Chain Reaction |\n| [MCQ Bank](#/mcq-bank) | All **59 class questions** from Sessions 1–20 with the official answer and WHY, plus 26 exam-style extras |\n| [Glossary A–Z](#/glossary) | About 125 terms; it powers the flashcards and quiz |\n| [Rapid Revision](#/rapid-revision) | The night-before page: the trigger → framework picker, numbers to quote, formulas, the 12 must-know frameworks, Chain Reaction in ten lines |\n\n## Session map 📘\n| S | Title | Tool you take away | CR round |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Introduction to AI in Operations & SCM | Four-Question Claim Audit; Q vs S | — |\n| 2 | AI Readiness I: Data & Technology | Six DQ dimensions; five-pillar scorecard | — |\n| 3 | AI Readiness II: Process & Organisation | Phased roadmap with gates; pilot matrix | — |\n| 4 | Agentic AI Systems & Smart Agents | Autonomy ladder; governance stack; suitability screen | — |\n| 5 | AI Platforms for SCM | Six platform filters; build/buy/blend | — |\n| 6 | Machine Learning Foundations | ML Framing Canvas | — |\n| 7 | AI Tools for Operations Analytics (lab) | Seven-step workflow; capability checklist | — |\n| 8 | AI-Driven Demand Forecasting | Signal audit; probability forecasting | — |\n| 9 | Forecasting Platforms (lab) | Tier matching; verdict table | — |\n| 10 | AI Inventory Optimisation (Command Your Agent game) | Guardrailed pilot | — |\n| 11 | Demand Forecasting: distributions, signals, loops | Signal audit | R1 Onboarding |\n| 12 | Forecasting Tiers | Bake-off ladder | R2 Forecasting bake-off |\n| 13 | Predictive Maintenance & Digital Twins | Twin-now / twin-later | R3 Aftermarket & maintenance |\n| 14 | Routing, Rerouting, Reality | Mandate Card | R4 The network opens |\n| 15 | Optimisation in Practice | Optimizer Mandate Checklist | R5 Setting the mandate |\n| 16 | NLP for Procurement | Clause-stakes checklist | R6 Chip contract |\n| 17 | APIs & Token Economics | Tune-or-retire | R7 Token economy |\n| 18 | GenAI in Supply Chain Design | Scenario portfolio checklist | R8 Scenarios |\n| 19 | The Amplification We Made (multi-agent) | ACAP self-check; coordination fixes | R9 Coordination |\n| 20 | Responsible AI: Closing the Loop | ACAP governance checklist | R10 Finale + debrief |\n\n## How to use this site in the last week\n1. **Day 1:** Exam Playbook, then attempt Trishul cold and compare with the model answers.\n2. **Day 2:** the Chain Reaction Playbook, then attempt Kaveri cold.\n3. **Day 3:** Course Frameworks (write each one from memory) and the Case Library.\n4. **Day 4:** Numericals Parts A and B.\n5. **Day 5:** chapters 7–13 (the applied units), then the MCQ bank in Self-test mode.\n6. **Day 6:** Mock Papers 1–3 under time.\n7. **Night before:** Rapid Revision and flashcards.\n"}, {"slug": "rapid-revision", "label": "Rapid Revision", "tag": "★", "group": "start", "title": "Rapid Revision — the night before the exam", "md": "# Rapid Revision — the night before the exam\nEverything high-yield on one page: the answer method, the frameworks by trigger, the numbers to quote, the formulas and the Chain Reaction lessons. Each row points to the full explanation.\n\n## ⭐⭐⭐ The exam in one box 📘\n- **Format:** a case study with exhibits (some irrelevant, some defective). **Section A** is diagnostics (calculations, data triage, exposing claims). **Section B** is design (rank, choose, mandate, gate). There is **no standalone theory**; numericals are straightforward; basic SCM concepts are required; **Chain Reaction** gets emphasis.\n- **Rule 1:** every verdict, ranking or recommendation **opens with \"Assumptions: A1. … A2. … A3. …\"**. An unstated assumption costs marks; a stated one earns them.\n- **Rule 2:** **show units and arithmetic.** One clean calculation beats three vague ones.\n- **Skeleton (A-N-V-C-D-T):** Assumptions → Number → Verdict → Confront the strongest counter-evidence → Design (mandate or gate: metric, constraint, trigger, L-level, owner) → Test (frozen baseline, success threshold, counter-metric, demotion trigger).\n- **Defect patterns to expose:**\n  - cherry-picked month;\n  - trend hidden in an average;\n  - activity reported as outcome;\n  - insurance logic run backwards;\n  - totals that don't add up;\n  - stale or contradicted figures;\n  - vanity items;\n  - evidence laundering (another firm's results).\n\nSee the [Exam playbook](#/exam-playbook).\n\n## ⭐⭐⭐ Trigger → framework (the picker)\n| If the case says… | Reach for | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"AI will cut inventory X%\" | **Q vs S** (S share caps the claim) + **Four-Question Claim Audit** | [Ch 1](#/ch01-ai-in-operations-and-scm) |\n| \"more accurate forecast\" | **Precision pays only when a decision changes** (pallets, trucks) | Ch 1 |\n| duplicates, latency, missing fields | **Six DQ dimensions**; binding dimension per use case | [Ch 2](#/ch02-ai-readiness-data-technology) |\n| a readiness score [x,x,x,x,x] | **Five-pillar scorecard sets the ambition ceiling**; predictive first on the cleanest domain | Ch 2 |\n| planners override or rebel; a board-deck target | **Trust design** (±15% with reasons); **phased roadmap with gates**; incentives | [Ch 3](#/ch03-ai-readiness-process-organisation) |\n| choose between pilots | **Pilot-selection matrix** (value, feasibility, blast radius) | Ch 3 |\n| \"agent\", \"autonomous\" | **Agent-washing test**, **autonomy ladder L1–L5**, **suitability screen**, **guardrails outside the model** | [Ch 4](#/ch04-agentic-ai-systems) |\n| platform choice, GPU shortage, token overrun | **Six filters in order**, build/buy/blend, FinOps | [Ch 5](#/ch05-ai-platforms) |\n| \"92% accurate model\" | **ML Framing Canvas**, naive benchmark, leakage, error cost both ways | [Ch 6](#/ch06-machine-learning-for-operations) |\n| new data (weather, POS, IoT) | **Signal audit** (lead, mechanism, decision, cost, weight) | [Ch 7](#/ch07-demand-forecasting) |\n| \"autonomous forecasting agent\" | **Update or execute?** | Ch 7 |\n| Excel vs Prophet vs SageMaker vs LSTM | **Tier matching / bake-off ladder** | Ch 7 |\n| \"DRL beats our policy by 20%\" | **~5% theory ceiling** + **guardrailed pilot (6 elements)** | [Ch 8](#/ch08-inventory-optimisation) |\n| sensors, RUL, digital twin | **Does it change the decision?** + **Twin-now / twin-later** | [Ch 9](#/ch09-predictive-maintenance-digital-twins) |\n| lane shock, mode choice | **Split shipment**; mode mix under capacity | [Ch 10](#/ch10-logistics-routing-optimisation) |\n| optimiser starved a zone | **Strategy lives in the objective** → **Optimizer Mandate Checklist** / **Mandate Card** | Ch 10 |\n| contract NLP, negotiation agent | **Clause-stakes checklist**; bounded autonomy (Pactum) | [Ch 11](#/ch11-nlp-procurement-token-economics) |\n| agent bill spiked | **Tune-or-retire**; caps in code; efficiency-capture gap | Ch 11 |\n| GenAI scenarios | **Scenario portfolio checklist**; GenAI for range + OR for rigour; antifragility | [Ch 12](#/ch12-genai-design-multi-agent) |\n| agents reacting to each other | **Collaboration paradox** → shared signal, smoothing, trained coordination | Ch 12 |\n| bias, EU AI Act, \"who answers?\" | **ACAP**; provider vs deployer; step down and disclose | [Ch 13](#/ch13-responsible-ai-ethics) |\n\n## ⭐⭐⭐ Numbers to quote (with the source session)\n| Number | Meaning | Session |\n|---|---|---|\n| **~30% / ~95%** | of AI adopters move beyond copilots / of GenAI pilots show no P&L impact | S1 |\n| **60–80%** | of effort in successful AI projects is data preparation and pipeline | S2, S7 |\n| **61%** | of planning decisions executed in spreadsheets beside an 11-year-old ERP | S2 |\n| **+14% / +19%** | mental effort / overload for close AI monitors (ironies of automation) | S3 |\n| **±15%** | bounded override that keeps trust | S3 |\n| **3.2×** | token spend vs forecast (FinOps) | S5 |\n| **42,840** | M5 series; ML won overall, simple methods competitive at SKU-store level | S6 |\n| **>$500M, ~25%** | Zillow write-down, staff cut | S6 |\n| **0** | weight learned on retail partners' forecasts | S8 |\n| **77% → 85%** | fill at constant inventory (~$20M released), 50,000 spare-part SKUs | S8, S10 |\n| **15–20% behind / within ~5% of optimal** | fresh DRL vs a myopic rule / myopic rule vs optimal | S10 |\n| **700+** | Global 2000 firms running enterprise inventory optimisation | S10 |\n| **~70 hours** | self-play to super-human Go (RL creates its own data) | S10 |\n| **FAST 0.85×/−18 · STANDARD 1.0× · CAREFUL 1.25×/+6** | Chain Reaction build options | S12 |\n| **~20% above peak / ~3 min** | load-test margin / auto-scale lag | S9 |\n| **~25%** | rail cheaper than road (Chain Reaction) | S14 |\n| **94.7% vs 79.3%** | domain-tuned vs general LLM on clause risk | S16 |\n| **~80%** | CPOs planning GenAI within 3 years (EY) | S16 |\n| **~3%, +35 days, 64–68%** | Walmart–Pactum savings, term extension, close rate | S1, S16 |\n| **20–30% vs 1–3%** | efficiency vs captured value (McKinsey) | S17 |\n| **24 / 2 / 1 / 1–4 / 2 / 4** | tokens per month / plan / regenerate / upkeep / terminate / actions per round | S17 |\n| **~18 weeks** | Walmart Trend-to-Product design-to-shelf cut | S18 |\n| **~20%** | firms with governance mature enough for autonomous agents | S20 |\n| **10%** | DeepFleet robot travel-time cut; 1M robots | S1, S14 |\n\n## ⭐⭐⭐ Formulas (all \"straightforward\")\n| What | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Inventory | **Q/2 + SS**; forecasting changes only SS |\n| Max forecasting saving | **SS ÷ (Q/2 + SS)** |\n| Safety stock | **z·σ·√L** (z: 90% 1.28, 95% 1.645, 97.5% 1.96, 99% 2.33) |\n| Reorder point | **d·L + SS** |\n| EOQ | **√(2DS/H)**; at EOQ ordering cost = holding cost |\n| Newsvendor | **CR = Cu/(Cu + Co)** → stock that quantile |\n| Carrying saving | **Δinventory × carrying rate** |\n| Weighted fill | **Σ(volume × fill) / Σ volume** |\n| Contribution forgone | **lost units × (price − variable cost)** |\n| Break-even saving rate | **annual cost ÷ addressable pool** |\n| Precision / recall / F1 | **TP/(TP+FP); TP/(TP+FN); 2PR/(P+R)** |\n| Bullwhip ratio | **Var(orders) / Var(demand)**, compounding by tier |\n| Act-on-alarm threshold | **p > Co/(Co + Cu)** (early-action cost ÷ total) |\n| Cost per decision | **run cost ÷ decisions**, compared with value per decision |\n| Chain Reaction value | **profit × reputation × trust** |\n| Q-learning | **Q ← Q + α[r + γ·maxQ′ − Q]** |\n\n## ⭐⭐⭐ The 12 frameworks you must be able to write from memory\n1. **Four-Question Claim Audit**: what baseline? measured how? which cost pool? what decision changes?\n2. **Six DQ dimensions**: accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, uniqueness, validity.\n3. **Five-pillar readiness**: data quality, master data, architecture, security, compute (1–5).\n4. **Pilot-selection matrix**: value × feasibility × blast radius. **Gate**: success metric + counter-metric + owner + demotion trigger + frozen baseline.\n5. **Autonomy ladder**: L1 inform · L2 recommend · L3 bounded act + escalate · L4 autonomous + monitor · L5 full.\n6. **Suitability screen**: frequency, reversibility, data/feedback, rule clarity, stakes (20+ / 12–19 / < 12).\n7. **ML Framing Canvas**: decision, target, data and features (known at decision time), error costs, baseline.\n8. **Signal audit**: lead time, mechanism, decision test, cost vs value, weight check.\n9. **Guardrailed pilot**: scope, bounds, fallback, evaluation, trust loop, ownership.\n10. **Mandate Card**: service floor, cost ceiling, mode priority, shock trigger, review cadence (plus the **Optimizer checklist**: service targets per zone, cost weights, review rule, re-solve trigger, verify the fit).\n11. **Tune-or-retire**: measure cost, measure value, compare, tune, retire. **Clause stakes**: list, decision, bar, tier, exception.\n12. **ACAP**: Accountability, Capability limits, Auditability, Proportionality, Post-incident learning (S19: Alignment, Process oversight, Coordination).\n\nPlus: **Twin-now test** (decision-changing, failure cost, data readiness, fidelity, verdict) and the **Scenario portfolio** (generate wide, label, select two, specify survival, guard the label). Full set in [Course frameworks](#/course-frameworks).\n\n## ⭐⭐⭐ Chain Reaction in ten lines\n1. **Network:** 3 plants → 4 RDCs → 7 zones; HATCH/SUV/EV.\n2. **Physics:** unrouted stock **auto-pushes to the plant's home RDC by road**, and zones pull only from their own RDC. **File a distribution plan.**\n3. **Modes:**\n   - **rail** is ~25% cheaper, booked a month ahead and charged whether used or not, so book base load;\n   - **road** is the default;\n   - **express** is for urgent top-ups.\n   - **MIX is locked until Tech 3.**\n4. **Dials:** distCover, distSafety, distFront (SPREAD/FRONT), distMode (ROAD/MIX). **Set policy, not flows.**\n5. **Tokens:** 24 per month; plan 2, regenerate 1; upkeep 1–4 per agent; terminate 2; max 4 actions per round. Decide on **cost per decision**.\n6. **Foundations D·P·T** gate every agent. **Below gate = full cost, a fraction of the effect, cut odds.**\n7. **Chips** are allocated against contracted volume, with **fees on the full book**. Size the book to real demand.\n8. **Value = profit × reputation × trust.** Breaches and incidents hit the multipliers.\n9. **Archetypes:**\n   - **Compounders won**: B12-Titans #1 (21 agents, rail every round, trust 110); A7-Kimi K3 #2 (zero below-gate).\n   - **Lean operators**: A8 #3 on 6 agents.\n   - **Transformation theatre** (8th–18th) and **foundations not run** (11th–20th) trailed.\n10. **Lessons:** **sequence beats speed · building is not operating · governance is the multiplier you feel late · efficiency is a real strategy.**\n\nFull detail in the [Chain Reaction playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook).\n\n## ⭐⭐ Past papers in six lines\n- **Trishul A1:** base 13.6 cr; the 30% claim = 4.08 > S of 2.6, so it fails; ceiling 19.1%; carrying value 0.49 vs a 1.6 cr fee.\n- **Trishul A2:** 11.0 + 2.6 + 1.2 = 14.8 ≠ 15.2; 480/2,890 = 16.6% duplicates, not \"< 2%\".\n- **Trishul A3/B1:** pool 58.2 cr, break-even 1.9%; vendor-master dedup first; ranking P2 > P3 > P1; L3; EU AI Act deployer oversight on P3.\n- **Kaveri A1:** the simple average 73.7% hides a slide to 58.2% (weighted 72.5%); M4 cherry-picked; \"foundations L4\" is activity; no-rail is insurance logic backwards.\n- **Kaveri A2:** the loss is physics, not luck. Forgone contribution ≈ ₹11 cr (5.8× the reported loss, 18× the congestion charge).\n- **Kaveri B1:** Distribution Planning Agent at L3, fill objective, cash ≥ −15 cr, triggers, CSCO owner, 60-day review vs the M5 baseline.\n\nFull answers: [Trishul](#/pyq-trishul) · [Kaveri](#/pyq-kaveri).\n\n## ⭐⭐ Basic SCM you must not miss\n- **Bullwhip** causes: demand-signal processing, batching, price swings, shortage gaming. **Cures:** POS sharing, VMI/CPFR, smaller batches, EDLP, allocation by past sales.\n- **Push vs pull; efficient vs responsive** (Fisher: functional products need efficient chains; innovative products need responsive ones).\n- **Inventory types:** cycle, safety, pipeline (in transit = d × L), seasonal, anticipation.\n- **KPIs:** fill rate (weighted), OTIF, inventory turns and days, cash-to-cash, perfect order, forecast accuracy/bias, cost-to-serve.\n- **SCOR:** Plan, Source, Make, Deliver, Return, Enable.\n- **S&OP:** one consensus number linking demand, supply and finance monthly. This is the cure for \"multiple forecasts with agendas\".\n\nSee [Ch 0: SCM fundamentals](#/ch00-scm-fundamentals).\n\n## ⭐ Phrases that score\n- \"Forecasting attacks safety stock, not cycle stock.\"\n- \"Precision pays only when a decision changes.\"\n- \"Foundations before intelligence.\"\n- \"Trust is designed, not demanded.\"\n- \"Guardrails live outside the model.\"\n- \"Autonomy is granted per decision.\"\n- \"The mandate is the management.\"\n- \"A perfectly solved LP with the wrong mandate gives a perfectly wrong plan.\"\n- \"Optimise the policy, not the forecast.\"\n- \"Learned policies wear proven guardrails.\"\n- \"Signals earn their weights.\"\n- \"The accuracy bar scales with what the clause decides.\"\n- \"Tune before you retire.\"\n- \"Label before you act.\"\n- \"Coordination beats speed.\"\n- \"Guardrails only count when you use them when it costs you something.\"\n- \"Sequence beats speed; building is not operating.\"\n"}, {"slug": "exam-playbook", "label": "Exam Playbook", "tag": "EX", "group": "focus", "title": "Exam Playbook — how to answer the case-based end-sem", "md": "# Exam Playbook — how to answer the case-based end-sem\nYour faculty's note 📘: *\"Go through all the frameworks covered during the sessions and refer to the attached question papers. The examination will focus on **case-based applications** and **direct numerical problems**. There will be **no standalone theory questions**, and the numericals will involve **straightforward calculations without requiring any additional formulas**. **Basic concepts of SCM** will also be required. … a comprehensive, application-based case study covering all concepts taught throughout the course, including the integration of AI and SCM … projects, class tests, case studies and the **Chain Reaction simulation** … Revise the case studies and frameworks, with particular emphasis on the **strategies, decisions and management approaches used in the Chain Reaction simulation**.\"*\n\nSo the paper will look like the two past papers ([Trishul](pyq-trishul.md), [Kaveri Motors](pyq-kaveri.md)): a 1½–2 page case with 3–4 exhibits (some deliberately irrelevant or defective), **Section A — diagnostics** (calculations, data triage, exposing a claim) and **Section B — a recommendation/mandate** (rank, choose, design a gate). This page is the method.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The two non-negotiable rules (they are printed on the paper)\n### Rule 1 — The Assumptions line ⭐⭐⭐\nAny answer that makes a **recommendation, ranking, ruling, verdict or go/no-go call** must **open** with:\n\n> *Assumptions: A1. … A2. … A3. …*\n\n- **Unstated assumption your answer depends on = marks lost. Stated assumption = marks earned.** 📘\n- Write **3–6** assumptions, numbered, each one sentence, each one something your answer **actually depends on**. Not filler (\"the data is correct\") — useful ones (\"cycle stock is set by full-truck multiples, so forecasting does not change Q\").\n- Good assumption types (pick what your calculation or choice leans on):\n\n| Type | Example |\n|---|---|\n| **Scope / base** | \"Base = cycle + safety stock (₹13.6 cr); in-transit excluded because lead time, not forecast, drives it.\" |\n| **Mechanism** | \"A better forecast reduces only safety stock; order quantity stays at full-truck multiples.\" |\n| **Mix / allocation** | \"Lost-sales mix = month-5 sales mix (219/96/59).\" |\n| **Cost definition** | \"Contribution = price − unit production cost; overheads are fixed and excluded.\" |\n| **Evidence quality** | \"Vendor reference results (another firm, another context) are a ceiling, not a forecast.\" |\n| **Readiness / timing** | \"Vendor-master dedup can be done in Phase 0 (6–8 weeks) by a named owner.\" |\n| **Regulatory** | \"EU AI Act high-risk duties already apply (enforceable Aug 2026).\" |\n| **Units** | \"₹1 crore = 100 lakh; figures are annual averages at cost.\" |\n\n### Rule 2 — Show units and arithmetic ⭐⭐⭐\n\"**One clean calculation beats three vague ones.**\" 📘 Write the calculation as a small table or a line: *what → arithmetic → result with units*. Then **state what the number means for the decision**. Examiners reward: the right **base**, the right **pool**, the **ratio to the claim**, and a **verdict**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The universal answer skeleton\nUse this for every Section B question and any Section A question that asks for a verdict.\n\n1. **Assumptions:** A1 … A2 … A3 … (numbered, decision-relevant).\n2. **The number(s):** one clean calculation with units (pool size, ceiling, break-even, contribution forgone, cost-per-decision…).\n3. **The verdict / choice / ranking** in one sentence.\n4. **The confrontation:** one line on the strongest evidence *against* your choice, and why it does not change it (Kaveri B1 asks for this explicitly).\n5. **The design** (if asked): mandate or gate — objective metric, constraint, escalation trigger, autonomy level (L1–L5) with a one-line justification, named owner.\n6. **The test:** frozen baseline → success metric(s) with thresholds → guarded counter-metric → the evidence that would make you **demote / scrap / change your mind**.\n\n> Memory hook: **\"A-N-V-C-D-T\"** — Assumptions, Number, Verdict, Confront, Design, Test.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data triage — the four defect patterns (and how to expose them)\nBoth papers deliberately plant noise and defects 📘. Treat every exhibit item as one of: **load-bearing**, **irrelevant**, **unreliable (stale/vendor/anecdote)**, or **defective (doesn't add up / contradicted)**.\n\n| Defect pattern (Kaveri paper's own list 📘) | What it looks like | How to expose it (show, don't assert) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cherry-picks its month** | \"Month 4 proved we self-correct (+₹3.6 cr)\" | Show *why* that month was different (production cut from ₹26.4 to ₹16.8 cr forced by chip allocation) and what the next month did (−₹1.9 cr). |\n| **Hides a trend inside an average** | \"Season fill averages 73.7%\" | List the series (86.0 → 85.9 → 73.8 → 64.5 → 58.2), give the current value and the volume-weighted figure (72.5%). |\n| **Reports activity as outcome** | \"Foundations L4, six capabilities built or building\" | Count what is *working*: 1 of 3 live agents hits its objective; routing agent net −₹2.6 L; key agent NOT BUILT. |\n| **Argues insurance logic backwards** | \"No rail — road has always sufficed; book-ahead charge is money on capacity we may never use\" | Insurance is valued against the bad state: road failed in M5 (₹60 L, 0% cover); rail is ~25% cheaper and the M2 alert flagged 0 slots on 12 lanes. |\n| **Doesn't add up** (Trishul) | \"Total ₹15.2 cr = 11.0 + 2.6 + 1.2\" | 11.0 + 2.6 + 1.2 = 14.8 ≠ 15.2 → ₹0.4 cr unexplained. |\n| **Stale and contradicted** (Trishul) | \"Duplicates resolved to under 2%\" (report 14 months old) | 2,890 − 2,410 = 480 surplus records → 480 ÷ 2,890 = 16.6% (≈18%). |\n| **Vanity / irrelevant** | App downloads, website bounce rate, GST compliance, CSR, lease, canteen, solar, HR survey | One line each: \"not linked to the cost pool / decision under review\". |\n| **Evidence laundering** | \"European reference customer\", \"well-known retail deployments\", market-size stats | Another firm's data ≠ mechanism proof here; treat as a ceiling (S1: \"judge mechanism and benchmark, not momentum\" 📘). |\n\n**Part (c) style questions** (\"what does the corrected figure do to your verdict?\") — answer honestly in one line, including **\"it does not change it\"** when that is the truth.\n\n**\"Which item is load-bearing and must be verified first?\"** — pick the item that (1) the next decision rests on, (2) commits money or capacity irreversibly / ahead of time, and (3) is unverified. In Kaveri that is the month-6 materials order + no-rail claim.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Framework picker — \"if the question says X, use Y\"\n| If the case asks you to… | Use this course framework | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Test a vendor's % claim (inventory, stockouts, savings) | **Four-Question AI Claim Audit** (mechanism, cost pool, decision changed, evidence) + **Q vs S pool sizing** | [Frameworks §1](course-frameworks.md), [S1](ch01-ai-in-operations-and-scm.md) |\n| Say whether a firm is ready | **Five-pillar Data & Tech Readiness Scorecard** (quality, masters, architecture, security, compute) + **six data-quality dimensions** + **predictive vs agentic requirements delta** | [S2](ch02-ai-readiness-data-technology.md) |\n| Choose / rank pilots | **Pilot-selection matrix** (value, data feasibility, decision impact, blast radius, sponsor, learning) | [S3](ch03-ai-readiness-process-organisation.md) |\n| Place a decision and set AI posture | **Three decision levels** (strategic = input only; tactical = augment; operational = delegate) | S3 |\n| Plan the roll-out | **Phased roadmap with gates** (Phase 0 foundation → 1 pilot → 2 scale → 3 transform; frozen baseline; readiness re-score; explicit autonomy decision) | S3 |\n| Decide how much autonomy | **Five-level autonomy ladder** (L1 assist → L5 self-improving) + **Agentic Suitability Screen** (frequency, reversibility, data/feedback, rule clarity, stakes; 20+ = L3–L4) | [S4](ch04-agentic-ai-systems.md) |\n| Design controls | **Governance stack** (hard constraints outside the model, identity & least privilege, observability, escalation & kill switch, named owner) | S4 |\n| Pick a platform / build vs buy | **Six filters in order** (use-case fit, data gravity, governance fit, ecosystem & skills, TCO & pricing risk, lock-in) + \"own the differentiating loop, rent the rest\" | [S5](ch05-ai-platforms.md) |\n| Supervise an ML model / accuracy claim | **ML Framing Canvas** (decision, target, features, benchmark, loss) + naive benchmark + leakage test | [S6](ch06-machine-learning-for-operations.md) |\n| Get a notebook to production | **Seven-step workflow** + **capability checklist** (reproducible, monitored, owner, fallback, on-call) | S7 |\n| Buy external data / demand signals | **Five-step Signal Audit** (lead time, mechanism, decision test, cost vs value, weight check) | [S8/S11](ch07-demand-forecasting.md) |\n| Choose a forecasting tool tier | **Tier matching / Bake-off ladder** (count it, audit readiness, name the skill, check governance, bake it off) | S9/S12 |\n| Evaluate a learned inventory policy (DRL \"20% better\") | **Guardrailed Pilot — six elements** (scope, bounds, fallback, evaluation, trust loop, ownership) + theory ceiling (myopic within ~5%) | [S10](ch08-inventory-optimisation.md) |\n| Fund sensors / a digital twin | **Twin-Now / Twin-Later Test** (decision-changing, failure cost, data readiness, fidelity, verdict) | [S13](ch09-predictive-maintenance-digital-twins.md) |\n| Hand logistics/flows to an agent | **Mandate Card** (service floor, cost ceiling, mode priority, shock trigger, review cadence) | [S14](ch10-logistics-routing-optimisation.md) |\n| Hand a network to a solver | **Optimizer Mandate Checklist** (service targets per zone, cost weights, review rule L2/L3/L4, re-solve trigger, verify fit) | S15 |\n| Spec contract/RFx NLP | **Clause-Stakes Specification Checklist** (list clauses, name decision, set bar per clause, pick tier, name the exception) | [S16](ch11-nlp-procurement-token-economics.md) |\n| Agent cost has jumped | **Tune-or-Retire Checklist** (cost per decision, value per decision, ratio, tune first, retire if still failing) | S17 |\n| Use GenAI scenarios | **Scenario Portfolio Checklist** (generate wide, label plausible/stretch/implausible, select two, specify survival, guard the label) | [S18](ch12-genai-design-multi-agent.md) |\n| Multiple agents interacting / bullwhip | **Shared demand signal, order smoothing/rate limits, trained coordination**; **ACAP self-check** | S19 |\n| Bias / incident / who is accountable | **ACAP governance checklist** (accountability, capability limits, auditability, proportionality, post-incident learning) + EU AI Act deployer duties | [S20](ch13-responsible-ai-ethics.md) |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Writing a mandate (the Section B centre-piece)\nEvery mandate answer has the same seven slots. Learn them as a card:\n\n| Slot | What to write | Good example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Objective metric (ONE)** | The single number the agent/initiative is accountable for | \"Volume-weighted fill % across all zones\" / \"invoice-verified net savings %\" |\n| **Constraint (may not breach)** | A hard limit enforced outside the model | \"Month-end cash ≥ −₹15 cr\"; \"no deal > ₹5 L/yr without approval\"; \"approved suppliers only\" |\n| **Escalation trigger** | The event that returns control to humans | \"Lane capacity loss > 20%\", \"zone fill forecast < 60%\", \"confidence below threshold\", \"anomaly in order size > 3σ\" |\n| **Starting autonomy** | L1–L5 with one-line justification | \"L3: routine cases end-to-end, exceptions approved — gate met but no track record\" |\n| **Named owner** | A person/role, not \"the team\" | \"CSCO accountable; Head of Distribution Planning operates\" |\n| **Frozen baseline** | Values frozen before go-live (ideally with a control group) | \"M5: fill 58.2%, lost 269, auto-push 276, rail 0, cash −₹13.3 cr\" |\n| **Success metric + threshold, guarded counter-metric, kill/demote evidence** | One or two success thresholds; one metric that must NOT get worse; the result that changes your mind | \"Fill ≥ 75% in M7; cost/car ≤ M5 +10%; scrap if placed-but-unsold\" |\n\n**Choosing the starting autonomy level — a quick rule:**\n| Situation | Start at |\n|---|---|\n| Regulated decision (EU AI Act high-risk, human oversight required), strategic, irreversible | **L1–L2** (recommend; human decides) |\n| Routine, reversible, bounded, but no track record / readiness just fixed | **L3** (act with approval gates/thresholds) |\n| High-frequency, low-value, reversible, clear mandate, proven in pilot | **L4** (bounded autonomy, monitor and audit) |\n| Self-improving, coordinating peers | **L5** — \"mostly aspirational, 2026\" 📘; never as a starting point (and never below gate — see Kaveri's repricing agent) |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Numericals — the patterns that keep appearing\n(All worked in full in [Numericals](numericals.md).)\n1. **Reachable-pool test:** claim × base vs the pool the tool can actually touch (S ÷ (Q/2 + S)).\n2. **Carrying-cost value:** inventory reduction × carrying rate vs annual fee.\n3. **Value pool & break-even rate:** addressable spend = total × un-addressed share; break-even = annual cost ÷ pool.\n4. **Contribution forgone:** lost units × mix × (price − unit cost).\n5. **Simple vs weighted averages** (fill, accuracy) — expose trends hidden in averages.\n6. **Exhibit reconciliation:** do components add to the total? Does the roll-forward (cash, stock) reconcile?\n7. **Cost per decision vs value per decision** (token economics).\n8. **Fill rate / service level, safety stock, EOQ, reorder point, inventory turns, days of supply** (basic SCM).\n9. **Scoring frameworks:** suitability screen totals (20+ / 12–19 / <12), readiness profiles ([2,1,3,2,5] → predictive pilot on the cleanest domain).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Time plan for a 10-mark case (≈ 60 minutes)\n| Minutes | Do |\n|---|---|\n| 0–8 | Read questions first, then the case. Mark every exhibit item L (load-bearing), I (irrelevant), U (unreliable), D (defective). |\n| 8–15 | Do the arithmetic on scrap: totals, averages, pools, ratios. Check that every total adds up. |\n| 15–35 | Section A answers — each with its calculation table and one-line verdict. |\n| 35–55 | Section B — assumptions, choice, confrontation, mandate table, gate table, demotion sentence. |\n| 55–60 | Check every verdict has an Assumptions line and every number has units. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Phrases that earn marks (course vocabulary)\n- \"Follow the cost pool, not the technology.\" · \"Forecasting attacks S, not Q.\" · \"Precision pays only when a decision changes.\"\n- \"Foundations before intelligence; semantic before agentic.\" · \"Data defects compound at machine speed.\" · \"Autonomy raises every bar.\"\n- \"Guardrails live outside the model.\" · \"The mandate is the management.\" · \"Autonomy is granted per decision and earned by evidence.\"\n- \"Stability is mechanism design, not intelligence.\" · \"Coordination beats speed.\"\n- \"No model without a naive benchmark.\" · \"Frame the decision before the model.\" · \"A model in a notebook is not a capability.\"\n- \"Optimize the policy, not the forecast.\" · \"Learned policies wear proven guardrails.\"\n- \"A perfectly solved LP with the wrong mandate produces a perfectly wrong plan.\"\n- \"Tune before you retire.\" · \"Label before you act.\" · \"Sequence beats speed; building is not operating.\" (Chain Reaction debrief 📘)\n- \"Guardrails only count as guardrails if you use them when it costs you something.\"\n\n## 9. Ten mistakes that cost marks\n1. No assumptions line on a verdict. 2. Numbers without units. 3. Using a total that doesn't add up. 4. Trusting a stale or vendor figure. 5. Treating revenue loss as profit loss. 6. Recommending L4/L5 for a regulated or irreversible decision. 7. \"A human will check it\" as the only control (the oversight paradox 📘). 8. Prompt instructions offered as controls. 9. A success metric with no counter-metric (gaming: cut inventory, buy stockouts). 10. No demotion/scrap evidence — a gate that cannot fail is not a gate.\n"}, {"slug": "pyq-trishul", "label": "Past Paper: Trishul", "tag": "PYQ", "group": "focus", "title": "Past Paper 1 — Trishul Consumer Products (questions + model answers)", "md": "# Past Paper 1 — Trishul Consumer Products (questions + model answers)\nThis is the **class-test / past-paper case** from your uploads 📘, typed out in full, followed by **fully worked model answers** in the mandatory format. Read the question block first, try it yourself for 25 minutes, then compare. Every answer opens with a numbered **Assumptions line**, shows **units and arithmetic**, and names the course framework it uses.\n\n> **How the marks work (from the paper's own instructions 📘):** \"Any answer that makes a recommendation, ranking, verdict, or go/no-go call must open with a numbered assumptions line in the form *Assumptions: A1. … A2. … A3. …* An unstated assumption that your answer depends on **costs** marks; a stated one **earns** them.\" And: \"Show units and arithmetic. **One clean calculation beats three vague ones.**\"\n\n---\n\n## The question paper (verbatim)\n\n### Instructions to candidates\n- **Assumptions line (mandatory).** Any answer that makes a recommendation, ranking, verdict, or go/no-go call must open with a numbered assumptions line in the form: *Assumptions: A1. … A2. … A3. …* An unstated assumption that your answer depends on costs marks; a stated one earns them.\n- **Show units and arithmetic.** One clean calculation beats three vague ones.\n\n### The Case: Trishul Consumer Products\nTrishul Consumer Products manufactures personal-care goods at three plants (Baddi, Vapi, Mysuru), selling through 1,900 distributors across India and, increasingly, to EU retailers (12% of revenue, growing ~30% annually). An internal readiness assessment scored the firm **[Data quality 2, Master data 2, Architecture 3, Security 3, Compute 4]**. The board has approved an \"AI acceleration\" budget of **₹6 crore per year**. Three proposals compete for it — the exhibits below contain the data the steering committee assembled. As in real steering packs, NOT everything in the exhibits matters.\n\n- **Proposal 1 — ML demand forecasting platform** — ₹1.6 crore/yr subscription + ₹0.9 crore integration. Vendor claim: \"30% inventory reduction and 50% fewer stockouts,\" citing a European reference customer.\n- **Proposal 2 — Autonomous procurement agent for indirect/MRO tail spend** — ₹1.1 crore/yr. Negotiates with small suppliers inside category-manager mandates. Vendor cites well-known retail deployments.\n- **Proposal 3 — Supplier-risk scoring model** — ₹0.8 crore/yr. Scores would automatically influence contract renewals, including for the EU-serving plants. The CEO wants at least one proposal live in six months and has asked what \"the agent decides\" would mean, legally and managerially, for each.\n\n### Exhibit 1 — Operations & finance extract (annual, consolidated)\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Revenue | ₹1,240 crore |\n| Marketing & brand spend | ₹86 crore |\n| Average cycle stock (Q/2) | ₹11.0 crore |\n| Average safety stock (S) | ₹2.6 crore |\n| Inventory carrying rate | 19% |\n| Estimated stockout revenue loss | ₹7.8 crore/yr |\n| Order quantities | full-truck multiples (freight economics) |\n| Replenishment frequency | weekly to all 4 regional DCs |\n| Direct materials spend | ₹410 crore (78 suppliers) |\n| Indirect/MRO tail spend | ₹64 crore (2,410 suppliers) |\n| Tail spend currently negotiated | ~9% of tail suppliers |\n| Corporate office lease | ₹4.2 crore/yr |\n| New product launches last year | 14 |\n| CSR programme budget | ₹3.1 crore |\n| Distributor count | 1,900 |\n| Average distributor payment terms | 21 days |\n\n### Exhibit 2 — Systems & data landscape\n- ERP: single instance, 11 years old; **61% of planning decisions executed in spreadsheets** alongside it\n- **Vendor master: 2,890 records for ~2,410 real suppliers (~18% duplicates); no named owner**\n- Item master: attribute conflicts on 11% of SKUs between ERP and the distributor portal\n- Distributor portal: legacy; sales data arrives via **overnight batch (up to 26h latency); covers 71% of revenue**\n- E-commerce & modern trade feeds: API-based, near-real-time; 29% of revenue\n- Cloud & compute: modern tenant, data-science team of 6, GPU credits unused\n- **API inventory: last audited 14 months ago; two vendor-facing credentials rotated after a phishing attempt**\n- Employee engagement survey: 74% favourable (up 3 points); IT helpdesk CSAT 4.1/5\n- Website relaunch: completed last quarter, ₹1.8 crore, bounce rate down 22%\n\n### Exhibit 3 — Supplier & EU exposure extract\n- Tail-spend supplier profile: **median annual spend ₹1.9 lakh; 84% single-quoted at renewal; median payment terms 30 days**\n- Direct-material supplier concentration: top 10 = 62% of direct spend; dual-sourced on 7 of 10\n- EU retailer contracts: 3 chains, renewal decisions quarterly; supplier-risk scores would feed these from Q3\n- **EU regulatory note (legal memo): high-risk AI obligations enforceable Aug 2026; duty split between provider and deployer; human oversight of scored decisions required**\n- Fleet & logistics: own fleet age 6.2 years; diesel price hedge expires in Q4\n- Plant canteen contract: renewed for 3 years, ₹0.6 crore/yr\n- Warehouse solar installation: 2.1 MW, commissioned last year, 31% of DC power\n\n### Exhibit 4 — Supplementary extract (circulated by the steering committee two days before the meeting)\n- **Working-capital note (finance): total average inventory ₹15.2 crore, comprising cycle stock ₹11.0 crore, safety stock ₹2.6 crore and in-transit stock ₹1.2 crore**\n- **Vendor-master health (IT status report, dated 14 months ago): \"duplicate supplier records resolved to under 2%\"**\n- Distributor mobile app: 41,000 downloads; average rating 4.3 stars\n- GST e-invoicing compliance: 99.2% of invoices cleared without error last quarter\n- Plant workforce attrition: 7.9%, against an industry benchmark of 11%\n\n### Section A — Case Diagnostics (5 marks: A2 carries 2 marks, all others 1 mark)\n**A1.** Proposal 1 claims a 30% inventory reduction. Using the exhibits, show the ONE calculation that best tests the claim, state your verdict, and open with a numbered assumptions line (at least two assumptions your calculation depends on). Be explicit about which supplied inventory figure you used as your base, and why.\n\n**A2. (2 marks) Data triage and reliability.** Which supplied data items are irrelevant or unreliable for the three proposal decisions? Across Exhibits 1–4: (a) name at least THREE such items, with one line each on why; (b) at least one supplied figure is defective — identify it and show the arithmetic or the contradiction that exposes it; (c) state in one line what the corrected figure does (or does not do) to your A1 verdict.\n\n**A3.** Using Exhibits 1 and 3, size the value pool Proposal 2 attacks (show the calculation), state the break-even savings rate the agent must achieve, and name the single readiness fix (Exhibit 2) that must precede it — with the concrete failure if skipped.\n\n**A4.** Using Exhibit 3, state the regulatory consequence for Proposal 3 and ONE design obligation it imposes on Trishul (not the vendor).\n\n### Section B — Design & Recommendation Task (5 marks)\n**B1.** Rank the three proposals for the six-month deadline using pilot-selection logic (value, feasibility, blast radius). Your answer must contain, in order:\n- a numbered assumptions line (*Assumptions: A1. … A2. … A3. …*) — the assumptions your ranking depends on;\n- the ranking with one line of justification for your #1;\n- the Phase 1 → Phase 2 gate for your #1: frozen baseline, two success metrics with thresholds, one guarded counter-metric, the starting autonomy level for any agentic component, and who decides;\n- one sentence on what evidence at the gate would make you DEMOTE your #1 — i.e., the result that would change your mind.\n\n---\n\n## What the paper is really testing (read this before the answers)\n| Question | Course tool it tests | Session |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | **Forecasting attacks S, not Q** — size the reachable pool first (Class Q 1.2 is almost the same question) | S1 📘 |\n| A2 | **Data triage** — six data-quality dimensions; uniqueness (duplicates); stale reports; vanity metrics; \"steering packs contain noise\" | S2 📘 |\n| A3 | **Value pool & break-even**; Walmart–Pactum tail-spend evidence; **master data before agents**; API security before write-access agents | S1, S2, S4 📘 |\n| A4 | **EU AI Act provider/deployer duty split**; human oversight cannot be delegated to the vendor (Class Q 5.5) | S2, S5, S20 📘 |\n| B1 | **Pilot-selection matrix** (value × feasibility × blast radius), **phased roadmap gate** (frozen baseline, guarded metrics), **autonomy ladder L1–L5**, named owner | S3, S4, S10 📘 |\n\n> **The trap list for this paper:** (1) taking the vendor's 30% at face value; (2) using the ₹15.2 cr total without checking it adds up; (3) believing the 14-month-old \"under 2% duplicates\" report over the live 2,890-vs-2,410 count; (4) letting Proposal 3 \"decide\" renewals automatically when the law requires human oversight; (5) ranking Proposal 1 first because forecasting \"sounds strategic\".\n\n---\n\n## A1 — Model answer (1 mark)\n\n**Assumptions:** A1. A forecasting platform reduces only **safety stock (S)**; **cycle stock (Q/2)** is set by full-truck order multiples and the weekly replenishment cadence, which Proposal 1 does not change. A2. The vendor's \"30%\" is measured on **on-hand inventory (cycle + safety)**, the stock an ordering policy controls. A3. In-transit stock (₹1.2 cr) is driven by lead time and transport, not forecast accuracy, so it is excluded from the base. A4. All figures are annual averages valued at cost, so they can be added. A5. The carrying rate of 19% applies to all inventory.\n\n**Base used and why:** ₹13.6 crore = cycle ₹11.0 cr + safety ₹2.6 cr (Exhibit 1). I do **not** use Exhibit 4's ₹15.2 cr because (i) it includes in-transit stock that forecasting cannot touch and (ii) it does not even add up (11.0 + 2.6 + 1.2 = 14.8, not 15.2 — see A2).\n\n**The one calculation (the reachable pool):**\n\n| Step | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Inventory reduction claimed | 30% × ₹13.6 cr | **₹4.08 cr** |\n| Maximum a perfect forecast can remove (all of S) | ₹2.6 cr | **₹2.6 cr** |\n| Ceiling as a share of the base | 2.6 ÷ 13.6 | **19.1%** |\n| Gap | 4.08 − 2.6 | ₹1.48 cr that cannot exist |\n\n**Verdict: the 30% claim fails on arithmetic before model quality is even discussed.** Even a perfect forecast (zero error → zero safety stock, which is impossible) caps the reduction at **19.1%**. Realistically, a better forecast trims part of S — say a 20–30% cut in forecast error → roughly ₹0.5–0.8 cr, i.e. **about 4–6%** of on-hand inventory. In money: the whole safety-stock pool is worth only 2.6 × 19% = **₹0.49 cr/yr** in carrying cost, against a **₹1.6 cr/yr** subscription (plus ₹0.9 cr integration). The inventory leg of the business case cannot pay for the tool; if Trishul wants a big inventory cut, the lever is **Q** — smaller, more frequent orders and shorter lead times (the **Campbell Soup lesson** 📘), not forecast precision. The \"50% fewer stockouts\" leg (worth at most a share of the ₹7.8 cr revenue loss, i.e. only its margin) must be tested separately on Trishul's own data.\n\n> **Why this is the \"ONE calculation\":** Session 1 Class Q 1.2 — *\"₹8 crore cycle stock, ₹2 crore safety stock, vendor claims 30% cut. What single calculation kills or saves this claim? → Size the safety-stock share of total inventory.\"* 📘 The lesson: **follow the cost pool, not the technology.**\n\n---\n\n## A2 — Model answer (2 marks)\n\n### (a) Irrelevant or unreliable items (name at least three)\n| # | Item (exhibit) | Irrelevant or unreliable? | One-line reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Marketing & brand spend ₹86 cr (Ex 1) | Irrelevant | None of the three proposals touches marketing; it is not part of any cost pool being attacked. |\n| 2 | Corporate lease ₹4.2 cr, CSR ₹3.1 cr (Ex 1), canteen ₹0.6 cr, solar 2.1 MW (Ex 3) | Irrelevant | Fixed overheads and sustainability facts with no link to inventory, tail spend or supplier risk. |\n| 3 | Website relaunch ₹1.8 cr, bounce rate −22% (Ex 2) | Irrelevant (vanity) | A marketing-site metric; says nothing about planning data, vendor data or API security. |\n| 4 | Distributor app 41,000 downloads, 4.3 stars (Ex 4) | Irrelevant (vanity/activity) | Measures adoption and sentiment, not the 26-hour data latency that actually limits forecasting. |\n| 5 | Employee engagement 74%, helpdesk CSAT 4.1, attrition 7.9% vs 11% (Ex 2, Ex 4) | Irrelevant | Healthy HR/IT-service signals, but none is a readiness pillar for these decisions. |\n| 6 | GST e-invoicing 99.2% (Ex 4) | Irrelevant / misleading | Shows invoice *compliance*, not vendor-master *uniqueness*; easily misread as \"our data is clean\". |\n| 7 | Fleet age 6.2 years, diesel hedge expiring Q4 (Ex 3) | Irrelevant | Logistics cost facts; no proposal is a fleet or fuel decision. |\n| 8 | Vendor's \"European reference customer\" (P1) | Unreliable | Another firm's data, different readiness and context — evidence laundering, not mechanism proof. |\n| 9 | IT status report \"duplicates under 2%\" (Ex 4) | Unreliable (stale and contradicted) | 14 months old; the live count in Exhibit 2 shows ~18%. |\n| 10 | Total average inventory ₹15.2 cr (Ex 4) | Unreliable (defective) | Its own components do not add up — see (b). |\n\n### (b) The defective figure(s) — show the arithmetic\n**Defect 1 — the working-capital total does not add up.**\nCycle ₹11.0 cr + safety ₹2.6 cr + in-transit ₹1.2 cr = **₹14.8 cr**, but the note states **₹15.2 cr** → ₹0.4 cr (≈2.7%) is unexplained. Either a component is missing or the total is wrong; it cannot be used as stated.\n\n**Defect 2 — the vendor-master \"under 2% duplicates\" claim is contradicted.**\nExhibit 2: 2,890 records for ~2,410 real suppliers → 2,890 − 2,410 = **480 surplus records**. 480 ÷ 2,890 = **16.6% of records are duplicates** (or 480 ÷ 2,410 = 19.9% over the true count) — about 18%, i.e. **8–10× the \"under 2%\" claimed**. The report is 14 months old, and the vendor master has **no named owner**, so nobody maintained it after the clean-up. (A data-quality *uniqueness* failure — Session 2's six dimensions 📘.)\n\n### (c) Effect of the corrected figure on the A1 verdict (one line)\nUsing the corrected total ₹14.8 cr, safety stock is 2.6 ÷ 14.8 = **17.6%** of inventory (and 30% of 14.8 = ₹4.44 cr, still far above the ₹2.6 cr pool), so **the correction does not rescue the claim — it lowers the ceiling further and the verdict \"30% is unreachable\" stands.**\n\n> Examiner tip: part (c) rewards saying **\"does not change the verdict\"** when that is true. Do not invent a change.\n\n---\n\n## A3 — Model answer (1 mark)\n\n**Assumptions:** A1. Tail spend is spread roughly evenly across suppliers (mean ₹64 cr ÷ 2,410 = ₹2.66 lakh vs median ₹1.9 lakh — mildly right-skewed, so this is a fair first cut). A2. The ~9% already-negotiated suppliers are excluded from the pool (the agent's value is on suppliers nobody negotiates with). A3. The ₹1.1 cr/yr fee is the full annual cost of the agent (no separate integration fee is quoted). A4. Savings are measured as recurring annual price reductions on renewed spend.\n\n**Value pool (Exhibits 1 and 3):**\n\n| Step | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total tail spend | given | ₹64.0 cr across 2,410 suppliers |\n| Share not negotiated today | 100% − 9% | 91% |\n| Un-negotiated suppliers | 2,410 × 0.91 | ≈ 2,193 suppliers |\n| **Addressable value pool** | ₹64 cr × 0.91 | **≈ ₹58.2 cr/yr** |\n| Supporting signal | 84% single-quoted at renewal; median terms 30 days | Little competitive pressure today → negotiable |\n\n**Break-even savings rate:**\n₹1.1 cr ÷ ₹58.2 cr = **1.89% ≈ 1.9%** of addressable spend. (On the full ₹64 cr it would be 1.1 ÷ 64 = 1.72%.)\n*Sensitivity:* if the agent closes only about 65% of negotiations (Walmart–Pactum's 64–68% close rate 📘), the effective pool is 58.2 × 0.65 = ₹37.8 cr and break-even rises to 1.1 ÷ 37.8 = **2.9%** — right at Pactum's ~3% average savings. So the case is **positive but thin**, and the pilot must measure realised savings, not quoted ones.\n*Upside not in the break-even:* Pactum also extended payment terms by ~35 days 📘. On ₹58.2 cr, 35 extra days frees 58.2 × 35/365 = **₹5.6 cr of working capital** (worth ≈ ₹0.56 cr/yr at an assumed 10% cost of capital).\n\n**The single readiness fix that must come first: de-duplicate the vendor master and give it a named owner (golden record per supplier).**\n**Concrete failure if skipped:** 2,890 records for 2,410 suppliers means the agent negotiates with the **same supplier under several records** — it opens parallel negotiations, agrees **different prices and payment terms on duplicate records**, under-states Trishul's real volume with that supplier (so it negotiates from a weaker position), and **double-counts savings**. A human buyer mentally merges duplicates; **an agent transacts on them at machine speed** 📘 (Session 2: \"data defects compound at machine speed\"; Class Q 2.1).\n\n> Also say in one line (it earns credit): **write-access to vendors must wait for an API inventory and per-agent credentials** — two vendor-facing credentials were just rotated after phishing and the API inventory is 14 months old (Class Q 2.3: \"Block until API inventory and access controls exist\" 📘). If the examiner's intended answer is \"security\", this line covers you; but the *single* fix the vendor-master data most directly blocks is dedup, because the agent's counterparties *are* vendor-master records.\n\n---\n\n## A4 — Model answer (1 mark)\n\n**Regulatory consequence:** Because Proposal 3's scores would **automatically influence contract renewals for EU-serving plants and the 3 EU retail chains from Q3**, the legal memo puts it under the **EU AI Act high-risk obligations, which became enforceable in August 2026 — i.e. they already apply** 📘. Duties are **split between the provider (the vendor) and the deployer (Trishul)**, and **human oversight of scored decisions is mandatory**. Therefore \"the agent decides\" is **not legally available**: the model may *score and recommend* (autonomy **L2**), but it may not automatically renew or drop a supplier.\n\n**ONE design obligation on Trishul (the deployer, not the vendor): build the human-oversight workflow.** A named, trained Trishul reviewer (for example the category head for EU-serving plants) must see each score with its main drivers, be able to **override it before any renewal decision executes**, and the decision and rationale must be **logged** for audit. Compliance \"handled by the platform\" is not enough — the vendor carries provider duties, but **oversight of Trishul's use is Trishul's by law** and cannot be delegated (Class Q 5.5 📘). Put the responsibility matrix in the contract.\n\n*(Other acceptable deployer obligations, if you want a second line: keep usage logs; use the system per the provider's instructions; monitor it and report serious incidents; check the input data is relevant and representative — e.g. that the duplicate vendor records do not distort scores.)* 📗\n\n---\n\n## B1 — Model answer (5 marks)\n\n**Assumptions:** A1. \"Live in six months\" means a real pilot making real decisions on a bounded scope, not a full roll-out. A2. Readiness scores [Data 2, Masters 2, Architecture 3, Security 3, Compute 4] are accurate; the unused GPU credits (Compute 4) do not offset weak data — compute is a stranded asset until foundations catch up 📘. A3. Vendor-master dedup (≈480 records) and rotation/inventory of vendor-facing API credentials can be completed in the first 6–8 weeks (Phase 0) by a named owner. A4. Tail spend is reachable as calculated in A3 (₹58.2 cr; break-even ≈1.9%) and Walmart–Pactum-like results (~3% savings, 64–68% close) are a ceiling, not a promise. A5. Gross margin on lost revenue is unknown, so the ₹7.8 cr stockout loss is revenue, not profit. A6. Proposal 3 under the EU AI Act can only run at L2 (human decides).\n\n### Scoring with the pilot-selection matrix 📘\n| Criterion | P1 ML forecasting | P2 Tail-spend agent | P3 Supplier-risk scoring |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Value at stake** | Inventory leg capped at ₹0.49 cr/yr carrying cost vs ₹2.5 cr year-1 cost; stockout leg unproven → **Low** | ₹58.2 cr pool; ~3% → ≈₹1.7 cr/yr vs ₹1.1 cr; plus term extension → **Medium-High** | Protects EU business (12% × ₹1,240 cr = **₹148.8 cr** revenue, growing 30%) and direct supply (top 10 = 62% × ₹410 cr = ₹254 cr) — real but hard to quantify → **Medium** |\n| **Data feasibility** | 71% of sales arrive by 26-hour batch (timeliness binds), 11% SKU conflicts, 61% planning in spreadsheets → **Low** | Needs vendor-master dedup (fixable in weeks) and credential hygiene → **Medium** | Needs supplier data (duplicates distort scores) + human-oversight design → **Medium** |\n| **Decision impact** | Full-truck multiples: forecast gains often map to the same order (rain-forecast reward) → **Low** | Every closed negotiation changes a price/term → **High** | Changes renewals — but quarterly → slow feedback |\n| **Blast radius** | Wrong forecasts hit 1,900 distributors' supply → Medium | Median deal ₹1.9 lakh, reversible, bounded by mandate → **Low** | Wrong score could drop a key EU-serving supplier; regulatory exposure → **High** |\n| **Agentic suitability screen** (frequency, reversibility, feedback, rule clarity, stakes) | Not agentic (predictive) | High frequency, reversible, fast feedback, clear mandate, low stakes → **≈21–23/25 → L3–L4 candidate** | Low frequency, low reversibility, slow feedback, politically loaded → **≈10–12/25 → keep human** |\n| **Sponsor & learning value** | Builds forecasting data pipeline — useful later | Builds clean vendor master + agent governance — reusable | Builds compliance capability |\n\n### Ranking\n**#1 Proposal 2 (tail-spend negotiation agent), #2 Proposal 3 (as L2 decision support only), #3 Proposal 1.**\n\n**One-line justification for #1:** P2 is the only proposal with a **material, measurable pool (₹58.2 cr) whose break-even (≈1.9%) sits below published evidence (~3%)**, the **lowest blast radius** (median ₹1.9 lakh, reversible, mandate-bounded) and a **readiness gap that can be closed inside the six months** — high value × feasible × low blast radius, exactly the Phase-1 profile 📘.\n\n*(Why P1 is last: its headline claim fails A1's arithmetic, its data is the least ready (26-hour latency on 71% of revenue), and its decisions are rain-forecast-shaped. Why P3 is second: cheap at ₹0.8 cr and strategically important for the EU channel, but the law forbids autonomous renewals, so it can only be a recommend-and-review tool — fine as a parallel L2 pilot once the vendor master is clean.)*\n\n### Phase 1 → Phase 2 gate for Proposal 2\n| Gate element | Specification |\n|---|---|\n| **Scope of Phase 1** | 3 MRO categories (e.g. packaging consumables, maintenance spares, lab supplies) ≈ 600 de-duplicated suppliers, ≈ ₹15 cr spend; 4–6 months. |\n| **Frozen baseline** | Frozen **before** go-live: last 12 months' price per item and payment terms per supplier for in-scope categories (median terms **30 days**; **84%** single-quoted; **9%** negotiated), plus supplier OTIF for the same period. Hold out a **randomly chosen control group** of comparable suppliers that the agent does not touch, so savings are measured against a counterfactual, not a moving baseline. |\n| **Success metric 1** | **Realised (invoice-verified) net savings ≥ 2.5%** of renewed spend vs frozen baseline — above the 1.9% break-even with margin for error. |\n| **Success metric 2** | **Median payment terms extended from 30 to ≥ 45 days** on closed deals (or close rate ≥ 40% of negotiations opened). |\n| **Guarded counter-metric** | **Supplier OTIF and quality on in-scope MRO items must not fall by more than 2 percentage points** vs baseline (and no maintenance downtime caused by a supplier walking away) — savings bought by worse service do not count. |\n| **Starting autonomy** | **L3 — act with approval gates.** The agent negotiates and closes inside the category-manager mandate (tradable levers, walk-away price, max term, approved-supplier list) for deals **under ₹5 lakh/yr**; anything above, any mandate edge case, or any non-standard clause is escalated. **Hard limits live outside the model** (spend caps and supplier allow-list in code, per-agent credentials, full action log, kill switch) 📘. Not L4 yet: no track record at Trishul and masters were just cleaned. |\n| **Who decides** | The **CPO (named business owner)** decides promotion at a gate review, with **finance (CFO's nominee) certifying the savings** against the frozen baseline; the head of indirect procurement owns the agent day to day. |\n\n### Demotion evidence (one sentence)\n**If, at the gate, invoice-verified savings are below the 1.9% break-even (or are achieved only with an OTIF drop of more than 2 points, or any transaction is executed on a duplicate or unapproved vendor record), I demote Proposal 2 and promote Proposal 3's L2 pilot to #1.**\n\n---\n\n## Quick-revision summary of this paper\n- **A1:** base = cycle + safety = ₹13.6 cr; 30% = ₹4.08 cr > S = ₹2.6 cr; ceiling 19.1% → claim fails; carrying-cost pool ₹0.49 cr/yr vs ₹1.6 cr/yr fee.\n- **A2:** irrelevant = marketing, lease, CSR, canteen, solar, website, app, GST, HR metrics, fleet; defective = 11.0 + 2.6 + 1.2 = 14.8 ≠ 15.2; \"<2% duplicates\" vs 480/2,890 = 16.6%; correction → 17.6% ceiling, verdict unchanged.\n- **A3:** pool = 64 × 0.91 = ₹58.2 cr; break-even = 1.1 ÷ 58.2 = 1.9%; fix = vendor-master dedup + named owner (else duplicate negotiations/terms/double-counted savings); plus API-credential gate.\n- **A4:** EU AI Act high-risk, already enforceable; provider/deployer split; human oversight mandatory → L2 only; Trishul must build the oversight workflow (named reviewer, override before execution, logging).\n- **B1:** P2 > P3 > P1; L3 start; frozen baseline + control group; savings ≥ 2.5% and terms ≥ 45 days; OTIF guard; CPO decides with finance; demote if savings < 1.9%.\n"}, {"slug": "pyq-kaveri", "label": "Past Paper: Kaveri Motors", "tag": "PYQ", "group": "focus", "title": "Past Paper 2 — Kaveri Motors: a turnaround inside Chain Reaction (questions + model answers)", "md": "# Past Paper 2 — Kaveri Motors: a turnaround inside Chain Reaction (questions + model answers)\nThe second past paper from your uploads 📘 is set **inside the Chain Reaction simulation** you played all season: same plants, dealer zones, physics and five-month calendar, on a composite board. You take over as **Chief Supply Chain Officer at the start of month 6**. This page types out the paper in full and gives **worked model answers** in the mandatory assumptions-first format. Read the [Chain Reaction Playbook](chain-reaction-playbook.md) alongside it.\n\n> **The paper's own rule 📘:** \"Data triage is part of this assessment: at least two claims are defective — a claim may **cherry-pick its month**, **hide a trend inside an average**, **report activity as outcome**, or **argue insurance logic backwards**. Using a defective claim costs marks; exposing one, with the arithmetic that exposes it, earns them.\" Those four defect types map one-to-one onto the four memo claims — learn them as a checklist.\n\n---\n\n## The question paper (verbatim)\n\n### Instructions\n- **Assumptions line (mandatory).** Any answer that makes a recommendation, ruling, verdict, or go/no-go call must open with a numbered assumptions line in the form: *Assumptions: A1. … A2. … A3. …* An unstated assumption that your answer depends on costs marks; a stated one earns them.\n- **Show units and arithmetic.** One clean calculation beats three vague ones.\n\n### The Case: Kaveri Motors — a turnaround review inside your own market\nKaveri Motors is one of twenty boards in the CHAIN REACTION market you have been trading in — same plants, same dealer zones, same physics, same five months. It is a composite board generated by the game engine on this season's calendar: deliberately NOT any team's actual record, and your own team's results are not examined. You have been brought in as Kaveri's new Chief Supply Chain Officer at the start of month 6: cash is negative, board confidence has started to fall, and service fill has declined five months in a row. The outgoing board left you the records below and its handover memo (Exhibit 4). The board has granted you two months and ONE initiative. Read the operation honestly, decide what its own data proves, and write the mandate for what runs next.\n\n### Exhibit 1 — Operating record, months 1–5 (from the company's own board packs)\n| | M1 · Calm | M2 · Monsoon + carrier | M3 · Credit slump + EV surge | M4 · Chip allocation | M5 · Port jam + congestion |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cars sold | 435 | 397 | 259 | 342 | 374 |\n| Lost sales (unmet demand) | 71 | 65 | 92 | 188 | 269 |\n| Service fill % | 86.0% | 85.9% | 73.8% | 64.5% | 58.2% |\n| Cars auto-pushed (unrouted, default lane) | 273 | 280 | 479 | 374 | 276 |\n| Revenue | ₹47.0 cr | ₹40.2 cr | ₹24.5 cr | ₹33.8 cr | ₹36.4 cr |\n| Production cost | ₹27.2 cr | ₹32.6 cr | ₹26.4 cr | ₹16.8 cr | ₹25.1 cr |\n| Monthly profit | +₹6.3 cr | −₹5.8 cr | −₹15.6 cr | +₹3.6 cr | −₹1.9 cr |\n| Cash at month-end | +₹6.3 cr | +₹0.5 cr | −₹15.1 cr | −₹11.5 cr | −₹13.3 cr |\n| Closing stock — plant / RDC / zone | 0 / 226 / 0 | 247 / 165 / 3 | 223 / 330 / 1 | 72 / 556 / 1 | 65 / 662 / 0 |\n| Chip sets contracted | 220 | 745 | 655 | 585 | 515 |\n| Decision tokens spent (of 24) | 14 | 20 | 20 | 17 | 24 |\n| RAIL slots booked for next month | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| Board confidence | 100 | 100 | 100 | 100 | 95 |\n\n*Physics reminder (as you already play under):* unrouted plant stock auto-pushes to the plant's home RDC by road; dealer zones pull only from their own region's RDC. RAIL slots must be booked a month ahead against the demand plan, are ~25% cheaper, and are charged whether used or not. Chip sets are allocated against contracted volume; fees are charged on the full book, used or not. Monthly profit is after freight, storage and fixed overheads (₹12.6 cr–₹13.2 cr a month, not itemised here).\n\n### Exhibit 2 — Ops-report and Transformation-page excerpts (verbatim)\n- M2: \"Rail slot alerts: 12 lanes reporting 0 booked slots for next month.\"\n- M3: \"Building Demand Sensing Agent — 3 tokens, ready M4. Build odds 55%. Commissioned BELOW its gate (its prerequisite, the 2-token Demand Signal Desk Agent, is not live): build odds cut, and whatever ships delivers a fraction of its designed effect until the gap is closed.\"\n- M4: \"CHIP ALLOCATION: contracted volume earned you 462 chip sets; production scaled to match.\"\n- M4: \"Live but underperforming: Mode & Routing Optimization Agent shipped, but it is not hitting its objective.\"\n- M5: \"Building Fully Autonomous Cross-Channel Repricing (Closed Loop) — 7 tokens, ready M7. Build odds 45%. Commissioned BELOW its gate (its prerequisite, the Price-Setting & Segmentation Agent, is not live). The board accepted the risk on the record.\"\n- M5: \"Corridor congestion stranded stock: expedited moves and missed dealer slots. ₹60.0 L charged and board confidence down 5. Cover on this hazard: 0%.\"\n- M5: \"276 unrouted cars auto-pushed from plant yards to the nearest RDC by road (push default).\"\n\n### Exhibit 3 — The transformation estate at end of month 5 (from the Transformation page)\n| Capability | Record | Status at Kaveri |\n|---|---|---|\n| Foundations — Data · Process · Tech | Raised M2, M4 and M5 — 27 tokens across the season | L4 · L4 · L4 |\n| Demand Sensing Agent | Built M3 below gate, 3 tokens | LIVE — underperforming; its prerequisite is still not live |\n| Forecast Error / Bias Tracking | Built M3, 2 tokens | LIVE — hitting its objective |\n| Mode & Routing Optimization Agent | Built M3, 2 tokens | LIVE — underperforming. Season ledger: ₹1.5 L freight saved · ₹4.1 L missed on lanes where no planned flows existed |\n| Cross-Channel Repricing (L5, closed loop) | Building since M5 below gate, 7 tokens; ready M7 | IN BUILD |\n| Distribution Planning Agent | Available — 2 tokens; gate D2 · P1 · T1 (met) | NOT BUILT — plans flows and placement against the demand plan; ends default auto-push |\n\n*Unit production cost:* HATCH ₹3.85 L · SUV ₹8.30 L · EV ₹8.00 L. *Month-5 prices:* ₹6.05 L · ₹15.50 L · ₹14.05 L (cut from list — ₹6.40 / ₹16.00 / ₹14.50 — in months 3–4, never restored). Terminating a capability, live or in build, costs 2 tokens; the build cost is sunk.\n\n### Exhibit 4 — Outgoing board's handover memo (four claims, circulated at handover)\n1. \"Transformation is on track: all three foundations are at Level 4 — as deep as anyone in this industry — and six capabilities are built or building. The estate is the asset the next board inherits.\"\n2. \"Month 4 proved the operating model self-corrects: profit returned (+₹3.6 cr) after the credit-slump month, and month 5 held near break-even (−₹1.9 cr) through the worst congestion of the season.\"\n3. \"Season service fill averages 73.7% — within industry norms for an event-heavy calendar; the shortfall months were market-wide.\"\n4. \"Materials on order for month 6: 245 HATCH · 105 SUV · 65 EV, booked against our month-5 demand plan. No rail for month 6 — road has always sufficed, and rail's book-ahead charge is money spent on capacity we may never use.\"\n\n*Data triage is part of this assessment: at least two claims are defective — a claim may cherry-pick its month, hide a trend inside an average, report activity as outcome, or argue insurance logic backwards. Using a defective claim costs marks; exposing one, with the arithmetic that exposes it, earns them.*\n\n### Section A — Read the running operation (5 marks)\n**A1. (2 marks)** Triage the handover memo against the exhibits. (a) Name TWO claims you would not rely on as stated, with one line each on why; (b) expose ONE of them with the arithmetic or the contradiction — shown, not asserted; (c) one line: which single item in the memo is load-bearing and must be verified first, and why?\n\n**A2. (3 marks)** Month 5 ended with 662 finished cars in RDCs and 269 lost sales in the same month (sales that month ran 219 HATCH · 96 SUV · 59 EV). (a) Using the physics reminder, explain how both can be true at once; (b) estimate the contribution forgone on the 269 lost sales at month-5 prices and unit costs — state your mix assumption — and set it against the month's reported −₹1.9 cr and the ₹60.0 L congestion charge; (c) state the cheapest standing change that stops the pattern recurring. Open with your assumptions line.\n\n### Section B — Write the turnaround mandate (5 marks)\n**B1.** You are Kaveri's incoming CSCO at the start of month 6. Cash is −₹13.3 cr, board confidence is 95 and falling, and 7 tokens are already sunk in a below-gate repricing agent that goes live in month 7. The board grants you TWO months and exactly ONE initiative: **(i) a distribution plan-of-record** — file real flows monthly, hand them to the Distribution Planning Agent (2 tokens, gate already met), place stock where the demand plan sells, and book a standing rail floor; or **(ii) cash stabilisation** — no new builds; rule on the in-build repricing agent (terminate for 2 tokens or let it land), right-size the chip contract, and restore prices toward list. Choose one and write its operating mandate. Your answer must contain, in order:\n- your numbered assumptions line; then the choice — with one line confronting the strongest evidence AGAINST it from the exhibits, and one line on what you STOP doing in month 6;\n- the mandate: the single objective metric it is accountable for; one constraint it may not breach; the escalation trigger that returns control to humans; the starting autonomy level, justified in one line; the named owner;\n- the 60-day review: the frozen baseline, ONE success metric with its threshold, one guarded counter-metric — and the evidence at day 60 that would make you scrap it (2 tokens, build cost sunk) rather than extend it.\n\n---\n\n## The numbers you need first (work these out before writing anything)\n| Check | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fill each month = sold ÷ (sold + lost) | 435/506, 397/462, 259/351, 342/530, 374/643 | 86.0, 85.9, 73.8, 64.5, 58.2% ✓ (board packs are internally consistent) |\n| Simple average of monthly fill (the memo's number) | (86.0+85.9+73.8+64.5+58.2) ÷ 5 = 368.4 ÷ 5 | **73.7%** |\n| Volume-weighted season fill | total sold 1,807 ÷ total demand (1,807 + 685) = 1,807 ÷ 2,492 | **72.5%** |\n| Lost sales trend | 71 → 65 → 92 → 188 → 269 | ×3.8 from M1 to M5 |\n| Implied overheads = revenue − production cost − profit | 47.0−27.2−6.3; 40.2−32.6+5.8; 24.5−26.4+15.6; 33.8−16.8−3.6; 36.4−25.1+1.9 | 13.5, 13.4, 13.7, 13.4, 13.2 cr ≈ the stated ₹12.6–13.2 cr band (+ freight) → profit lines are credible |\n| Cash roll-forward | 6.3 → 6.3−5.8 = 0.5 → 0.5−15.6 = −15.1 → −15.1+3.6 = −11.5 → −11.5−1.9 = −13.4 | ≈ −13.3 ✓ |\n| Finished cars in the system at end-M5 | plant 65 + RDC 662 + zone 0 | **727 cars** |\n| Unit contribution (M5 price − unit cost) | HATCH 6.05−3.85; SUV 15.50−8.30; EV 14.05−8.00 | **₹2.20 L / ₹7.20 L / ₹6.05 L** |\n| M5 revenue check from the sales mix | 219×6.05 + 96×15.50 + 59×14.05 = 1,324.95 + 1,488.0 + 828.95 | ₹3,641.9 L = **₹36.4 cr ✓** (the mix and prices reconcile) |\n| Price cuts still costing money in M5 | 219×0.35 + 96×0.50 + 59×0.45 | ₹151.2 L ≈ **₹1.5 cr/month** given away |\n| Mode & Routing agent net | ₹1.5 L saved − ₹4.1 L missed | **−₹2.6 L** (net loss) |\n| Season tokens used | 14 + 20 + 20 + 17 + 24 | 95 of 120 |\n| Rail slots booked all season | 0+0+0+0+0 | **0**, despite the M2 alert on 12 lanes |\n\n---\n\n## A1 — Model answer (2 marks)\n\n### (a) Two claims I would not rely on as stated\n- **Claim 3 (fill \"averages 73.7%\", \"market-wide\") — hides a trend inside an average.** The 73.7% is a simple average of five falling months (86.0 → 58.2%); it conceals five consecutive declines and a current fill of 58.2%, and \"market-wide\" is asserted without evidence while Kaveri's own RDCs held 662 unsold cars.\n- **Claim 2 (\"Month 4 proved the operating model self-corrects\") — cherry-picks its month.** M4's profit came from a forced production cut (chip allocation: production cost fell from ₹26.4 cr to ₹16.8 cr), not from any correction; fill fell to 64.5% and cash stayed negative, and the \"near break-even\" M5 hides ≈₹11 cr of forgone contribution (A2b).\n- *(Also defective, and acceptable as a second pick:)* **Claim 1 — reports activity as outcome** (L4 foundations and \"six capabilities\" vs. only one of three live agents hitting its objective, one routing agent net −₹2.6 L, one repricing agent below gate, the one agent that fixes placement NOT BUILT; and only 4 capabilities are actually built or building, not six). **Claim 4 — argues insurance logic backwards** (rail is treated as wasted money because \"road has always sufficed\", in the very month road failed: ₹60 L congestion charge with 0% cover).\n\n### (b) Exposing Claim 3 with the arithmetic\n| | M1 | M2 | M3 | M4 | M5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fill % | 86.0 | 85.9 | 73.8 | 64.5 | **58.2** |\n| Lost sales | 71 | 65 | 92 | 188 | **269** |\n\n- Simple average = 368.4 ÷ 5 = **73.7%** — correct arithmetic, misleading statistic.\n- Volume-weighted season fill = 1,807 ÷ 2,492 = **72.5%** — lower still, because the bad months carried more demand (M5 demand 643 vs M1 506).\n- The *current* run-rate is **58.2%**, i.e. **15.5 points below** the quoted average, after **five consecutive declines**; lost sales rose **3.8×** (71 → 269).\n- \"Shortfall months were market-wide\" is contradicted by Kaveri's own stock: in M5 it lost 269 sales **while holding 662 finished cars in its RDCs** — that is a placement failure inside Kaveri, not a market-wide shortage.\n\n*(Alternative exposure of Claim 2:* M4 production cost ₹16.8 cr vs ₹26.4 cr in M3 = **−₹9.6 cr**, forced by the 462-chip-set allocation (\"production scaled to match\"). Revenue 33.8 − production 16.8 − overheads ≈13.4 = **+₹3.6 cr** — the profit is a by-product of producing less, while fill fell 9.3 points to 64.5% and cash was still −₹11.5 cr. One month later the \"model\" lost money again.)*\n\n### (c) The load-bearing item to verify first\n**Claim 4 — the month-6 materials order (245 HATCH · 105 SUV · 65 EV = 415 cars) and \"no rail for month 6\".** It is the only forward-looking commitment in the memo: it locks month-6 cash and capacity **before** month 6 runs (rail must be booked a month ahead; chip fees are charged on the full book), and it was \"booked against the month-5 demand plan\" without netting the **727 finished cars** already in plant yards and RDCs. If it is wrong, month 6 repeats month 5 — more unrouted stock, road-only, 0% cover — so verify it against zone-level demand and current stock before anything else.\n\n---\n\n## A2 — Model answer (3 marks)\n\n**Assumptions:** A1. The 269 lost sales had the **same model mix as the 374 cars sold in M5** (219 HATCH · 96 SUV · 59 EV, i.e. 58.6% / 25.7% / 15.8%). A2. **Contribution per car = month-5 price − unit production cost**; freight, storage and fixed overheads are excluded because they are largely fixed and not itemised. A3. The lost sales were genuine demand that would have bought at month-5 prices (no further discount needed). A4. The 662 RDC cars were saleable models, so the failure is location (and partly mix), not quality. A5. ₹1 crore = 100 lakh.\n\n### (a) How 662 cars in RDCs and 269 lost sales can both be true\nUnder the game's physics a dealer zone can **only pull from its own region's RDC**, and any plant stock that is **not routed** by a plan **auto-pushes by road to the plant's home RDC** (the default lane). Kaveri never filed a distribution plan-of-record (the **Distribution Planning Agent is NOT BUILT**) and booked **zero rail** all season, so in M5 **276 cars** were pushed to the plants' home RDCs regardless of where demand was. Stock therefore piled up in the RDCs **near the plants** (RDC stock 226 → 165 → 330 → 556 → **662**) while zones served by **other** RDCs ran dry; there is no automatic cross-RDC rebalancing, and corridor congestion in M5 stranded what expedited moves were attempted (₹60 L). Aggregate stock was high; **stock in the right region and mix was low**. It is a **placement failure, not a supply failure** — exactly the Mode & Routing ledger's diagnosis (\"₹4.1 L missed on lanes where **no planned flows existed**\").\n\n### (b) Contribution forgone on the 269 lost sales\n| Model | Share of M5 sales | Lost units (269 × share) | Unit contribution (price − cost) | Contribution forgone |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HATCH | 219/374 = 58.6% | 157.5 | 6.05 − 3.85 = ₹2.20 L | 157.5 × 2.20 = **₹346.5 L** |\n| SUV | 96/374 = 25.7% | 69.0 | 15.50 − 8.30 = ₹7.20 L | 69.0 × 7.20 = **₹497.1 L** |\n| EV | 59/374 = 15.8% | 42.4 | 14.05 − 8.00 = ₹6.05 L | 42.4 × 6.05 = **₹256.7 L** |\n| **Total** | | **269** | weighted avg ₹4.09 L/car | **≈ ₹1,100 L = ₹11.0 cr** |\n\n*(Shortcut: weighted contribution per car = (219×2.20 + 96×7.20 + 59×6.05) ÷ 374 = 1,529.95 ÷ 374 = ₹4.09 L; × 269 = ₹1,100 L.)*\n\n**Set against the reported figures:**\n- Forgone contribution **₹11.0 cr ≈ 5.8× the reported loss of ₹1.9 cr** — had those 269 sales been made, M5 would have shown roughly **+₹9.1 cr** (−1.9 + 11.0) instead of \"near break-even\". The memo's \"held near break-even\" hides an ₹11 cr hole.\n- It is **≈ 18× the ₹60 L congestion charge** (1,100 ÷ 60 = 18.3). The visible charge is the small cost; the invisible one (lost contribution) is the big one.\n- And because the cars **already existed** in RDCs, the true cash forgone is closer to the **revenue** on those sales: 269 × ₹9.74 L avg price = **≈ ₹26.2 cr** — almost double the month-end cash deficit of ₹13.3 cr. Selling the stock it already has is Kaveri's biggest cash lever.\n\n### (c) Cheapest standing change that stops the pattern recurring\n**File a monthly distribution plan-of-record — commission the Distribution Planning Agent (2 tokens; its D2·P1·T1 gate is already met by L4 foundations, so it delivers full effect) — so that every car leaving a plant is routed against the zone-level demand plan and the default auto-push ends, with a standing rail floor booked a month ahead on the planned lanes.** Cost: 2 tokens (one-off) plus rail book-ahead charges that are ~25% cheaper than road; benefit at stake: up to ₹11 cr of contribution a month. It also gives the existing Mode & Routing agent the planned flows it was missing.\n\n---\n\n## B1 — Model answer (5 marks)\n\n**Assumptions:** A1. The binding constraint is **placement, not supply or price** — 727 finished cars exist while 269 sales were lost in M5 (A2). A2. The month-5 demand plan is broadly right at zone level (Forecast Error/Bias Tracking is the one capability **hitting its objective**), so placing stock against it will convert into sales. A3. The Distribution Planning Agent's gate (D2·P1·T1) is met by Kaveri's L4 foundations, so it ships at full designed effect and is not subject to below-gate build odds. A4. Rail slots booked a month ahead are ~25% cheaper than road and charged whether used or not; a floor sized to the demand plan's base load will be mostly used. A5. The repricing agent lands in M7 regardless (I am not choosing option ii), so its upkeep is a cost I accept for two months. A6. Monthly token budget stays at 24.\n\n### The choice: **(i) distribution plan-of-record**\nIt attacks the largest pool on the page (₹11.0 cr/month of forgone contribution, or ≈₹26 cr of revenue on cars already built), and it is also the **best cash move**: converting the 727 finished cars into sales stabilises cash faster than any price or chip change.\n\n**Confronting the strongest evidence against it:** cash is −₹13.3 cr and board confidence is falling, and the exhibits show that the last distribution agent Kaveri bought (Mode & Routing) is underperforming (net −₹2.6 L) — so spending 2 more tokens plus rail book-ahead charges looks like more \"transformation theatre\". **My answer:** Mode & Routing failed precisely \"on lanes where **no planned flows existed**\" — it optimises routes but nobody gave it a plan; the Distribution Planning Agent *is* that missing plan, its gate is met (unlike the below-gate builds), and its cost (2 tokens + rail charges ~25% below road) is tiny against ₹11 cr/month lost.\n\n**What I STOP in month 6:** letting any plant stock leave unrouted (no more default auto-push), and producing to the 415-car month-5 materials order without first netting the 727 finished cars already in the system.\n\n### The operating mandate\n| Element | Mandate |\n|---|---|\n| **Single objective metric** | **Volume-weighted service fill % across all 7 dealer zones** (cars sold ÷ (cars sold + lost sales)), reported monthly by zone. |\n| **Constraint it may not breach** | **Month-end cash may not fall below −₹15.0 cr** (≈ ₹1.7 cr of headroom below today's −₹13.3 cr) — rail floor, expedites and transfers must fit inside it. *(Alternative acceptable constraint: distribution cost per car sold ≤ M5 level + 10%.)* |\n| **Escalation trigger (control returns to humans)** | Any **capacity shock that removes more than 20% of a lane's capacity** (port jam, corridor congestion, carrier failure), **or any zone forecast to fall below 60% fill for the coming month**, **or any plan needing expedited/trailer-express spend above ₹20 L** → the agent stops executing and the CSCO re-plans (mandate card \"shock trigger\" 📘). |\n| **Starting autonomy level** | **L3 — act with approval gates:** the agent files the plan-of-record and executes routine flows and the rail floor inside the mandate; exceptions (expedites, rail above the floor, re-plans after a shock) need CSCO approval. *Justification:* gate met and foundations at L4 make execution safe, but the agent has no track record at Kaveri and its sister routing agent underperformed — evidence must be earned before L4. |\n| **Named owner** | **The CSCO (me)** is accountable for outcomes; the Head of Distribution Planning is the day-to-day agent owner. |\n\n*(Mandate card / optimizer mandate checklist mapping 📘: service floor = protect the zones that stocked out most in M5; cost ceiling = cash constraint; mode priority = rail floor for base load, road for flex, trailer-express only by exception; shock trigger = above; review cadence = monthly plan review + day-60 gate.)*\n\n### The 60-day review\n| Element | Specification |\n|---|---|\n| **Frozen baseline (M5, frozen at start of M6)** | Fill **58.2%**; lost sales **269**; RDC stock **662** (727 finished in system); cars auto-pushed **276**; rail slots **0**; cash **−₹13.3 cr**; congestion/expedite charges **₹60 L**. |\n| **ONE success metric + threshold** | **Volume-weighted fill ≥ 75% in month 7** (≈ +17 points; ≈ 160 of the 269 lost sales recovered), with **zero cars auto-pushed unrouted**. |\n| **Guarded counter-metric** | **Distribution cost per car sold** (freight + rail book-ahead + expedites) must not rise more than 10% above M5 — fill must not be bought with expensive expedites or unused rail. *(Equally acceptable: finished stock must not rise above 727 cars — fill must come from placement, not from building more stock.)* |\n| **Scrap (not extend) evidence at day 60** | **If the agent files a plan every month and auto-push falls to zero, but fill improves by less than 5 points (still below ≈63%) and lost sales stay above 200 — i.e. cars are now placed where the plan said yet still do not sell — then placement was not the binding constraint, the demand plan itself (mix/price) is wrong, and I terminate the agent (2 tokens, build cost sunk) and redirect to demand and pricing.** Also scrap if more than half of the booked rail floor goes unused for two months with no fill gain. |\n\n### If you had chosen (ii) instead — what a passing answer looks like\n*(Not recommended, but marks are for reasoning, so know the shape.)* Assumptions would centre on cash being the binding constraint. **Rule on the repricing agent: terminate (2 tokens)** — it is below gate (45% odds, prerequisite not live, fraction of effect), L5 closed-loop pricing on a board that cannot place stock is the wrong autonomy, and 7 tokens are sunk either way. **Right-size the chip book** to real production need (fees are charged on the full book: 515 contracted vs a 415-car materials order). **Restore prices toward list** (the cuts give away ≈₹1.5 cr/month and did not fix fill, because the shortfall is placement). Objective metric: month-end cash; constraint: fill not below 58.2%; trigger: board confidence below 90. Strongest evidence against (ii): it leaves the ₹11 cr/month placement leak untouched and does nothing about 727 idle cars — which is why (i) is the better answer.\n\n---\n\n## Quick-revision summary of this paper\n- **Four memo claims ↔ four defect types:** C1 activity-as-outcome · C2 cherry-picked month · C3 trend hidden in an average · C4 insurance logic backwards.\n- **Key arithmetic:** simple avg fill 73.7% vs weighted 72.5% vs current 58.2%; lost sales 71 → 269; M4 profit = production cut −₹9.6 cr; contribution/car 2.20 / 7.20 / 6.05 L; forgone ₹11.0 cr = 5.8× reported loss, 18× congestion charge; revenue on lost sales ≈ ₹26.2 cr; price cuts cost ₹1.5 cr/month; Mode & Routing net −₹2.6 L; 727 finished cars.\n- **Load-bearing item:** Claim 4 (415-car order + no rail) — the only forward commitment; verify against 727 cars in stock.\n- **Physics answer:** zones pull only from their own RDC + unrouted stock auto-pushes to home RDC + no plan + no rail = stock in the wrong RDCs.\n- **Mandate:** choose (i); fill metric; cash ≥ −₹15 cr constraint; shock/60%-fill/expedite triggers; L3; CSCO owner; baseline M5; fill ≥ 75% in M7; cost-per-car guard; scrap if placed-but-unsold.\n"}, {"slug": "chain-reaction-playbook", "label": "Chain Reaction Playbook", "tag": "CR", "group": "focus", "title": "Chain Reaction Simulation — strategies, decisions and management approaches", "md": "# Chain Reaction Simulation — strategies, decisions and management approaches\nYour faculty asked for **\"particular emphasis on the strategies, decisions and management approaches used in the Chain Reaction simulation\"** 📘. The Kaveri Motors paper was set *inside* the game. This page rebuilds the game's rules from the session decks and the past paper, lists the decisions you make each round, the strategies that won and lost the season, and the course framework behind each one.\n\n> Sources: Sessions 11–20 \"Coming up next\" round briefs 📘, the Kaveri Motors paper's physics reminder and exhibits 📘, and the Session 20 season debrief (four archetypes, twenty teams) 📘. Where a mechanic is inferred rather than stated, it is marked 📗.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The world you play in\n| Element | What the game uses 📘 |\n|---|---|\n| **Market** | ~**20 companies (boards)** trading in one market, same physics, same calendar of shocks. Teams were labelled A1–A10 and B11–B20 (e.g. B12-Titans, A7-Kimi K3, A8-The Odyssey). |\n| **Your company** | A car maker: **3 plants**, **4 regional distribution centres (RDCs)**, **7 dealer zones**. |\n| **Products** | **HATCH, SUV, EV.** Kaveri's unit production costs: HATCH ₹3.85 L · SUV ₹8.30 L · EV ₹8.00 L. List prices ₹6.40 / 16.00 / 14.50 L. |\n| **Transport modes** | **Rail** — cheap (~25% cheaper than road), slow, **slot-constrained**, booked **a month ahead** against the demand plan, **charged whether used or not**. **Road** — the flexible workhorse; also the **default lane** for unrouted stock. **Trailer-express** — fast, expensive, capacity-limited (for urgent top-ups). |\n| **Physics** | **Unrouted plant stock auto-pushes to the plant's home RDC by road**. **Dealer zones pull only from their own region's RDC.** → if you do not plan flows, stock piles up near the plants and other zones stock out. |\n| **Components** | **Chip sets are allocated against contracted volume**; **fees are charged on the full book, used or not** (M4 Kaveri: \"contracted volume earned you 462 chip sets; production scaled to match\"). |\n| **Money** | Monthly profit is after freight, storage and **fixed overheads (~₹12.6–13.2 cr/month)**; cash rolls forward month to month. |\n| **Shocks (event calendar)** | M1 Calm · M2 Monsoon + carrier failure · M3 Credit slump + EV surge · M4 Chip allocation · M5 Port jam + corridor congestion (Kaveri's season). Hazards can be insured — \"**Cover on this hazard: 0%**\" means no insurance was bought. |\n| **Attention budget** | **24 decision tokens a month**; **cap of 4 transformation actions a round**; **2 tokens to generate an operating plan, 1 to regenerate it**; **every agent you own charges 1–4 tokens a month in upkeep**; **terminating a capability costs 2 tokens** (its build cost is sunk). |\n| **Foundations** | Three foundation ladders — **Data · Process · Tech (D·P·T)** — each from L1 to L5. Raising them costs tokens (Kaveri: 27 tokens across the season to reach L4·L4·L4). |\n| **Capabilities (agents)** | Bought from a catalogue in families (demand, production, distribution, pricing, procurement, governance). Each has a **gate** of minimum foundation levels, e.g. Distribution Planning Agent **D2·P1·T1**; Network-Intelligence Orchestrator **D3·P3·T3**. Some have **prerequisites** (Demand Sensing Agent needs the 2-token Demand Signal Desk Agent; Cross-Channel Repricing needs the Price-Setting & Segmentation Agent). |\n| **Below-gate builds** | Allowed, but \"**build odds cut, and whatever ships delivers a fraction of its designed effect until the gap is closed**\" — \"**a below-gate agent runs at full cost and a fraction of the effect**.\" Example odds: 55%, 45%, 20%, 12%. |\n| **Build speed** (Round 2) | **FAST 0.85× cost at quality −18 · STANDARD 1.0× · CAREFUL 1.25× over two rounds at quality +6**, no failure chance either way. |\n| **Scoring** | **Company value = profit × reputation × trust.** Board confidence, trust and reputation fall after incidents (Kaveri M5: board confidence −5 after the congestion charge). |\n| **Distribution dials** (from Round 4) | **distCover** (coverage), **distSafety** (safety stock), **distFront** (SPREAD or FRONT — spread stock across RDCs or front-load it), **distMode** (ROAD or MIX — **MIX, i.e. rail in the mix, locked until tech maturity 3**). The engine builds the flows from these four parameters. |\n| **Named catalogue agents** | PRD_MAINT_2/3 Predictive & Prescriptive Maintenance (production family); DIS_NET_2/3 Network Design & Digital Twin; DIS_EXCP_2/3 Dynamic Exception & Re-Routing (distribution family) — \"**L2 specialist first, L3 only as an upgrade on top of it**\". Others seen in the debrief: Demand Signal Desk, Demand Sensing, Forecast Error/Bias Tracking, Mode & Routing Optimization, Distribution Planning, Price-Setting & Segmentation, Cross-Channel Repricing (L5 closed loop), Production Intelligence Orchestrator, Plant Orchestrator, Scheduling agent, quality agent, data pipeline, network-topology model, Control Tower, Network-Intelligence Orchestrator. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Round-by-round: what each round tested and the right move\n| Round (session) | Theme 📘 | The decision | The winning approach | Course tool |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **R1 (S11)** | Onboarding: manual ops, first forecast, learn the console | Forecast and plan manually | Forecast a **distribution, not a number** from the first click; stock to a quantile (service level) | Probability forecasting |\n| **R2 (S12)** | **Forecasting bake-off** — choose your forecasting-agent tier against your own history; FAST / STANDARD / CAREFUL build | Which tier, which build speed | Pick the tier your **volume, readiness, skills and governance** justify (not the flashiest); CAREFUL when quality matters and you can wait; never FAST on a core capability (−18 quality) | Bake-off ladder; tier matching |\n| **R3 (S13)** | **Aftermarket & maintenance** — telemetry investment | Buy PRD_MAINT / DIS_NET? | Fund telemetry **only if it changes a real decision downstream**; **L2 specialist first, L3 as upgrade** | Twin-Now/Twin-Later test |\n| **R4 (S14)** | **The network opens** — set distCover, distSafety, distFront, distMode; a basic network agent becomes deployable | Distribution parameters | **Set policy, not flows**; protect high-value zones; plan for mode mix; book rail where eligible (needs tech maturity 3 for MIX) | Mandate card |\n| **R5 (S15)** | **Setting the mandate** — the parameters *are* the objective the solver executes | Service targets & cost weights | Differentiate zones (a high-margin zone ≠ a cheap-to-serve one); name re-solve triggers; check the plan against the service floor before it runs | Optimizer mandate checklist |\n| **R6 (S16)** | **Chip contract negotiation** — your procurement agent negotiates under a mandate (like Walmart–Pactum) | Contract volume and clauses | Size the chip book to **real** demand (fees on the full book); specify the clauses that matter **before** the agent signs; strictest bar on penalty/liability terms | Clause-stakes checklist |\n| **R7 (S17)** | **The token economy** — 24 tokens/month, 4 actions/round, upkeep 1–4 per agent | Keep, tune or retire agents | Decide on **cost-per-decision vs value-per-decision**, not on the size of the bill; tune first, retire only if still under water | Tune-or-retire checklist |\n| **R8 (S18)** | **Scenarios** — no scenario/crisis agent exists; nearest are DIS_EXCP and DIS_NET | Which resilience capability | Generate scenarios yourself, **label** them plausible/stretch/implausible, buy the capability that **survives more than one** of them | Scenario portfolio checklist |\n| **R9 (S19)** | **Coordination under pressure** | How agents react to each other | **Shared demand signal**, a fast-but-not-panicked reaction (order smoothing), a **clear owner** of the call | Multi-agent fixes; ACAP |\n| **R10 (S20)** | **Finale** — file the post-incident review in-game; endgame scoring locks | Honest incident write-up | Disclose, step autonomy down when needed, check against ACAP | ACAP governance checklist |\n\n*(Session 10's \"Command Your Agent\" game in July was the bullwhip warm-up: twenty teams' agents produced \"a textbook bullwhip blowup live on the shared screen\" 📘 — the source of Session 19's \"amplification we made\".)*\n\n---\n\n## 3. How the season played out — the four archetypes (S20 debrief 📘)\nTwo axes decided the season: **how much AI capability a team built** (live agents) and **how well it kept operating** (operating discipline). Bubble size = final company value.\n\n| Archetype | Teams | Pattern | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Compounders — WON** | 6 (B12-Titans #1, A7-Kimi K3 #2, A2-Doomsday #4, B15-Neural 15 #6, B17-BLTB #7, A3-OpsManiacs #9) | **Built capability on foundations they had actually laid — and kept the business running.** | Top of the table |\n| **Lean operators** | 3 (A8-The Odyssey #3, A9-Skynet #5, B20-Andromeda #12) | **Minimal transformation, deep foundations, excellent execution.** Capital-efficient — lower ceiling, thin cover. | #3 on six agents; #12 on one agent |\n| **Transformation theatre** | 7 (B14-Six Sigma #8, A10-Decepticons #10, B18-Promptplay #13, A6-Smooth Ops #15, A5-Tokenizer #16, B11-Dream11 #17, A1-Tankers #18) | **Built the most, on the least foundation. Activity mistaken for outcome.** Below-gate builds, build failures (12%, 20% odds), guardrail breaches. | 8th to 18th |\n| **Foundations, not run** | 4 (B13-Kolirt #11, B16-nEYtra #14, B19-Utkarshan #19, A4-Sapiens #20) | **Laid deep ground — and never ran on it. Bought the platform, didn't operate the business.** | 11th to 20th |\n\n### Team snapshots worth quoting in an answer\n- **B12-Titans — CHAMPION (value 213):** widest estate (21 agents), **rail every round**, **trust held at 110 through every shock**; not flawless (an R10 Control-Tower build failed, fill settled at 79) — \"**won on breadth and governance, not perfect execution**\".\n- **A7-Kimi K3 — #2 (212):** \"**the textbook build — ZERO below-gate all season**, deep L5, rail every round, best cash (₹1,212 cr), highest trust (111)\"; lost by 1.1 points as fill drifted to 80.\n- **A8-The Odyssey — #3 (191) on just 6 agents:** every foundation at L5, trust past 100, ₹1,181 cr cash — \"**the efficiency champion**\".\n- **A2-Doomsday — #4 (186):** gates respected, trust ~100, fill 84 — but \"**a late distribution miss (338 cars auto-pushed to the nearest RDC)**\" kept it off the podium. *(This is exactly Kaveri's failure.)*\n- **B20-Andromeda — #12:** \"the purest operator\", best reputation (0.92), 95% plan accuracy, fill 85 **on a single agent** — but **zero governance estate → trust bottomed at 78**.\n- **B14-Six Sigma — #8:** clean operation, trust 98, fill 76 → 84 — but \"**built on sand**\": the R10 Network-Intelligence Orchestrator **failed at 12% odds, below its D3·P3·T3 gate**, like every build before it.\n- **B18-Promptplay — #13:** a **Scheduling agent breached its guardrails in R10 and was pulled**; lowest reputation (0.75) — \"**trust alone never paid**\".\n- **A6-Smooth Ops — #15:** transit losses to zero, ₹239 cr final-month profit — but 12 agents, several below gate, an earlier governance failure.\n- **A1-Tankers — #18:** 12 agents, deep P5 foundations, but **fill never held (65 → 77)** — \"scale never became service\".\n- **A4-Sapiens — #20 (46):** a deep L5 estate, yet **fill 92 → 69, trust 61 (last)**, chip stock depleted.\n- **B13-Kolirt — #11:** drove every ladder to L5 but only 5 live agents; in R10 **\"attention ran out (24 tokens)\" so two builds were shelved** — \"kept queuing capability it never operated\".\n- **A3-OpsManiacs — #9:** disciplined (trust 99, ten well-gated agents) but **R10 attention ran out (25/24 tokens) and rail slots fell short**.\n\n### The four lessons the faculty drew 📘 (memorise — these are the \"management approaches\")\n1. **Sequence beats speed.** The top teams built **on** foundations they had laid; the rest built agents faster than the **D·P·T ground** could support. *A below-gate agent runs at full cost and a fraction of the effect.*\n2. **Building is not operating.** Estate size never predicted rank on its own — **operating the estate did: booking rail, filing a distribution plan, sizing the chip book to real demand.** (#3 ran 6 agents, #12 ran one; four 12–13-agent estates finished 15th–18th.)\n3. **Governance is the multiplier you feel late.** **Company value = profit × reputation × trust.** The champion held trust at 110 through every shock; teams that skipped the resilience/governance estate watched trust collapse (B20 → 78, A4 → 61) \"and the multiplication did the rest\".\n4. **Efficiency is a real strategy.** A8 finished #3 on six agents and B20 held top-tier service on one — **deep foundations amplified a tiny estate**. Lean works, *provided you still buy the insurance*.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The strategy rulebook — decisions that win (and the trap behind each)\n| # | Decision area | Winning rule | The trap (seen in the game / Kaveri) | Course link |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Foundations vs agents** | Raise D·P·T to each agent's **gate before** you commission it; check prerequisites | Commissioning below gate (Kaveri's Demand Sensing at 55% odds, Repricing at 45%); B14's 12% orchestrator | Readiness sets the ceiling (S2); phased gates (S3) |\n| 2 | **Distribution** | **File a plan-of-record every month**; route every car against the demand plan; never leave stock unrouted | Default auto-push to the home RDC → stock in the wrong region (Kaveri 276 cars; A2-Doomsday 338 cars) | Mandate card (S14); min-cost flow (S15) |\n| 3 | **Rail** | **Book a standing rail floor a month ahead** for base load (cheaper ~25%); road for flex; trailer-express only for urgent splits | \"Road has always sufficed\" → port jam/congestion with 0% cover; 0 rail slots despite alerts on 12 lanes | Mode-mix judgment (S14 Q4.1); insurance logic |\n| 4 | **Mode mix under a shock** | **Split the shipment** — urgent part by trailer-express now, the rest by rail as backfill | Holding, cancelling, or one-mode rerouting | S14 Class Q 4.1 |\n| 5 | **Distribution parameters** | Set **per-zone** service floors and cost weights; protect the high-margin zone; MIX once tech ≥ 3 | Identical cost weights → solver routes everything by cheap slow rail and the premium zone's fill slips | S15 Class Q 5.1 |\n| 6 | **Chip contract** | Contract volume to **real** production need with clauses specified before signing | Over-booking (fees on the full book) or under-booking (allocation cut → forced production cut, as in Kaveri M4) | S16 clause-stakes; Pactum mandate |\n| 7 | **Pricing** | Don't cut price to fix a placement problem; restore toward list when the constraint is distribution | Kaveri cut prices in M3–M4 and never restored them → ≈₹1.5 cr/month given away while lost sales kept rising | Diagnose the binding constraint |\n| 8 | **Tokens (attention)** | Budget 24 tokens: keep the **2-token monthly plan**, upkeep, and ≤ 4 transformation actions; tune or retire agents on **value per decision** | Running out of attention in R10 (B13, A3); agents queued but never operated | S17 tune-or-retire |\n| 9 | **Autonomy level** | **L2 specialist first, L3 as an upgrade**; L4 only with evidence; never L5 below gate | \"Fully autonomous cross-channel repricing (L5 closed loop)\" commissioned below gate on a board that cannot place stock | Autonomy ladder (S4) |\n| 10 | **Forecasting** | Distribution forecasts; buy the tier you can run; track error/bias (the one Kaveri agent that worked) | Buying sensing before the signal desk exists | S8, S11, S12 |\n| 11 | **Coordination** | Every agent sees the **same end-customer demand**; smooth orders; one owner per call | Reacting to each other's orders → bullwhip (Command Your Agent finale) | S19 |\n| 12 | **Governance & trust** | Build the resilience/governance estate; disclose incidents; step autonomy down when a guardrail fires | Guardrail breach (B18's Scheduling agent); zero governance estate (B20's trust 78) | S20 ACAP; value = profit × reputation × trust |\n| 13 | **Insurance / hazard cover** | Buy cover on hazards the calendar signals (port, congestion) when the expected loss exceeds the premium | 0% cover → full ₹60 L hit + trust loss | \"Argues insurance logic backwards\" |\n| 14 | **Reporting** | Report outcomes (fill, lost sales, cash), not activity (tokens spent, levels reached, agents built) | Kaveri's memo: \"six capabilities built or building\" while fill fell five months | Claim audit (S1) |\n\n---\n\n## 5. The Kaveri pattern — diagnosing a sick board in five checks\nUse these on any Chain Reaction-style exhibit:\n1. **Fill vs stock:** is fill falling while finished stock rises? → **placement failure** (zones pull only from their own RDC). Kaveri: fill 86 → 58% while RDC stock 226 → 662.\n2. **Auto-push count:** any cars \"unrouted, default lane\"? → no plan-of-record. Kaveri: 273–479 every month.\n3. **Rail slots booked:** zero? → road-only network exposed to congestion; paying ~25% more than needed.\n4. **Below-gate builds:** which live/in-build agents are under their D·P·T gate or missing prerequisites? → full cost, fraction of effect.\n5. **Profit drivers:** did profit improve because operations improved, or because production was cut (chip allocation) or prices were cut? → don't let a cherry-picked month pass as recovery.\n\nThen compute the **contribution forgone** (lost sales × mix × (price − unit cost)) and compare it with the visible charges — the hidden number is almost always the bigger one (Kaveri: ₹11.0 cr vs ₹60 L).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Practice questions on the simulation (with answers)\n**Q1. Your West RDC has full HATCH stock but the Mumbai zone is about to stock out; the only road lane between them just lost 40% capacity; trailer-express is open but costly; rail has slots but a two-day lead time you don't have. What do you do?**\nSplit the shipment: send the urgent portion by trailer-express now to protect the zone at highest stockout risk, and backfill the remainder by rail. Under a capacity shock the answer is rarely \"pick one mode\" — it is rebalancing the mode mix in real time (S14 Class Q 4.1 📘).\n\n**Q2. Under time pressure a team sets identical cost weights for every zone; the solver routes nearly everything by rail and the zone driving 40% of premium-margin sales slips badly. What went wrong?**\nThe mandate, not the solver: they should have set a higher cost weight or a service floor for the high-value zone before solving. \"A perfectly solved LP with the wrong mandate produces a perfectly wrong plan\" (S15 Class Q 5.1 📘).\n\n**Q3. A negotiation agent's run cost doubles after a platform reprice, but its output quality is unchanged. You have 90 seconds.**\nCompare value-per-decision with cost-per-decision, then tune (shorter context, caching, cheaper tier, fewer calls) or retire. A price shock is a signal to re-run the math, not a verdict (S17 Class Q 7.1 📘).\n\n**Q4. Your GenAI scenario tool says a celebrity post will spike one paint colour 400% in 72 hours; a teammate wants to fund paint-shop flex capacity now.**\nLabel it \"stretch\", check for a real mechanism and base rate, then look for a cheap reversible mitigation. Vividness is not evidence (S18 Class Q 8.1 📘).\n\n**Q5. One agent orders 15% extra \"to be safe\"; two tiers up another agent doubles its order in reaction. What single change prevents the amplification?**\nGive every agent the same real end-customer demand signal instead of each other's orders — coordination beats speed (S19 Class Q 9.1 📘).\n\n**Q6. Your L4 forecasting agent has quietly beaten the manual process, but two weeks before the board review you find its training data under-represents your fastest-growing region and has skewed allocation for months.**\nDrop it to L2, disclose the bias and its likely impact in the incident log, and take both to the board as a governance finding. Guardrails only count if you use them when it costs you something (S20 Class Q 10.1 📘).\n\n**Q7. Your model flags a press bearing at 68% failure probability; your weekly inspect-and-replace protocol already catches this wear.**\nDon't act on the flag — it does not change the decision or its timing; the prediction is informationally idle (S13 Class Q 3.1 📘). Fund telemetry only where it changes a decision.\n\n**Q8. 60 SKUs, 4 dealerships, one Excel analyst, lenders need a plain auditable rationale; a vendor shows an LSTM platform. Which tier?**\nThe spreadsheet/smoothing tier — it matches volume, skills and audit needs; the LSTM needs data maturity 3+ and produces the opaque output lenders don't want (S12 Class Q 2.1 📘).\n\n**Q9. Kaveri's board asks whether to terminate the below-gate repricing agent (7 tokens sunk, 45% odds) or let it land. What reasoning applies?**\nSunk cost is irrelevant: compare the remaining cost (termination 2 tokens vs upkeep plus the risk of an L5 closed-loop pricing agent running at a fraction of effect without its Price-Setting prerequisite) with expected value. With placement the binding constraint and prices already cut, the expected value of autonomous repricing is low; terminating is defensible under a cash-stabilisation mandate, but if you choose the distribution initiative you accept its upkeep for two months and re-decide at the gate.\n\n**Q10. Which is the better predictor of final rank: number of agents built or operating discipline? Give evidence.**\nOperating discipline. #3 (A8) ran six agents and #12 (B20) one agent, while four estates of 12–13 agents finished 15th–18th; the champion won on breadth **plus** governance (trust 110), and A7 lost by 1.1 points with zero below-gate builds. Size never predicted rank on its own; booking rail, filing a distribution plan and sizing the chip book did 📘.\n"}, {"slug": "course-frameworks", "label": "All 37 Course Frameworks", "tag": "FW", "group": "focus", "title": "Course Frameworks — every tool from the 20 sessions, ready to apply", "md": "# Course Frameworks — every tool from the 20 sessions, ready to apply\nYour faculty: **\"Please go through all the frameworks covered during the sessions\"** 📘. Each session ended with \"The tools you take with you\" — this page collects **every one of them** in the order taught, with the steps, when to use it, scoring rules, and a one-paragraph applied example in exam style. The [Exam Playbook](exam-playbook.md) has the \"if the case asks X, use Y\" picker.\n\n> Quick index: 1 Four-Question Claim Audit · 2 AI Adoption Roadmap (6 stages) · 3 Readiness barriers (4 categories) · 4 Six data-quality dimensions · 5 Data & Tech Readiness Scorecard · 6 Predictive vs Agentic requirements delta · 7 Three decision levels · 8 Process readiness agenda · 9 Three non-negotiables · 10 Phased roadmap with gates · 11 Pilot-selection matrix · 12 Scoping methods (design, systems, scenario, Delphi, BSC) · 13 Five generations of automation · 14 Six building blocks of an agent · 15 Design patterns · 16 Autonomy ladder L1–L5 · 17 Governance stack · 18 Agentic suitability screen · 19 AI stack (5 layers) · 20 Build vs buy vs blend · 21 Six platform filters · 22 Learning taxonomy · 23 ML framing canvas · 24 Seven-step ML workflow + capability checklist · 25 Probability forecasting & newsvendor · 26 Signal audit · 27 Tier matching & bake-off ladder · 28 Inventory policy ladder · 29 Guardrailed pilot · 30 Twin-now/twin-later · 31 Mandate card · 32 Optimizer mandate checklist · 33 Clause-stakes checklist · 34 Tune-or-retire · 35 Scenario portfolio · 36 Multi-agent stability fixes · 37 ACAP governance\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Four-Question AI Claim Audit ⭐⭐⭐ (S1) 📘\n\"Apply this audit to every vendor pitch, case study and headline for the rest of the course.\"\n| # | Question | Ask |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Mechanism?** | What exactly does the AI do differently — and is there a well-grounded reason it should work here? |\n| 2 | **Cost pool?** | Which cost/revenue pool does it touch, and how big is that pool? (**Q vs S logic — \"is there juice in this lemon?\"**) |\n| 3 | **Decision changed?** | Does the output change an actual decision, or map to the same action as before? (**Rain-forecast reward function**) |\n| 4 | **Evidence?** | Named deployment, independent numbers — does it survive **out-of-sample and out-of-pilot**? (MIT's 95% warning) |\n\n**Applied (Trishul P1):** Mechanism — better forecasts shrink safety stock. Pool — S = ₹2.6 cr of ₹13.6 cr (19.1%), carrying value ₹0.49 cr/yr. Decision — full-truck multiples mean many forecast gains map to the same truck. Evidence — a European reference customer, not Trishul's data. → **Claim fails at Q2 before Q4 is even needed.**\n\n**The two structural limits of forecasting (S1):** (1) forecasting touches only part of cost — inventory = cycle stock from batch Q + safety stock S; a perfect forecast removes only S; most inventory sits in Q, driven by pallets, trucks, MOQs. (2) Diminishing returns — **archery reward** (every gain pays) vs **rain-forecast reward** (80% vs 95% rain → same umbrella). \"124 vs 159 units forecast — either way you ship one 240-unit pallet.\"\n\n## 2. AI Adoption Roadmap — six stages (S1) 📘\n**1 Ideation → 2 Defining the project → 3 Data curation & governance → 4 Prototyping → 5 Production → 6 Thriving with the AI lifecycle.** Ideation asks: *What problem are we solving? How do we operate today? How could AI benefit us?* Value is proven by small, recoverable failures — **\"fail quickly, fail regularly, fail tiny.\"** (Sessions 2–3 = stage 3 and the organisation; Sessions 6–10 = ML and deployment stages.)\n\n## 3. Four categories of readiness barriers (S2) 📘\n| Category | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| **Data & technology infrastructure** | data quality and accessibility; legacy systems and integration; security and privacy |\n| **Cost & sustainability** | high implementation cost; regulatory volatility (EU AI Act, China labelling, Canada AIDA); energy and water footprint |\n| **Talent & organisational capability** | skills gaps, citizen data scientists, resistance to change, complacency and de-skilling risk |\n| **Strategy, leadership & collaboration** | executive expectations, silver-bullet mentality, missing AI strategy, functional silos |\n\n## 4. Six dimensions of data quality — SCM edition ⭐⭐⭐ (S2) 📘\n| Dimension | Definition | SCM failure example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Accuracy** | values reflect reality | system says 240 on hand, cycle count finds 175 → phantom inventory triggers false replenishment |\n| **Completeness** | no critical gaps | lead-time field blank for 40% of SKUs → planning falls back to a 14-day default |\n| **Timeliness** | fresh enough for the decision | POS arrives weekly; demand sensing needs daily — \"the AI just smooths stale data\" |\n| **Consistency** | same fact, same value everywhere | 8801-A in ERP, 8801A in WMS, \"8801 A\" in the supplier portal = three products |\n| **Uniqueness** | one record per entity | same vendor six times with different terms → spend analytics undercounts by 60% |\n| **Validity** | values conform to rules | negative lead times and 1900-01-01 dates silently skew the model |\n\n**Rule:** quality dimensions are **per-use-case requirements** — 24-hour-old data is fine for weekly planning and fatal for real-time sensing. **Diagnose the binding dimension before the model.** (Trishul: timeliness binds P1 — 71% of revenue on a 26-hour batch; uniqueness binds P2 — 18% duplicate vendors.)\n\n## 5. Data & Technology Readiness Scorecard — five pillars ⭐⭐⭐ (S2) 📘\n| Pillar | Evidence to score 1–5 |\n|---|---|\n| **1 Data quality** | six dimensions on critical entities (items, vendors, customers, lead times); profiling stats, cycle-count accuracy |\n| **2 Master data & governance** | ownership assigned? golden records? dedup rate? DQ KPIs on a dashboard someone is paid to watch? |\n| **3 Architecture & integration** | batch vs streaming; API coverage of core systems; % of planning still in spreadsheets; lineage documented? |\n| **4 Security & compliance** | API/model attack surface managed; access controls; readiness for EU AI Act / DPDP-class regulation |\n| **5 Compute & tooling** | cloud/GPU in the standard workflow; MLOps maturity; can a model reach production without heroics? |\n\n**Read the profile as a roadmap.** [2,1,3,2,3] → masters first, architecture second, no autonomous agents yet. Class Q 2.5: **[Data 2, Masters 1, Architecture 3, Security 2, Compute 5] → \"predictive pilot on the cleanest domain first\"** — masters at 1 poisons everything; security at 2 forbids write access; compute 5 is a **stranded asset**. \"The scorecard sets the ceiling; money cannot shortcut discipline.\" (Trishul's [2,2,3,3,4] → same reading: predictive/narrow pilots, fix masters before any write-access agent.)\n\n## 6. Predictive vs Agentic — the requirements delta (S2) 📘\n| Requirement | Predictive model (e.g. forecast) | Autonomous agent (e.g. replenishment agent) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data freshness | batch (daily/weekly) often fine | streaming / near-real-time — agent acts on current state |\n| Data access | read-only extracts | governed **read AND write** APIs into systems of record |\n| Integration | one-way pipeline to dashboards | two-way: MCP-style tool connections, transaction posting |\n| Identity & permissions | data-science team credentials | **per-agent identity, least-privilege scopes, spend limits** |\n| Observability | model accuracy monitoring | full action logs, decision traces, rollback paths, **kill switch** |\n| Failure mode | wrong number on a screen | **wrong purchase order in the ERP — errors are transactions** |\n\"**Autonomy raises every bar.**\" Use this table as the go/no-go tool for any agent proposal.\n\n## 7. Three levels of supply chain decisions — and AI fit ⭐⭐ (S3) 📘\n| Level | Examples | Cadence & owner | Realistic AI role today |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Strategic** | network design, NPI, portfolio & pricing, MTS/MTO/ATO, postponement | quarterly–annual; executives | **auxiliary insight / input only** — futures unlike the past defeat history-trained models |\n| **Tactical** | S&OP/SIOP, seasonal capacity & logistics planning, production planning | weekly–monthly; planning orgs | **useful assistant — augment planners**, don't automate them away |\n| **Operational** | dynamic procurement, lead-time quotation, inventory setting, order fulfilment & shipment | daily–hourly; front line + systems | **strongest fit — delegate** (frequent, repetitive, data-rich), but noisy environments demand robustness |\nClass Q 3.1: AI network redesign = strategic/input-only; AI-assisted S&OP = tactical/augment; autonomous reorder of spares < ₹50,000 = operational/delegate.\n\n## 8. The process-readiness agenda (S3) 📘\n1. **Map reality, not the SOP** (process mining on event logs; \"you cannot hand an agent a process you cannot describe\").\n2. **Standardize where variation is waste** (harmonize O2C, P2P variants first).\n3. **Redesign, then automate** — no lift-and-shift; self-learning AI **amplifies** existing limitations.\n4. **Respect embedded expertise** — codify the WHY, not just the flow.\n5. **Decide what agents inherit** — per step: **automate** (rules), **augment** (AI recommends, human decides), or **delegate** (agent acts within an envelope).\nAlso: Who/What/How/Why process interviews; **before/after information-flow** diagrams; idea bank; score each process against five stakeholders (customers, vendors, employees, investors, governments) — low customer-value, high-effort steps are automation targets; rank ideas by **impact × effort** (capital > 20% of profit = large).\n\n## 9. Three non-negotiables of successful transformations (S3) 📘\n1. **Senior executive sponsorship plus multi-functional resources** (named executives with skin in the game).\n2. **Enforced data management and process adherence** — \"governance with teeth\".\n3. **Phased rollout with active stakeholder participation** inside and outside the enterprise.\n\n## 10. Phased AI adoption: Crawl, Walk, Run — with gates ⭐⭐⭐ (S3) 📘\n| Phase | Duration | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Phase 0 · Foundation** | 0–6 months | data & process readiness fixes on ONE value stream; governance stood up; **baseline metrics frozen**; talent assessment; quick BI wins fund credibility |\n| **Phase 1 · Pilot** | 3–9 months | 1–2 use cases: **HIGH feasibility × HIGH value × LOW blast radius**; predictive/augmenting first, human-in-the-loop; success gate measured against the **frozen baseline** |\n| **Phase 2 · Scale** | 9–24 months | winning pilots → adjacent categories, sites, regions; industrialize (MLOps, retraining, adoption KPIs in scorecards, reskilling) |\n| **Phase 3 · Transform** | 18 months + | redesign the operating model around proven capability; autonomy envelopes widen; org restructures around exceptions |\n**Each gate = value evidence + readiness re-score + explicit autonomy decision.** \"Skipping gates is how you join the predicted 40% of cancelled agentic projects.\" Class Q 3.5: a board-deck target (\"25% inventory cut in 6 months, company-wide\") must be rewritten as **scoped, baselined and evidence-gated** — and guarded against gaming (cut inventory, buy stockouts).\n\n## 11. Pilot-selection matrix ⭐⭐⭐ (S3) 📘\n| Criterion | Ask | Green flag | Red flag |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Value at stake** | which cost/revenue pool, how big? | material pool, measurable baseline | vanity metric, no baseline |\n| **Data feasibility** | Session 2 scorecard on THIS use case | clean, owned, streaming where needed | masters dirty, data political |\n| **Decision impact** | does output change real decisions? | clear action mapping | rain-forecast redundancy |\n| **Blast radius** | worst case if it is wrong? | reversible, bounded, low-value transactions | irreversible, customer-facing |\n| **Sponsor & adoption** | who owns it, who must use it? | named sponsor, users co-design | orphaned, imposed on users |\n| **Learning value** | what capability does it build? | reusable data products, skills | dead-end point solution |\nThe Trishul paper calls this \"**pilot-selection logic (value, feasibility, blast radius)**\".\n\n## 12. Scoping methods (S3) 📘\n- **Design thinking** for the new: persona → what they do/think/feel/say → user stories \"*As a <role> I want <feature> so that <benefit>*\".\n- **Systems thinking** for the existing: boundaries (input/output/stress), subsystems aligned to the whole.\n- **Scenario planning** for the uncertain: scan **STEEP** (Social, Technological, Economic, Environmental, Political), ~7 scenarios → four in a 2×2.\n- **Delphi**: anonymous multi-round expert surveys defuse the loudest-voice bias.\n- **Balanced Scorecard**: Financial, Customer, Internal Process, Learning & Growth — so one metric is not maximised to another's ruin.\n\n## 13. Five generations of \"automation\" (S4) 📘\n**1 Scripts & macros** (fixed keystroke replay) → **2 RPA** (rule-based bots on structured tasks: PO creation, invoice matching) → **3 ML-assisted** (predictions embedded in workflows; human decides) → **4 GenAI copilots** (drafting, summarising, what-if; human initiates every action) → **5 Agentic AI** (goal-directed: perceive, plan, act through tools, learn — inside guardrails). \"The step-change at stage 5: the system **owns a goal and a decision loop**, not just a task.\"\n**Agent-washing test (Class Q 4.1):** goal ownership + tool-mediated action within an envelope. \"Emails your planner a prioritized action list\" = **not an agent** (only recommends). Reads supplier emails, drafts replies and **posts confirmed dates into ERP once approved** = **an agentic system** low on the autonomy scale (Class Q 1.1).\n\n## 14. Inside an agent — six building blocks (S4) 📘\n**SENSE** Perception (ERP/WMS/TMS, IoT, EDI, email, market feeds) · **THINK** Reasoning engine (LLM with ReAct, chain-of-thought) · **THINK** Planning (decompose goals, sequence tool calls, replan) · **REMEMBER** Memory (short-term context + long-term stores, vector DBs, RAG) · **ACT** Tool use (APIs, databases, solvers, code; standardised via **MCP**) · **GOVERN** Guardrails & orchestration (permissions, spend limits, policy checks, escalation, audit logs, orchestration layer).\n\n## 15. Agent design patterns (S4) 📘\n**Reflection** (self-critique before acting) · **Tool use** (math to solvers, facts to databases) · **Planning / orchestrator-worker** (planner dispatches specialist workers) · **Multi-agent collaboration** (peer roles negotiate/vote) · **Human-in-the-loop** (approves each action) vs **human-on-the-loop** (monitors exceptions while the agent executes) — chosen per decision class by the autonomy ladder.\n\n## 16. The Five-Level Autonomy Ladder ⭐⭐⭐ (S4) 📘\n| Level | Agent does | Human does | SCM example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **L1 · Assist** | retrieves, summarizes, drafts | decides and acts | copilot drafts a supplier email; planner edits and sends |\n| **L2 · Recommend** | analyses and proposes ranked actions | approves each action | reorder proposals with reasons; planner clicks approve |\n| **L3 · Act with approval gates** | executes routine cases end-to-end | approves exceptions & thresholds | auto-PO under ₹5 L within contract; above → human |\n| **L4 · Bounded autonomy** | owns the decision inside an envelope | monitors dashboards, audits, tunes envelope | Pactum negotiations inside mandate; DeepFleet routing |\n| **L5 · Self-improving** | learns, adapts policy, coordinates peers | governs goals, ethics, kill switch | autonomous planning networks (mostly aspirational, 2026) |\n\"Autonomy is a managerial choice per decision-class: climb on evidence, capped by readiness and blast radius.\" Bounded override is the **adoption mechanism** (algorithm aversion remedy: let users adjust ±10–15%) — \"what L2–L3 should feel like\".\n\n## 17. The governance stack for autonomous agents ⭐⭐⭐ (S4) 📘\n1. **Hard constraints** — spend limits, approved-supplier lists, contract envelopes, rate limits, forbidden actions — **enforced outside the model, in code and permissions, not in the prompt**.\n2. **Identity & least privilege** — each agent has its own credentials; \"an agent with tool access is an attack vector with a budget\".\n3. **Observability** — decision traces, action logs, explanation on demand, override mechanisms.\n4. **Escalation & kill switch** — confidence and anomaly thresholds route to humans; every loop has a documented stop.\n5. **Accountability assignment** — name the human owner of every agent's outcomes **before** go-live (\"it's not me, it's the machine\").\nClass Q 4.3: a system prompt saying \"never exceed ₹5 lakh, never use unapproved suppliers\" is **not sufficient** — prompts are not enforceable controls (\"the prompt shapes behaviour; the envelope bounds it\"). Cautionary file: **Air Canada** chatbot (invented policy, airline liable), **$1 Chevy Tahoe** (prompt injection), **McDonald's AI drive-thru** (bounded pilot with a working exit gate).\n\n## 18. The Agentic Suitability Screen ⭐⭐⭐ (S4) 📘\nScore 1–5 each:\n| Factor | High score when… |\n|---|---|\n| **Frequency & volume** | high-frequency, high-volume decisions amortise the agent investment |\n| **Reversibility** | a wrong action can be undone cheaply (low blast radius) |\n| **Data & feedback** | rich state visibility and **fast** outcome feedback |\n| **Rule clarity** | the mandate can be specified (limits, levers, walk-aways) — \"if experts cannot state the envelope, the agent cannot inherit it\" |\n| **Stakes & politics** | low stakes; strategic, irreversible, politically loaded decisions stay human |\n**Total: 20+ = strong agentic candidate (start L3–L4); 12–19 = augment (L1–L2); below 12 = keep human, revisit later.** Class Q 4.5: warehouse robot routing > tail-spend reorder > strategic supplier negotiation (\"physical autonomy first, transactional next, strategic never — so far\").\n*Worked scoring (Trishul):* P2 tail spend — frequency 5, reversibility 4, feedback 4, rule clarity 5, stakes 5 (low) = **23 → L3–L4**. P3 risk-scored EU renewals — frequency 2 (quarterly), reversibility 2, feedback 2, rule clarity 3, stakes 2 = **11 → keep human (L2 at most)**.\n\n## 19. The AI technology stack — five layers (S5) 📘\n**1 Silicon & hardware** (NVIDIA GPUs, TSMC >60% of fab, ASML sole EUV supplier, ~70% rare-earth processing in China) → **2 Infrastructure** (hyperscale data centres, power, cooling, GW-scale demand) → **3 Foundation models** (GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama — rented via API) → **4 Platform & orchestration** (Azure AI Foundry, AWS Bedrock/SageMaker, Vertex AI; agent runtimes, guardrails, MLOps) → **5 Applications** (SAP, Oracle, Dynamics; Blue Yonder, Kinaxis, o9; Pactum, ToolsGroup). \"Most SCM organisations buy at layers 4–5 — but power, cost and risk cascade down from layers 1–2.\" **Every platform is a supply chain; audit it like a supplier.**\n\n## 20. Build vs Buy vs Blend (S5) 📘\n- **Buy** (suite features, SaaS agents): fastest; vendor carries model risk; best when the use case is common and differentiation LOW; costs: subscription creep, roadmap dependency.\n- **Build** (custom agents on hyperscaler platforms): exactly your process; best when the decision IS your edge (DeepFleet, JD.com) and talent exists; costs: talent scarcity, MLOps burden, you own every failure.\n- **Blend** — \"buy the backbone, build the edge\". **\"Own the differentiating loop; rent the rest.\"** First ask: **does a good service already exist?** (Class Q 5.6). Build-vs-buy is decided by **real differentiation only** (Class Q 5.3). Platform fates: TradeLens shut down (no industry collaboration), Walmart Element (build for control), Ocado Smart Platform (build until it becomes the product).\n\n## 21. Choosing a platform — six filters IN ORDER ⭐⭐ (S5) 📘\n**1 Use-case fit** (start from roadmap pilots) → **2 Data gravity** (compute near systems of record) → **3 Governance fit** (agent identity, guardrails, audit, regional compliance — can it enforce YOUR autonomy ladder?) → **4 Ecosystem & skills** (Microsoft shop vs AWS-native vs SAP country) → **5 TCO & pricing risk** (licences + tokens + integration + change; price volatility; exit costs) → **6 Lock-in & optionality** (MCP, model-agnostic runtimes, egress terms).\nClass Q 5.2: a cheaper feature-rich platform that cannot enforce per-agent spend limits or produce complete action logs is **disqualified** for an L4 procurement agent — \"governance before features\". Class Q 5.4: token spend 3.2× forecast → **fix usage discipline (FinOps)**, not the platform.\n\n## 22. The learning taxonomy (S6) 📘\n| Paradigm | Needs | Answers | OSCM examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Supervised** | labelled history | \"given these features, predict this value/class\" | demand forecasting (regression); supplier default/delay (classification: **SVM**, boosted trees) |\n| **Unsupervised** | unlabelled data | \"what structure hides here?\" | SKU/customer segmentation (**k-means**), anomaly detection, store clustering |\n| **Semi-supervised** | few labels + much unlabelled | | when labels are expensive |\n| **Reinforcement** | environment + reward | \"what POLICY maximises long-run reward?\" | dynamic reordering, robot routing, dynamic pricing (**Q-learning**) |\n| **Decision-focused** | decision cost as the loss | trains on what you actually pay | newsvendor quantile, not the mean |\nClass Q 6.3: k-means clusters have **no ground truth** to be \"accurate\" against — validate by whether segments produce different, profitable treatments.\n\n## 23. The ML Framing Canvas — five boxes before any model ⭐⭐⭐ (S6) 📘\n| Box | Question |\n|---|---|\n| **1 Decision** | who acts, at what cadence, within what constraints? **No owner, no project.** |\n| **2 Target** | the variable to predict at **decision granularity** (SKU-store-week, not national-month) |\n| **3 Features** | inputs that pass the test: **knowable at decision time; no leakage** |\n| **4 Benchmark** | the **naive rule** and the incumbent the model must beat — priced in decisions |\n| **5 Loss** | training objective that mirrors decision costs — **asymmetric where the business is asymmetric** |\nFour ways operations ML goes wrong: no decision owner; **accuracy theatre** (\"92%\" with no naive benchmark — Class Q 6.4: 92% vs naive 90.5% = **unproven**); **leakage** (\"goods-receipt date\" known only after delivery — Class Q 6.2); asymmetric costs ignored (Zillow). Class Q 6.1: whether 87% accuracy is good enough depends on **the decision's cost and reversibility**.\n\n## 24. Seven-step operations ML workflow + capability checklist (S7) 📘\n**1 Frame** (canvas) → **2 Profile** (six DQ dimensions in code) → **3 Benchmark** (fit the naive rule FIRST) → **4 Model** (simplest credible first) → **5 Evaluate** (honest holdout; error AND decisions changed; **slice by segment** — averages hide failures) → **6 Deploy** (pipeline, not laptop) → **7 Monitor** (drift alerts, retraining cadence, named owner, documented fallback).\n**Capability checklist:** reproducible, monitored, owned, documented fallback, someone on call. \"A model in a notebook is not a capability\" (Class Q 7.1). Class Q 7.2: model wins on staples, loses on promo/new items → **deploy where it wins, keep naive elsewhere** (champion–challenger by segment). Class Q 7.3: 99% accuracy on a 3%-churn problem → accuracy is misleading; check leakage.\n\n## 25. Probability forecasting — \"the quantile IS the decision\" ⭐⭐ (S8/S11) 📘\n- A probability forecast gives mean, spread and tails per SKU-location-period; the point forecast is only a summary.\n- **Service level = which quantile you hold**: a 95% service level = stocking the 95th percentile of lead-time demand. Safety stock becomes a **costed choice on an explicit curve**.\n- **Newsvendor:** when over- and under-stocking costs differ, the optimal order is the **critical quantile = Cu ÷ (Cu + Co)**.\n- Evaluate with **quantile (pinball) loss and calibration**, not MAPE alone; MAPE is **undefined on zero actuals** (intermittent spares — Class Q 10.2).\n- Class Q 8.2: two SKUs forecast 1,000 (±50 vs ±400) with the same \"forecast + 2 weeks\" rule → **the rule ignores demand uncertainty**.\n\n## 26. The Signal Audit — five steps before any data purchase ⭐⭐⭐ (S8/S11) 📘\n| Step | Test |\n|---|---|\n| **1 Lead time** | does the signal arrive before the decision must be made — by enough to act? |\n| **2 Mechanism** | is there an honest causal story, or a correlation mined from one training window? |\n| **3 Decision test** | does the improved prediction cross an order/capacity threshold? |\n| **4 Cost vs value** | acquisition + integration cost against the decisions actually changed |\n| **5 Weight check** | after deployment: what weight did the model LEARN? demote or drop losers ruthlessly |\nSignal verdicts (S8 table): weather (buy IF horizon matches order lead time) · promotions calendar (mandatory: signal + history repair) · POS/sell-through (bullwhip antidote) · installed base & utilisation (spare-parts winner) · social/search (test rigorously) · **customer forecasts (incentive-laden — learned weight ZERO at the Fortune 150 OEM)**.\n\n## 27. Forecasting tiers & the Bake-Off Ladder ⭐⭐ (S9/S12) 📘\n| | Tier 1 Spreadsheet | Tier 2 Open-source (Prophet, scikit-learn) | Tier 3 Cloud AutoML (SageMaker-class) | (LSTM / sequence models) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Volume | tens–hundreds of series | thousands (scripted) | tens of thousands + | long history, rich data |\n| Skills | spreadsheet literacy | Python + statistics | cloud admin + data eng + FinOps | data maturity 3+ |\n| Readiness | clean-enough extracts | pipeline discipline | governed data platform (S2 ≥ 3.5) | high |\n| Explainability | total | high | variable, often opaque | low |\n| Cost shape | hidden (hours, errors) | skills + maintenance | subscription + consumption | |\n**Four deciding questions in order: volume, readiness, skills, governance** — accuracy joins the table but does not chair it. **Bake-off ladder: 1 Count it → 2 Audit readiness → 3 Name the skill → 4 Check governance → 5 Bake it off on your own data.** Class Q 9.1: 12-SKU bakery → spreadsheet. Class Q 9.2: AutoML +2% accuracy at 6× cost without the skills → **Prophet tier wins today** (name the condition that flips it). Class Q 9.3: production readiness = **does it run reliably on schedule** (40,000 SKUs by 6 a.m.).\n\n## 28. The inventory policy ladder (S10) 📘\n| Generation | Logic | Strength | Where it strains |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **EOQ (1913)** | batch size from order-vs-holding trade-off | transparent; right for stable demand | assumes steady demand, static costs |\n| **Basestock / (s,S) + service levels** | order up to a target set by the lead-time demand distribution | decades of theory; auditable; near-optimal when tuned | complex networks, dual sourcing, lost sales |\n| **Stochastic optimisation / EIO** | multi-echelon under uncertainty (700+ deployments in Global 2000) | network-aware; proven | static parameters; slow to absorb signals |\n| **DRL policies** | neural policy learned in a simulator: state → action for reward | handles complexity; ms inference | data/tuning hungry; brittle off-regime; opaque |\n| **One-step (data-to-decision)** | learn the decision function from features, constraints, KPIs | skips forecast errors; interpretable weights; Fortune 150 scale | needs the digital twin + data layer |\n\"**Optimize the policy, not the forecast**\" (Class Q 10.3: replacing the weeks-of-supply rule beats a 15% forecast gain).\n\n## 29. The Guardrailed Pilot — six design elements ⭐⭐⭐ (S10) 📘\n| # | Element | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Scope** | one category, honest volume, long enough for intermittent statistics (quarters, not weeks) |\n| 2 | **Bounds** | basestock floors/ceilings from the trusted incumbent; spend envelopes; no unbounded orders |\n| 3 | **Fallback** | drift and anomaly triggers that **auto-revert to the incumbent policy** — tested before go-live |\n| 4 | **Evaluation** | **randomised or matched-control vs incumbent AND naive benchmark**; decision-frontier metrics (service per rupee) |\n| 5 | **Trust loop** | interpretable recommendations; bounded planner override with reason capture feeding retraining |\n| 6 | **Ownership** | named expert owner for the policy; named business owner for outcomes; review cadence with widen/narrow/revert decision |\n\"This design IS the honest answer to every '20% better' pitch: fund the pilot, on these terms.\" Theory ceiling: a simple **myopic policy sits within ~5% of optimal**, so a 20% backtest win over a tuned basestock policy most likely contains **overfitting or a weak baseline** (Class Q 10.1); freshly trained DRL often runs **15–20% behind** the myopic policy. Class Q 10.4: \"4 weeks shadow, then full control\" is missing **bounds, fallback and a real evaluation**.\n\n## 30. The Twin-Now / Twin-Later Test (S13) 📘\n**1 Decision-changing** (would a better prediction change what you'd do, or when? If no, stop) → **2 Failure cost** (downtime, safety, warranty high enough?) → **3 Data readiness** (sensor stream exists, or instrumentation from zero?) → **4 Fidelity needed** (can a lightweight simulation of the relevant subsystem answer the what-if?) → **5 Verdict: twin-now (build), twin-later (park), or simulation-lite substitute.** Target **Remaining Useful Life (RUL)**, not a binary alarm. \"Fidelity-for-purpose, not maximum realism.\"\n\n## 31. The Mandate Card — set before you hand flow to any agent ⭐⭐⭐ (S14) 📘\n| # | Field | Decide |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Service floor** | the zones/segments where a stockout is unacceptable and must be protected first |\n| 2 | **Cost ceiling** | maximum acceptable cost-per-unit-delivered, by mode |\n| 3 | **Mode priority** | default cost-vs-speed preference (the agent's tiebreaker) |\n| 4 | **Shock trigger** | what capacity or demand shift forces a full re-plan vs a local patch |\n| 5 | **Review cadence** | how often a human re-checks the agent's allocation against outcomes |\n\"Agents operate the model, they don't replace it — the mandate is yours, execution at scale is the agent's.\"\n\n## 32. The Optimizer Mandate Checklist (S15) 📘\n**1 Service targets** (per zone, not one network-wide number) → **2 Cost weights** (reflect strategic priority, not just unit economics) → **3 Review rule** (human sign-off L2, exception-based escalation L3, or fully autonomous L4) → **4 Re-solve trigger** (cost shock, capacity loss, demand spike) → **5 Verify the fit** (check the returned plan against the service floor before it executes — \"optimal is not automatically right\").\nMin-cost-flow: plants, RDCs, zones = nodes; lanes = edges with capacity and cost; the solver finds the lowest-cost flow meeting demand under constraints. **AI-OR symbiosis:** agents decide **when** to invoke the solver, **what** to optimise for and **how much autonomy** its plan gets — JD.com's \"dual engine\".\n\n## 33. The Clause-Stakes Specification Checklist (S16) 📘\n**1 List the clauses** (payment terms, renewal windows, liability caps, penalty triggers, termination, indemnification) → **2 Name the decision** each feeds → **3 Set the bar** per clause by the cost of being wrong → **4 Pick the tier** (general extraction fine for logistics/contact fields; **domain-tuned for anything touching liability** — 94.7% vs 79.3%) → **5 Name the exception** — the one clause type you will never fully automate the interpretation of (penalty/indemnification).\n\n## 34. The Tune-or-Retire Checklist (S17) 📘\n**1 Measure cost** = total run-cost (tokens, calls, compute) ÷ decisions made → **2 Measure value** = money/margin impact of those decisions over the same window → **3 Compare the ratio** (value per decision comfortably > cost per decision → no action) → **4 Tune first** (cut context, cache, batch calls, downgrade model tier; re-measure) → **5 Retire if it still fails** (fall back to the next-best option rather than run at a loss on principle). Spend caps belong **in code**, not in prompts.\n\n## 35. The Scenario Portfolio Checklist (S18) 📘\n**1 Generate wide** (GenAI for structurally diverse scenarios) → **2 Label honestly** (plausible / stretch / implausible by mechanism and precedent, not detail) → **3 Select your two** (one plausible, one high-impact stretch) → **4 Specify survival** (the capability each requires — flex capacity, dual sourcing, buffer — and its cost) → **5 Guard the label** (name the mechanism connecting the scenario to your business; if you can't, it's a story). \"The generator proposes; the optimizer disposes.\" Design goal: **antifragility** — gain from disruption.\n\n## 36. Restoring multi-agent stability (S19) 📘\n1. **Shared demand signals** — every agent sees real end-customer demand, not a neighbour's order (removes most of the amplification loop).\n2. **Rate limits and order smoothing** — cap or dampen how much an order can move per period.\n3. **Trained coordination** — agents trained jointly on one shared objective; **consensus-seeking** orchestration makes agents reconcile before acting.\nEvidence: decentralised LLM agents **amplify bullwhip** in beer-game experiments (the \"collaboration paradox\"); jointly trained agents beat both a central agent and uncoordinated agents **on cost AND variability** (POM 2025). \"**Stability is mechanism design, not intelligence.**\"\n\n## 37. ACAP governance — the self-check (S19) and final checklist (S20) ⭐⭐⭐ 📘\n| S19 self-check for an agent design | S20 checklist \"one last time\" |\n|---|---|\n| **Accountability** — who owns a decision the agent makes; traceable to a person? | **Accountability** — a named human owner, not just a team |\n| **Capability boundaries** — what the agent is and is not authorised to decide alone | **Capability limits** — scope and autonomy level explicitly bounded to its ladder rung |\n| **Alignment** — does it optimise the same objective as the humans/agents around it? | **Auditability** — a log detailed enough to reconstruct what it did and why |\n| **Process oversight** — a checkpoint **before** high-impact actions execute | **Proportionality** — oversight scales with real-world stakes |\n| **Coordination mechanism** — what stops agents amplifying each other's noise | **Post-incident learning** — does an incident change the next deployment? |\n(The WEF \"AI Agents in Action\" playbook (2026) introduced **ACAP — a per-agent capability & authorization profile** covering delegation policy, system design and oversight 📘.)\n"}, {"slug": "numericals", "label": "Numericals (50 worked)", "tag": "#", "group": "focus", "title": "Numericals — every calculation type, fully worked", "md": "# Numericals — every calculation type, fully worked\nThe faculty said the numericals will be **\"straightforward calculations without requiring any additional formulas\"** 📘 — i.e. arithmetic on the exhibit, not derivations. The past papers prove it: every number in them is a share, a sum, a difference, a ratio, a weighted average or a break-even. Part A covers the **case-numerical patterns** from the papers and class questions; Part B covers the **basic SCM numericals** you may need for \"basic concepts of SCM\". Each problem shows the **assumptions, arithmetic with units, and the one-line meaning**.\n\n> Exam habit: write each calculation as **What → Arithmetic → Result (units) → So what**. Always open a verdict with *Assumptions: A1 … A2 …*.\n\n---\n\n# Part A — Case numericals (the patterns the papers use)\n\n## A1. The reachable-pool test (forecasting attacks S, not Q) ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. A distributor holds ₹8 crore of cycle stock (weekly truckload deliveries) and ₹2 crore of safety stock. A vendor claims its forecasting AI will cut total inventory 30%. Test the claim.** 📘 (S1 Class Q 1.2)\n*Assumptions:* A1. Forecast accuracy affects only safety stock; truckload size and weekly frequency (cycle stock) are unchanged. A2. Figures are average inventory at cost.\n- Claimed cut: 30% × (8 + 2) = **₹3.0 cr**.\n- Maximum possible: all of S = **₹2.0 cr** → ceiling = 2 ÷ 10 = **20%**.\n- **Verdict:** the 30% claim is arithmetically impossible from forecasting; even a perfect forecast reaches 20%. The bigger lever is Q (smaller, more frequent deliveries, shorter lead times — the Campbell Soup lesson).\n\n**Q2. Same firm: what is the claim worth in money if forecasting halves safety stock, at a 20% carrying rate?**\n- Inventory freed = 50% × ₹2.0 cr = ₹1.0 cr (10% of total, not 30%).\n- Annual carrying saving = ₹1.0 cr × 20% = **₹0.2 cr/yr** (plus a one-off ₹1.0 cr cash release).\n- Compare with the tool's annual fee — if the fee is above ₹0.2 cr/yr, the inventory case alone does not pay.\n\n**Q3 (Trishul A1).** Cycle ₹11.0 cr, safety ₹2.6 cr, carrying 19%, subscription ₹1.6 cr/yr. → 30% × 13.6 = ₹4.08 cr vs S = ₹2.6 cr; ceiling 19.1%; carrying value of the entire S pool = 2.6 × 0.19 = **₹0.49 cr/yr < ₹1.6 cr/yr**. Claim fails. See [Trishul paper](pyq-trishul.md).\n\n## A2. \"Precision pays only when a decision changes\" (rain-forecast reward) ⭐⭐\n**Q4. Your forecast error improves from ±40 to ±15 units; orders must be placed in full pallets of 240 units. Demand forecast for next week moves from 159 to 124. What changes?** 📘 (S1 Class Q 1.3)\n- Pallets needed at 159 = ⌈159 ÷ 240⌉ = 1 pallet; at 124 = ⌈124 ÷ 240⌉ = 1 pallet.\n- **Same decision → little to no business impact.** Accuracy pays only up to the point where the decision stops changing.\n- *When would it pay?* Only if demand sits near a threshold, e.g. 230 vs 250 → 1 vs 2 pallets.\n\n## A3. Value pool and break-even savings rate ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q5 (Trishul A3).** Tail spend ₹64 cr over 2,410 suppliers; 9% of suppliers already negotiated; agent costs ₹1.1 cr/yr.\n| Step | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Un-negotiated share | 100% − 9% | 91% |\n| Addressable pool | 64 × 0.91 | **₹58.2 cr** |\n| Break-even rate | 1.1 ÷ 58.2 | **1.89%** |\n| With 65% close rate | 1.1 ÷ (58.2 × 0.65 = 37.8) | **2.9%** |\n| Expected savings at Pactum's ~3% (all closed) | 58.2 × 0.03 | ₹1.75 cr/yr → net +₹0.65 cr |\n*So what:* break-even below published evidence → worth a pilot, but thin once close rates are applied → measure realised savings.\n\n**Q6. Payment-term extension value.** The agent extends median terms from 30 to 65 days (+35 days, Pactum's average) on ₹58.2 cr of annual spend; cost of capital 10%.\n- Working capital released = 58.2 × 35 ÷ 365 = **₹5.58 cr** (one-off).\n- Annual value = 5.58 × 10% = **₹0.56 cr/yr** — nearly half the agent's fee from terms alone.\n\n**Q7. Mean vs median check.** ₹64 cr ÷ 2,410 = **₹2.66 lakh mean** vs ₹1.9 lakh median → mean > median → spend is right-skewed (a few larger suppliers). If the 9% negotiated suppliers are the large ones, the un-negotiated *spend* share is below 91% — state that as an assumption.\n\n## A4. Duplicate-record arithmetic (data quality: uniqueness) ⭐⭐\n**Q8 (Trishul A2).** Vendor master 2,890 records for ~2,410 real suppliers; an old report says \"duplicates < 2%\".\n- Surplus records = 2,890 − 2,410 = **480**.\n- As a share of records = 480 ÷ 2,890 = **16.6%**; as a share of real suppliers = 480 ÷ 2,410 = **19.9%** (≈ \"18%\").\n- **8–10× the claimed 2%** → the report is stale and contradicted.\n**Q9 (S2 Class Q 2.1).** Your largest supplier shows 4% of spend, but it is duplicated across six vendor records of equal size. True share = 6 × 4% = **24%** → analytics measured record-keeping, not concentration.\n\n## A5. Do the exhibit numbers add up? (reconciliation) ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q10 (Trishul Ex 4).** \"Total average inventory ₹15.2 cr = cycle 11.0 + safety 2.6 + in-transit 1.2.\" → 11.0 + 2.6 + 1.2 = **14.8 ≠ 15.2** → ₹0.4 cr unexplained → defective. With 14.8, S share = 2.6 ÷ 14.8 = **17.6%** (verdict on a 30% claim unchanged).\n**Q11 (Kaveri Ex 1) — cash roll-forward.** 6.3 → 6.3 − 5.8 = 0.5 → 0.5 − 15.6 = −15.1 → −15.1 + 3.6 = −11.5 → −11.5 − 1.9 = −13.4 ≈ −13.3 ✓.\n**Q12 (Kaveri) — implied overheads.** Revenue − production cost − profit: M1 47.0 − 27.2 − 6.3 = 13.5; M5 36.4 − 25.1 − (−1.9) = 13.2 → consistent with the stated ₹12.6–13.2 cr band (plus freight) → the profit lines are credible.\n\n## A6. Simple vs weighted averages — exposing a hidden trend ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q13 (Kaveri A1).** Monthly fill 86.0, 85.9, 73.8, 64.5, 58.2% with sold 435, 397, 259, 342, 374 and lost 71, 65, 92, 188, 269.\n- Simple average = 368.4 ÷ 5 = **73.7%** (the memo's figure).\n- Volume-weighted = Σsold ÷ Σdemand = 1,807 ÷ (1,807 + 685) = 1,807 ÷ 2,492 = **72.5%**.\n- Current month = **58.2%** — 15.5 points below the average; five consecutive declines; lost sales ×3.8 (71 → 269).\n*So what:* the average is arithmetically right and decision-wise misleading.\n\n**Q14. Fill check.** Fill = sold ÷ (sold + lost): M4 = 342 ÷ 530 = **64.5%** ✓.\n\n## A7. Contribution forgone on lost sales (with a mix assumption) ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q15 (Kaveri A2b).** 269 lost sales; M5 sales mix 219 HATCH · 96 SUV · 59 EV; prices ₹6.05 / 15.50 / 14.05 L; unit costs ₹3.85 / 8.30 / 8.00 L.\n*Assumptions:* A1. Lost mix = sales mix. A2. Contribution = price − unit production cost (overheads fixed).\n| | HATCH | SUV | EV | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit contribution (₹ L) | 2.20 | 7.20 | 6.05 | |\n| Lost units (269 × 219/374, 96/374, 59/374) | 157.5 | 69.0 | 42.4 | 269 |\n| Contribution forgone (₹ L) | 346.5 | 497.1 | 256.7 | **1,100 L = ₹11.0 cr** |\n- Ratio to the reported loss: 11.0 ÷ 1.9 = **5.8×**; to the ₹60 L congestion charge: 1,100 ÷ 60 = **18×**.\n- Revenue on the lost sales (cars already in stock): avg price 3,641.9 ÷ 374 = ₹9.74 L × 269 = **₹26.2 cr**.\n\n**Q16. Weighted contribution shortcut.** (219 × 2.20 + 96 × 7.20 + 59 × 6.05) ÷ 374 = 1,529.95 ÷ 374 = **₹4.09 L per car**.\n\n**Q17. Cost of an unrestored price cut (Kaveri).** List 6.40 / 16.00 / 14.50 vs M5 6.05 / 15.50 / 14.05 → cuts of 0.35 / 0.50 / 0.45 L × 219 / 96 / 59 = 76.65 + 48.0 + 26.55 = **₹151.2 L ≈ ₹1.5 cr per month** given away.\n\n**Q18. Was the \"profitable month\" a recovery?** (Kaveri M4) Production cost fell from ₹26.4 cr (M3) to ₹16.8 cr (M4) = **−₹9.6 cr**, because chip allocation cut production to 462 sets. Profit +₹3.6 cr = revenue 33.8 − production 16.8 − overheads ≈13.4 → the \"recovery\" is a forced production cut (cherry-picked month).\n\n## A8. Net value of a \"live\" agent ⭐\n**Q19 (Kaveri Ex 3).** Mode & Routing agent: ₹1.5 L freight saved, ₹4.1 L missed → net = 1.5 − 4.1 = **−₹2.6 L** → it destroys value until planned flows exist.\n\n## A9. Token economics — cost per decision vs value per decision ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q20.** A negotiation agent costs ₹3.0 L a month to run and makes 1,500 decisions; each closed decision is worth on average ₹1,200 of margin. The platform doubles its price. Keep, tune or retire?\n- Cost per decision before = 3,00,000 ÷ 1,500 = **₹200**; after = **₹400**.\n- Value per decision = **₹1,200** → ratio 3.0× after the price rise (6× before).\n- **Verdict:** still clearly positive → keep, and tune (shorter context, caching, cheaper tier) to win back margin. \"A price shock is a signal to re-run the math, not a verdict.\"\n**Q21.** Same agent, but each decision is worth ₹300. After repricing, cost ₹400 > value ₹300 → **tune first**; if a tuned version (say ₹250/decision) still cannot clear ₹300 with margin, **retire** and fall back to the next-best option.\n**Q22 (Chain Reaction tokens).** 24 tokens/month; plan 2 tokens; four agents with upkeep 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 → remaining for transformation = 24 − 2 − 10 = **12 tokens** (and at most 4 transformation actions). Terminating one 4-token-upkeep agent costs 2 tokens once and saves 4 a month → pays back in month 1 if its value per decision no longer clears cost.\n\n**Q23. Efficiency-capture gap (McKinsey, S17 📘).** Agentic procurement gave 20–30% staff-efficiency gains but only 1–3% bottom-line capture. If procurement staff cost ₹10 cr/yr: efficiency \"value\" 25% × 10 = ₹2.5 cr, but realised P&L ≈ 1–3% of the relevant base — always compare **captured margin**, not efficiency, to the run cost.\n\n## A10. Accuracy claims vs naive benchmarks ⭐⭐\n**Q24 (S6 Class Q 6.4).** New model 92% accurate; naive \"same as last week\" 90.5%; orders in full-truck multiples.\n- Gain = 1.5 points; error falls from 9.5% to 8.0% → relative error reduction = 1.5 ÷ 9.5 = **15.8%**.\n- With full-truck ordering, much of that maps to the same truck → **unproven** until priced in decisions changed.\n**Q25 (S7 Class Q 7.3).** Churn is 3% of customers; a model shows 99% accuracy. The trivial \"never churns\" rule scores **97%** → the model's real edge is 2 points at most, and 99% often signals leakage.\n**Q26. Confusion matrix.** 1,000 weeks; model flags demand spikes: TP 40, FP 20, FN 10, TN 930.\n- Accuracy = (40 + 930) ÷ 1,000 = **97%** (looks great).\n- Precision = 40 ÷ (40 + 20) = **66.7%**; Recall = 40 ÷ (40 + 10) = **80%**; F1 = 2 × 0.667 × 0.8 ÷ (0.667 + 0.8) = **0.727**.\n- \"Always predict no spike\" would score 950 ÷ 1,000 = 95% accuracy with 0% recall → accuracy on a skewed problem is misleading; use precision/recall/F1.\n\n## A11. The DRL \"20% better\" claim vs the theory ceiling ⭐⭐\n**Q27 (S10 Class Q 10.1).** A simple myopic policy is known to be within ~5% of optimal cost; your tuned basestock policy is at least as good. A vendor claims its DRL cuts cost 20% vs your incumbent.\n- Incumbent cost ≤ 1.05 × optimal → best possible improvement = 1 − 1/1.05 = **4.8%**.\n- A 20% claim would put the DRL policy **below the optimum** → impossible → the backtest most likely contains **overfitting or a weak baseline**. Fund only a guardrailed pilot.\n\n## A12. Fill rate at constant inventory vs inventory released ⭐\n**Q28 (S8/S10 📘).** One-step planning at a Fortune 150 spare-parts business lifted fill from **77% to 85% at constant inventory**, *or* released **~$20M of inventory at constant service**. If a planner has 10,000 order lines a month: 85% − 77% = 8 points → **800 more lines filled a month** at the same inventory — \"the frontier shifted, not just a point on it\".\n\n## A13. Mode economics — rail floor vs road ⭐⭐\n**Q29.** Base load of 300 cars/month on a lane; road costs ₹12,000 per car; rail is ~25% cheaper (₹9,000) but slots are booked a month ahead and charged whether used or not.\n- All road: 300 × 12,000 = **₹36.0 L**.\n- Book a rail floor of 250 slots + 50 by road: 250 × 9,000 + 50 × 12,000 = 22.5 + 6.0 = **₹28.5 L** → saves ₹7.5 L/month.\n- Worst case: only 200 of the 250 slots used, 100 cars by road: 250 × 9,000 (paid regardless) + 100 × 12,000 = 22.5 + 12.0 = **₹34.5 L** → *still cheaper than all-road*.\n- Break-even utilisation of a slot: pay 9,000 for a slot that saves 12,000 if used → worth booking if P(used) > 9,000 ÷ 12,000 = **75%**. Size the floor to the base load you are ≥ 75% sure of.\n*So what:* \"rail's book-ahead charge is money spent on capacity we may never use\" (Kaveri Claim 4) argues insurance backwards — a floor sized to base load pays even when partly unused, and protects you when road congests.\n\n**Q30 (S14 Class Q 4.1). Split shipment.** Zone needs 120 cars within 1 day; trailer-express ₹30,000/car (1 day), rail ₹9,000/car (2 days). Urgent need 50 cars (stock covers the rest for 2 days) → 50 × 30,000 + 70 × 9,000 = 15.0 + 6.3 = **₹21.3 L** vs all express 120 × 30,000 = **₹36.0 L** → split saves ₹14.7 L and still protects service.\n\n## A14. Scoring frameworks as numericals ⭐⭐\n**Q31. Agentic suitability screen.** Robot routing in your own warehouse: frequency 5, reversibility 4, feedback 5, rule clarity 4, stakes 5 = **23 → L3–L4**. Tail-spend reorder: 5, 4, 4, 4, 4 = **21 → L3–L4**. Strategic supplier partnership: 1, 1, 2, 2, 1 = **7 → keep human**. Rank c > b > a (S4 Class Q 4.5).\n**Q32. Readiness profile.** [Data 2, Masters 1, Architecture 3, Security 2, Compute 5]: mean = 13 ÷ 5 = 2.6, but **the weakest pillar (masters = 1) sets the ceiling**, not the mean → predictive pilot on the cleanest domain; no write-access agents (S2 Class Q 2.5).\n\n## A15. Gate thresholds from a break-even ⭐\n**Q33.** Agent break-even = 1.9%; measurement error on savings ±0.5 point → set the success threshold at **≥ 2.5%** (break-even + margin) and the demotion trigger at **< 1.9%**. Always pair with a counter-metric (e.g. supplier OTIF not down > 2 points).\n\n## A16. Revenue at risk and exposure sizing ⭐\n**Q34 (Trishul).** EU revenue = 12% × ₹1,240 cr = **₹148.8 cr**, growing 30% → next year ≈ 148.8 × 1.3 = **₹193 cr**. Direct-material concentration: top 10 suppliers = 62% × ₹410 cr = **₹254 cr**; dual-sourced on 7 of 10 → 3 single-sourced top suppliers. Use these to argue P3's strategic value (and its high blast radius).\n**Q35.** Stockout revenue loss ₹7.8 cr/yr; \"50% fewer stockouts\" → ₹3.9 cr revenue recovered; at an assumed 40% gross margin = **₹1.56 cr contribution** — revenue is not profit.\n\n---\n\n# Part B — Basic SCM numericals (refresher)\nThese use the standard SCM relationships you already know; the exam may give the formula or expect simple arithmetic. See [SCM Fundamentals](ch00-scm-fundamentals.md).\n\n## B1. EOQ and cycle stock\n**Q36.** Annual demand D = 12,000 units; ordering cost S = ₹1,500/order; holding cost H = ₹20/unit/yr.\n- EOQ = √(2DS ÷ H) = √(2 × 12,000 × 1,500 ÷ 20) = √1,800,000 = **1,342 units**.\n- Orders/yr = 12,000 ÷ 1,342 = **8.9**; average cycle stock = Q/2 = **671 units**.\n- Annual ordering cost = 8.94 × 1,500 = ₹13,410; holding = 671 × 20 = ₹13,420 → equal at EOQ (check ✓).\n*Course link:* if a truck holds 2,000 units and you must ship full trucks, Q is set by the truck, not EOQ — this is why forecasting cannot touch cycle stock.\n\n## B2. Safety stock and reorder point\n**Q37.** Daily demand d = 40 units (σ = 10/day); lead time L = 9 days; service level 95% (z = 1.65).\n- Safety stock = z × σ × √L = 1.65 × 10 × 3 = **49.5 ≈ 50 units**.\n- Reorder point = d × L + SS = 40 × 9 + 50 = **410 units**.\n- *\"The quantile IS the decision\":* a 95% cycle service level = holding the 95th percentile of lead-time demand. Halving σ (better forecast) halves SS to ~25 — but cycle stock (Q/2) is untouched.\n\n**Q38. Same mean, different variance (S8 Class Q 8.2).** SKU A ±50, SKU B ±400 on a forecast of 1,000 (z = 1.65): SS_A = 82.5, SS_B = 660 → \"forecast + 2 weeks of supply\" for both is wrong for both.\n\n## B3. Newsvendor — critical ratio\n**Q39.** A seasonal item costs ₹60, sells for ₹100, leftovers salvage at ₹20.\n- Cost of under-stocking Cu = 100 − 60 = ₹40; over-stocking Co = 60 − 20 = ₹40.\n- Critical ratio = Cu ÷ (Cu + Co) = 40 ÷ 80 = **0.50** → order the median of demand.\n- If salvage rises to ₹50 → Co = 10 → CR = 40 ÷ 50 = **0.80** → order the 80th percentile. *Asymmetric costs → order a quantile, not the mean* (decision-focused learning, S6/S8).\n\n## B4. Inventory turns, days of supply, cash-to-cash\n**Q40.** COGS ₹96 cr/yr; average inventory ₹12 cr.\n- Turns = 96 ÷ 12 = **8×/yr**; days of supply = 12 ÷ (96/365) = **45.6 days**.\n**Q41.** DIO 45 days, DSO 30 days (distributors pay in 30), DPO 40 days → cash-to-cash = 45 + 30 − 40 = **35 days**. Extending supplier terms by 35 days (Pactum) cuts it to **0 days**.\n\n## B5. Fill rate, service level, OTIF\n**Q42.** 1,000 order lines; 930 shipped complete and on time; 40 complete but late; 30 short. Line fill rate = (930 + 40) ÷ 1,000 = **97%**; OTIF = 930 ÷ 1,000 = **93%**.\n\n## B6. Forecast error metrics\n**Q43.** Actual 100, 120, 90, 110; forecast 110, 100, 100, 100.\n- Errors (A − F): −10, +20, −10, +10 → **MAD = 50 ÷ 4 = 12.5**; **MAPE** = (10/100 + 20/120 + 10/90 + 10/110) ÷ 4 = **11.7%**; **bias** (sum of errors) = **+10** (slight under-forecast).\n- MAPE fails on zeros (intermittent spares 0,0,0,4,0,0,2,0 → undefined) — use distribution/quantile metrics instead (S10 Class Q 10.2).\n**Q44. Exponential smoothing (α = 0.3).** F₁ = 500; demand 520, 480, 560 → F₂ = 500 + 0.3 × 20 = **506**; F₃ = 506 + 0.3 × (−26) = **498.2**; F₄ = 498.2 + 0.3 × 61.8 = **516.7**. (Tier-1 spreadsheet method — S9 lab.)\n\n## B7. Bullwhip ratio\n**Q45.** Variance of retail demand = 100; variance of distributor orders = 400; factory orders = 1,600. Bullwhip ratio = 400 ÷ 100 = **4** at the distributor and 1,600 ÷ 400 = **4** again upstream (cumulative **16×**). *Fix (S19):* shared end-customer demand, order smoothing, trained coordination — not faster agents.\n\n## B8. Risk pooling\n**Q46.** Four DCs each face demand σ = 100; safety stock at z = 1.65 → 4 × 165 = **660 units**. Centralising (independent demand) → σ = 100 × √4 = 200 → SS = **330 units** (50% less). Trade-off: longer last-mile distance — the Kaveri physics (zones pull only from their own RDC) means pooling only works if flows are planned.\n\n## B9. Maintenance economics (predictive vs reactive)\n**Q47.** A press fails on average once a quarter; unplanned failure costs ₹8 L (downtime + rush part); planned replacement costs ₹2 L. A predictive model catches 75% of failures early; the sensor kit costs ₹6 L/yr.\n- Reactive cost/yr = 4 × 8 = **₹32 L**.\n- Predictive: 3 caught × 2 + 1 missed × 8 = **₹14 L** + ₹6 L sensors = ₹20 L → saves **₹12 L/yr**.\n- *But* (S13 Class Q 3.1): if a weekly inspect-and-replace routine already catches the wear, the model changes no decision → value ≈ 0.\n**Q48. OEE.** Availability 90% × performance 95% × quality 98% = **83.8%**.\n\n## B10. Landed cost / TCO for sourcing\n**Q49.** Supplier A quotes ₹100/unit, lead time 30 days, 2% defects; Supplier B ₹96/unit, lead time 75 days, 5% defects; defect cost ₹50/unit; holding 20%/yr on pipeline stock.\n- A: 100 + 0.02 × 50 + 100 × 0.20 × 30/365 = 100 + 1.0 + 1.64 = **₹102.6**.\n- B: 96 + 0.05 × 50 + 96 × 0.20 × 75/365 = 96 + 2.5 + 3.95 = **₹102.5** → near-tie on TCO; B's longer lead time also adds safety stock and risk → **A preferred** once risk is counted. *(Use for supplier-risk scoring cases.)*\n\n## B11. Little's Law\n**Q50.** An RDC ships 600 cars/month and holds 662 cars on average → time in system = 662 ÷ 600 ≈ **1.1 months**. If fill is falling at the same time, the stock is in the wrong place (Kaveri).\n\n---\n\n## One-page formula reminder (in case the paper gives them)\n| Quantity | Relationship |\n|---|---|\n| Average inventory | cycle stock (Q/2) + safety stock (S) (+ in-transit) |\n| EOQ | √(2DS/H) |\n| Safety stock | z × σ_d × √L |\n| Reorder point | d × L + SS |\n| Critical ratio (newsvendor) | Cu ÷ (Cu + Co) |\n| Fill rate | units (or lines) delivered ÷ units demanded = sold ÷ (sold + lost) |\n| Turns / days of supply | COGS ÷ avg inventory; avg inventory ÷ daily COGS |\n| Cash-to-cash | DIO + DSO − DPO |\n| Contribution | price − variable (unit) cost |\n| Break-even savings rate | annual cost ÷ addressable pool |\n| Working capital from terms | annual spend × extra days ÷ 365 |\n| Cost per decision | run cost ÷ decisions made |\n| Precision / recall | TP ÷ (TP + FP) / TP ÷ (TP + FN) |\n| Bullwhip ratio | var(orders) ÷ var(demand) |\n"}, {"slug": "case-library", "label": "Case Library", "tag": "CS", "group": "focus", "title": "Case Library — every course case, resolved in one line and in depth", "md": "# Case Library — every course case, resolved in one line and in depth\nEvery session opened with a case, and the exam is **\"a comprehensive, application-based case study covering all concepts\"**. This page lets you revise all of them in one sitting. For each case you get the **facts**, the **trap**, the **framework that cracks it** and the **resolution** in exam language. Named real-world deployments and failures follow, as **evidence to cite** in your answers.\n\n> **How to use:** when a new exam case arrives, find the course case it most resembles in the table below and reuse that case's framework and structure. Most exam cases are recombinations of these.\n\n---\n\n## Quick map — case → trap → framework\n| # | Case (session) | The trap | Framework that cracks it | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **NorthStar Foods** (S1) | \"better forecasting\" proposed when stock is order-size driven | **Q vs S**; Four-Question Claim Audit | [Ch 1](#/ch01-ai-in-operations-and-scm) |\n| 2 | **Meridian Apparel** (S2) | AI pilot on dirty, late, siloed data | **Six data-quality dimensions**; **five-pillar readiness scorecard** | [Ch 2](#/ch02-ai-readiness-data-technology) |\n| 3 | **Continental Parts** (S3) | model ignores phone orders; an ambitious board-deck target; plants that obeyed stocked out | **Phased roadmap with gates**; pilot-selection matrix; trust design | [Ch 3](#/ch03-ai-readiness-process-organisation) |\n| 4 | **Vantage Logistics** (S4) | scaling the pilot with the biggest % gain | **Autonomy ladder**; **agentic suitability screen**; omitted objectives | [Ch 4](#/ch04-agentic-ai-systems) |\n| 5 | **Solstice Industrials** (S5) | choosing a platform from a demo under GPU scarcity | **Six platform filters**; build/buy/blend | [Ch 5](#/ch05-ai-platforms) |\n| 6 | **SuryaMart** (S6) | \"92% accuracy in the PoC\" | **ML Framing Canvas**; naive benchmark | [Ch 6](#/ch06-machine-learning-for-operations) |\n| 7 | **Meghna Foods** (S7) | a 94% notebook only one analyst can run | **Seven-step workflow**; capability checklist | [Ch 6](#/ch06-machine-learning-for-operations) |\n| 8 | **Nandini Beverages** (S8) | buying the \"autonomous agent\" pitch | **Signal audit**; update vs execute; distribution forecasting | [Ch 7](#/ch07-demand-forecasting) |\n| 9 | **Trident Distributors** (S9/S12) | MD wants \"what the big retailers have\" | **Tier matching / bake-off ladder** | [Ch 7](#/ch07-demand-forecasting) |\n| 10 | **Krishna AgroMachinery** (S10) | \"20% better in our backtest\" | **Benchmark ceiling (~5%)**; **guardrailed pilot** | [Ch 8](#/ch08-inventory-optimisation) |\n| 11 | **Bearing / telemetry call** (S13) | acting on a prediction that changes nothing | **Twin-now / twin-later test** | [Ch 9](#/ch09-predictive-maintenance-digital-twins) |\n| 12 | **Capacity shock / LP mandate** (S14–15) | single-mode answer; cost-only objective | **Split shipment**; **Mandate Card**; Optimizer Mandate Checklist | [Ch 10](#/ch10-logistics-routing-optimisation) |\n| 13 | **Clause budget / chip contract** (S16) | one accuracy bar for all clauses | **Clause-stakes checklist** | [Ch 11](#/ch11-nlp-procurement-token-economics) |\n| 14 | **Repriced agent** (S17) | retire or ignore by reflex | **Tune-or-retire** | [Ch 11](#/ch11-nlp-procurement-token-economics) |\n| 15 | **Paint-colour scenario** (S18) | vivid = likely | **Scenario portfolio checklist** | [Ch 12](#/ch12-genai-design-multi-agent) |\n| 16 | **Command Your Agent bullwhip** (S19) | smart agents reacting to orders | **Shared signal, smoothing, trained coordination; ACAP** | [Ch 12](#/ch12-genai-design-multi-agent) |\n| 17 | **Bias before the board** (S20) | hide it until after the meeting | **ACAP**; step down and disclose | [Ch 13](#/ch13-responsible-ai-ethics) |\n| PYQ | **Trishul Consumer Products** | vendor % claims, data defects, procurement agent | Claim audit; Q vs S; data triage; autonomy + mandate | [Trishul](#/pyq-trishul) |\n| PYQ | **Kaveri Motors** | averages hiding trends, cherry-picked months, physics read as luck | Claim audit; contribution arithmetic; Mandate Card | [Kaveri](#/pyq-kaveri) |\n\n---\n\n## 1. NorthStar Foods — Forecasting fix or something else? (S1) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:** high inventory. Two proposals: an **ML forecasting upgrade** or a **lead-time/order-frequency initiative**. Finance says order-size-driven stock costs **about 4× the uncertainty buffer**.\n- **Trap:** assuming that better forecasting cuts all inventory.\n- **Framework:** **Q vs S**. Inventory = cycle stock (Q/2, set by batch size and order frequency) + safety stock (z·σ·√L, set by uncertainty and lead time). **Forecasting attacks S only**; lead-time and order-frequency work attacks **both Q and S**.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Q ≈ 4 × S, so even a perfect forecast (S → 0) removes only about 20% of inventory.\n  - **Fund the lead-time/frequency initiative first.**\n  - Pilot forecasting later on volatile SKUs.\n- **Numeric:** total inventory ₹50 cr with Q-part ₹40 cr and S-part ₹10 cr.\n  - A 30% better forecast gives about −₹3 cr.\n  - Halving the order size gives −₹20 cr (Q/2 halves).\n  - Halving the lead time cuts S by about 29% (1 − 1/√2), i.e. −₹2.9 cr, on top.\n\n## 2. Meridian Apparel — Are we ready for AI? (S2) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - The CEO wants a **Zara-style trend-detection pilot now**.\n  - Problems: duplicate SKUs, **24-hour data latency**, no GPU budget, an unknown API inventory.\n  - The **e-commerce division is clean and API-first**.\n- **Trap:** a company-wide AI launch on a broken foundation.\n- **Framework:** **six data-quality dimensions** (accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, uniqueness, validity) and the **five-pillar readiness scorecard** (Data, Technology, Process, Organisation, Governance; 1–5 each).\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Not ready company-wide (uniqueness and timeliness fail, and trend detection needs freshness).\n  - **Pilot in the clean e-commerce division** where data is ready.\n  - In parallel, run SKU master-data dedup and a latency fix as gates.\n  - Spend first on foundations, not GPUs.\n\n## 3. Continental Parts — The roadmap rebellion (S3) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - **Plants 1–2 ignored** the forecasting tool and avoided stockouts.\n  - **Plants 3–4 obeyed**, cut inventory and **stocked out**.\n  - The model **missed 15% of demand that arrives by phone**.\n  - A **25%-in-six-months** target was set for a board deck.\n- **Trap:** blaming the \"rebels\"; pushing harder for compliance.\n- **Framework:** the **phased roadmap with gates and frozen baselines**; **trust design** (overrides are information); process standardisation before automation.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - The rebels were right: the model's data foundation omitted the phone channel.\n  - Capture phone orders, re-baseline, and **reset the target** to an evidence-based gate.\n  - Run a bounded-override pilot with reason capture.\n  - Scale only after gate metrics hold on a **frozen baseline vs control**.\n- **Recurring callback:** \"a planner asked to follow unexplainable order quantities re-runs Continental Parts\" (S10).\n\n## 4. Vantage Logistics — Which pilot do you scale? (S4) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - **routing agent +14%**, with driver-safety concerns;\n  - **picking robots +18%**, with a hardware backlog;\n  - **delay rebooking +6%**, with the highest satisfaction.\n- **Trap:** scaling the biggest number.\n- **Framework:** the **autonomy ladder L1–L5**, the **agentic suitability screen** (20+ / 12–19 / <12), and \"**agents fail on what their objective omits**\".\n- **Resolution:**\n  - **Scale delay rebooking**: low blast radius, reversible, users like it.\n  - Fix the routing agent's objective (add safety constraints) before promoting it.\n  - Robots are hardware-constrained, so scaling them is a capex timing decision.\n  - Before promoting any pilot, ask for the overridden cases, error costs in both directions, the omitted objectives and a frozen baseline.\n\n## 5. Solstice Industrials — Platform choice under constraint (S5) ⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - The estate is **Microsoft-centric**.\n  - **AWS offers a faster pre-built pilot**.\n  - A **GPU shortage delays either choice by a quarter**.\n  - The sustainability office asks about emissions.\n- **Trap:** deciding from the feature demo, or because of the delay.\n- **Framework:** the **six platform filters**, applied in order (use-case fit, data gravity, skills, governance fit, TCO including tokens, exit/lock-in), plus **build/buy/blend**.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Data gravity and skills favour Azure.\n  - Demand governance evidence from both vendors (per-agent identity, spend caps, logs).\n  - The GPU delay changes the **plan**, not the **decision**: sequence low-compute predictive pilots first.\n  - Ask for region carbon intensity and PUE.\n  - Negotiate exit terms.\n\n## 6. SuryaMart — Three ML pitches, one budget (S6) ⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - A: forecasting for 40,000 SKUs.\n  - B: customer segmentation.\n  - C: supplier-delay early warning.\n  - \"92% accuracy in the PoC\".\n- **Framework:** the **ML Framing Canvas** (decision, target, data, error costs, baseline).\n- **Resolution:**\n  - **Fund C first**: clear owner, fast feedback, priced asymmetric cost.\n  - Reshape A with tier matching and a naive benchmark.\n  - B needs a decision (what changes per segment?) before it can be validated.\n\n## 7. Meghna Foods — The 94% notebook (S7) ⭐⭐\n- **Facts:** only the analyst can run it, from CSVs on her laptop.\n- **Framework:** the **seven-step workflow** and the **capability checklist** (reproducible, monitored, owned, fallback, versioned, permissions and logging).\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Dig, then delay.\n  - The 94% needs a naive benchmark, a time-based holdout and evaluation at decision granularity.\n  - Production breaks first on the **bus factor** and manual CSV exports.\n  - \"Done\" = a scheduled pipeline feeding planning by cut-off, monitored, owned, with a fallback.\n\n## 8. Nandini Beverages — Three forecasting pitches before the monsoon (S8) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - **A**: statistical, ₹40 L. **B**: ML + weather/IoT sensing, ₹1.4 cr. **C**: \"autonomous agent\", ₹2.1 cr.\n  - Sales inflates and planning deflates.\n  - The **monsoon drives 40% of variance**.\n  - Failures cluster in **promo and monsoon-transition weeks**.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - **Sequence A → targeted B**: benchmark, repair promo history, set a single consensus number, then audit and pilot weather/promo signals on the failing weeks with distribution forecasts.\n  - Ask C \"**update or execute?**\".\n  - None of the pitches fixes the incentive politics; that needs architecture and transparency.\n  - Full answer in [Ch 7 §8](#/ch07-demand-forecasting).\n\n## 9. Trident Distributors — Which tier fits? (S9, S12) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - **8,000 SKU-locations**, ordered weekly;\n  - 2 analysts (one knows a little Python);\n  - readiness **[3,2,3,3,3]**;\n  - **₹25 L** budget;\n  - the MD saw a cloud demo;\n  - 8-year spreadsheet incumbent.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - **Tier 2 (Prophet-class), scripted**, with the spreadsheet as benchmark and fallback.\n  - It must beat the analyst's forecasts on a holdout, per segment, and prove it to the analyst and the MD.\n  - Cloud becomes right when T ≥ 3.5, a data engineer is in place, volume is in the tens of thousands and the bake-off uplift pays for 6× the cost.\n\n## 10. Krishna AgroMachinery — The 20% claim (S10) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - DRL vendor claims **−20% inventory** vs basestock \"in our backtest\";\n  - intermittent demand;\n  - the incumbent hits **89% fill**;\n  - planners override **~30%**.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - 20% exceeds the theoretical headroom (myopic ≈ within 5% of optimal), so suspect a **weak baseline or overfitting**.\n  - **Fund a guardrailed pilot**: scope = one family for 2 quarters; bounds = incumbent floors/ceilings; fallback = auto-revert; evaluation = randomised/matched control, service per ₹; trust loop = override with reasons; owners named.\n  - Mine the overrides.\n  - Full answer in [Ch 8 §8](#/ch08-inventory-optimisation).\n\n## 11. The bearing and the telemetry call (S13) ⭐⭐\n- **Facts:** a 68% failure flag, but the **weekly inspect-and-replace** protocol already catches the failure.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - **Don't act.** The prediction doesn't change the decision.\n  - Fund telemetry only where the twin-now test passes all of its steps: decision-changing, high failure cost, data readiness, minimal fidelity needed.\n\n## 12. The capacity shock and the LP mandate (S14–S15) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:**\n  - The Mumbai zone is about to stock out.\n  - The road lane is at −40%.\n  - Express is costly; rail is too slow.\n  - Separately, a team set **identical cost weights**, and the solver starved the zone that drives **40% of premium-margin sales**.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - **Split the shipment**: express for the urgent part, rail as backfill.\n  - Put zone priority **into the objective** (cost weights or service floors) **before solving**.\n  - Write the **Mandate Card**.\n\n## 13. The clause budget and the chip contract (S16) ⭐⭐\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Tune **penalty and indemnification** extraction first: uncapped exposure, discovered only when the supplier invokes it.\n  - Domain-tuned (94.7%) beats general (79.3%).\n  - Never fully automate interpreting liability clauses.\n\n## 14. The repriced agent (S17) ⭐⭐\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Compare **value-per-decision vs cost-per-decision**.\n  - Tune first (context, cache, batch, tier), and retire only if it still fails.\n  - Spend caps go **in code**.\n\n## 15. The celebrity paint-colour scenario (S18) ⭐⭐\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Label it **stretch**.\n  - Check the mechanism and base rate.\n  - Prefer a **cheap, reversible mitigation** over flex capex.\n\n## 16. Command Your Agent — the amplification we made (S19) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Facts:** 20 teams' agents created a textbook bullwhip on the class screen.\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Use a **shared end-customer demand signal**, order smoothing and trained coordination.\n  - Name the coordination mechanism and run **ACAP**.\n\n## 17. Bias before the board (S20) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Resolution:**\n  - Step down to **L2**.\n  - Log the incident.\n  - Disclose to the board as a **governance finding**.\n  - Fix the data, verify parity and re-promote.\n  - \"Guardrails count only when you use them when it costs you something.\"\n\n---\n\n## Named real-world deployments (evidence to cite) ⭐⭐\n| Company | What | Result | Use it to argue |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Amazon (Kiva → Amazon Robotics)** | goods-to-person robots (Kiva bought 2012, ~$775M) | pickers stay put; density and pick rate up | automation vs agentic |\n| **Amazon DeepFleet** (Jun 2025) | GenAI foundation model coordinating 1M+ robots, trained on SageMaker | **10% less robot travel time** | fleet-level self-coordination |\n| **Walmart × Pactum** | autonomous tail-spend negotiations within manager-set bounds | **~3% savings, +35-day terms, 64–68% close rate** | bounded autonomy (L3/L4) and mandates |\n| **Walmart Trend-to-Product** | GenAI product design loop | **up to 18 weeks** off design-to-shelf | GenAI speed in design |\n| **Unilever** | AI across ~20 control towers | stockouts −25%, retailer sales +15–35% | predictive control towers |\n| **JD.com** | dual engine: AI sensing + classical solver | national-scale routing and placement | AI–OR symbiosis |\n| **Fortune 150 OEM** (course text Ch 11) | one-step planning | **retailer forecasts got zero weight** | signals earn weights |\n| **Semiconductor spare parts** (course text Ch 11) | installed-base signals, 50,000 SKUs | **fill 77% → 85% at constant inventory (~$20M)** | upstream drivers, one-step |\n| **E-commerce RCTs / Alibaba field study** | learned replenishment vs buyers | better in-stock and turnover | evaluate by experiment |\n| **Siemens** | digital twins across factories and customers' lines | simulate before touching equipment | fidelity-for-purpose twins |\n| **IoT-triggered 3D-printed spares** | print the predicted part | weeks → days | collapse knowing-to-acting |\n| **Danone / Zara / Lenovo** | ML planning, trend sensing, +5% forecast accuracy, +4% OTD | measured, incremental | sensing and incremental value |\n| **M5 competition** (Walmart data, 42,840 series) | ML beat statistics overall | simple methods competitive at SKU-store | price the gap before paying for clever |\n| **McKinsey chemicals pilot** | agentic procurement | **20–30% efficiency, 1–3% captured** | efficiency-capture gap |\n| **EY CPO survey** | ~80% of CPOs plan GenAI within 3 years | spend analytics, contracts first | adoption context |\n\n## Named failures (the cautionary tales) ⭐⭐⭐\n| Failure | What went wrong | Lesson |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nike & i2 (2001)** | ~$400M programme; the planning system over-ordered slow sellers; ~$100M shortfall; −20% share price | systems amplify their data and process foundations; test on your own history |\n| **Zillow Offers (2021)** | ML price forecasts bought houses; >$500M write-down, ~25% of staff cut | the decision defines the model; irreversible decisions need proportionate guardrails |\n| **2020 demand-model collapse** | history-trained models predicted the vanished world | regime-change fragility; monitoring and override |\n| **Two stalled DRL pilots** (course text Ch 10) | teams couldn't interpret outputs, convergence or retraining | expertise and the production cliff |\n| **DRL 15–20% behind a myopic rule** | learned the simulator, not the world | benchmark before trust |\n| **1990s black-box scheduler** | better but illegible, so rejected | explainability beats an opaque optimum |\n| **Air Canada chatbot (2024)** | airline held liable for its bot's wrong answer | the deployer answers for its agent |\n| **Chain Reaction theatre teams** | built below gate; B11 and B14 orchestrators failed at 20% and 12% odds | sequence beats speed |\n"}, {"slug": "ch00-scm-fundamentals", "label": "SCM Fundamentals", "tag": "00", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 0 — SCM Fundamentals (the basics the exam assumes)", "md": "# Ch 0 — SCM Fundamentals (the basics the exam assumes)\nYour faculty's note: **\"Basic Concepts of SCM will also be required.\"** 📘 The cases sit on top of ordinary supply chain logic — cycle vs safety stock, service level, bullwhip, mode choice, network flows, procurement. This chapter is the refresher, written so that every concept is tied to where it appears in the AI course and the past papers. Concepts not in the decks are marked 📗 (standard SCM texts: Chopra & Meindl, Simchi-Levi, Fisher, Stadtler & Kilger).\n\n---\n\n## 1. What a supply chain is\n- **Supply chain** 📗: all parties involved, directly or indirectly, in fulfilling a customer request — suppliers, manufacturers, distributors (RDCs), retailers/dealers, customers — linked by **three flows**: **product** (downstream), **information** (both ways) and **funds** (upstream).\n- **Supply chain management** 📗: managing those flows to maximise total supply-chain surplus (customer value − total supply chain cost).\n- **The core problem never changed** 📘: \"matching supply and demand under uncertainty remains the discipline's core\" (OM community vision, S1) — AI reshapes the *practice*, not the problem.\n- **Chain Reaction's chain** 📘: chip suppliers → 3 plants → 4 RDCs → 7 dealer zones → customers, with rail/road/trailer-express links.\n\n## 2. Decision levels (and where AI fits) ⭐⭐\n| Level | Horizon | Examples | AI posture (S3 📘) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Strategic (design)** | years | network design, sourcing strategy, make/buy, capacity, MTS/MTO/ATO, postponement | input only |\n| **Tactical (planning)** | months–quarters | S&OP/SIOP, aggregate production plan, inventory policy, transport contracts | augment planners |\n| **Operational** | days–hours | order promising (ATP), scheduling, replenishment, routing, dispatch | delegate (within envelopes) |\n**The supply chain planning matrix** (Stadtler & Kilger, the course e-book 📘): long-term strategic network planning; mid-term master planning; short-term demand planning, production planning & scheduling, distribution & transport planning, purchasing & materials requirement planning, demand fulfilment & ATP. APS (Advanced Planning Systems, late 1990s) layered LP/MILP optimisation and hierarchical planning on ERP; AI adds always-on re-planning, ML with external signals, RL scheduling and dynamic re-routing (S1 table 📘).\n\n## 3. Strategic fit — Fisher (1997) and the Triple-A chain ⭐⭐\n| | **Functional products** (stable, predictable, low margin — e.g. HATCH, toothpaste) | **Innovative products** (volatile, short life, high margin — e.g. fashion, new EVs) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Right chain | **Efficient** (cost-focused: full trucks, high utilisation, low inventory) | **Responsive** (speed and flexibility: buffer capacity, postponement, sensing) |\n- **Lee's Triple-A (2004)** 📗: **Agility** (respond to short-term changes), **Adaptability** (adjust design to structural shifts), **Alignment** (align incentives across partners). The course mentions the \"agility-adaptability-alignment triad\" and a 2026 \"digital super-agility\" recalibration 📘.\n- *Course link:* Zara's ML trend detection serves a **responsive** chain design — \"the tool matched the chain\" (S6 📘). A tool that does not match the chain's strategy is wasted.\n\n## 4. Drivers of supply chain performance 📗\n**Facilities** (plants, RDCs — location, capacity), **inventory** (cycle, safety, seasonal, pipeline), **transportation** (mode, route, network), **information** (visibility, forecasts, coordination), **sourcing** (in-house vs outsourced, supplier selection), **pricing** (price and promotion levers). Each trades **responsiveness vs efficiency**.\n\n## 5. Inventory — types and why they exist ⭐⭐⭐\n| Type | Why it exists | Driven by | What reduces it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Cycle stock (Q/2)** | ordering/producing in batches (economies of scale) | batch size Q: trucks, pallets, MOQs, set-ups | smaller, more frequent orders; lower set-up/order cost; shorter lead times |\n| **Safety stock (S)** | buffer against demand and supply uncertainty | σ of demand over lead time, service level, lead-time variability | **better forecasts**, shorter/less variable lead times, pooling |\n| **Pipeline / in-transit** | goods moving between nodes | lead time × flow rate | faster modes, shorter distances |\n| **Seasonal / anticipation** | build ahead of peaks | capacity vs seasonal demand | flexible capacity |\n| **Decoupling / WIP** | buffers between stages | process variability | flow, lean |\n**The course's key point (S1 📘):** inventory follows a **saw-tooth** — cycle stock from batch Q plus safety stock S. **A perfect forecast eliminates only S**; most inventory sits in Q, driven by physical constraints. → Forecasting AI attacks S, not Q (Trishul A1; Class Q 1.2). **Campbell Soup:** sharing POS data to improve forecasts gave marginal benefit; **cutting order-processing lead time and ordering more often (Q↓) was the big win**.\n\n## 6. Inventory policies ⭐⭐\n- **EOQ** (Harris, 1913): Q* = √(2DS/H) balances ordering and holding cost; right for stable demand (S10 📘).\n- **Continuous review (s, Q)**: when inventory position hits the reorder point s (= d × L + SS), order Q.\n- **Periodic review / basestock (order-up-to S)**: every period, order up to a target set by the demand distribution over (review + lead time). \"Decades of theory; auditable; near-optimal when tuned\" (S10 📘).\n- **(s, S)**: review; if below s, order up to S.\n- **Multi-echelon inventory optimisation (MEIO/EIO)**: sets buffers jointly across echelons; 700+ Global 2000 deployments (S10 📘).\n- **Service level**: **cycle service level** (probability of no stockout in a cycle) vs **fill rate** (share of demand met from stock). \"Service level = which quantile you hold\" (S8 📘).\n- **Weeks-of-supply rules** convert forecasts crudely into stock — \"the damage happens AFTER the forecast\" (S8 📘): inflated forecast → excess; conservative → shortages; identical means with different variances get identical stock.\n- **Intermittent demand** (spares: 0,0,0,4,0,0,2,0): point forecasts near-meaningless; use distributions, pooled signals (installed base); MAPE undefined on zeros (S10 📘).\n\n## 7. Forecasting basics ⭐⭐\n- **Components**: level, trend, seasonality, noise; causal drivers (price, promotions, weather, installed base).\n- **Methods**: naive (\"same as last week/year\"), moving average, exponential smoothing (Holt-Winters), regression with features, ML (gradient boosting won the M5), Prophet (trend + seasonality + holidays by default), LSTM/transformers (long histories, high maturity).\n- **Error metrics**: MAD, MAPE (fails at zero), RMSE, **bias** (systematic over/under), **forecast value added (FVA)** vs naive; **quantile/pinball loss and calibration** for probability forecasts.\n- **Principles**: forecasts are always wrong; aggregate forecasts are more accurate than disaggregate; shorter horizons are more accurate; **forecast the distribution, not the number** (S8 📘); **no model without a naive benchmark** (S6 📘).\n- **Organisational reality** (S8 📘): many competing forecasts (sales in ₹ monthly, planning in units weekly, each partner separately), each a negotiating position — sales inflates, planning deflates. A deployed system gave the **customers' own forecasts zero weight**.\n\n## 8. The bullwhip effect ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Definition** 📗: order variability **amplifies** as you move upstream (retailer → distributor → manufacturer → supplier), even when consumer demand is stable (Lee, Padmanabhan & Whang 1997; Forrester 1961).\n- **Causes**: demand-signal processing (each tier forecasts from the orders it sees), **order batching**, **price fluctuations / promotions** (forward buying), **rationing and shortage gaming** (over-ordering under allocation), long lead times.\n- **Classic remedies**: share POS/end-customer demand (information sharing, VMI, CPFR), shorten lead times, smaller more frequent orders, everyday low pricing, allocate on past sales not orders.\n- **Course twist ⭐⭐⭐ (S4, S19 📘):** **LLM agents can amplify bullwhip** — in beer-game experiments most decentralised LLM agents created MORE instability than simple baselines (\"collaboration paradox\"); your own class reproduced it in July's Command Your Agent finale. Fixes: **shared demand signal, order smoothing/rate limits, trained coordination** — \"stability is mechanism design, not intelligence\". **Inverse bullwhip** (S3 📘): better forecasts at the customer can *increase* variability at the manufacturer under quantity discounts.\n\n## 9. Distribution and network design ⭐⭐\n- **Network structures** 📗: direct shipping, via DCs/RDCs, cross-docking, milk runs, hub-and-spoke; **push vs pull** (make-to-stock pushes to RDCs on forecast; pull ships on orders). Chain Reaction's **default auto-push** is a pure push with no plan — the reason stock ended up in the wrong RDCs.\n- **Risk pooling** 📗: centralising stock reduces safety stock by ~√n for independent demand, at the cost of longer delivery distance.\n- **Postponement** 📗: delay differentiation (paint colour, packaging, final assembly) until demand is known.\n- **Network flow optimisation** 📘 (S15): plants, RDCs, zones as nodes; lanes as edges with capacity and cost; a **min-cost-flow LP** finds the cheapest flow meeting demand — but only for the objective (service targets, cost weights) you set.\n- **Asian Paints** 📘 (S1): demand data drove direct-to-dealer distribution that removed a distributor tier → turns ~3–4× peers — information transformative because it enabled a **structural** change.\n\n## 10. Transportation ⭐⭐\n| Mode | Cost | Speed | Flexibility | Chain Reaction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Rail** | lowest per unit (≈25% below road) | slow | slot-constrained, book ahead | booked a month ahead, charged used or not |\n| **Road (FTL/LTL)** | medium | medium | high — door to door | default lane; flexible workhorse |\n| **Express / air / trailer-express** | highest | fastest | capacity-limited | urgent top-ups |\n| **Sea / container** | very low | very slow | port-dependent | (port jam event) |\n- **Economies of scale**: full-truck loads cut cost per unit → large Q (why FTL multiples dominate cycle stock).\n- **Mode choice = cost vs speed vs reliability**, made per lane; AI re-evaluates the trade-off \"every lane, every hour, not weekly\" (S14 📘). Under a shock, **split** across modes.\n- **Line-haul dominates routing cost** — better demand forecasts barely shorten the out-and-back distance (S1 📘).\n- **Vehicle routing (VRP)** 📗: Google **OR-Tools**, **OptaPlanner** (constraint solver), **Q-learning**-based dynamic routing (syllabus tools 📘).\n\n## 11. Sourcing and procurement ⭐⭐\n- **Procure-to-pay (P2P)** 📗: need → requisition → RFx (RFI/RFQ/RFP) → evaluation → negotiation → contract → PO → receipt → invoice → payment.\n- **Spend categories**: **direct** (materials in the product — Trishul ₹410 cr, 78 suppliers) vs **indirect/MRO** (maintenance, repair, operations; **tail spend** = the long tail of many small suppliers — Trishul ₹64 cr, 2,410 suppliers, median ₹1.9 L).\n- **Kraljic matrix** 📗: profit impact × supply risk → **strategic** (partner), **leverage** (compete/negotiate hard), **bottleneck** (secure supply), **non-critical/routine** (simplify, automate) — *tail spend is mostly non-critical → automate (Pactum); strategic suppliers stay human (S4 Class Q 4.5).*\n- **TCO / landed cost** 📗: price + freight + duties + quality cost + inventory carrying + risk.\n- **Supplier risk** 📗: concentration (top-10 share), single vs dual sourcing, financial stress, geopolitics, lead-time reliability — feeds supplier-risk scoring (Trishul P3).\n- **Contracts**: payment terms (days payable), volume commitments, **penalty/indemnification clauses** (strictest accuracy bar — S16 📘), renewal windows. **Take-or-pay / capacity reservation** (Chain Reaction chip book: fees on the full book used or not).\n\n## 12. S&OP / IBP 📗\nMonthly cross-functional cycle: **data gathering → demand review (consensus forecast) → supply review (capacity) → pre-S&OP reconciliation → executive S&OP** — one plan for sales, operations and finance. AI role: scenario generation in minutes, always-on re-planning (S1 table 📘); still **tactical → augment** (S3 📘).\n\n## 13. Performance metrics ⭐⭐\n| Area | KPI |\n|---|---|\n| Service | **fill rate**, cycle service level, **OTIF**, order cycle time, lost sales, backorders |\n| Inventory | **turns**, days of supply (DOS/DIO), inventory value, obsolescence |\n| Cost | cost to serve, freight cost per unit, carrying cost (rate × value), landed cost |\n| Cash | **cash-to-cash cycle** = DIO + DSO − DPO; working capital |\n| Forecast | MAPE/WMAPE, bias, forecast value added |\n| Procurement | savings %, spend under management, negotiated share, payment terms |\n| Assets | OEE (availability × performance × quality), utilisation, uptime |\n| Balanced view | **Balanced Scorecard** (financial, customer, internal process, learning & growth — S3 📘) |\n**Guarded metrics** (course rule): every success metric needs a counter-metric — inventory cut must not buy stockouts; savings must not buy worse supplier OTIF; fill must not be bought with expedites.\n\n## 14. Supply chain risk and resilience 📗\n- **Risk types**: demand (EV surge, credit slump), supply (chip allocation), logistics (port jam, corridor congestion, carrier failure), environmental (monsoon), cyber (NotPetya cost Maersk ~$300M in 2017 📘), regulatory.\n- **Resilience levers**: buffers (inventory, capacity), redundancy (dual sourcing, multiple modes), flexibility (postponement, flexible plants), visibility (control towers — Unilever ~20, P&G ~110 plants 📘), insurance/hedges (hazard cover, diesel hedge), scenario planning (GenAI scenario engines — S18 📘), **digital twins** (\"primary enabler of resilience\", ASCM 2026 📘).\n- **Antifragility** 📘: design that gets stronger because the shock happened.\n\n## 15. IT systems in the supply chain ⭐\n**ERP** (one database of record: materials, orders, money — MRP 1960s–70s → MRP II → ERP 1990s) · **APS** (optimisation over ERP) · **WMS** (warehouse) · **TMS** (transport) · **MES** (shop floor) · **EDI / APIs / RFID / barcodes** (data capture) · **control towers** (end-to-end visibility) · **data warehouse → lake → lakehouse + streaming → semantic layer → agent-ready data products** (S2 📘). **The spreadsheet ring**: 61% of Trishul's planning decisions run in spreadsheets beside an 11-year-old ERP 📘.\n\n## 16. Quick SCM-to-AI concept map\n| Basic SCM concept | AI course application |\n|---|---|\n| Cycle vs safety stock | Forecasting AI attacks S only → size the pool first (S1) |\n| Service level & quantiles | Probability forecasting; newsvendor critical ratio (S8) |\n| Bullwhip | LLM agents amplify it; shared signals fix it (S4, S19) |\n| Network flows | Min-cost-flow LP operated by an agent under a human mandate (S15) |\n| Mode choice | AI re-evaluates mode mix per lane per hour; split under shocks (S14) |\n| Tail spend & Kraljic | Autonomous negotiation (Walmart–Pactum) inside mandates (S4, S16) |\n| Supplier risk | Risk scoring under EU AI Act human oversight (S5, S20) |\n| Maintenance | RUL prediction, digital twins, twin-now/twin-later (S13) |\n| S&OP | GenAI scenarios + optimiser; augment planners (S3, S18) |\n| KPIs & guarded metrics | Frozen baselines, counter-metrics, gates (S3, S10) |\n"}, {"slug": "ch01-ai-in-operations-and-scm", "label": "AI in Ops & SCM", "tag": "01", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 1 — Introduction to AI in Operations & SCM (Session 1)", "md": "# Ch 1 — Introduction to AI in Operations & SCM (Session 1)\n**Syllabus unit 1 📘:** *Introduction to AI in Operations & SCM — agentic systems such as Amazon Kiva robots; platforms such as Azure AI, AWS AI.* **Session 1 📘:** \"The evolution of intelligent supply chain systems — and an evidence-based look at what AI has (and has not) changed.\" Opening case: **NorthStar Foods — Forecasting Fix or Something Else?**\n\n> **Exam use:** this chapter gives you the **Four-Question Claim Audit**, the **Q vs S** logic and the **rain-forecast** idea — the tools behind Trishul A1. Every vendor % claim in a case should be put through them.\n\n---\n\n## 1. A working taxonomy: from rules to agents ⭐⭐⭐\n| Layer | What it is | SCM use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rule-based systems** | if-then logic, MRP run rules, reorder points; deterministic, auditable, brittle | still runs most of the world's ERP transactions |\n| **Machine learning** | learns patterns from data: regression, trees, SVM, k-means, neural nets | demand sensing, risk scoring, quality inspection |\n| **Deep learning** | multi-layer neural nets (CNN, RNN, LSTM, transformers); excels at perception | images, text, sequences, sensor streams |\n| **Predictive (analytical) AI** | forecasts and classifies from history | demand, delays, failures, fraud — \"the workhorse of supply chain analytics today\" |\n| **Generative AI** | creates new content: text, scenarios, code, designs (LLMs: GPT, Claude, Gemini) | scenario generation, drafting, design-to-shelf |\n| **Agentic AI** | **acts autonomously toward goals with minimal human intervention: perceives, plans, uses tools, executes within guardrails** — \"this course's focus\" | negotiation, replenishment, routing, robot fleets |\n**Precise vocabulary is your first defence against agent-washing** 📘. Class Q 1.1: software that reads supplier emails, drafts replies and — once approved — **posts confirmed delivery dates into the ERP** = **an agentic AI system** (perceives, reasons, acts on systems of record); approval-gating puts it **low on the autonomy scale**. A copilot stops at drafting; RPA follows fixed rules without reasoning over language.\n\n**What AI actually is** 📘: **weak (narrow) AI** is all we have — it does one job (classify, recommend, forecast); strong AI remains science fiction. Its real talent is **pattern-matching** at scale (chess, Jeopardy, Go). The frontier is **learning over memorising** (a system shown eight dogs must generalise to a ninth). **Fit test:** work that runs on set rules and heavy pattern-matching benefits first; creativity or sparse-data judgement keeps a human in charge. AI has **seasons** (winters and summers) — judge capability, not the weather.\n\n## 2. Technology timeline — from paved roads to agentic AI 📘\n1. **Physical backbone** — paved roads, standardised containers (1956), combustion engine → cheap transport enabled large networks and global sourcing.\n2. **Data capture** — barcodes (1974), EDI, RFID → transactions become machine-readable; visibility begins.\n3. **Transactional systems** — MRP (1960s–70s), MRP II, ERP (1990s) → one database of record.\n4. **Planning intelligence** — APS (late 1990s): optimisation and hierarchical planning on ERP.\n5. **Learning systems** — e-commerce, IoT, cloud, ML forecasting and DRL (2010s).\n6. **Generative & agentic** — LLMs (2022–), copilots, autonomous agents (2024–): systems that converse, reason and act.\n**The test for each generation:** did it merely speed up existing tasks, or **enable supply chains that operate in fundamentally new ways**?\n\n## 3. What APS already did — and where AI adds 📘\n| Planning task | APS approach (1998–2015) | What AI/agents change |\n|---|---|---|\n| Demand planning | statistical time series + judgment | ML with external signals; continuous demand sensing |\n| Master / S&OP planning | LP/MILP runs weekly–monthly | always-on re-planning; scenarios in minutes |\n| Production scheduling | heuristics, finite-capacity schedulers | reinforcement learning; self-adjusting schedules |\n| Distribution & transport | batch route optimisation | dynamic re-routing on live telematics and traffic |\n| Available-to-promise | rule-based allocation from ERP | conversational, LLM-mediated commitments with reasoning |\n\n## 4. The \"modest impact so far\" argument (Cachon, core reading) ⭐⭐\n- **The claim:** AI has not yet made supply chains operate in fundamentally different ways or satisfy new customer needs.\n- **The benchmark:** the Internet + mobile DID transform — Amazon's third-party marketplace abandoned the three classic retail tasks (location, assortment, pricing) — built **without** extensive AI.\n- **The concession:** AI is young (ChatGPT barely four years old); judgement may be premature.\n- **The pattern-recognition caveat:** classification works brilliantly (product ID, fraud) — useful, but it does not change supply chain design.\n- **Discussion:** agree 2–3 observable tests of transformation (new operating model, structural cost change, new customer promise) and apply them to every case.\n\n## 5. Why forecasting disappoints — two structural reasons ⭐⭐⭐\n**1. Forecasting touches only part of cost.** Inventory is a **saw-tooth**: cycle stock from batch quantity **Q** + safety stock **S** against uncertainty. **A perfect forecast eliminates only S.** Most inventory sits in Q, driven by physical constraints (pallets, trucks, MOQs). In delivery routing, the out-and-back line-haul distance dominates; better forecasts barely shorten it, and matter less as volume scales.\n**2. Diminishing returns to accuracy.** **Archery reward** (every gain pays) vs **rain-forecast reward** (80% vs 95% chance of rain → same umbrella decision). Supply chains are mostly rain-forecast settings: \"124 vs 159 units forecast — either way you ship one 240-unit pallet.\" First gains are real; last increments map to the **same decision**.\n- **Class Q 1.2 ⭐⭐⭐:** ₹8 cr cycle + ₹2 cr safety; vendor claims 30% cut → the single calculation is **size the safety-stock share of total inventory** (20% max). \"Follow the cost pool, not the technology.\"\n- **Class Q 1.3:** error ±40 → ±15 with full 240-unit pallets → **little to no business impact**. \"Precision pays only when a decision changes.\"\n\n## 6. The Campbell Soup lesson ⭐⭐⭐\nA classic study of Campbell Soup's continuous-replenishment programme: the industry assumed sharing retail sales data would improve forecasts and therefore inventory. **Finding: improved forecasts had LIMITED impact; the real gains came from using the same technology to cut order-processing lead times and to order more frequently — reducing the inbound batch quantity Q, the true driver of inventory.** General lesson: ask which lever a technology actually moves — the glamorous one in the pitch, or the structural one in the physics.\n*(NorthStar Foods, the opening case, poses exactly this choice: ML forecasting upgrade vs lead-time/order-frequency initiative; finance says order-size-driven stock costs ~4× the uncertainty buffer → Q is 4× S → fund the lead-time/frequency initiative first.)*\n\n## 7. Where the real leverage is — three transformative candidates 📘\n1. **Transportation economics** — everything that made transport radically cheaper restructured supply chains; autonomous vehicles would replace the driver's variable cost with engineering's fixed cost. 2016 MBA classes predicted majority-autonomous roads by 2026 — wrong; but Waymo runs commercially and freight pilots (Aurora, Kodiak, Gatik, Nuro) expand corridor by corridor. Manage with **options thinking and staged bets**.\n2. **Strategic forecasting & counterfactuals** — a Walmart-scale chain runs on hundreds of separate systems nobody fully understands; ML can estimate counterfactuals of your *own* organisation (\"what if we add a cross-dock layer?\") and evaluate hundreds of structural ideas a year. Tactical forecasting optimises within the system; strategic forecasting redesigns it.\n3. **Radical decentralisation — millions of virtual employees** — \"Because an LLM speaks both 'human' and 'computer', it can act as the translator.\" Replace a few over-guarded mega-systems with millions of narrow LLM agents (e.g. one per product family) instructed in plain language; many OK decisions taken quickly, each with bounded risk, can beat one slow best decision — **kaizen applied to information systems**. **Class Q 1.4:** why it fails *today* even if each agent works → **agents amplify each other's actions** (bullwhip); robustness comes from coordination design, not agent count.\n\n## 8. Named deployments 2024–26 ⭐⭐\n| Company | Application | Reported result | Type |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Amazon** | **DeepFleet** generative-AI coordination of 1M+ warehouse robots (Jun 2025) | **10% cut in robot fleet travel time** | Agentic |\n| **Walmart** | **Pactum** autonomous supplier negotiations (tail spend) | **~3% avg savings; 35-day payment-term extension; 64–68% close rate** | Agentic |\n| **Unilever** | AI across ~20 global control towers | stockouts −25%; retailer sales +15–35% | Predictive |\n| **P&G** | AI control tower across ~110 plants | deadhead trucking −15%; waste down up to 80% on key lines | Predictive |\n| **Lenovo** | Blue Yonder demand/supply/factory planning | +5% forecast accuracy; +4% on-time; +10% delivery accuracy | Predictive |\n| **Danone** | ML demand planning, multi-country | materially higher forecast accuracy | Predictive |\n| **Walmart** | Trend-to-Product GenAI design-to-shelf | lead times cut by up to 18 weeks | Generative |\n\"Treat vendor-reported numbers with care.\"\n\n### Amazon — one million robots and a foundation model (and the Kiva lineage) ⭐⭐⭐\n- In **June 2025** Amazon deployed its **one-millionth** warehouse robot and launched **DeepFleet**, a generative-AI foundation model that coordinates robot traffic across fulfilment centres, **trained on Amazon's own fleet-movement data using AWS SageMaker** 📘. \"An intelligent traffic-management system for a city filled with cars.\" **10%** less robot travel time; **75%** of Amazon orders touched by robotics.\n- **Kiva Systems (syllabus example) 📗:** Amazon bought Kiva in **2012 for ~$775M** and renamed it **Amazon Robotics**. Kiva drive units are squat mobile robots that slide under **inventory pods (shelving units)** and carry them to stationary human pickers — **\"goods-to-person\"** instead of pickers walking aisles. Each robot follows **fiducial (barcode) markers on the floor**; a central system assigns tasks and paths. Effect: pickers stay put, storage density rises, pick rates rise several-fold and travel time disappears.\n- **Why it matters (course framing):** Kiva is **physical automation** with central planning; DeepFleet is **fleet-level agentic coordination** — \"not one smart robot, but a learned policy that orchestrates a million of them\" 📘. It is Session 1's decentralisation logic **implemented in the physical layer first**, and why the suitability screen ranks **warehouse robot routing as the most agent-suitable** decision (high frequency, reversible, fast feedback, own premises).\n\n### Nike & i2 (2001) — the $400M forecasting lesson ⭐⭐\nNike deployed a state-of-the-art demand-planning system as the centrepiece of a **~$400M** programme; within months it ordered slow-moving shoes and under-ordered hot sellers (implementation shortcuts, mismatched data granularity, untested demand logic). Result: **~$100M** quarterly shortfall, **~20%** share-price fall, and the CEO's line \"*This is what you get for $400 million?*\" **Lesson:** forecasting systems **amplify** the data and process foundations under them; accuracy claims mean nothing until tested against decisions on your own history.\n\n### What \"transformative\" looks like 📘\n**Ocado** (online grocer → robotics-platform company; tech sold to Kroger, Aeon, Sobeys — the supply chain became the product) · **Cainiao** (asset-light orchestration via electronic waybills; coordination as the product) · **Asian Paints** (demand data drove direct-to-dealer distribution that **removed a distributor tier** — turns ~3–4× peers). All pass the transformation tests: new operating model, structural cost change, new customer promise.\n**Across industries** 📘: FANUC (predict robot failures → uptime), H&R Block with IBM Watson (AI surfaces deductions; the professional owns the call — augmentation), BlackRock Aladdin (25,000+ users; two decades of compounding foundations). AI pays off where it moves a **named decision or cost pool** and meets disciplined execution.\n\n## 9. Platforms named in the syllabus — Azure AI and AWS AI (preview of Ch 5)\n- **Microsoft Azure** 📘: Azure AI Foundry (OpenAI GPT models, agent service), Azure Machine Learning (MLOps), Microsoft Fabric (lakehouse), Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management with embedded agents (demand planning with external signals, inventory rebalancing, picking-route optimisation), Supplier Communications Agent, Copilot Studio, Power Automate, Purview, Entra for agent identity. **Best fit: Microsoft-centric estates.**\n- **Amazon Web Services** 📘: Amazon Bedrock (multi-model: Claude, Llama, Nova, OpenAI), SageMaker (build/train/deploy — DeepFleet was trained on it), Trainium/Inferentia chips, **Bedrock AgentCore** (model-agnostic agent runtime, MCP tool gateway, Guardrails), **AWS Supply Chain** (\"30 years of Amazon operational science\" productised). **Best fit: engineering-strong organisations wanting model choice.**\n\n## 10. Why AI investments disappoint — the numbers and the problem set ⭐⭐\n**Market context 2026 📘:** SCM software with agentic AI: **$2B → $53B (2025→2030)** (Gartner); **60%** of SCM-software enterprises expected to adopt agentic features by 2030 (from 5%); **55%** of 509 SC leaders expect agentic AI to cut entry-level hiring; **40% of agentic AI projects predicted cancelled by end-2027**. MIT 2025: **~95% of GenAI pilots show no measurable P&L impact**; Melbourne CBA: ~80% of analytics projects fail; BCG 2026: **44%** deploy AI in SC but only **~30%** of those advance beyond copilots (\"copilot-stuck\"); McKinsey's **\"gen AI paradox\"**.\n**The Session 1 problem set:** **misallocated spend** (glamorous informational levers funded, structural levers unfunded), **phantom accuracy value** (gains that never cross a decision threshold), **hype whiplash** (RFID/blockchain arc), **evidence laundering** (market-size stats and logos standing in for mechanism proof). Class Q 1.5: \"44% already deploy, market will hit $53B\" → **ask which adopters show measurable value** (\"adoption statistics measure purchasing, not value\"). Class Q 1.6: the soundest first test of \"learns your business and answers any question\" → **which decision it changes, and its benchmark**.\n**House style:** \"pragmatic optimism — open to possibilities, alert to limitations, allergic to hype.\"\n\n## 11. Principles older than AI 📘\nThe **cost-structure lens** (cost lives in batch sizes, lead times, network structure); **chain-fit strategy** (Fisher 1997; agility-adaptability-alignment 2004; \"digital super-agility\" 2026); the **feedback-system lens** (bullwhip governs how AI agents react to each other); the OM community's position (AI reshapes practice; matching supply and demand under uncertainty remains the core). \"Vendors sell tools; this course teaches principles. Tools expire quarterly.\"\n\n## 12. The tools you take with you\n- **Four-Question AI Claim Audit**: Mechanism? Cost pool? Decision changed? Evidence? → [Frameworks §1](course-frameworks.md).\n- **AI Adoption Roadmap**: Ideation → Defining → Data curation & governance → Prototyping → Production → Thriving with the lifecycle; \"fail quickly, fail regularly, fail tiny\".\n\n## Key takeaways (S1 recap 📘)\n1. AI's supply chain impact to date is real but modest — judge by mechanism, not momentum.\n2. Forecasting is structurally limited: it addresses only the safety-stock share, with diminishing returns.\n3. The Campbell lesson: the winning lever is often lead time and order frequency (Q), not forecast precision (S).\n4. Transformative candidates: autonomous transport, strategic counterfactuals, radical decentralisation via narrow agents.\n5. 2026 commits billions while 40% of projects are predicted to fail — the Four-Question Audit is your edge.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. A vendor says its AI \"will cut your inventory 30%\". Your stock is ₹12 cr cycle and ₹3 cr safety. What do you write?**\nAssumptions: forecasting changes S only; Q is set by trucks and cadence. Pool = 3 ÷ 15 = 20% maximum → 30% is impossible from forecasting alone; ask what lever touches Q (lead time, order frequency). Then run the other three audit questions.\n\n**Q2. Why can a big accuracy improvement have almost no business value?**\nBecause supply chain decisions are discrete (pallets, trucks, MOQs): once a forecast change no longer changes the order, extra precision is decoration — the rain-forecast reward function.\n\n**Q3. Classify: (a) a tool that drafts supplier emails for a planner to send; (b) a tool that emails a prioritised action list each morning; (c) a tool that negotiates and closes tail-spend deals inside a mandate.**\n(a) GenAI copilot, L1; (b) not an agent — recommends only (L2-type output, nothing executed); (c) agentic AI at L4 (bounded autonomy, Pactum-style).\n\n**Q4. What makes Amazon's DeepFleet \"agentic\" when Kiva robots were \"automation\"?**\nKiva robots execute centrally assigned moves along fixed markers; DeepFleet is a learned, continuously improving generative policy that coordinates a million robots' traffic toward a goal (less travel time) — goal-owning, adaptive orchestration within physical guardrails.\n\n**Q5. A board member says \"everyone is doing agentic AI — 44% of firms deploy it\". Reply in two lines.**\nAdoption measures purchasing, not value: only ~30% of those firms get beyond copilots and ~95% of GenAI pilots show no P&L impact. Show me which adopters in our situation moved a named cost pool, and by how much.\n"}, {"slug": "ch02-ai-readiness-data-technology", "label": "Readiness: Data & Tech", "tag": "02", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 2 — AI Readiness I: Data & Technology Foundations (Session 2)", "md": "# Ch 2 — AI Readiness I: Data & Technology Foundations (Session 2)\n**Session 2 📘:** \"Data quality, master data, systems integration and the technical stack that decides whether AI creates value or amplifies chaos.\" Opening case: **Meridian Apparel — Are We Ready for AI?** (the CEO wants a Zara-style trend-detection pilot now; problems: duplicate SKUs, 24-hour data latency, no GPU budget, unknown API inventory; the e-commerce division is clean and API-first).\n\n> **Exam use:** this is the chapter behind **Trishul A2 and A3** (duplicate vendor records, stale report, 26-hour latency, phished credentials) and the readiness profile **[2,2,3,3,4]** in the case stem. Whenever a case gives you a readiness score or a systems exhibit, read it with this chapter's scorecard and requirements-delta table.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The core thesis\n\"AI's effectiveness in the supply chain hinges on access to accurate, timely, and integrated data … Without high-quality, accessible data, AI cannot deliver reliable predictions or real-time insights.\" (Robinson, core reading 📘). Readiness is **the variable that flips project expected value**: ~95% of unready GenAI pilots fail, while ready deployments report **10–20% cost reduction and 10–15% service improvement** (McKinsey) 📘.\n\n## 2. Four categories of readiness barriers 📘\n| Category | Covered |\n|---|---|\n| **Data & technology infrastructure** (today) | data quality & accessibility; legacy systems & integration; security & privacy |\n| **Cost & sustainability** | high implementation cost; regulatory volatility (EU AI Act, China labelling, Canada AIDA); energy & water footprint |\n| **Talent & organisational capability** (S3) | skills gaps, citizen data scientists, resistance, complacency, de-skilling risk |\n| **Strategy, leadership & collaboration** (S3) | executive expectations, silver-bullet mentality, no AI strategy, functional silos |\n\n## 3. Six dimensions of data quality — SCM edition ⭐⭐⭐\n| Dimension | Definition | Supply chain failure example 📘 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Accuracy** | values reflect reality | system says 240 on hand; count finds 175 → phantom inventory → false replenishment |\n| **Completeness** | no critical gaps | lead time blank for 40% of SKUs → planning defaults to 14 days |\n| **Timeliness** | fresh enough for the decision | weekly POS for a daily demand-sensing model → \"the AI just smooths stale data\" |\n| **Consistency** | same fact, same value everywhere | 8801-A (ERP) / 8801A (WMS) / \"8801 A\" (portal) → three products |\n| **Uniqueness** | one record per entity | same vendor 6 times with different terms → spend analytics undercounts by 60% |\n| **Validity** | values conform to rules | negative lead times, 1900-01-01 dates → silently skew training |\n(Standard **DAMA** dimensions applied to SCM.) **Class Q 2.2:** a \"real-time\" demand-sensing pilot when 70% of sales arrive by overnight batch → **timeliness is binding — the data is too stale for the promise**. Quality dimensions are **per-use-case requirements**: 24-hour data is fine for weekly planning, fatal for real-time sensing. \"Diagnose the binding dimension before the model, not after the disappointment.\"\n*Trishul mapping:* uniqueness (2,890 vendor records for ~2,410 suppliers), consistency (11% SKU attribute conflicts ERP vs portal), timeliness (26-hour batch on 71% of revenue), and an **accuracy** problem with the *reports themselves* (a 14-month-old \"under 2%\" claim).\n\n## 4. Master data — \"where AI projects go to die\" ⭐⭐⭐\n- **What it is:** the slow-changing reference backbone — item, vendor, customer, location masters, BOMs, routings. Transactions inherit whatever the masters say.\n- **Why AI amplifies MDM debt:** a human planner recognises the duplicate vendor and mentally corrects; **a model — and worse, an autonomous agent — takes the data literally and acts on it at machine speed and scale**.\n- **Self-learning makes it worse:** \"existing process and data flaws are AMPLIFIED, not averaged away.\"\n- **The governance answer:** data ownership and stewardship roles, golden-record policies, dedup and validation pipelines, measured data-quality KPIs — \"boring, decisive, and a prerequisite to everything\".\n- **Field evidence:** SAP-ecosystem guides converge — clean, governed material and vendor masters unlock planning accuracy, visibility and reliable AI; enterprises now run formal \"data readiness assessments\".\n- **Class Q 2.1:** largest supplier shows 4% of spend but is duplicated across six records → analytics **measured record-keeping, not true concentration**. \"Data defects compound at machine speed.\"\n\n## 5. Data architecture generations ⭐\n**1 Data warehouse** (curated, batch; great for BI, too rigid for ML) → **2 Data lake** (cheap raw storage; often a \"data swamp\") → **3 Lakehouse + streaming** (lake economics, warehouse discipline; Kafka-style real-time pipelines) → **4 Semantic / knowledge layer** (ontologies and knowledge graphs define \"customer\", \"on-time\", \"lead time\" ONCE) → **5 Agent-ready data products** (governed, documented, API-accessible products agents can query autonomously, with permissions, lineage and audit trails). Firms that treat data pipelines, ontologies and stewardship as long-term investments — **on par with warehouses and ERP** — convert AI experiments into enterprise value.\n\n## 6. Outside-in data — external signals\nModern planning should **start from market signals** (channel demand, weather, geolocation, reviews, social trends), not internal order history. Zara (real-time trend detection), Danone (ML across countries and horizons). IoT/telemetry creates the perception layer for agents. **The catch:** every new source multiplies quality, integration and privacy questions — **external data is a force multiplier only on top of clean internal data.**\n\n## 7. Where training data comes from 📘\n**Own it, license it, or make it** (most systems use all three). Ground truth is the truth the model learns; keep a validation slice. **Balanced vs proportional** sampling: match class frequencies to reality except for rare events (fraud, defects) where you over-sample. Licensing levers: model-use vs display, derived vs raw, scale, recency. **The 60–80% rule:** successful deployments spend most effort finding, cleaning, labelling and consolidating data — not modelling. **Class Q 2.6:** identical demand-sensing model at two firms → the predictor of success is **Firm A's data governance and accessibility** (one owner per domain, nightly DQ reports). \"Foundations before intelligence; semantic before agentic.\"\n\n## 8. The real enterprise stack\nThe \"spaghetti reality\": a single ERP plus dozens of modules, best-of-breed planning/WMS/TMS, stitched by patchwork upgrades, mergers and tactical fixes — plus the **shadow layer** of Excel macros and Access databases running mission-critical processes; point-to-point interfaces, overnight batch jobs, undocumented dependencies. Lack of GPU access or modern architecture forces clunky side projects. **\"AI bolted onto a fractured stack inherits every fracture — at machine speed.\"** (Board sketch: one ERP box, ringed by best-of-breed tools, ringed by spreadsheets — where does planning really happen? Almost always the spreadsheet ring. Trishul: **61%** of planning decisions in spreadsheets.)\n\n## 9. Two honest modernisation strategies ⭐⭐\n| Replatform first (purist) | Wrap and modernise (pragmatic) |\n|---|---|\n| consolidate ERP instances, harmonise masters, build the lakehouse, then deploy AI | leave the core; expose it via APIs/middleware; build a governed data layer alongside; deploy narrow AI use cases that fund the journey |\n| **Pro:** AI inherits integrity; nothing built twice | **Pro:** value in quarters; learning compounds; sponsorship survives |\n| **Con:** multi-year, politically brutal, benefits late — sponsors may not survive | **Con:** risks permanent scaffolding — needs discipline to retire debt |\nCourse verdict: **wrap-and-modernise beats big-bang replatforming for most firms, if debt retirement stays disciplined.** (Trishul: the API-based e-commerce/modern-trade feeds — 29% of revenue — are the natural first domain for a predictive pilot, like Meridian's clean e-commerce division.)\n\n## 10. Cloud and scaling realities 📘\nAuto-scale has a lag (expect up to **~3 minutes** of inaccessibility while scaling — bites at month-end and promotions); **load-test ~20% above peak**; deployment ladder bare metal → VMs (IaaS) → containers (Docker/Kubernetes, infrastructure as code) → managed AI clouds (pay per execution); **chaos tools** kill random components to expose gaps; the **SLA is a worst-case promise**, not the average.\n\n## 11. Security and privacy — the expanding attack surface ⭐⭐⭐\n- Every model endpoint, API connection and pipeline is a new door; **NotPetya cost Maersk ~$300M in 2017** — one unpatched dependency.\n- **AI-specific threats:** data poisoning, **prompt injection** against agents, model theft, sensitive-data leakage through outputs.\n- **Regulatory volatility:** EU AI Act (risk-based), China's AI-content labelling, Canada's AIDA, **India's DPDP Act** (personal data). Compliance is a **design input**.\n- **Privacy-preserving techniques:** federated learning, differential privacy, structured licensing.\n- **Cross-tier tension:** traceability needs transparency; suppliers resist exposing quality and sourcing data.\n- **Class Q 2.3 ⭐⭐⭐:** an attacker phished a vendor-facing API credential last year; nobody has a complete inventory of which systems talk to which; the CIO proposes purchasing agents with **write access** to the ERP → **Block until API inventory and access controls exist.** Write-capable agents inherit and extend the attack surface; **prompt instructions are not controls**; identity, scopes and audit are preconditions. \"**Autonomy raises every bar.**\" *(Trishul: API inventory 14 months old; two vendor-facing credentials rotated after phishing → the same verdict for P2's write access.)*\n\n## 12. Predictive vs agentic — the readiness delta ⭐⭐⭐\n| Requirement | Predictive model | Autonomous agent |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data freshness | batch often fine | streaming / near-real-time |\n| Data access | read-only extracts | governed read **and write** APIs into systems of record |\n| Integration | one-way into dashboards | two-way: MCP-style tools, transaction posting |\n| Identity & permissions | data-science credentials | **per-agent identity, least privilege, spend limits** |\n| Observability | accuracy monitoring | action logs, decision traces, rollback, **kill switch** |\n| Failure mode | wrong number on a screen | **wrong purchase order in the ERP** |\n**This is your go/no-go table** for any \"agent\" proposal.\n\n## 13. Foundations in the field ⭐⭐\n**The cautionary canon:**\n| Case | What happened | Lesson |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Target Canada (2013–15)** | item master built under deadline pressure; post-mortems found record accuracy **near 30%** at launch (wrong dimensions, currencies, units); empty shelves beside overflowing DCs; exit cost **C$2B+**, 17,600 jobs | master data is the supply chain's DNA |\n| **Lidl + SAP (2011–18)** | seven years and **~€500M**, then cancelled; the new system was forced to mirror a legacy quirk (inventory valued at retail price) | don't lift-and-shift process onto technology |\n| **Hershey (1999)** | big-bang ERP + planning + CRM go-live weeks before Halloween; **~$100M** of orders missed; ~8% share fall in a day | the transformation dose exceeded survivable |\n**The positive canon:** **Danone** (years of master-data and pipeline work underneath ML), **Walmart Element** (internal model-agnostic ML platform, lock-in avoidance), **Unilever** (~20 control towers on harmonised data: stockouts −25%, retailer sales +15–35% — the semantic layer made real), **Microsoft's cloud supply chain** (production LLM decision support: planners ask what-ifs in natural language — \"what happens to deployment dates if the Dublin DC is delayed two weeks?\" — the LLM calls existing optimisation models and explains results; it worked because **data was already unified and the OR models already existed** — \"the LLM was the last mile\").\n**Class Q 2.4:** same LLM assistant succeeds on unified data and dies on fragmented spreadsheets → the difference is **prior foundations in place**.\n\n## 14. A practitioner \"Are you data ready?\" gate 📘\n**Collected and sufficient** (labelled history the use case needs) · **Accessible** (not trapped in silos) · **Governed with named ownership** (stewardship and data-scientist role before modelling) · **Feedback loops close** (corrections flow back — readiness is a loop, not a one-time audit).\n\n## 15. Beyond the textbook (2026) 📘\nPractitioners: **semantic layer first** — bolting agents onto legacy planning stacks is \"AI Stupid\". BCG: the ~70% stuck at copilots share fragmented data; the ~30% who advance invested in unified platforms and end-to-end workflow ownership. Academic **TOE/absorptive-capacity** research: technology is consistently the **easiest** dimension. **EU AI Act: readiness becomes law** — high-risk obligations enforce from **2 August 2026**: data-governance duties include **bias examination, provenance tracking and quality protocols**, propagating along supply chains like product compliance.\n\n## 16. Principles for upgrading the stack 📘\nPrioritise by strategic vision (not the vendor's demo) · demand transparency and explainability · design for integration and scalability · prefer public cloud where possible (capex → opex, GPUs on demand, hyperscaler security) · mind the energy and water footprint.\n\n## 17. The Data & Technology Readiness Scorecard ⭐⭐⭐\nScore each pillar **1–5 with evidence**: **1 Data quality** (six dimensions on critical entities; profiling stats, cycle-count accuracy) · **2 Master data & governance** (owner? golden records? dedup rate? DQ KPIs watched?) · **3 Architecture & integration** (batch vs streaming; API coverage; % planning in spreadsheets; lineage) · **4 Security & compliance** (attack surface, access controls, EU AI Act/DPDP readiness) · **5 Compute & tooling** (cloud/GPU in the workflow; MLOps; production without heroics).\n**Read the profile as a roadmap.** **Class Q 2.5:** [Data 2, Masters 1, Architecture 3, Security 2, Compute 5] → **predictive pilot on the cleanest domain first** (masters at 1 poisons everything; security at 2 forbids write access; compute 5 is a stranded asset). \"The scorecard sets the ceiling; money cannot shortcut discipline.\"\n\n### Worked reading — Trishul [Data 2, Masters 2, Architecture 3, Security 3, Compute 4]\n| Pillar | Evidence in the exhibits | Implication |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data quality 2 | 26-hour batch on 71% of revenue; 11% SKU conflicts | P1 demand sensing not feasible on most revenue; pilot on the API-fed 29% |\n| Masters 2 | 18% duplicate vendors, no owner; stale \"<2%\" report | Dedup + named owner before any procurement agent |\n| Architecture 3 | single 11-year ERP + 61% spreadsheet planning; modern cloud tenant | wrap-and-modernise; APIs exist for e-com/MT |\n| Security 3 | API inventory 14 months old; phishing → credentials rotated | refresh API inventory, per-agent identity before write access |\n| Compute 4 | GPU credits unused, DS team of 6 | stranded asset until 1–2 catch up |\n**Ceiling:** predictive and bounded-transaction pilots; no high-autonomy write agents until masters and security reach ≥3–4.\n\n## Key takeaways (S2 recap 📘)\n1. Readiness flips project expected value: 95% of unready pilots fail; ready ones report 10–20% cost gains.\n2. Data quality has six diagnosable dimensions — master data is the highest-leverage, least glamorous fix.\n3. Self-learning systems amplify data and process flaws; agents act on them at machine speed. Clean first, automate second.\n4. Legacy integration is the structural barrier: wrap-and-modernise beats big-bang for most firms.\n5. Agentic AI raises every bar — streaming data, write APIs, per-agent identity, audit trails; the requirements delta is your go/no-go tool.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. A firm wants a real-time demand-sensing agent; 70% of its sales data arrives by overnight batch. Which data-quality dimension binds, and what do you recommend?**\nTimeliness. The promise (\"real-time\") is impossible on stale data; either fix the feed (API/streaming from the legacy POS) or scope the pilot to the channels that are already near-real-time.\n\n**Q2. Why is a 14-month-old IT report saying \"duplicates under 2%\" not evidence?**\nIt is stale, no one owns the vendor master to keep it true, and live counts contradict it (2,890 records for 2,410 suppliers ≈ 17–20% duplicates). Prefer current, measured evidence over historical assertions.\n\n**Q3. Give three reasons a write-access purchasing agent must wait at a firm that was just phished.**\nNo complete API inventory (unknown attack surface); credentials already compromised once (the agent becomes \"an attack vector with a budget\"); prompt instructions are not controls — you need per-agent identity, least-privilege scopes, spend limits and audit logs first.\n\n**Q4. Compute scores 5, masters score 1. What is the first move?**\nA predictive pilot on the cleanest domain, while fixing masters (owner, golden records, dedup). Compute is a stranded asset until foundations catch up; no agents with write access.\n\n**Q5. What did Microsoft's cloud supply chain have that a spreadsheet-based firm did not, when it deployed an LLM planning assistant?**\nUnified data and existing optimisation models — the LLM only added a conversational translation layer on top of ready foundations.\n"}, {"slug": "ch03-ai-readiness-process-organisation", "label": "Readiness: Process & Org", "tag": "03", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 3 — AI Readiness II: Process & Organisational Foundations (Session 3)", "md": "# Ch 3 — AI Readiness II: Process & Organisational Foundations (Session 3)\n**Session 3 📘:** \"Process standardisation, incentives, politics, people — and a phased roadmap that survives contact with a real organisation.\" Opening case: **Continental Parts — the Roadmap Rebellion** (Plants 1–2 ignored the forecasting tool and avoided stockouts; Plants 3–4 obeyed, cut inventory and stocked out; the model missed 15% of demand that arrives by phone; a 25%-in-six-months target was set for a board deck).\n\n> **Exam use:** the **pilot-selection matrix**, **phased roadmap with gates**, **frozen baselines**, **guarded metrics** and the **three decision levels** come from here — they are the skeleton of every Section B answer (Trishul B1 \"Phase 1 → Phase 2 gate\", Kaveri B1 \"60-day review\").\n\n---\n\n## 1. Organisations meeting AI — the theory\n\"Genuine transformation requires more than technology: it demands organisational will, cultural change, operational pragmatism, and cross-enterprise agreements — elements that technology alone cannot supply.\" (Tayur, core reading 📘)\n- **No single objective function.** Optimisation assumes one goal; real firms are \"a Byzantine maze\" of departments with competing incentives and limited visibility.\n- **Managers are political actors**, not autonomous agents; different buyer types judge AI on different risk-reward criteria.\n- **Capital allocation is political** — supply chain competes with marketing, R&D and finance; AI ranks low until a crisis.\n- **Sabotage without villains** — turf wars over data ownership, budget control, bureaucratic inertia.\n- **Heterogeneity warning** — manufacturers differ across industries *and* between business units (Caterpillar, Deere); distrust any one-size pitch.\n\n## 2. The agency problem — \"what is in it for me?\" ⭐⭐\nSupply **chains** span enterprises; the most valuable use cases need **other companies' data**. History: ignoring incentives doomed RFID and blockchain at scale (value to one party, costs to another; TradeLens shut down for lack of industry collaboration — S5). **The inverse bullwhip** (Altintas, Erhun & Tayur 2008): forecast improvements at the **customer** can rationally **increase** variability at the manufacturer under quantity discounts — \"one firm's AI win is another's planning nightmare\". **Design implication:** multi-enterprise AI needs **contract and incentive design** as much as data pipes — who pays, who gains, who carries the risk of acting on shared signals?\n\n## 3. Three levels of decisions — and AI fit ⭐⭐⭐\n| Level | Examples | Cadence & owner | Realistic AI role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Strategic** | network design; NPI; portfolio & pricing; MTS/MTO/ATO; postponement | quarterly–annual; executives | **auxiliary insight only** — futures unlike the past defeat models trained on history |\n| **Tactical** | S&OP/SIOP; seasonal capacity & logistics; production planning | weekly–monthly; planners | **useful assistant — augment** planners; plans crumble under disruption |\n| **Operational** | dynamic procurement; lead-time quotation; inventory setting; fulfilment & shipment | daily–hourly; front line + systems | **strongest fit — delegate**; localised successes (routing, picking) but noisy environments demand robustness |\n**Class Q 3.1:** (a) AI network redesign for the 5-year footprint → **strategic / input-only**; (b) AI-assisted S&OP scenarios → **tactical / augment**; (c) autonomous reorder of spares under ₹50,000 → **operational / delegate**. \"Place the use case before choosing the AI.\"\n\n## 4. The process-readiness agenda ⭐⭐\n1. **Map reality, not the SOP** — process mining on event logs exposes the workarounds, loops and heroics; \"you cannot hand an agent a process you cannot describe\".\n2. **Standardise where variation is waste** — harmonise O2C and P2P variants before automating; every surviving variant multiplies agent complexity.\n3. **Redesign, then automate** — no lift-and-shift; self-learning AI **amplifies** existing limitations.\n4. **Respect embedded expertise** — decades-old processes encode judgement; bulldozing tacit knowledge creates fragility — \"codify the WHY, not just the flow\".\n5. **Decide what agents inherit** — for each step: **automate** (rules), **augment** (AI recommends, human decides) or **delegate** (agent acts within an envelope); revisit as trust builds.\n**Map before you automate:** flowcharts and SOPs show where a decision actually happens; compare **before-AI and after-AI information flows** (which hand-offs the system removes, which it must preserve); run an **idea bank**; measure with a **balanced scorecard** (service, quality, learning — not only cost). **Class Q 3.6:** \"add AI to procurement\" → the first step is **map the process**. \"Automating a broken process only makes the mess run faster.\"\n\n## 5. Human–AI collaboration pitfalls (de Véricourt & Gurkan) 📘\n**Complementary strengths** (humans frame, machines predict) · **different mistakes** (AI errs in new ways; net quality depends on catching each other's errors) · **the trust dilemma** (between blind trust and algorithm aversion lies **calibrated trust**; \"how good is your machine? You may never know\" in non-stationary settings) · **speed is not throughput** (bottlenecks move to human verification and exceptions) · **delegation effects** (\"it's not me, it's the machine\" — reassign accountability explicitly).\n\n## 6. Algorithm aversion and its proven remedy ⭐⭐⭐\n**Landmark finding (Dietvorst, Simmons & Massey 2015):** after seeing an algorithm and a human make the **same** errors, people lose confidence in the algorithm far faster and choose inferior human forecasts even when the algorithm outperforms. Error tolerance is **asymmetric** — machines are not forgiven; one visible failure can destroy adoption (Continental Parts).\n**Proven remedy (2018):** give forecasters even **slight ability to modify** the output (e.g. ±10%) → dramatically higher use, satisfaction and belief in the algorithm, at trivial accuracy cost. **\"Bounded override is not a concession; it is the adoption mechanism\"** — what L2–L3 should feel like.\n**Class Q 3.3:** Design A (show back-tested record, allow ±15% adjustment with reasons captured) vs Design B (mandate unmodified use) → **A wins — bounded control keeps trust**; B breeds shadow spreadsheets. \"Trust is designed, not demanded.\"\n\n## 7. The oversight paradox — \"ironies of automation\" ⭐⭐\n**Bainbridge (1983):** the better the automation, the less practice humans get — yet the more critical their intervention when it fails. **2026 confirmation:** ~1,500 workers — intensive AI oversight is the most taxing work: **+14% mental effort, +12% fatigue, +19% information overload**. Triple failure mode: **automation bias**, **complacency** (vigilance decay), **skill atrophy**. Design consequence: \"a human will check it\" is a **constraint to engineer around**, not a safety net — budget skill-maintenance rotations, simulation drills, realistic exception rates; and **hard guardrails outside the model** matter more. **Class Q 3.4:** each planner supervising 12 agent workstreams \"checking anything odd\" → **fails — vigilance and skills decay**.\n\n## 8. Organisations in the field 📘\n**Klarna (2024–25) — fire, then rehire ⭐⭐:** Feb 2024, its AI assistant did the work of **~700** agents (2.3M conversations in month one; resolution 11 → under 2 minutes). Within ~18 months the CEO reversed: \"We focused too much on efficiency and cost. The result was lower quality.\" Rehired into a **hybrid** model (AI for routine volume; guaranteed human access). Theory predicted it: service quality lives at the judgement edges; efficiency metrics invited the outcome they measured; full replacement removed the human capacity needed for exceptions.\n**Four retreats:** **Walmart shelf-scanning robots (ended 2020)** — a simpler, cheaper lever (pickers capture the data as a by-product) won · **Adidas Speedfactory (2016–20)** — showcased a trend, not the chain's binding constraint; upgraded Asian suppliers won · **GE Predix (2015–18)** — top-down \"platform for everything industrial\"; heterogeneity and overreach; dose not survivable · **IBM Watson Health (sold 2022)** — moonshot claims met data and process reality.\n\n## 9. Incentives — the metric misalignment nobody fixes ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Front line** judged on OTD, cost, turns — not AI adoption → rationally ignores a tool with a learning curve.\n- **Middle management** owns the system AI might replace; champions have \"currency in being the smartest person in the room\".\n- **Executives** expect silver bullets; disappointment triggers abandonment just before the learning curve pays.\n- **The fix:** change what success MEANS — adoption and data-quality KPIs in scorecards, recognition for exception-handling skill, protected retraining time.\n- **Class Q 3.2:** planners paid on service and turns, now also measured on \"recommendation adherence\" → **they make adherence fit their metrics** (game it). \"When a measure becomes a target, it stops measuring\" (Goodhart). Fix: align metrics **before** the tool — exception-handling quality and **forecast value added** in the scorecard.\n- **Gartner 2026:** 55% of SC leaders expect agentic AI to reduce entry-level hiring; **86%** say adoption requires new talent-pipeline processes — \"if juniors no longer learn by doing routine work, where do future experts come from?\"\n\n## 10. Workforce transformation 📘\nRole shift from swivel-chair data entry → **automation, exception handling, escalation and improvement**. New skill stack: SCM discipline + financial acumen + communication + citizen-data-scientist literacy (understand models, interpret outputs, **spot hallucinations**). Traits: **adaptability and curiosity**. New roles: **AI steward**, agent supervisors, data-product owners, AI governance leads. **Complacency risk:** letting AI \"firefight faster than any human\" erodes critical thinking.\n\n## 11. Three non-negotiables ⭐⭐\n1. **Senior executive sponsorship + multi-functional resources** (named executives with skin in the game; resources from every affected function — Caterpillar, Deere, processed-food makers).\n2. **Enforced data management and process adherence** — including data management *of* the data management.\n3. **Phased rollout with active stakeholder participation** inside and outside the enterprise.\nWhy phased? MIT's successful 5% **embrace friction** as the crucible of adaptation; phasing meets friction in survivable doses.\n\n## 12. Phased AI adoption: Crawl, Walk, Run — with gates ⭐⭐⭐\n| Phase | Time | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **0 Foundation** | 0–6 mo | data & process fixes on ONE value stream; governance stood up; **baseline metrics frozen**; talent assessment; quick BI wins |\n| **1 Pilot** | 3–9 mo | 1–2 use cases: **HIGH feasibility × HIGH value × LOW blast radius**; predictive/augmenting first; human-in-the-loop; success gate vs **frozen baseline** |\n| **2 Scale** | 9–24 mo | winning pilots → adjacent categories, sites, regions; industrialise (MLOps, retraining, adoption KPIs, reskilling) |\n| **3 Transform** | 18 mo+ | redesign the operating model; autonomy envelopes widen; org restructures around exceptions |\n**Each gate:** value evidence + readiness re-score + **explicit autonomy decision**. \"Skipping gates is how you join the predicted 40% of cancelled agentic projects.\" **Class Q 3.5:** \"25% inventory reduction in 6 months, company-wide\", set before any diagnostic → rewrite as **scoped, baselined and evidence-gated** — a board-theatre target cannot govern (moving baseline; single unguarded metric invites gaming — cut inventory, buy stockouts). \"Transform in survivable doses.\"\n\n### How to write a gate (exam template)\n| Gate element | What to write |\n|---|---|\n| Scope | which category/site/lane, how long (long enough for the statistics) |\n| **Frozen baseline** | the KPIs at a fixed date before go-live — and ideally a **control group** |\n| Success metric(s) + threshold | e.g. savings ≥ 2.5% (above 1.9% break-even); fill ≥ 75% |\n| **Guarded counter-metric** | the metric that must not get worse (OTIF, stockouts, cost per unit) |\n| Autonomy decision | stay / climb / step down a rung on the ladder |\n| Who decides | named business owner (+ finance certifies value) |\n| Demote / scrap evidence | the result that changes your mind |\n\n## 13. Pilot selection — scoring candidate use cases ⭐⭐⭐\n| Criterion | Ask | Green flag | Red flag |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Value at stake** | which cost/revenue pool, how big? | material pool, measurable baseline | vanity metric, no baseline |\n| **Data feasibility** | S2 scorecard on THIS use case | clean, owned, streaming where needed | masters dirty, data political |\n| **Decision impact** | does output change real decisions? | clear action mapping | rain-forecast redundancy |\n| **Blast radius** | worst case if wrong? | reversible, bounded, low-value txns | irreversible, customer-facing |\n| **Sponsor & adoption** | who owns it, who must use it? | named sponsor, users co-design | orphaned, imposed |\n| **Learning value** | what capability does it build? | reusable data products, skills | dead-end point solution |\n*Worked:* Trishul ranks P2 (₹58 cr pool, ₹1.9 L median deals, mandate-bounded) above P3 (high blast radius, regulated) above P1 (fails the pool test, 26-hour data). See [Trishul B1](pyq-trishul.md).\n\n## 14. Scoping and finding the work to automate 📘\n**Design thinking** (persona; user stories \"As a <role> I want <feature> so that <benefit>\") · **Systems thinking** (boundaries, subsystems aligned to the whole) · **Scenario planning** (STEEP drivers → ~7 scenarios → four in a 2×2) · **Delphi** (anonymous multi-round expert convergence) · **Balanced Scorecard** (Financial, Customer, Internal Process, Learning & Growth). **Idea bank** (cross-department capture and ranking; 20%-time produced Gmail and Maps); **Who/What/How/Why** process interviews, timed to expose bottlenecks; **before/after information-flow** diagrams; **five-stakeholder scoring** (customers, vendors, employees, investors, governments) — low customer-value, high-effort steps are targets; rank by **impact × effort** (time, capital — > 20% of profit = large — risk, how many asked). Never delete a low-priority idea.\n\n## Key takeaways (S3 recap 📘)\n1. AI value is gated by organisation, not algorithms — silos, politics and incentives defeat perfect models.\n2. Three-level framework: strategic = judgement with AI auxiliary; tactical = augmented planners; operational = automation frontier.\n3. Standardise processes before automating — but codify tacit expertise; self-learning AI amplifies what it inherits.\n4. Calibrate trust; design confidence displays, overrides and accountability explicitly.\n5. Roadmaps are risk instruments: gates with frozen baselines, pilot selection by feasibility × value × blast radius, and the three non-negotiables.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. Continental Parts: Plants 1–2 ignored the tool and avoided stockouts; Plants 3–4 obeyed, cut inventory and stocked out. What do you conclude?**\nThe planners held information the model lacked (15% of demand arrives by phone — a completeness gap) and a single unguarded metric (inventory) was optimised at service's expense. Don't mandate compliance (algorithm aversion → shadow spreadsheets); fix the data gap, add bounded override with reason capture, and guard inventory targets with a service counter-metric.\n\n**Q2. Rewrite \"25% inventory reduction in 6 months, company-wide\" as a gate-compatible target.**\nScope: one category at two plants; frozen baseline: last 6 months' inventory and fill by SKU; success: inventory −10% at fill ≥ baseline; counter-metric: stockout days not up; review at month 6 by the COO with finance; widen only on evidence.\n\n**Q3. Why does \"each planner will supervise 12 agent workstreams\" fail?**\nThe ironies of automation: oversight is the most taxing work, vigilance decays, skills atrophy; humans become poor monitors. Bound spans, rotate, drill, and put hard limits outside the model.\n\n**Q4. Where does each decision sit, and what AI posture: (a) choose a new RDC location; (b) monthly S&OP capacity plan; (c) daily reorder of packaging under ₹50,000?**\n(a) strategic — AI as input only; (b) tactical — augment planners; (c) operational — delegate inside an envelope.\n\n**Q5. Klarna replaced 700 agents with AI and then rehired humans. Which course ideas explain it?**\nEfficiency metrics invited the outcome they measured; quality lives at the judgement edges (tacit layer); removing humans removed the capacity for exceptions — the oversight paradox. The fix is a hybrid with guaranteed human access.\n"}, {"slug": "ch04-agentic-ai-systems", "label": "Agentic AI Systems", "tag": "04", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 4 — Agentic AI Systems & Smart Agents (Session 4)", "md": "# Ch 4 — Agentic AI Systems & Smart Agents (Session 4)\n**Session 4 📘:** \"Inside the agent: architectures, autonomy levels, multi-agent coordination — and the deployments already negotiating, routing and replenishing.\" Opening case: **Vantage Logistics — Which Pilot Do You Scale?** (routing agent 14% gain with driver-safety concerns; picking robots 18% gain with a hardware backlog; delay rebooking 6% gain with the highest satisfaction).\n\n> **Exam use:** the **autonomy ladder L1–L5**, the **agentic suitability screen**, the **governance stack** (\"guardrails live outside the model\") and the **mandate/envelope** idea are demanded in every Section B (\"starting autonomy level, justified\", \"escalation trigger\", \"named owner\"). This is the most-quoted chapter in both model answers.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Five generations of \"automation\" ⭐⭐\n| # | Generation | What it does | Human role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Scripts & macros** | fixed keystroke replay; breaks when anything changes | builds and fixes |\n| 2 | **RPA** | rule-based bots on structured digital tasks — PO creation, invoice matching, compliance checks | designs rules |\n| 3 | **ML-assisted** | predictions embedded in workflows — risk scores, forecasts, anomaly flags | **decides** |\n| 4 | **GenAI copilots** | conversational assistance, drafting, summarising, what-if querying | **initiates every action** |\n| 5 | **Agentic AI** | goal-directed systems that **perceive, plan, act through tools, and learn** with minimal intervention, inside guardrails | governs the envelope |\n**The step-change at stage 5: the system OWNS a goal and a decision loop, not just a task** — which is why it demands the readiness of Sessions 2–3.\n\n**Agent-washing detection ⭐⭐⭐ (Class Q 4.1):** \"Our AI agent reviews your inventory dashboard every morning and emails your planner a prioritised action list\" → **not an agent — it only recommends; nothing is executed.** Test: **goal ownership + tool-mediated action within an envelope.** Naming discipline matters commercially: agent-grade governance costs money — don't pay copilot problems with agent budgets (or vice versa).\n\n## 2. Inside an agent — six building blocks ⭐⭐\n| Block | Function | Enterprise reality |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SENSE · Perception** | interfaces to the world | ERP/WMS/TMS records, IoT telemetry, EDI, email, market feeds — \"sensing is mostly reading systems of record\" |\n| **THINK · Reasoning engine** | decides what to do next | typically an LLM with **ReAct** (reason + act) and chain-of-thought |\n| **THINK · Planning** | decomposes goals into steps | sequences tool calls; replans when observations contradict expectations |\n| **REMEMBER · Memory** | short- and long-term | context window + vector DBs, **RAG** over policies and history |\n| **ACT · Tool use** | the agent's hands | APIs, databases, **optimisation solvers**, web search, code — standardised via **MCP** |\n| **GOVERN · Guardrails & orchestration** | bounds and coordination | permissions, spend limits, policy checks, escalation, audit logs; orchestration across agents and humans |\n\n## 3. Design patterns 📘\n- **Reflection** — the agent critiques and revises its output before acting (cheap accuracy, slower cycles).\n- **Tool use** — math to solvers, facts to databases, execution to APIs; \"the LLM routes and interprets rather than computes\".\n- **Planning / orchestrator–worker** — a planner decomposes the goal and dispatches specialists (data-pull, forecast, negotiation agents).\n- **Multi-agent collaboration** — peer agents with distinct roles negotiate or vote (mirrors S&OP teams).\n- **Human-in-the-loop vs on-the-loop** — IN: approves each consequential action; ON: monitors exceptions while the agent executes. Chosen **per decision class** by the autonomy ladder.\n**Lineage:** multi-agent supply chains were proposed in **1998** (Swaminathan, Smith & Sadeh) — \"a sci-fi world at that time; the future is here\". LLMs supplied cheap, linguistically competent agents that read contracts and talk to planners.\n\n## 4. Agents in the field ⭐⭐⭐\n### Physical — warehouse autonomy (Amazon, JD.com, BMW)\nAmazon's newest FCs (MQY1, SHV1) run AI-coordinated robots moving inventory pods and optimising human–robot collaboration; **DeepFleet (2025)** — a generative foundation model that plans, learns and orchestrates **1M+ robots**, \"an intelligent traffic-management system\" (10% travel-time gain). **JD.com** runs some of the world's most automated FCs (sort, pack, move with minimal humans). **BMW × Figure AI** humanoids for materials retrieval and machine tending. Researchers call this \"**smart execution of work**\".\n\n### Transactional — Walmart × Pactum autonomous negotiation ⭐⭐⭐\nWalmart hands **tail-spend** negotiations to Pactum's AI: the agent emails/chats with suppliers, negotiates **payment terms, discounts and clauses** against a **mandate set by category managers**, and closes agreements without human involvement.\n- **Why tail spend?** Thousands of suppliers Walmart could never negotiate with individually — the agent negotiates with **all** of them in parallel.\n- **The mandate (walk-away points, tradable levers) is the guardrail**; the agent optimises within it.\n- **Results:** **~3% average savings**; **64–68% close rate** (vs a 20% pilot target); **~11-day turnaround**; **35-day average payment-term extension**; **83%** of suppliers found it easy, ~¾ preferred negotiating with the bot. Maersk runs similar deployments.\n- **Class Q 4.2:** the agent closes deals with 2,400 tail suppliers inside mandates → the manager's job is now **designing the envelope and auditing outcomes** — not reviewing every deal (that would delete the economics). \"**The mandate IS the management.**\"\n\n### The wider agentic portfolio 📘\nWalmart **Trend-to-Product** (lead times −18 weeks) · **ThredUp** recommerce classification and matching · **IBM Food Trust** — freshness indices on blockchain trigger automated payments (\"blockchain newsvendor\" — automating contract **enforcement**) · **predictive 3D printing** of spares triggered by IoT condition · **adaptive pricing & ordering** (DDPO) for perishables under non-stationarity.\n**Market:** agentic AI in SC & logistics **$8.7B (2025) → ~$16.8B (2030)**; **40%** of enterprise apps to embed task-specific agents by end-2026 (from <5%); agentic and physical AI top Gartner's 2026 trends; McKinsey: **$2.6–4.4T** annual value potential.\n\n### Plumbing — protocols and platforms 📘\n**Model Context Protocol (MCP)** — open standard connecting AI to tools/data (\"the USB port of agent tool use\"); **agent-to-agent (A2A)** standards for cross-vendor negotiation; enterprise agent platforms (Microsoft Dynamics 365 agents & Copilot Studio, AWS Bedrock AgentCore, SAP Joule agents across Ariba/Fieldglass, GA June 2026). **Microsoft's Supplier Communications Agent** handles order confirmations, schedule changes and document requests — an **L3–L4 agent shipped as product**. \"Protocol choices are supply chain design choices now.\"\n\n### Radical decentralisation, revisited\nThe pieces exist (LLM reasoning, MCP, per-agent identity, orchestration — Pactum and DeepFleet are L4 fragments). **Still missing:** cross-enterprise incentive agreements, system-level stability guarantees, accountability law, organisations restructured around exceptions. \"The gradient from L1 to L5 is where strategy lives for the next decade.\"\n\n## 5. The problems agents create ⭐⭐\n- **Agent-washing** — copilots and RPA sold as agents (a leading driver of the predicted 40% cancellations).\n- **Unearned autonomy** — L4 granted on demo evidence; the overridden cases where humans were quietly catching expensive errors are discovered only after the humans are gone.\n- **Interaction instability** — excellent agents amplifying each other into machine-speed oscillation (bullwhip at silicon speed).\n- **Unbounded commitments** — agents whose words legally bind the firm, governed only by prompt-level politeness.\n\n### The agentic cautionary file (2023–25) ⭐⭐⭐\n| Case | What happened | Lesson |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Air Canada chatbot (2024)** — LIABILITY | a tribunal held the airline liable after its chatbot **invented a bereavement-fare policy**, rejecting the argument that the bot was \"a separate legal entity\" | the agent's word binds the firm — **grounding and envelopes are legal controls** |\n| **The $1 Chevy Tahoe (2023)** — INJECTION | a dealership chatbot was **prompt-injected** into \"agreeing\" to sell a ~$76,000 SUV for $1 (\"no takesies backsies\") | instructions in prompts are suggestions — **hard limits must live outside the model** |\n| **McDonald's AI drive-thru (2021–24)** — EXIT GATE | after a 100+ restaurant pilot, ended following viral order errors (bacon on ice cream; 260 nuggets) | the positive lesson: **bounded rollout, honest feedback and a working exit gate** |\n\n## 6. The research frontier — agent behaviour ⭐⭐⭐\n- **LLM agents can AMPLIFY bullwhip** — beer-game experiments (2024–26): in decentralised settings most LLM agents create **more** order instability than simple baseline policies.\n- **Structure stabilises, intelligence does not** — a centralised orchestrator or communication constraints restore stability; **multi-agent learning with trained coordination** beats single agents on cost AND produces less bullwhip.\n- **Consensus-seeking agents** (2025) automate routine inter-company coordination and lower SME entry barriers.\n- **Institutional governance** — WEF \"AI Agents in Action\" (2026) introduces **ACAP**, a per-agent capability & authorisation profile.\n**The design law: stability is a property of MECHANISM DESIGN — shared signals, constraints, orchestration — not of agent intelligence. \"Smarter agents without structure fail faster.\"** **Class Q 4.4:** your replenishment agent and your distributor's ordering agent react to each other → **order oscillation, unless signals are shared**.\n\n## 7. The Five-Level Autonomy Ladder ⭐⭐⭐\n| Level | Agent does | Human does | SCM example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **L1 · Assist** | retrieves, summarises, drafts | decides and acts | copilot drafts a supplier email; planner edits and sends |\n| **L2 · Recommend** | analyses, proposes ranked actions | approves each action | reorder proposals with reasons; planner clicks approve |\n| **L3 · Act with approval gates** | executes routine cases end-to-end | approves exceptions & thresholds | **auto-PO under ₹5 L within contract; above → human** |\n| **L4 · Bounded autonomy** | owns the decision inside an envelope | monitors, audits, tunes the envelope | **Pactum negotiations inside mandate; DeepFleet routing** |\n| **L5 · Self-improving** | learns, adapts policy, coordinates peers | governs goals, ethics, kill switch | autonomous planning networks (**mostly aspirational, 2026**) |\n\"Autonomy is a managerial choice **per decision class**: climb the ladder **on evidence**, capped by **readiness and blast radius**.\"\n*Chain Reaction rule:* \"**L2 specialist first, L3 only as an upgrade on top of it**\" (S13 round brief 📘). *Kaveri trap:* an **L5 closed-loop repricing agent commissioned below gate** on a board that could not place its stock.\n\n## 8. The governance stack ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Hard constraints** — spend limits, approved-supplier lists, contract envelopes, rate limits, forbidden actions — **enforced OUTSIDE the model, in code and permissions, not in the prompt**.\n2. **Identity & least privilege** — each agent its own credentials scoped to the minimum (\"an agent with tool access is an attack vector with a budget\" — prompt-injection defence).\n3. **Observability** — decision traces, action logs, explanation on demand, override mechanisms — \"AI should enhance, not obscure, accountability\".\n4. **Escalation & kill switch** — confidence and anomaly thresholds route to humans; every autonomous loop has a documented stop.\n5. **Accountability assignment** — **name the human owner** of every agent's outcomes **before** go-live.\n**Risk registers:** agentic ecosystems risk loss of oversight, emergent systemic failures, accountability gaps; agents in fragile processes add unpredictability. \"**Governance is the product, not the paperwork.**\"\n**Class Q 4.3 ⭐⭐⭐:** a system prompt says \"Never exceed ₹5 lakh per order. Never order from unapproved suppliers.\" Sufficient? → **No — prompts alone are not enforceable controls.** \"The prompt shapes behaviour; the envelope bounds it.\" \"**Guardrails live outside the model.**\"\n\n## 9. The Agentic Suitability Screen ⭐⭐⭐\n| Factor | Ask |\n|---|---|\n| **Frequency & volume** | high-frequency, high-volume decisions amortise agent investment; rare ones do not |\n| **Reversibility** | can a wrong action be undone cheaply? low blast radius → more autonomy |\n| **Data & feedback** | rich state visibility and **fast** outcome feedback (agents learn only when the loop closes quickly) |\n| **Rule clarity** | can the mandate be specified (limits, levers, walk-aways)? \"If experts cannot state the envelope, the agent cannot inherit it\" |\n| **Stakes & politics** | strategic, irreversible, politically loaded decisions stay human |\n**Score 1–5 each: 20+ = strong candidate (start L3–L4); 12–19 = augment (L1–L2); < 12 = keep human, revisit later.**\n**Class Q 4.5:** (a) strategic supplier partnership negotiations, (b) tail-spend reorder of consumables, (c) dynamic robot routing in your own warehouse → **c > b > a** — reproducing the industry sequence: **physical autonomy first, transactional next, strategic never (so far)**.\n\n*Vantage Logistics resolved:* the **delay-rebooking pilot** (6% gain, highest satisfaction) is often the safest to scale — low blast radius, reversible, users like it — while the routing agent's driver-safety issue was **absent from its objective** (agents fail on what their objective omits) and the picking robots are constrained by hardware. Before promoting any pilot to autonomy, demand: the overridden cases, error costs in both directions, the omitted objectives, and a frozen baseline.\n\n## 10. Intelligence vs automation — which do you need? 📘\nAutomation repeats; intelligence learns (the 1939 exhibition robot blew up balloons on cue but could not decide when). Most \"AI\" is automation in disguise — RPA copies steps \"like a tape recorder\". **Automated vs intuitive:** automate where history is rich and rules stable; keep humans where data is thin (in the March-2020 shock data-starved AI faltered and human intuition took over). Ask first: does this task need intelligence, or will disciplined automation do?\n\n## 11. Human-in-the-loop by design ⭐⭐\n- **Confidence thresholds gate autonomy** — the agent acts alone above the line and hands off below it.\n- **Escalation is architecture** — a production assistant routes low-confidence cases to a person by design.\n- **Guide rails** for off-topic asks; route by **tone** as well as words; design for the **80% rule** (agent handles ~80%, humans fill the gaps; reliance on humans falls as it learns from logs).\n- \"AI = **augmented** intelligence.\"\n**Class Q 4.6:** an \"autonomous\" returns agent — keep a human **on low-confidence, high-impact cases only**.\n\n## Key takeaways (S4 recap 📘)\n1. Agentic AI = goal ownership + a perceive–reason–plan–act loop with tools and memory; name agent-washing.\n2. Architecture is composable: reflection, tool use, orchestrator–worker, multi-agent — with OR solvers as power tools, not replacements.\n3. Field evidence is sequenced: physical autonomy (DeepFleet, JD.com) → transactional (Pactum) → planning (mostly aspirational).\n4. Autonomy is a managerial choice per decision class — climb the ladder on evidence, capped by readiness and blast radius.\n5. Governance is engineering: hard constraints outside the model, per-agent identity, observability, kill switches, a named owner.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. Write the starting autonomy level for a tail-spend negotiation agent at a firm whose vendor master was just cleaned, and justify it in one line.**\nL3 — it closes routine deals under ₹5 L inside the category-manager mandate and escalates the rest; the decision is reversible and low-value, but there is no track record yet, so L4 must be earned with gate evidence.\n\n**Q2. Your agent's prompt says \"never order from unapproved suppliers\". The CISO is unhappy. Why, and what do you add?**\nPrompts are not enforceable (injection, model updates, edge cases). Add an approved-supplier allow-list and spend caps enforced in code, per-agent credentials with least privilege, full action logs and a kill switch.\n\n**Q3. Score robot routing in your own warehouse on the suitability screen.**\nFrequency 5, reversibility 4, data/feedback 5, rule clarity 4, stakes 5 (low) = 23 → strong candidate, start L3–L4 (DeepFleet operates at L4).\n\n**Q4. Two individually excellent agents — yours and your distributor's — start reacting to each other's orders. Predict and prevent.**\nOrder oscillation (machine-speed bullwhip). Prevent with a shared end-customer demand signal, order smoothing/rate limits, or an orchestrator — stability is mechanism design.\n\n**Q5. What did Air Canada, the $1 Tahoe and McDonald's each teach?**\nAir Canada: the bot's words bind the firm (ground it, bound it). Tahoe: prompt injection beats prompt instructions — hard limits outside the model. McDonald's: bounded pilots with an honest exit gate are a success, not a failure.\n"}, {"slug": "ch05-ai-platforms", "label": "AI Platforms", "tag": "05", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 5 — AI Platforms for SCM: Azure, AWS and Enterprise Ecosystems (Session 5)", "md": "# Ch 5 — AI Platforms for SCM: Azure, AWS and Enterprise Ecosystems (Session 5)\n**Syllabus 📘:** platforms such as **Azure AI, AWS AI**. **Session 5 📘:** \"The platform landscape from chips to copilots — and the invisible physical supply chain that brings AI itself to life.\" Opening case: **Solstice Industrials — Platform Choice Under Constraint** (Microsoft-centric estate vs AWS's faster pre-built pilot; a GPU shortage delays either choice by a quarter; the sustainability office asks about emissions).\n\n> **Exam use:** platform questions in a case usually test **data gravity, governance fit and the provider/deployer duty split** (Trishul A4 is exactly Class Q 5.5). Token-cost overruns test **FinOps, not platform switching** (Class Q 5.4).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The AI technology stack — where value and power sit ⭐⭐\n| Layer | Contents | Concentration / risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1 Silicon & hardware** | GPUs/accelerators (NVIDIA), fabs (TSMC), lithography (ASML), memory, networking | extreme concentration; geopolitical chokepoints |\n| **2 Infrastructure** | hyperscale data centres, power, cooling, connectivity; sovereign/edge | GW-scale power demand |\n| **3 Foundation models** | GPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini (Google), Llama (Meta), domain models | rented via API, rarely owned |\n| **4 Platform & orchestration** | Azure AI Foundry, AWS Bedrock/SageMaker, Vertex AI; agent runtimes, guardrails, MLOps | lock-in terms |\n| **5 Applications** | ERP/SCM suites (SAP, Oracle, Dynamics), planning specialists (Blue Yonder, Kinaxis, o9), vertical agents (Pactum, ToolsGroup) | point-solution sprawl |\n\"Most SCM organisations buy at layers 4–5 and should — but power, cost and risk cascade down from layers 1–2.\"\n\n### The AI hardware supply chain in five numbers 📘\n**>60%** of global chip fabrication controlled by **TSMC** (advanced AI nodes even more concentrated in Taiwan) · **1** supplier of EUV lithography machines (**ASML**, Netherlands) · **~70%** of rare-earth processing in **China** · **GW-scale** new data-centre power demand straining grids — energy is now a binding constraint on AI growth.\n**From wafer to warehouse:** accelerators + high-bandwidth memory + InfiniBand-class networking (own shortages and lead times); copper and optical dependencies; a **cooling crunch** (coolant distribution units on the critical path); power contracts (PPAs, even nuclear restarts) are now AI supply-chain decisions; lifecycle and **e-waste**. \"When you sign a platform deal, you inherit its upstream risks — evaluate them like any supplier.\"\n**Class Q 5.1:** your cloud region has a multi-month GPU waitlist; two vendors blame each other → **sequence around the shared constraint** (it binds every hyperscaler; contract design and phase sequencing manage it). \"**Every platform is a supply chain; audit it like a supplier.**\"\n\n## 2. Microsoft Azure — strongest enterprise-workflow integration ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Core AI services:** **Azure AI Foundry** (models incl. OpenAI GPT family, agent service), **Azure Machine Learning** (MLOps), **Microsoft Fabric** (data lakehouse), **Dataverse** (unified data layer).\n- **SCM-native applications:** **Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management** with embedded agents — demand planning with external signals, inventory rebalancing, picking-route optimisation.\n- **First-party SCM agents (2026):** **Supplier Communications Agent** (order confirmations, schedule changes, document chasing); Copilot monitors external risk (weather, supplier financial stress, geopolitics) and drafts mitigation actions — agents that \"reason, decide, and act within governed boundaries\".\n- **Orchestration & governance:** **Copilot Studio** (custom agents), **Power Automate** (process orchestration), **Purview** (data governance), **Entra** (agent identity).\n- **Best fit:** organisations already on Microsoft 365/Dynamics/Teams — agent surfaces live where planners already work.\n- **Production proof:** Microsoft's own cloud supply chain runs LLM decision support over unified data and existing optimisation models (S2).\n\n## 3. Amazon Web Services — deepest infrastructure + Amazon's own ops DNA ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Core AI services:** **Amazon Bedrock** (multi-model: Anthropic Claude, Meta Llama, Amazon Nova, now OpenAI models), **SageMaker** (build/train/deploy ML; **Canvas** no-code variant), custom silicon **Trainium/Inferentia**.\n- **Agent platform:** **Bedrock AgentCore** — model-agnostic agent runtime with an **MCP-based tool gateway**, managed web-search grounding, **Guardrails** screening for prompt injection and sensitive data, and trace-based agent-improvement loops.\n- **SCM application layer:** **AWS Supply Chain** — demand planning, inventory visibility and n-tier insights on a unified data lake (\"30 years of Amazon operational science\" productised); 25+ specialised SC tools in the Connect Decisions suite.\n- **Proof at home:** **DeepFleet** trained on SageMaker — Amazon runs its million-robot network on this stack.\n- **Best fit:** engineering-strong organisations wanting model choice, custom agents and infrastructure control; retailers wary of Amazon as a vendor may not (competitive tension).\n\n## 4. Google Cloud and the real question 📘\nGoogle Cloud: **Vertex AI** (Gemini, AutoML), **BigQuery**, strong data/ML heritage; supply chain via partners (o9, Blue Yonder on GCP) and **OR-Tools**. **Multi-cloud reality:** large enterprises average 2+ clouds; governance must span them. **The real questions:** where does your **data** already live (gravity)? which ERP/planning suite anchors you (SAP → Azure bias; retail → GCP/Azure; engineering → AWS)? what do your regulators require (sovereignty, EU AI Act)? **Cost discipline:** token/compute pricing is volatile and usage explodes silently — **FinOps for AI** is a 2026 discipline: committed-use discounts and **per-agent budgets** (guardrails again).\n\n## 5. SCM suites and specialists — the 2026 field ⭐⭐\n| Platform | Centre of gravity | AI/agentic capability (2026) | Watch for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **SAP (IBP, Ariba)** | ERP-anchored planning & procurement | **Joule agents** GA across Ariba Intake/Contracts and Fieldglass (Jun 2026); \"Autonomous Spend Management\"; agent runtime free through 2026 | depth vs lock-in; BTP rebuild maturity |\n| **Oracle** | ERP + SCM cloud | embedded AI across Fusion SCM; agents rolling out | anchor logic like SAP |\n| **Blue Yonder** | retail/mfg planning & fulfilment | cognitive planning, agentic roadmap; **Lenovo: +5% forecast accuracy, +4% OTD, +10% delivery accuracy** | Panasonic-owned; architecture transition |\n| **Kinaxis** | concurrent planning (RapidResponse/Maestro) | embedded explainable agentic capability; **Reckitt: schedulers shift from building schedules to analysing scenarios** | best-of-breed integration burden |\n| **o9 Solutions** | \"digital brain\" planning graph | knowledge graph + GenAI planning agents | implementation discipline decides value |\n| **Specialists** | narrow, deep agents | **Pactum** (negotiation), **ToolsGroup** (probabilistic planning), Coupa Navi, Zycus GenAI S2P | point-solution sprawl |\n\n## 6. What a platform actually assembles 📘\nLanguage splits three ways — **speech recognition**, **understanding**, **generation** — invest in what your use case leans on; **decision engines** that encode business logic and explain decisions are a distinct tier; frameworks under the services: **TensorFlow, Keras, PyTorch, scikit-learn**. A no-code virtual-agent builder, a managed-ML service and a custom framework build are three different SLAs, costs and skill demands — \"pick to fit, not to impress\". Retest any benchmark on your own data.\n\n## 7. When a firm builds the whole stack — JD.com's \"dual engine\" ⭐⭐\nJD.com — China's largest retailer by revenue — logistics reaching ~99% of the population, **~90% of orders delivered within 24 hours**, >1,000 million self-operated SKUs — built its own AI supply chain platform. Principle: the **\"dual engine\" — AI (forecasting, learning, perception) coupled with OR (optimisation, guarantees)** — neither alone. Why build? At JD's scale the supply chain **IS** the product (a new operating model). **Class Q 5.3:** an average CPG firm wants to build its own platform \"like the famous 24-hour retailer\" → build vs buy is decided by **real differentiation only**; \"copying the build without the differentiation copies the cost without the moat\". \"**Own the differentiating loop; rent the rest.**\"\n\n### Three platform fates 📘\n**TradeLens (2018–23)** — Maersk–IBM blockchain shipping platform shut down citing the missing \"level of industry collaboration\" (rivals would not join a competitor's platform — the agency problem) · **Walmart Element** — internal, model-agnostic ML platform built for control and lock-in avoidance · **Ocado Smart Platform** — built until licensing it (Kroger, Aeon, Sobeys) rivals the grocery business. \"Platform outcomes track **differentiation and adoption incentives**, not feature lists.\"\n\n## 8. Build, buy or blend ⭐⭐⭐\n| Buy (suite features & SaaS agents) | Build (custom agents on hyperscalers) |\n|---|---|\n| fastest time-to-value; vendor carries model risk, upgrades, much governance | exactly your process, guardrails, data; reusable data products compound |\n| best when the use case is **common** and differentiation **low** (demand planning, supplier comms) | best when the decision **is** your edge (DeepFleet, JD.com) and talent exists |\n| costs: subscription creep, roadmap dependency, shallow fit at the edges | costs: talent scarcity, MLOps burden, you own every failure |\n**Most firms should BLEND: buy the backbone, build the edge.** Ask first: **does a good service already exist?** (Class Q 5.6: before building a custom OCR/document-classification pipeline for invoices). Compose with APIs and microservices; **rent scarce talent for the build, own it for the loop**; select tools on a **capability × cost × integration × skills** matrix; keep a **model library**; **read the SLA like a supplier contract** (latency, uptime, exit terms are the real product).\n\n## 9. The 2026 adoption economics 📘\n**74%** of manufacturers plan agentic AI within two years — only **~1 in 5** has mature governance for autonomous agents (Deloitte) · **55%** of SC leaders raising tech investment; 60% of them budget >$1M, 19% >$10M (MHI + Deloitte) · **44% → 30%**: firms deploying AI in SCM vs those advancing beyond copilots (BCG) — \"the copilot trap in two numbers\" · **Time-series foundation models** (\"rent your forecasting\") remain promising but **not dominant** vs tuned models in operational benchmarks.\n**Class Q 5.4:** six months in, copilot token spend is **3.2× forecast**; was the platform wrong? → **No — fix usage discipline.** Token spend grows silently; that is FinOps territory (like freight overruns not invalidating a warehouse location). Revisit the platform only if **governed** consumption still breaks the case. \"TCO includes consumption governance, reviewed quarterly.\"\n\n## 10. Choosing a platform — six filters in order ⭐⭐⭐\n| # | Filter | Question |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Use-case fit** | start from the roadmap pilots (S3) — suite feature, custom agent, or both? |\n| 2 | **Data gravity** | moving data is cost, latency and risk — prefer compute near systems of record |\n| 3 | **Governance fit** | agent identity, guardrails, audit, regional compliance (EU AI Act, DPDP) — **can the platform enforce YOUR autonomy ladder?** |\n| 4 | **Ecosystem & skills** | what can your team (and hiring market) actually run — Microsoft shop vs AWS-native vs SAP country? |\n| 5 | **TCO & pricing risk** | licences + tokens + integration + change management; model price volatility; exit costs |\n| 6 | **Lock-in & optionality** | open standards (MCP), model-agnostic runtimes, data-egress terms — keep the ability to switch models mid-decade |\n**Class Q 5.2:** a cheaper, feature-rich platform cannot enforce per-agent spend limits or produce complete action logs, and your roadmap has an L4 procurement agent next year → **disqualified — no envelope enforcement**. \"Filters in order; governance before features.\"\n\n## 11. Duties that cannot be delegated — the EU AI Act provider/deployer split ⭐⭐⭐\n**Class Q 5.5:** your supplier-risk model's scores feed **contract renewals for EU-serving plants**; the vendor says compliance \"is handled by the platform\" → you must verify **the provider/deployer duty split, which is yours**. High-risk AI duties are allocated along the AI supply chain: the **vendor carries provider duties**; **oversight of your USE of the system is yours by law**. Write the **responsibility matrix into the contract** and **design the human-oversight workflow now**. \"Platform contracts must allocate regulatory duties explicitly.\" → This is **Trishul A4** almost word for word.\n\n### Solstice Industrials resolved (sketch)\nAssumptions: the estate is Microsoft-centric (data gravity, skills); the GPU shortage hits every hyperscaler. Decision: **filters in order** — use-case fit and data gravity favour Azure/Dynamics; governance fit must be proven by both vendors (per-agent identity, spend caps, logs); the GPU delay changes the **plan** (sequence predictive pilots that need less compute first), not the **decision**. Emissions: ask both vendors for region-level carbon intensity and PUE; choose regions and model tiers accordingly. Refuse to decide this week on feature demos; demand governance evidence, TCO including tokens, and exit terms.\n\n## Key takeaways (S5 recap 📘)\n1. The AI stack is a supply chain: silicon → infrastructure → models → platforms → applications; power concentrates upstream; risk flows into your contracts.\n2. Energy, cooling and hardware lifecycles are binding constraints that platform prices and quotas carry.\n3. Hyperscalers converge on capability; choose by data gravity, governance fit, skills and lock-in.\n4. The application layer is where SCM context lives — suites for the backbone, custom agents for edge cases, specialists for sharp edges.\n5. Module 1 arc: impact realism (S1) → data/tech readiness (S2) → process/org readiness (S3) → agentic machinery (S4) → platform economics (S5).\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. A vendor says its platform \"handles EU AI Act compliance\" for your supplier-risk scoring. Reply.**\nIt can carry provider duties, but as deployer we own human oversight of how scores are used; we will write a responsibility matrix into the contract and design a review-before-renewal workflow with logging.\n\n**Q2. Token spend is 3.2× forecast. Switch platforms?**\nNo. Fix consumption governance first: per-agent budgets in code, caching, shorter contexts, cheaper model tiers, committed-use discounts. Revisit the platform only if governed consumption still breaks the case.\n\n**Q3. Why might a cheaper, more feature-rich platform be disqualified?**\nIf it cannot enforce per-agent spend limits and complete action logs, it fails the governance filter, which comes before cost and features — at L4 that gap is unbounded financial exposure.\n\n**Q4. When is \"build\" right?**\nWhen the decision is your differentiation (the supply chain is the product, as at JD.com or Amazon), you have unified data and engineering depth, and the economics justify owning every failure. Otherwise buy the backbone and build only the differentiating loop.\n\n**Q5. Name the Azure and AWS components you would use for (a) custom agent identity and governance, (b) model training, (c) an agent runtime with prompt-injection screening.**\n(a) Azure: Entra (identity), Purview (data governance), Copilot Studio; (b) Azure Machine Learning / AWS SageMaker; (c) AWS Bedrock AgentCore with Guardrails (Azure equivalent: AI Foundry agent service).\n"}, {"slug": "ch06-machine-learning-for-operations", "label": "ML for Operations", "tag": "06", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 6 — Machine Learning for Operations (Sessions 6–7)", "md": "# Ch 6 — Machine Learning for Operations (Sessions 6–7)\n**Syllabus unit 2 📘:** *Machine Learning for Operations — algorithms such as **SVM, k-Means**; tools like **Python (scikit-learn), RapidMiner**.* **Session 6 📘:** \"Supervised, unsupervised and decision-focused learning — framed the way operators need them.\" Case: **SuryaMart — Three ML Pitches, One Budget** (A: forecasting for 40,000 SKUs; B: customer segmentation; C: supplier-delay early warning; \"92% accuracy in the PoC\"). **Session 7 📘 (lab):** \"From a notebook that impresses to a capability that survives.\" Case: **Meghna Foods — the 94% Notebook** (only the analyst can run it, from CSVs on her laptop).\n\n> **Exam use:** any accuracy number in a case (\"92%\", \"94%\", \"87%\") must be met with the **ML Framing Canvas**, a **naive benchmark**, the **leakage test**, and the question **\"what does an error cost, in each direction?\"**\n\n---\n\n## 1. The learning taxonomy — with operations uses ⭐⭐⭐\n| Paradigm | Needs | Answers | OSCM examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Supervised** | labelled history: inputs + known outcomes | \"given these features, predict this value/class\" | demand forecasting (regression); supplier default risk, delay prediction (classification — e.g. **SVM**, boosted trees) |\n| **Unsupervised** | unlabelled data | \"what structure hides in here?\" | SKU/customer segmentation (**k-means**), anomaly detection, store clustering for assortment |\n| **Semi-supervised** | a little labelled + lots of unlabelled | | when labels are expensive |\n| **Reinforcement** | an environment + a reward; learns by acting | \"what POLICY maximises long-run reward?\" | dynamic reordering, robot routing, dynamic pricing (S10) |\n\"At bottom, supervised ML is **labelling data at scale** — the scarce input is good labels, not algorithms.\" **Class Q 6.5:** three years of sales but no \"stockout-corrected true demand\" field → the core difficulty is that **the label you need doesn't exist yet** (sales ≠ demand when you stocked out). Frame the decision and construct the label before reaching for a model.\n**The AI family tree:** deep learning ⊂ machine learning ⊂ AI. ML = \"getting computers to do things they weren't specifically programmed to do\" (a 1959 checkers program beat its author). Six moving parts: learner, data, algorithm, **parameters** (dials the machine turns), **hyperparameters** (dials a human sets), and the resulting model.\n\n## 2. The algorithm zoo — and the two syllabus algorithms ⭐⭐\n| Algorithm | Idea | Ops use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Decision tree** | flow-chart of yes/no splits (prune useless branches) | late-delivery rules, credit holds |\n| **Naïve Bayes** | conditional probability, predictors treated as independent | text/spend classification |\n| **k-Nearest Neighbour** | classify by the majority of the k closest points | similar-SKU demand for new items |\n| **k-Means** | cluster into k groups around moving centroids | SKU/customer/supplier segmentation |\n| **Regression** | trend line through points → continuous value | **the forecasting workhorse** |\n| **SVM** 📗 | finds the boundary (hyperplane) with the **widest margin** between classes; kernels handle non-linear boundaries | supplier-delay / default classification, defect detection |\n| **Ensembles** | **bagging** averages many models (cuts variance; random forests); **boosting** reweights hard cases (gradient boosting — won the M5); **stacking** blends via a meta-learner (Netflix-Prize winners stacked 30+ models) | almost all winning forecasters |\n**Choose empirically:** decision → tree; classify or cluster → kNN, k-means, naïve Bayes; predict a number → regression — **train two and keep the more accurate**.\n\n### SVM in operations language 📗\nA supplier-delay classifier with features (supplier's past on-time %, order size, lead time, port congestion index). SVM draws the boundary that separates \"late\" from \"on-time\" orders with the **maximum margin**, using only the hardest cases near the boundary (**support vectors**). Strengths: works well with many features and modest data; robust. Weaknesses: less interpretable, needs feature scaling, outputs a class not a calibrated probability (needs calibration before you use it for a **cost-weighted** decision). Exam framing: an SVM's \"accuracy\" means nothing until you price a missed delay vs a false alarm.\n\n### k-Means in operations language 📗\nSteps: choose k → place k centroids → assign each SKU to its nearest centroid → move each centroid to the mean of its members → repeat until stable. Choose k with the **elbow method** or by usefulness. Typical features: volume, volatility (coefficient of variation), margin, lead time → segments like \"fast-stable\", \"slow-intermittent\", \"promo-driven\". **Validation is not \"accuracy\"** — **Class Q 6.3:** \"validate the k-means clustering's accuracy\" is conceptually wrong — **there's no ground truth**; judge segments by whether they lead to **different, profitable treatments** (different stocking policies, forecasting tiers, service targets). \"The decision, not a metric, validates unsupervised work.\"\n\n## 3. How a neural net learns 📘\nA neuron weighs inputs, adds a bias and fires through an activation function (a 25×25 image → 625 inputs → ~13,100 connections). A **cost function** measures wrongness; **gradient descent** rolls downhill to reduce it; **backpropagation** assigns blame backwards. **Bias vs variance:** too simple = consistently wrong (underfit); too complex = inconsistent (overfit). Aim between — capture signal, not noise.\n\n## 4. Training, validation and the overfitting trap ⭐⭐\n- **The split is sacred:** train on one slice, validate on another, **test on data the model never influenced** (the M5's authority comes from strict test design).\n- **Overfitting in ops terms:** memorising last year — perfect on history, useless next quarter. Complexity buys flexibility AND fragility.\n- **Non-stationarity is the ops disease:** promotions, competitors, pandemics shift regimes; models degrade silently → monitoring and retraining are not optional.\n- **Features carry the value:** promotion calendars, installed base, lead signals usually beat algorithm choice — \"the data groundwork of Session 2 IS the model's ceiling\".\n\n## 5. Decision-focused learning ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Predict-then-optimise** trains on prediction error (e.g. MSE) then feeds an optimiser — simple and modular, but **misaligned**: the loss the model minimises is not the cost the business pays.\n- **Concretely:** a newsvendor's optimal order is a **quantile** of demand, not the mean; over- and under-prediction cost differently; a symmetric loss optimises the wrong target \"from the first epoch\".\n- **Decision-focused learning** trains with the **decision cost as the loss** — errors that would change decisions expensively are penalised more → consistent decision-cost improvements.\n- **Where it leads:** S10's **one-step** paradigm learns the decision function itself, skipping the standalone forecast.\n\n## 6. ML in the field ⭐⭐\n### The M5 competition — ML wins, with an asterisk ⭐⭐⭐\nWalmart's hierarchical retail data — **42,840 daily SKU-store series**, thousands of teams. **For the first time ML (gradient-boosted trees) decisively beat classical statistics; every top entry was ML.** The asterisk: **simple, nearly free methods stayed competitive at the SKU-store level where ordering happens**; the uncertainty track showed **distributional forecasting** is where hard value lives; naive benchmarks embarrassed many sophisticated entries. Twin lessons: ML's real edge AND the stubbornness of simplicity → \"**price the gap between clever and simple before paying for clever**\".\n\n### Named wins 📘\n**Danone** (ML demand planning on years of harmonised data) · **Lenovo** (+5% forecast accuracy, +4% OTD, +10% delivery accuracy — incremental, measured, real) · **Alibaba** published field study (automated ML replenishment vs human buyers — the algorithm won on stockouts and turnover) · **Zara** (real-time trend detection serving a **responsive** chain — \"the tool matched the chain\").\n\n### Zillow Offers (2021) — when the model writes the cheques ⭐⭐⭐\nZillow used ML price forecasts to **buy houses directly** — an irreversible, capital-intensive decision at scale. Late 2021: unit shut, inventory written down **>$500M**, **~25%** of workforce cut. The same models powered the popular Zestimate (fine as **information**). Failure chain: point forecasts feeding automatic offers in a shifting market (non-stationarity); **asymmetric error costs** (overpaying fatal, underbidding just loses deals); guardrails not proportionate to blast radius. \"**The decision defines the model** — an 87%-accurate price model can be excellent for browsing and ruinous for buying.\" **Class Q 6.1:** whether 87% is good enough depends on **the decision's cost and reversibility**.\n\n## 7. Why operations ML disappoints — four failure modes ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **No decision owner** — the model predicts something nobody acts on (\"analytics as decoration\").\n2. **Accuracy theatre** — \"92% accuracy\" with no naive benchmark, no incumbent comparison, no decision-terms translation. **Class Q 6.4:** 92% vs naive same-as-last-week 90.5%, orders in full trucks → **unproven — barely beats naive**. \"No model without a naive benchmark.\"\n3. **Leakage and hindsight** — features unknown at decision time sneak in. **Class Q 6.2:** a supplier-delay model using **goods-receipt date** (known only after delivery) → **data leakage** — the most common cause of the demo-to-production cliff. \"Features must pass the 'known at decision time' test.\"\n4. **Asymmetric costs ignored** — symmetric loss while the business bleeds asymmetrically (newsvendor, Zillow).\n\n## 8. The ML Framing Canvas — five boxes before any model ⭐⭐⭐\n| Box | Question | SuryaMart Pitch C (supplier-delay warning) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1 Decision** | who acts, at what cadence, within what constraints? no owner, no project | buyers expedite or re-source weekly; owner = procurement head |\n| **2 Target** | the variable at **decision granularity** | \"PO line arrives > 3 days late\", per PO line |\n| **3 Features** | knowable at decision time; no leakage | supplier history, order size, lead time, port index — **not** goods-receipt date |\n| **4 Benchmark** | naive rule + incumbent, priced in decisions | \"flag suppliers with < 85% OTD last quarter\" |\n| **5 Loss** | mirrors decision costs — asymmetric | missed delay (stockout) costs ≫ false alarm (an expedite call) → weight recall |\n*SuryaMart resolved:* fund **Pitch C** first (clear decision owner, fast feedback, asymmetric cost you can price); reshape Pitch A (40,000 SKUs needs tier-matching and a naive benchmark — \"92%\" answers nothing about benchmark, holdout or granularity); Pitch B needs a decision (what treatment changes per segment?) before it can be validated. Ask \"which pitch could a simple rule replace?\" — that is the naive benchmark.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Session 7 — the workflow that survives ⭐⭐⭐\n**Seven steps, in order — skipping one is how projects die:**\n1. **Frame** — the S6 canvas.\n2. **Profile** — six data-quality dimensions on the actual data, programmatically, before any model.\n3. **Benchmark** — fit the **naive rule FIRST**; it is the floor.\n4. **Model** — simplest credible model first; complexity only when it pays on the holdout.\n5. **Evaluate** — honest holdout; error metrics AND **decisions changed**; **slice by segment** (averages hide failures).\n6. **Deploy** — pipeline, not laptop: versioned data in, decisions out, permissions, logging.\n7. **Monitor** — drift alerts, retraining cadence, **named owner**, documented **fallback**.\n**Class Q 7.2:** the model beats naive by 6% overall but **loses** on promotional and new items → **deploy where it wins; keep naive elsewhere** (champion–challenger by segment).\n\n## 10. The real stack — tools named in the syllabus ⭐⭐\n- **Python + scikit-learn** 📘 — \"the lingua franca of operations analytics\": free, battle-tested, deep hiring market; covers classification, clustering, regression; runs on all major clouds. Typical lab flow 📗: `pandas` to load and profile → `train_test_split` (or a time-based split for forecasting) → a naive baseline → `LinearRegression` and `GradientBoostingRegressor` → score on the same holdout (MAE/MAPE) → slice by category.\n- **RapidMiner-class AutoML & visual tools** 📘 — point-and-click model building for citizen data scientists; useful for prototyping and teams without Python depth; \"**the tool automates fitting, not judgment**\" — the same evaluation discipline applies. (RapidMiner 📗: drag-and-drop \"operators\" chained into a process — retrieve data, split, train, apply model, measure performance; AutoModel compares algorithms automatically.)\n- **Managed ML clouds** 📘 — Amazon SageMaker, Azure ML, IBM Watson ML, Google Cloud AI (differ on clustering, anomaly detection, recommendation support).\n- **Language/chatbots** — Dialogflow, Watson Assistant, LUIS, Lex, wit.ai; no-code agents Power Virtual Agents, Teneo.\n- **Deep-learning frameworks** — TensorFlow and Keras to start; PyTorch, CNTK.\n- **Walmart Element** — what the notebook grows into at scale: a model-agnostic ML platform with pipelines, monitoring and governance.\n- \"The newest tool is not always the best — sometimes a table, a chart or an expert system answers faster than a neural net.\" **Data mining ≠ ML:** BI sorts and summarises what you ask for; ML finds patterns you did not know to ask about.\n\n## 11. The demo-to-production cliff ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Leakage flattery** — the backtest used information unavailable at decision time (happens in half of first notebooks).\n- **Environment drift** — last year's regime, mix, formats; nobody watches; decay is silent.\n- **The bus factor** — one author, no documentation, manual steps from memory.\n- **No fallback, no owner** — the model misbehaves at 2 a.m. before cut-off: who is called, what rule takes over?\n**Class Q 7.1:** a brilliant analyst's model beats the incumbent by 8% in backtest, but she exports data manually, runs cells from memory, and keeps the only copy on her laptop → the firm owns **little durable — a one-person demo**. \"A model in a notebook is not a capability.\" Conversion work is **60–80% of effort**.\n**Class Q 7.3:** a churn model shows **99%** accuracy when only 3% churn → **accuracy is misleading; check for leakage** (\"never churn\" scores 97%).\n**Four words of advice for the lab:** don't mix training and test data; don't overstate accuracy; know your algorithm (if you can't explain it you can't defend it); select tools deliberately (capability, cost, integration, skills). **Test in three layers:** unit, integration, acceptance; fix bugs **test-first**.\n\n### The capability checklist\nReproducible (runs without its author) · monitored (drift, accuracy, decisions) · owned (named owner, on call) · documented fallback (rule that takes over) · versioned data and model · permissions and logging. \"This is what separates the 5% that produce P&L impact from the 95% that produce slides.\"\n\n*Meghna Foods resolved:* **dig, then delay** — the 94% needs a naive benchmark, an honest time-based holdout and decision-granularity evaluation; production first breaks on the bus factor and manual CSV exports. \"Done\" = a scheduled pipeline feeding the planning system by cut-off, monitored, with a named owner and a fallback rule, beating naive on the segments where it is deployed.\n\n## Key takeaways (S6–S7 recaps 📘)\n1. ML's victory is real (M5) and so is simplicity's stubbornness — price the gap before paying for clever.\n2. The decision defines the model: the same 87% is excellent for information and ruinous for autonomous buying.\n3. Supervised predicts, unsupervised structures, reinforcement decides — decision-focused learning trains on the cost you pay.\n4. Leakage, overfitting and non-stationarity are why backtests flatter; holdout discipline is management.\n5. The framing canvas is your permanent supervision tool. The tools are free and models easy; the capability is neither — 60–80% of effort is pipeline, monitoring and hand-off. Profile first, naive first, slice by segment, and write the honest verdict memo.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. \"Our model is 92% accurate.\" List the three questions that number does not answer.**\nBetter than what (naive and incumbent on the same holdout)? Measured how (honest time-based holdout, no leakage)? At what granularity and worth what in decisions (does it change orders given full-truck multiples)?\n\n**Q2. Why can't you report the \"accuracy\" of a k-means segmentation?**\nThere is no ground truth label; validate by whether segments lead to different, profitable actions (e.g. different stocking policies) that a planner can act on.\n\n**Q3. A delay model's best feature is goods-receipt date. What happened?**\nLeakage — the feature is known only after the delivery, so the backtest is flattered and production collapses. Every feature must be knowable at decision time.\n\n**Q4. When would you pick an SVM, and what must you add before using it in a cost-weighted decision?**\nFor a classification task (late vs on-time, defect vs good) with many features and moderate data; add probability calibration and an asymmetric cost-based threshold, and compare against a naive rule.\n\n**Q5. A model wins overall but loses on promotions. Deploy?**\nDeploy it on the segments where it wins (staples) and keep the naive/incumbent method for promotions and new items — champion–challenger by segment, revisited as data accrues.\n"}, {"slug": "ch07-demand-forecasting", "label": "Demand Forecasting", "tag": "07", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 7 — AI-Driven Demand Forecasting (Sessions 8, 9, 11, 12)", "md": "# Ch 7 — AI-Driven Demand Forecasting (Sessions 8, 9, 11, 12)\n**Syllabus unit 3 📘:** *Demand Forecasting Using Agentic AI — auto-updating models using IoT and reinforcement learning; Excel vs. Prophet vs. SageMaker.*\n**Session 8 📘:** \"Probability forecasting, demand sensing with IoT and external signals, and the planning paradigm that stops worshipping the forecast.\" Case: **Nandini Beverages — Three Forecasting Pitches Before the Monsoon**.\n**Session 9 📘 (lab):** \"One dataset, three tool tiers, one verdict.\" Case: **Trident Distributors — Which Tier Fits?**\n**Session 11 📘:** a recap of the S8 ideas that led straight into **Chain Reaction Round 1 (Onboarding)**.\n**Session 12 📘:** \"Buy the tool the workload deserves.\" It covers the four tiers including LSTM, the M5 footnote and the bake-off ladder, and led into **Round 2 (Forecasting bake-off)**.\n\n> **Exam use:** demand-forecasting questions in a case almost never ask \"which algorithm?\". They ask one of four things.\n> (1) **Point vs distribution**: is the safety stock reading the uncertainty?\n> (2) **Signal audit**: is the new data worth buying?\n> (3) **Tier match**: Excel, Prophet, AutoML or LSTM, given volume, readiness, skills and governance?\n> (4) **Update vs execute**: is the \"autonomous forecasting agent\" actually an autonomy grant?\n> Kaveri Motors' Claim 4 (\"the forecast is good, so we can cut the buffer\") is a forecasting question in disguise: **forecasting attacks safety stock only, never cycle stock (Q vs S).**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Probability forecasting — \"the quantile IS the decision\" ⭐⭐⭐\n- **From number to distribution.** A probability forecast gives the full range of outcomes with their likelihoods (mean, spread, tails) for each SKU, location and period. The point forecast is only one summary of it, and rarely the useful one.\n- **A service level is a quantile choice.** Stocking to a 95% service level means holding the **95th percentile of demand over the lead time**. Put that way, safety stock stops being folklore and becomes a **costed choice on an explicit curve**.\n- **Newsvendor logic generalises it.** When over-stocking and under-stocking cost different amounts, the optimal order is the **critical quantile**:\n  - **CR = Cu / (Cu + Co)**, where Cu is the cost of one unit short (lost margin and goodwill) and Co is the cost of one unit left over (holding, markdown, obsolescence).\n  - Example: margin ₹60 lost per short unit and ₹20 lost per unsold unit gives CR = 60/80 = 0.75, so stock the **75th percentile**. Fashion and perishables have low CR; high-margin spares have high CR.\n- **Evaluation changes too.** Distribution forecasts are scored with **quantile (pinball) loss** and **calibration checks** (\"do 90% of actuals fall inside the 90% band?\"), not MAPE alone. The **M5 uncertainty track** exists because this is where planning value concentrates.\n- **Point forecasts force a workaround.** Planners have to bolt the uncertainty back on with rules of thumb such as \"weeks of supply\". Distributions let safety stock read straight off the data.\n\n**Worked numeric (exam style).** Assume normal demand with a weekly mean of 1,000 and σ = 200, a lead time of 4 weeks and a 95% service level (z = 1.645).\n- σ over the lead time = 200 × √4 = **400**.\n- **SS = 1.645 × 400 ≈ 658**.\n- Reorder point = 4,000 + 658 = **4,658**.\n- If a better forecast cuts σ to 150, SS becomes 1.645 × 300 ≈ **494**. That is 164 units less safety stock, and **cycle stock (Q/2) is unchanged**.\n\n### Class Q 8.2 / 11-1.1 — two SKUs, same forecast ⭐⭐⭐\nBoth SKUs forecast 1,000. SKU A is steady (±50) and SKU B is wild (±400). The rule is \"forecast + 2 weeks of supply\" for both.\n**Answer: A, the rule ignores demand uncertainty.** Identical means with different variances need different buffers.\n- The rule overstocks A (its buffer is far larger than ±50 needs).\n- It strands service on B (its buffer is far smaller than ±400 needs).\n- The fix information is **the distribution (σ or quantiles) for each SKU**, not a better point forecast.\n- *Lesson: forecast the distribution, not the number.*\n\n## 2. Regression, accuracy metrics and the confusion-matrix trap ⭐⭐\n- **Regression is the forecasting workhorse.** It finds relationships (\"squeeze an independent variable and the dependent one bulges, like twisting a balloon animal\"). It predicts **how many**, whereas classification predicts **which class**.\n  - **Features must lead, not lag.** A card issuer spotted trouble early when groceries and utilities appeared on a card previously used for electronics.\n  - **Outliers distort the line.** A festival day or a promotion pulls the trend; more and cleaner data steadies it.\n- **The confusion matrix** has four outcomes (TP, FP, FN, TN).\n  - **Precision** = TP/(TP+FP): when it flags a spike, is it right?\n  - **Recall** = TP/(TP+FN): of the real spikes, how many did it catch?\n  - **F1** is their harmonic mean. Use it as the single comparison number and as the baseline for future improvement.\n- **Beware the skew.** On a 1%-event problem, \"always predict no\" scores 99% accuracy and is useless. **Always beat a naive baseline.**\n- **Ensemble the forecast.** Combine a strong average-case model with one tuned for turning points. Bagging and boosting are standard because no single learner wins everywhere.\n\n### Class Q 8.4 — Model X vs Model Y ⭐⭐⭐\nX has the lower average error. Y is worse on average but far better at flagging spikes before promotions.\n**Answer: D, Model Y. It wins where it matters.**\n- Average error rewards the easy middle.\n- A safety-stock or promotion-readiness decision lives **in the tail and at turning points**.\n- *Match the metric to the decision.* (This is the same logic as Kaveri's \"simple average vs volume-weighted fill\".)\n\n**Worked numeric.** A spike-detector flags 40 weeks as \"spike\". 30 of them really were spikes, and there were 50 real spikes in total.\n- Precision = 30/40 = **75%**.\n- Recall = 30/50 = **60%**.\n- F1 = 2 × 0.75 × 0.6 / 1.35 = **66.7%**.\n\n## 3. Demand sensing — audit signals before you buy them ⭐⭐⭐\n| Signal | Leads demand by | Mechanism | Verdict pattern |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Weather forecast** | days to weeks | temperature and rain drive consumption directly (beverages, ice cream, fashion) | **buy IF the horizon matches the order lead time** |\n| **Promotions calendar** | weeks | price and display causally lift demand, and distort history if unmodelled | **mandatory**: both the signal AND history repair |\n| **POS / channel sell-through** | days | end-consumer truth vs distorted orders: **the antidote to the bullwhip** | high value where access is negotiable |\n| **Installed base & utilisation** (IoT on machines in the field) | weeks to months | machines in the field drive parts and service demand | **the spare-parts deployment's winning signal** |\n| **Social / search trends** | weeks | attention precedes purchase (fashion, launches) | test rigorously; the mechanism is often flimsy |\n| **Customer forecasts** | varies | incentive-laden negotiating positions | let the model weight them (recall: **zero**) |\n\n**IoT as a demand signal.** Sensors on installed equipment (run-hours, utilisation, scheduled maintenance) tell you which parts will be consumed **before** an order arrives. That is why IoT turns spare-parts forecasting from guesswork into an engineering estimate. The same sensors feed predictive maintenance (Ch 9).\n\n### The Signal Audit — five steps before any data purchase ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n1. **Lead time:** does the signal arrive before the decision must be made, early enough to act on?\n2. **Mechanism:** is there an honest causal story, or a correlation mined from one training window?\n3. **Decision test:** does the improved prediction cross an order or capacity threshold (i.e. change an order)?\n4. **Cost vs value:** acquisition plus integration cost, weighed against the decisions actually changed.\n5. **Weight check:** after deployment, what weight did the model LEARN? Demote or drop the losers ruthlessly.\n\n**Applied to Nandini's weather data.**\n- Lead: weather forecasts must reach 2–3 weeks ahead if the bottling and dispatch lead time is 2–3 weeks; a 5-day forecast cannot change a 3-week order.\n- Mechanism: honest (heat drives thirst).\n- Decision test: does it move production or dispatch quantities in the monsoon-transition weeks?\n- Cost: the subscription plus integration, weighed against the stockouts avoided in those weeks.\n- Weight: review after one season.\n\n## 4. Adaptive and \"autonomous\" forecasting — what the loop really is ⭐⭐⭐\n- **The adaptive loop.** New observations arrive, weights update and the plan refreshes. The deployed one-step system recomputed its decision function **in minutes, weekly**. This was a **deliberate cadence** that balances responsiveness against **nervousness** (plans that churn every hour destroy execution).\n- **Reinforcement-flavoured forecasting.** Where forecasts feed repeated decisions with observable outcomes (pricing, replenishment), learning can target the **decision reward** directly. Ch 8 completes this thread.\n- **The \"autonomous forecasting agent\" claim, audited.** Strip the label and ask which of three things it is:\n  - (a) **adaptive re-estimation**: mature and buyable, a feature;\n  - (b) **decision-function learning**: the one-step paradigm;\n  - (c) **an agent with write-access to orders**: an **AUTONOMY decision**, governed by the S4 ladder rather than being a forecasting upgrade.\n- **The human role that remains.** Regime breaks (2020-class) still need human recognition and override. Guardrails and planner market knowledge are **complements, not rivals**.\n\n### Class Q 8.3 — the most clarifying vendor question ⭐⭐⭐\nThe Pitch C brochure says: \"continuously retrains… adjusts forecasts in real time with zero planner effort.\"\n**Answer: B, \"Does it update forecasts, or execute orders?\"**\n- Updating is a feature.\n- Executing is an autonomy grant that needs the ladder (L1–L5), guardrails and the readiness bar.\n- \"Conflating them is how forecasting upgrades quietly become ungoverned agents.\"\n\n## 5. The paradigm case — zero weight for the customer's forecast ⭐⭐⭐\n**Fortune 150 consumer-electronics OEM** (course text Ch. 11):\n- six products, contract-manufactured in Asia;\n- sold through about **3,000 stores** via partners such as Walmart and Best Buy;\n- deployed the **one-step planning system**, whose learned decision function weighted every input by measured usefulness.\n\n**Headline finding:** the retail partners' own forecasts earned a **weight of ZERO**. They were historically so error-prone that the system learned to ignore them. Seasonality and the OEM's internal signals carried the weight instead.\n\n**The sister deployment** was a semiconductor-equipment maker with **50,000 spare-part SKUs**.\n- Its best signals lay **outside the demand history**: installed base, scheduled maintenance and equipment utilisation at customer sites.\n- **Fill rate rose from 77% to 85% at constant inventory**, or equivalently about **$20M of inventory released** at the old fill rate.\n\n**Two durable lessons:**\n1. Let the system **price every signal by contribution**, including politically important ones.\n2. The best predictors of demand are often **upstream drivers of demand**, not demand's own past.\n\n### Class Q 8.1 — the politically important partner ⭐⭐\nThe partner expects its forecast to be used, but it misleads.\n**Answer: D, weight it by measured contribution.**\n- The interpretable weight visualisation became the tool that let the OEM **influence its partners' inventory decisions**.\n- *\"Signals earn their weights; the politics are managed, not obeyed.\" \"Evidence beats etiquette when the evidence is showable.\"*\n\n## 6. The wider record ⭐⭐\n- **Danone / Zara.** Multi-country ML planning, and real-time trend sensing driving production. Both are **signal stories**: channel and trend data that lead demand, feeding decisions fast enough to matter.\n- **The 2020 breakdown.** Lockdowns broke history-trained models en masse. Grocery demand doubled overnight in some categories while apparel collapsed, and the models kept predicting the vanished world. This is non-stationarity at planetary scale: **history-only forecasting carries a regime-change fragility** you must design for (monitoring, override, scenario plans).\n- **Weather-driven planning** in beverages, ice cream and fashion works because the mechanism is honest and the lead time usable. The test is always the same: **does the signal arrive early enough to change an order?**\n- **Probability forecasting in products.** Modern planning platforms (ToolsGroup and others) ship distribution forecasts as the default, so safety stocks and service levels read directly off quantiles.\n\n## 7. Why forecasting disappoints even when accurate — four pathologies ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Multiple competing forecasts.**\n   - Sales forecasts monthly in rupees/dollars to secure supply.\n   - Planning forecasts weekly in units to avoid obsolescence.\n   - Each retail partner forecasts separately.\n   - None agree, and none improve as the period approaches. *Which one drives orders?* (Documented verbatim at the OEM.)\n2. **Incentive distortion.** Every forecast is also a negotiating position: **sales inflates** (before quota-setting), **planning deflates** (for safety), and customers over-order under allocation. *The numbers carry agendas before they carry information* (Nandini's exhibit).\n3. **Heuristic conversion.** \"Weeks of supply\" rules turn even good forecasts into bad inventory: an inflated forecast gives excess stock and a conservative one gives shortages. **The damage happens AFTER the forecast.**\n4. **Point-blindness.** A single number hides the uncertainty the decision needs.\n\n**Fix:** pathologies 1–2 are **organisational**. Accuracy cannot fix incentive distortion; **architecture and transparency** can (a single consensus number, signals weighted by evidence, a visible weight chart). Pathologies 3–4 are fixed by **distribution forecasting and quantile-based policies**.\n\n## 8. Nandini Beverages — the case resolved ⭐⭐⭐\n**Facts.** Monsoon season is ahead and three pitches are on the table:\n- **Pitch A**: statistical upgrade, ₹40 L.\n- **Pitch B**: ML + weather/IoT sensing, ₹1.4 cr.\n- **Pitch C**: \"autonomous forecasting agent\", ₹2.1 cr.\n\nOther facts:\n- Sales inflates and planning deflates.\n- The monsoon drives **40% of demand variance**.\n- Fill-rate failures concentrate in **promo weeks and monsoon-transition weeks**.\n\n**Model answer (numbered assumptions first).**\n1. *Assume* the incumbent method has never been benchmarked against a naive (seasonal-naive) forecast on a holdout.\n2. *Assume* weather feeds with a 2–3 week horizon are available and that the bottling/dispatch lead time is about 2–3 weeks.\n3. *Assume* promo history is not tagged (so it is distorted).\n\n**Analysis.**\n- The failure is concentrated in promo and monsoon weeks. **That is where forecast value lives**, not in the average week.\n- None of the pitches fixes the inflate/deflate politics, because that is an architecture problem: one consensus number, the model weights the inputs, and a visible weight chart.\n\n**Verdict: a sequenced combination.**\n- (i) Run a **Pitch-A-level baseline** and benchmark it, repair promo history and set a single-number S&OP process (cheap; fixes pathologies 1–3).\n- (ii) Pilot **Pitch B's** weather and promo signals **only on the monsoon-transition and promo SKUs**, after a **signal audit**, with a distribution forecast feeding quantile safety stocks.\n- (iii) Ask Pitch C **\"update or execute?\"**:\n  - if it updates, it is a pricier Pitch B;\n  - if it executes, it is an autonomy grant Nandini is not ready for (an L2/L3 discussion later).\n\n**What would change the answer in 12 months:** the weather signal earning material weight and cutting monsoon-week stockouts, plus clean promo data. Then scale B, and consider bounded execution (L3) for replenishment.\n\n## 9. Forecasting tiers — buy the tool the workload deserves (S9, S12) ⭐⭐⭐\n### What each tier assumes and delivers\n| Dimension | Tier 1 · **Spreadsheet (Excel)** | Tier 2 · **Open-source (Prophet, scikit-learn)** | Tier 3 · **Cloud AutoML (AWS SageMaker / Canvas)** | Tier 4 · **Sequence models (LSTM / transformers)** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Comfortable volume | tens to hundreds of series | thousands (scripted) | tens of thousands + | very large, rich, long histories |\n| Skills assumed | spreadsheet literacy | Python + statistics | cloud admin + data engineering + FinOps | deep-learning engineering |\n| Readiness assumed | clean-enough extracts | pipeline discipline (S7) | governed data platform (S2 ≥ 3.5) | **Data Maturity 3+**, long history |\n| Explainability | total (every cell visible) | high (code and models open) | variable, often opaque | low |\n| Cost shape | hidden (hours, errors) | skills + maintenance | subscription + consumption | compute + scarce talent |\n| Governance/audit | weak (version chaos) | good if engineered | strong tooling, lock-in terms | hard to explain to a lender |\n\n- **Excel:** exponential smoothing (Holt-Winters) is a legitimate, auditable baseline at modest scale. Recall S2: **61% of planning decisions are executed in spreadsheets beside an 11-year-old ERP**.\n- **Prophet:** built at Meta to **democratise business forecasting**. It handles trend, seasonality and holidays by default. It fills the gap \"between naive methods and deep learning at scale\" but does not replace either at SKU level.\n- **SageMaker-class AutoML** (including no-code Canvas): scale, automation and integration with the S5 platform world. **Consumption pricing, quota realities and lock-in** come with it.\n- **LSTM / transformers:** need long history, rich data and high maturity. At ordinary SKU-location-week granularity, **simple methods stay embarrassingly competitive**. Running one without the pipeline and skills \"isn't sophistication, it's over-tiering\".\n- **The frontier that is NOT yet a tier:** foundation forecasting models (\"rent a pretrained forecaster\"). 2026 benchmarks call them **promising, not dominant** against tuned simpler methods at operational granularity.\n- **Worth watching:** **multi-agent RL coordinating replenishment** across a chain beats one smart agent alone, with **lower cost AND less bullwhip**. Coordination, not a bigger model, is where the next bullwhip gains are.\n\n### The four tier questions (in order) and the Bake-Off Ladder ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n1. **Count it:** your real SKU × location × frequency volume, not next year's hoped-for volume.\n2. **Audit readiness:** can you trace, clean and timestamp the history this tier needs today?\n3. **Name the skill:** who will run and explain it in plain words? (\"Not 'we'll hire someone'.\")\n4. **Check governance:** does it give an auditable, explainable number when a lender, board or regulator asks?\n5. **Bake it off:** run the candidate tier on your own recent data before signing any subscription.\n\n*Accuracy joins the verdict table but does not chair it.*\n\n**Two failure directions and two buying sins:**\n- **Under-tooling at scale:** analysts hand-forecasting thousands of series weekly; error-prone, unauditable and dependent on one person (the bus factor).\n- **Over-tooling above readiness:** enterprise ML idling above teams without the pipelines or skills (the [2,1,3,2,5] scorecard as a purchase order).\n- **Tool-first buying:** the demo runs on curated data with expert drivers.\n- **TCO blindness:** \"free\" Tier 2 costs skills and maintenance; Tier 3 meters consumption (the **3.2× token overrun**). Price the whole loop: **build + run + retrain + govern**.\n\n### Class questions on tiers ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Q 9.1, the 12-SKU bakery:** the answer is **B, a spreadsheet already fits**. Tiers are workload matches, not maturity badges.\n- **Q 9.2, cloud AutoML beats Prophet by 2% at 6× cost** with skills Trident lacks: the answer is **A, the Prophet tier wins today**. The verdict is multi-column and should name the **conditions under which it flips**.\n- **Q 9.3, a notebook must run nightly for 40,000 SKUs and feed planning by 6 a.m.:** the answer is **D, whether it runs reliably on schedule**.\n- **Q 12-2.1, 60 SKUs, 4 dealerships, an Excel-only analyst, and lenders wanting an auditable rationale; an LSTM demo shows bullwhip charts:** the answer is **C, the spreadsheet/smoothing tier**. The LSTM needs Data Maturity 3+ and produces exactly the opaque output a lender does not want.\n\n### Trident Distributors — the case resolved ⭐⭐⭐\n**Facts:**\n- 8,000 SKU-locations, ordered weekly;\n- 2 analysts (one knows some Python);\n- readiness [D3, T2, P3, O3, G3];\n- ₹25 L tooling budget;\n- the MD wants \"what the big retailers have\";\n- the senior analyst's spreadsheets have run the business for 8 years.\n\n**Answer:**\n1. 8,000 weekly series is past the comfortable spreadsheet range.\n2. The technology score of 2 and a single Python-capable analyst rule out cloud AutoML today (it assumes a governed platform ≥ 3.5, data engineering and FinOps).\n3. So the recommendation is **Tier 2 (Prophet-class), scripted and scheduled**, with the spreadsheet kept as the **benchmark and fallback**.\n4. The successor must **beat the senior analyst's forecasts on a holdout of Trident's own data**, per segment, and prove it **to the analyst and the MD** (trust design).\n5. The MD's demo assumes curated data, expert operators and a governed platform.\n\n**Flip condition (cloud in two years):** a governed data platform (T ≥ 3.5), a data engineer, volumes in the tens of thousands, and a bake-off showing that the uplift **in decisions** pays for 6× the cost.\n\n### Prototype to production (S9) ⭐⭐\n- **Promote through environments:** dev → stage (\"production-sized, the last line of defence\") → prod, with automated **unit, integration and acceptance tests**, plus a **model regression suite** so retraining does not break old skills.\n- **Blue/green deployment:** run two environments and re-point traffic once the new one passes QA, so there is no downtime.\n- **Design for spikes:** load-test about **20% above peak**, remember that **auto-scale lags about 3 minutes**, and let the **SLA** decide whether the nightly forecast lands by cut-off. \"The SLA is the product.\" \"A notebook that cannot hit the 6 a.m. handoff is not a capability.\"\n- **Verify vs validate:**\n  - Verification means the code runs.\n  - Validation means it does the useful thing (usually a human check).\n  - Report accuracy only after a large held-out test.\n  - Keep a **model-library entry** (precision/recall/F1, framework, train/validation counts, step size, epochs).\n  - Schedule retraining, because outdated models give bad predictions.\n- **The major clouds differ** in which capabilities they support (clustering, anomaly detection, recommendation). Choose on the **capability matrix, not the brand**.\n\n### The S9 lab — the three-tier bake-off\n1. Tier 1 exponential smoothing in the spreadsheet; score it on a holdout and note the setup time.\n2. Tier 2 Prophet on the same series; note what the defaults handled and what needed tuning.\n3. Tier 3 AutoML; note its score, configuration surface, pricing basis and what stays opaque.\n4. Complete the **verdict table** (accuracy vs naive, setup, cost, explainability, governance), with the **flip condition**.\n\n### The M5 footnote ⭐⭐\nML won on aggregate accuracy, but simple, cheap methods stayed remarkably competitive **at the SKU-store level, where planning decisions are made**. The winning methods ran on **modest infrastructure**. Spreadsheets persist because they match the volume, skills and governance most teams have. \"Don't let an aggregate leaderboard sell you an enterprise platform for a 200-SKU business.\"\n\n## 10. Chain Reaction link — Rounds 1 & 2 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **R1 (Onboarding):** manual operations, the first forecast, learning the console. Use the **distribution-not-a-number** habit from the first click.\n- **R2 (Forecasting bake-off):** each company builds its own forecasting agent and chooses a build speed.\n  - **FAST:** 0.85× cost, quality −18.\n  - **STANDARD:** 1.0×.\n  - **CAREFUL:** 1.25× cost over two rounds, quality +6.\n  - There is **no failure chance** either way.\n- Use the four tier questions, not the flashiest option. The trade is speed now vs agent quality for the rest of the season; a −18 quality forecaster compounds into every later planning decision. See the [Chain Reaction playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook).\n\n## Key takeaways (S8, S9, S11, S12 recaps 📘)\n1. The decision consumes a **quantile, not a mean**. Probability forecasting turns safety stock from folklore into a costed choice.\n2. The best demand signals are demand's **upstream drivers** (installed base, weather, promotions). Every signal must pass the audit: lead time, mechanism, decision impact.\n3. Organisations have **several forecasts, each with an agenda**. Accuracy cannot fix incentive distortion; architecture and transparency can (the zero-weight lesson).\n4. History-only models carry **regime-change fragility** (2020). Adaptive loops, monitoring and human override are design requirements.\n5. \"Autonomous forecasting\" claims decompose under one question: **update or execute?**\n6. **Tier follows the workload** (volume, readiness, skills, governance), not the demo. Bake-offs beat brochures.\n7. LSTMs win with long history and high maturity; at SKU-level granularity simple methods stay competitive. Coordinating multiple agents is where the next bullwhip gains show up.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. A planner says, \"Our new ML forecast is 15% more accurate, so we can cut inventory by 15%.\" Assess it.**\nAccuracy gains reduce **σ of forecast error**, which reduces **safety stock only**. Cycle stock (Q/2) depends on the order quantity and is untouched. If safety stock is 30% of the inventory, a 15% cut in σ trims about 15% of SS, i.e. about 4.5% of the total. The claim confuses Q with S.\n\n**Q2. Cu = ₹90, Co = ₹30. Demand ~ Normal(500, 100). What is the optimal order quantity?**\nCR = 90/120 = 0.75, and z(0.75) ≈ 0.674. Q* = 500 + 0.674 × 100 ≈ **567 units**.\n\n**Q3. A vendor offers social-media trend data for a B2B industrial-fastener business. Run the signal audit.**\nLead time is unclear. The mechanism is flimsy (fastener buyers don't trend on social media). The decision test fails (it won't cross an order threshold). The cost is a subscription plus integration for no changed decisions. Verdict: don't buy. Installed-base or customer production-schedule data would be better.\n\n**Q4. Why did the OEM's system give the retailers' forecasts zero weight, and what did the OEM do with that?**\nThe forecasts were historically error-prone and incentive-laden, so the model learned they added nothing. The OEM used the **interpretable weight chart** to influence partners' inventory decisions: evidence beats etiquette.\n\n**Q5. A 300-SKU distributor with one Excel analyst and no data engineer is pitched SageMaker. Recommend.**\nTier 1/2 now: smoothing in Excel, or Prophet if the analyst can script, benchmarked against naive. SageMaker assumes a governed platform, data engineering and FinOps. Flip condition: volume in the thousands-plus, a data platform ≥ 3.5, and a bake-off uplift that pays for the cost in decisions.\n\n**Q6. \"Our autonomous forecasting agent adjusts in real time with zero planner effort.\" What do you ask, and why?**\n\"Does it update forecasts, or execute orders?\" Executing is an autonomy grant (S4 ladder, guardrails, readiness). Updating is a feature to bake off.\n\n**Q7. Give two reasons Model Y (worse on average, better at spikes) can be the right choice.**\nStocking and promo decisions live in the tail; a missed spike costs lost sales and expediting, which outweighs small average errors. The metric must match the decision, e.g. pinball loss at the service quantile, or recall on spikes.\n\n**Q8. How do the four forecasting pathologies split between technology fixes and organisation fixes?**\nMultiple forecasts and incentive distortion are organisational: one consensus number, transparent weights. Heuristic conversion and point-blindness are technical/policy: distribution forecasts and quantile safety stock.\n"}, {"slug": "ch08-inventory-optimisation", "label": "Inventory Optimisation", "tag": "08", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 8 — AI Inventory Optimisation: From EOQ to Learned Policies (Session 10)", "md": "# Ch 8 — AI Inventory Optimisation: From EOQ to Learned Policies (Session 10)\n**Syllabus unit 4 📘:** *Inventory Optimisation with AI (EOQ, Dynamic Models) — AI dynamic modelling using RNN, LSTM; differentiate with real-time reordering examples.*\n**Session 10 📘:** \"Classical policies, deep reinforcement learning, and the one-step paradigm, with the guardrails the evidence demands.\" It was delivered experientially through the **Command Your Agent** game; the deck is the self-study reference.\n**Case: Krishna AgroMachinery — The 20% Claim.**\n\n> **Exam use:** whenever a case says \"AI will cut inventory X%\" or \"the agent will take over ordering\", answer with three tools.\n> (1) **Q vs S**: which stock does it touch?\n> (2) **The benchmark ceiling**: a myopic rule sits within about 5% of optimal, so 20% needs explaining.\n> (3) **The Guardrailed Pilot**: scope, bounds, fallback, evaluation, trust loop, ownership.\n> Learned policies wear proven guardrails.\n\n---\n\n## 0. The inventory basics you must compute (straightforward numericals) ⭐⭐⭐\n| Item | Formula | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **EOQ** | √(2DS/H) | D = annual demand, S = cost per order, H = holding cost per unit per year (= i × unit cost) |\n| Orders per year | D/Q | |\n| Annual ordering cost | (D/Q) × S | equals holding cost at EOQ |\n| Annual holding cost (cycle) | (Q/2) × H | |\n| **Cycle stock** | Q/2 | driven by batch size, **untouched by forecasting** |\n| **Safety stock** | z × σ_d × √L | z: 90% → 1.28, 95% → 1.645, 97.5% → 1.96, 99% → 2.33 |\n| **Reorder point** | d × L + SS | |\n| **Average inventory** | Q/2 + SS | |\n| **Inventory turns** | annual COGS / average inventory value | days of inventory = 365 / turns |\n| **Fill rate** | units shipped on time / units demanded | **weight by volume, not a simple average** |\n| **Newsvendor critical ratio** | Cu/(Cu+Co) | the order is that quantile of demand |\n\n**Worked example.**\n- Inputs: D = 12,000 units, S = ₹1,500 per order, unit cost ₹500, i = 20% so H = ₹100.\n- EOQ = √(2 × 12,000 × 1,500 / 100) = √360,000 = **600 units**.\n- That means 20 orders per year, with ordering cost = holding cost = ₹30,000 each, a total of ₹60,000.\n- With weekly σ = 40, L = 2 weeks and 95% service: SS = 1.645 × 40 × √2 = **93 units**.\n- ROP = (12,000/52) × 2 + 93 ≈ 462 + 93 = **555**.\n- Average inventory = 300 + 93 = **393 units**.\n- If AI forecasting cuts σ by 25%, SS becomes 70, and average inventory falls from 393 to 370, a fall of **5.9%, not 25%**. This is the Q vs S lesson.\n\n## 1. The Inventory Policy Ladder ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n| Generation | Logic | Strengths | Where it strains |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **EOQ (1913)** | batch size from the order-vs-holding cost trade-off | transparent; still right for stable demand | assumes steady demand and static costs |\n| **Basestock / (s,S) + service levels** | order up to a target set by the demand distribution over lead time | decades of theory; auditable; near-optimal when tuned | complex networks, dual sourcing, lost sales |\n| **Stochastic optimisation / MEIO** | multi-echelon optimisation under uncertainty (**700+ Global 2000 deployments**) | network-aware; proven at enterprise scale | static parameters; slow to absorb new signals |\n| **DRL policies** | neural policy learned by acting in a simulator: state → action, for reward | handles complexity no formula covers; millisecond inference | data- and tuning-hungry; **brittle off-regime**; opaque |\n| **One-step (data-to-decision)** | learn the decision FUNCTION from features, constraints and KPIs directly | skips forecast errors; interpretable weights; deployed at Fortune 150 scale | needs the **digital twin + data architecture** beneath it |\n\n**The ladder is cumulative.** The classical rungs supply the **guardrails** (floors, ceilings, safety stock) for the learned ones.\n\n## 2. What deep RL actually learns — in operations language ⭐⭐⭐\n- **The loop:**\n  - **STATE**: stock position, pipeline, demand signals;\n  - **ACTION**: order quantity;\n  - **REWARD**: −(holding + shortage + ordering costs).\n  - It repeats across **simulated years** until the policy maps states to profitable actions.\n- **Q-learning:** the agent learns a quality score **Q(state, action)** for each action in each state. It updates the score with the reward received plus the discounted best future value, then picks the highest-Q action. Learning is **by reward, not labels**. The agent **creates its own data** by trial and error (a famous system reached super-human Go in about 70 hours of self-play).\n- **Why it can win:**\n  - No closed-form policy exists for realistic networks (lost sales + dual sourcing + capacity).\n  - Neural policies approximate what formulas cannot express, and infer in **milliseconds** once trained, which enables **real-time reordering**.\n  - It **optimises the decision directly**: the output is a replenishment policy, not a forecast handed to a separate optimiser, so there are fewer places for error to hide.\n- **Why it can lose:**\n  - **It learns the simulator, not the world.** Mis-specified dynamics, unseen regimes and tuning sensitivity produce the **15–20%-behind pattern**.\n  - Training is expensive, and retraining is a discipline.\n- **The craft frontier:** differentiable simulators for stable training; **reward shaping from known heuristics**; restricting policies to sensible families. In every case, **classical theory steers the learner** (AI–OR symbiosis).\n\n### RNN / LSTM in inventory 📘\nRecurrent networks read **sequences**: demand history, lead-time history and sensor streams. **LSTM** (long short-term memory) gates let them remember long patterns such as seasonality and promotion after-effects. Uses:\n- (a) **sequence demand forecasting** feeding the policy;\n- (b) **end-to-end deep inventory models** (data straight to order quantities, validated in production at a major retailer).\n\nThe caveat is the same as in Ch 7: they need **long history and Data Maturity 3+**, and at SKU-week granularity simple methods stay competitive.\n\n### Real-time reordering and dynamic safety stock 📘\nA learned policy re-evaluates the state continuously (stock, in-transit, live POS, supplier ETA) and triggers orders the moment the position crosses a learned threshold. Safety stock becomes **dynamic**: it recomputes as forecast uncertainty and lead-time variance change, instead of a static \"2 weeks of cover\".\n- **Guard against nervousness:** set a deliberate re-plan cadence (the one-step system re-computed weekly) and use frozen zones.\n\n### Class Q 10.5 — RL beats the classical policy by 18% in simulation ⭐⭐⭐\nThe 18% was measured on two years of stable demand.\n**Answer: B, cage it with a benchmark and guardrails.**\n- Learned policies are brittle on regimes they never trained on (intermittent demand, shocks, promotions).\n- The safeguard is a naive benchmark, **classical guardrails outside the policy** and a human escalation path, not a bigger model.\n- *Learned policies wear proven guardrails.*\n\n## 3. One-step planning — where Module 2 meets Module 3 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **The diagnosis (S8):** firms hold multiple distorted forecasts, then convert them to stock with crude rules, so **accuracy gains die downstream**.\n- **The repair:** learn the **DECISION function** directly.\n  - Stocking levels are computed as a learned, weighted function of **demand features** (sales history, promotions, installed base) and **supply features** (lead times, capacity).\n  - It is trained so the decisions optimise **KPIs** (fill rate, profit) under real constraints.\n  - The standalone forecast **disappears**, and each signal is priced by its contribution to the DECISION (the zero-weight customer forecasts).\n- **Deployed results (Fortune 150, spare parts):**\n  - fill rate **~77% → ~85% at constant inventory**, or **~$20M inventory released at constant service**;\n  - **interpretable weights** let planners see exactly why each recommendation moved.\n  - **\"The frontier shifted, not just a point on it.\"**\n- **The architecture beneath it:** the digital twin and end-to-end data layer (S2, S13).\n- **1 step:** data → decision replaces predict-then-optimise's error-compounding two steps.\n\n### Class Q 10.3 — forecast +15% or a better policy? ⭐⭐⭐\n- (a) Improve forecast accuracy by 15% and keep the weeks-of-supply rule.\n- (b) Keep the forecasts and replace the rule with a policy optimised on the demand distribution and costs.\n\n**Answer: D, (b). The conversion step is where value dies.**\n- The failure chain (multiple forecasts → crude rules) wastes accuracy before it reaches the shelf.\n- Zero-heavy demand limits forecastability anyway.\n- *Optimise the policy, not the forecast.*\n\n## 4. The evidence — both sides of the ledger ⭐⭐⭐\n### The gold standard: randomised trials\n- A **major global e-commerce player** trained a deep policy on its own simulator (\"**direct backpropagation through the simulator**\"). It reported substantial inventory-cost reductions **in randomised evaluation**, and the approach now underpins production replenishment.\n- A published **field study at a large Asian marketplace** (Alibaba) ran automated ML replenishment **vs experienced human buyers**: better in-stock rates and turnover on randomised categories.\n- A **third large retailer** published an **end-to-end deep model** (data straight to order quantities) in a top management journal, validated in production.\n- **Common architecture:** learned policy inside, **hard bounds and fallback rules outside**, and **EVALUATION BY EXPERIMENT, not backtest theatre**.\n\n### The sobering record\n- **The 15–20% surprise:** freshly trained DRL models often run **15–20% BEHIND a simple myopic policy**, which itself sits **within about 5% of optimal**.\n- **Two stalled pilots:** two established firms' DRL pilots died before production. The teams could not **interpret outputs, navigate convergence or manage retraining**; the barrier was missing deep technical expertise, not the AI (the notebook-to-production cliff, at policy scale).\n- **Enterprise inventory optimisation's long game:** 700+ Global 2000 deployments, so the bar any learner must clear is \"high, tuned and trusted\".\n- **Zillow's shadow:** a learned model writing irreversible purchases without proportionate guardrails carries existential tail risk. Inventory cheques are smaller, but the architecture lesson is identical.\n\n### Class Q 10.1 — the vendor's 20% backtest ⭐⭐⭐\nThe incumbent is a tuned basestock policy, and a myopic rule is known to sit within about 5% of optimal.\n**Answer: B, likely overfitting or a weak baseline.**\n- If the incumbent is within about 5% of optimal, **no policy can beat it by 20%**: the maximum possible gain is about 5%.\n- Ask what the incumbent in the test actually was, on whose data, and with what holdout.\n- *Benchmarks with a theory-informed ceiling.*\n\n**Numeric framing.** Incumbent cost = 100 and optimal ≥ 100/1.05 ≈ 95.2, so the **maximum saving is about 4.8%**. A \"20% saving\" implies cost 80, which is below optimal and impossible against a properly tuned incumbent. So the backtest compared against a weaker baseline, or overfitted.\n\n## 5. Explainability vs optimality — when the better answer loses ⭐⭐\n- A **1990s black-box supply-chain solver** gave objectively better schedules, but planners accountable for results could not understand or fix odd answers.\n- A **constraint-based, steepest-descent \"clean-up\" solver** was widely preferred, even though its solutions were **provably less optimal**.\n- **Lesson:** when a human must own the decision, **a transparent, slightly-worse answer can beat an opaque optimum**. Optimise the policy, but keep it legible: explainable guardrails and a documented reason for each recommendation.\n- **Automated vs intuitive:** automate the routine reorder where data is rich. Keep human judgement for **the shock, the new SKU and the regime the model never saw**.\n\n## 6. The four hard problems of learned inventory ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Intermittent demand.** Spare parts sell zero most weeks: long strings of zeros punctuated by lumps. Point forecasts are near-meaningless. The workable routes are **distributions** and **pooled/global signals (installed base)**.\n2. **Non-stationarity.** Lifecycles, promotions and shocks mean a policy trained on one regime degrades silently in the next. Adaptive retraining helps; monitoring and fallbacks are mandatory.\n3. **Delayed, entangled feedback.** Today's order shows its consequences weeks later, entangled with lead times. Learning signals are slow and noisy, so simulators carry the load and **sim-to-real gaps bite**.\n4. **Black-box trust.** A planner asked to follow unexplainable order quantities re-runs **Continental Parts**: aversion, overrides, shadow spreadsheets. **Interpretability and bounded override are deployment requirements.**\n\n### Class Q 10.2 — MAPE on 0,0,0,4,0,0,2,0 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Answer: A, MAPE is undefined on zero actuals** (you divide by zero).\n- **Replace it with** calibrated demand distributions over the lead time, and the **decision frontier: service level achieved per rupee of inventory**.\n- Alternatives are MASE or pinball loss.\n- *Metrics follow the decision.*\n\n**Numeric aside.** That series has a mean of 6/8 = 0.75 per week; 6 of 8 weeks are zeros (75% zero), so the average demand interval is 8/2 = 4 weeks. Croston-type or distributional methods suit this; MAPE does not.\n\n### Class Q 10.4 — \"shadow 4 weeks, then full control\" ⭐⭐⭐\n**Answer: B, bounds, fallback and a real evaluation are missing.**\n- Intermittent demand makes 4 weeks **statistically nearly empty** (e.g. 2 demand events).\n- Shadow accuracy is not decision value.\n- Unbounded \"full control\" ignores the stalled-pilot record.\n- The leaders ran **RCTs inside guardrails**.\n\n## 7. The Guardrailed Pilot — six design elements ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n| # | Element | What it means |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Scope** | one category, honest volume, **long enough for intermittent statistics (quarters, not weeks)** |\n| 2 | **Bounds** | basestock **floors and ceilings from the trusted incumbent**; spend envelopes; no unbounded orders |\n| 3 | **Fallback** | **drift and anomaly triggers that auto-revert** to the incumbent policy, **tested before go-live** |\n| 4 | **Evaluation** | **randomised or matched-control** vs incumbent AND naive benchmark; decision-frontier metrics (**service per rupee**) |\n| 5 | **Trust loop** | interpretable recommendations; **bounded planner override with reason capture** feeding retraining |\n| 6 | **Ownership** | named **expert owner** for the policy; named **business owner** for outcomes; review cadence with a **widen / narrow / revert** decision |\n\n\"This design IS the honest answer to every '20% better' pitch: **fund the pilot, on these terms**.\"\n\n## 8. Krishna AgroMachinery — the case resolved ⭐⭐⭐\n**Facts:**\n- The vendor claims DRL cuts spare-parts inventory by 20% vs basestock, \"as proven in our backtest\".\n- Most SKUs sell zero most weeks.\n- The incumbent basestock policy is tuned over years and hits **89% fill**.\n- Planners override about **30%** of recommendations.\n\n**Model answer (numbered assumptions first):**\n1. *Assume* the vendor's backtest used Krishna's history with no holdout disclosed.\n2. *Assume* the incumbent is near the myopic-optimal frontier (within about 5%).\n3. *Assume* overrides are logged with reasons.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- The 20% exceeds the theoretical headroom of about 5%, so it is likely overfitting or a weak baseline.\n- Intermittent demand weakens learning from history (few events per SKU) and breaks MAPE.\n- The 30% override rate is **information to mine, not a problem to eliminate**. Overrides carry planner knowledge (field campaigns, dealer intel); capture the reasons and feed them back.\n\n**Decision:** do **not** fund as pitched. **Fund a guardrailed pilot**:\n- **Scope:** one high-volume parts family for **two quarters**.\n- **Bounds:** the incumbent basestock ±X% as floor and ceiling, plus a spend cap.\n- **Fallback:** auto-revert if fill drops more than 2 points below 89% for 2 weeks, or on a drift trigger.\n- **Evaluation:** matched-control families vs incumbent and naive, measuring fill per rupee of inventory.\n- **Trust:** interpretable recommendations, an override band of ±20% with mandatory reasons.\n- **Owners:** a named policy owner (vendor + internal analyst) and the parts-business head.\n\n**Widen the envelope if,** after two quarters, the frontier has moved (same fill with less inventory, or higher fill at the same inventory) **in the randomised comparison**, and overrides have fallen because trust grew, not because overrides were banned.\n\n**Alternative worth naming:** the bigger win may be replacing weeks-of-supply rules with **distribution-based basestock using installed-base signals** (Q 10.3), which is cheaper and auditable.\n\n## 9. Chain Reaction link ⭐⭐\n- Inventory and replenishment agents follow the same rule: build to the **foundation gate**.\n- Pick L2 (recommend) before L3 (bounded act).\n- Distribution dials (**distCover, distSafety**) are the in-game equivalents of cycle cover and safety stock. **Raising distSafety buys service with working capital**, and that cost must be visible.\n- **Command Your Agent** (the S10 game) taught granting autonomy only with bounds and a revert path. See the [Chain Reaction playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook).\n\n## Key takeaways (S10 recap 📘)\n1. The evidence ceiling is real (randomised-trial wins at the leaders) and so is the floor (15–20% behind a simple policy). **Engineering discipline, not algorithm choice, separates them.**\n2. Intermittent demand breaks point thinking: **distributions over lead time and upstream signals (installed base)** are the route for spare parts.\n3. The policy ladder is cumulative (EOQ → basestock → MEIO → DRL → one-step), and **classical rungs supply the guardrails** for learned ones.\n4. **Optimise the policy, not the forecast.** One-step shifted the service-inventory frontier (77 → 85% fill).\n5. The **guardrailed pilot** is your permanent answer to every learned-policy pitch.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. D = 24,000, S = ₹2,000, H = ₹60. Find EOQ, orders per year and total cost.**\nEOQ = √(2 × 24,000 × 2,000 / 60) = √1,600,000 ≈ **1,265**. Orders per year ≈ 19. Ordering cost ≈ holding cost ≈ ₹37,950 each, about **₹75,900** in total.\n\n**Q2. A vendor says its RL agent beats your tuned (s,S) policy by 25% in backtest. What is your first question, and why?**\n\"What exactly was the baseline, on whose data, with what holdout?\" A tuned myopic or basestock policy sits within about 5% of optimal, so 25% is beyond the headroom. It signals a weak baseline or overfitting.\n\n**Q3. Why is a 30% planner override rate information, not a problem?**\nOverrides encode knowledge the model lacks (dealer campaigns, field failures). Capture the reasons, check which overrides were right, and feed them into retraining. Banning overrides destroys trust and the learning signal.\n\n**Q4. Name the six elements of a guardrailed pilot.**\nScope, bounds, fallback, evaluation, trust loop, ownership.\n\n**Q5. Explain \"learned policies wear proven guardrails\" with an example.**\nA DRL replenishment policy chooses quantities, but floors and ceilings from the incumbent basestock, spend caps, drift-triggered auto-revert and human escalation sit outside it. Guardrails live outside the model.\n\n**Q6. Why can a transparent but slightly worse solver beat an optimal black box?**\nThe planner who owns the outcome must understand and correct odd answers. Without legibility they override everything or keep shadow spreadsheets, so the \"optimal\" plan is never executed (the 1990s solver and Continental Parts).\n\n**Q7. Average inventory is 1,000 units, of which SS = 250. AI cuts forecast σ by 40%. What is the new average inventory?**\nSS falls to 150, so average inventory = 750 + 150 = **900** (−10%). The cycle stock of 750 is unchanged.\n"}, {"slug": "ch09-predictive-maintenance-digital-twins", "label": "Maintenance & Digital Twins", "tag": "09", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 9 — Predictive Maintenance & Digital Twins (Session 13)", "md": "# Ch 9 — Predictive Maintenance & Digital Twins (Session 13)\n**Syllabus unit 5 📘:** *Self-adaptive Maintenance using Agentic AI — predictive & prescriptive ML examples using TensorFlow; link with digital twin platforms.*\n**Session 13 📘:** \"From Vibration to Verdict.\" Sensor sequences lead to failure prediction, then prescriptive action, then **fidelity-for-purpose** digital twins. It led into **Chain Reaction Round 3: Aftermarket & Maintenance**.\n\n> **Exam use:** two tests decide every maintenance or twin question.\n> (1) **Does the prediction change a decision, or its timing?** If not, it is informationally idle.\n> (2) **Twin-Now / Twin-Later**: decision-changing, failure cost, data readiness, fidelity needed, then a verdict.\n> Sensors and twins are **infrastructure bets made before the model proves its value**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Sensors are just another sequence ⭐⭐⭐\n- **One model, any sequence.** A sequence model trained on demand history can, with **no change to its architecture**, read vibration, temperature or pressure instead, and forecast \"how many days until the machine breaks\".\n- **Telemetry is a time series:** vibration, bearing temperature, oil particulate counts, sampled every few seconds instead of every week.\n- **The target is Remaining Useful Life (RUL),** not \"will it fail?\" but \"**how many hours are left?**\". A binary alarm gives no room to plan; RUL gives a window to schedule work and position parts.\n- **The real gate is infrastructure, not model sophistication.** Demand data already lives in your ERP. Sensor data needs **installing, wiring and streaming**, a cost you take on **before the model helps at all**.\n- **Tools 📘:** TensorFlow/Keras LSTM or 1D-CNN models on sliding windows of sensor readings; survival models; anomaly detection (unsupervised) where failures are rare and unlabelled.\n\n### The maintenance maturity ladder 📗\n| Type | Trigger | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Reactive (run-to-failure)** | breakdown | no planning cost | unplanned downtime, safety risk, expediting |\n| **Preventive (time-based)** | calendar or run-hours | simple, plannable | replaces healthy parts; misses early failures |\n| **Condition-based** | threshold on a sensor (vibration > x) | acts on the actual state | late warning, single-signal |\n| **Predictive** | ML-predicted RUL / failure probability | plans ahead, fewer surprises | needs sensors, data, labels (failures are rare) |\n| **Prescriptive** | predicted failure → **recommended action** (when, which part, which crew, where to stock the part) | closes the loop to the decision | needs optimisation and a twin; an autonomy question |\n\n### Class Q 3.1 (S13) — the 68% bearing flag ⭐⭐⭐\nThe model says 68% failure probability within 2 weeks. The protocol already **inspects every bearing weekly and replaces any showing wear**.\n**Answer: B, no. The weekly inspection already catches this, so the prediction doesn't change the decision.**\n- *A prediction earns its keep only when it changes what you'd decide, and when, not just what you'd know.*\n- This is also the first step of the Twin test.\n\n## 2. From forecast to fix, from sensor to simulation ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Predicting RUL is half the job.** What matters is **when to schedule maintenance and where to pre-position the part**, a **scheduling and sourcing problem**, not a forecasting one.\n- **Predict-then-optimise vs decision-focused:**\n  - Predict-then-optimise forecasts RUL, then hands it to a separate scheduler.\n  - **Decision-focused** trains the prediction with the scheduling cost built in. Errors that would cause a missed failure (costly) are weighted more than errors causing an early swap.\n- **Digital twins make \"then what?\" concrete.** A twin is a **live virtual replica** fed by real data, on which you test an intervention **before committing in the real world** (change a schedule, reroute a line, alter a network).\n- **Fidelity is the judgement call.** A twin only needs to answer **the ONE question you're asking**; a full physics model of every bolt is usually overkill. Build to **fidelity-for-purpose, not maximum realism**.\n\n### Kinds of digital twin 📗\n- **Asset twin:** one machine; RUL and what-ifs.\n- **Process / line twin:** throughput and bottlenecks.\n- **Network / supply-chain twin:** plants, DCs, lanes. Used for network design and disruption what-ifs, and it sits beneath the one-step paradigm (Ch 8).\n\n## 3. Practice — collapsing the gap between knowing and acting ⭐⭐⭐\n- An **IoT-triggered spare-parts network 3D-prints the specific part predicted to fail**, based on sensor data. The gap from \"it's about to break\" to \"the part is on site\" shrinks **from weeks to days**.\n- **Siemens** runs digital twins across its own factories and customers' lines, simulating changes **before touching physical equipment**.\n- **ASCM's 2026 research** named digital twins **the primary enabler of supply-chain resilience**.\n- **The payoff is the time collapse**, not the sensor or the model alone. Value = (downtime avoided + parts expediting avoided + inventory of slow spares reduced) − (instrumentation + data + model + process change).\n\n**Inventory link (Ch 7–8).** Installed-base and utilisation signals from the same sensors drove the **77 → 85% fill** in the 50,000-SKU spare-parts deployment. **Predictive maintenance is also a demand signal for spares.**\n\n## 4. The Twin-Now / Twin-Later Test ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n| # | Step | Question |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Decision-changing** | Would a better prediction actually change what you'd do, or when? **If no, stop.** |\n| 2 | **Failure cost** | Is the downtime, safety or warranty cost of this failure mode high enough to justify the spend? |\n| 3 | **Data readiness** | Do you already have the sensor stream, or are you building instrumentation from zero? |\n| 4 | **Fidelity needed** | Can a **lightweight simulation of just the relevant subsystem** answer your what-if? |\n| 5 | **Verdict** | **Twin-now** (build it), **twin-later** (park it), or a **simulation-lite substitute**. |\n\n### Worked application (exam style)\nA bottling line's filler motor fails about twice a year. Each failure costs 18 hours of downtime at ₹1.5 L/hour contribution, plus ₹4 L of emergency parts and labour. The current protocol is a monthly visual check. Instrumentation, platform and model would cost ₹22 L once plus ₹5 L/year.\n\n1. *Assumptions:* failure frequency stays at 2/yr; prediction catches 70% of failures with enough lead to schedule repair in a planned 4-hour window; the planned repair costs ₹1 L of parts.\n2. **Cost per failure today** = 18 × 1.5 + 4 = **₹31 L**, so ₹62 L/yr.\n3. **With PdM:** caught failures (1.4/yr) cost 4 × 1.5 + 1 = ₹7 L each = ₹9.8 L. Missed failures (0.6/yr) cost ₹31 L each = ₹18.6 L. The total is **₹28.4 L/yr**.\n4. **Saving** = 62 − 28.4 = ₹33.6 L/yr; net of running cost it is **₹28.6 L/yr**, so the **payback is ≈ 0.8 year** on ₹22 L.\n5. Step 1 passes, because the monthly visual check would not catch the failure in time. Step 2 passes (high cost). Step 3: data has to be built. Step 4: an asset-level model suffices; no full line twin is needed.\n6. **Verdict: twin-now at asset level (simulation-lite),** with a 6-month review of catch rate, and keep the monthly check as a fallback.\n\n## 5. Common traps ⭐⭐\n- **Predicting what you already catch** (the bearing question): an idle prediction.\n- **Rare failures, few labels:** use anomaly detection, physics-informed features, pooled fleets, and judge by **catch rate at a given false-alarm rate** (precision/recall), not accuracy (99% \"no failure\" is trivial).\n- **False alarms erode trust.** Too many early swaps and the technicians ignore the alerts. Set the threshold by the **cost ratio** of a missed failure to an unnecessary swap.\n- **Twin gold-plating:** a full physics model when a queue simulation of one subsystem answers the question.\n- **Sensors last:** buying the model before wiring the data stream.\n- **No action path:** an alert without a scheduled slot, a crew or a part on hand. Prescriptive means the part and the slot are lined up.\n\n**Threshold numeric.** A missed failure costs ₹31 L and an unnecessary early swap costs ₹1.5 L. Act when p(failure) > Co/(Co + Cu) = 1.5/(1.5 + 31) ≈ **4.6%**. The 68% bearing flag would clear that bar, but the decision is already covered by the weekly inspection, so it still doesn't change anything. **Test 1 comes before the cost ratio.**\n\n## 6. Chain Reaction link — Round 3: Aftermarket & Maintenance ⭐⭐⭐\n- Your telemetry investment only pays off if it changes a real decision downstream, exactly like the bearing question.\n- Two catalogue agents carry it:\n  - **PRD_MAINT_2/3**: Predictive & Prescriptive Maintenance (production family).\n  - **DIS_NET_2/3**: Network Design & Digital Twin (distribution family).\n- **Build the L2 specialist first; L3 only as an upgrade on top of it.**\n- Check the **foundation gate (D·P·T)** before building. A below-gate build runs at full cost for a fraction of the effect. See the [Chain Reaction playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook).\n\n## Key takeaways (S13 recap 📘)\n1. Sequence models don't care what the sequence is: demand and vibration are the same problem to the network.\n2. RUL prediction only pays off when it **changes a scheduling or sourcing decision**, not just your knowledge.\n3. Digital twins should be built to **fidelity-for-purpose**, not maximum realism.\n4. Sensors and twins are **infrastructure bets made before the model proves its value**, not after.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. Why is RUL a better target than a binary \"will fail\" alarm?**\nRUL gives a time window to schedule the repair, book the crew and pre-position the part. A binary alarm says only \"soon\", which leaves no room to plan.\n\n**Q2. A plant replaces every pump seal on a fixed 6-month cycle and has had no seal failures in 5 years. A vendor offers PdM for the seals. Run Twin test step 1–2.**\nStep 1: would a prediction change the decision? Possibly yes, if it extended seal life and cut parts cost. Step 2: the failure cost is currently near zero (no failures), so the value lies only in parts and labour saved from longer intervals. It is probably **twin-later**, unless seal costs are material.\n\n**Q3. What does \"fidelity-for-purpose\" mean? Give an example.**\nModel only what the question needs. To decide whether to add a second filler, a queue/throughput simulation of that line is enough. A bolt-level physics twin is overkill.\n\n**Q4. A missed failure costs ₹20 L and an early swap ₹2 L. Above what predicted failure probability should you act?**\nAct when p > 2/(2 + 20) ≈ **9.1%**.\n\n**Q5. How does predictive maintenance connect to inventory planning?**\nPredicted failures and installed-base utilisation are leading demand signals for spare parts: pre-position the predicted part, cut emergency freight and safety stock, and raise fill (the 77 → 85% deployment, IoT-triggered 3D printing).\n\n**Q6. Why are sensors \"an infrastructure bet made before the model proves its value\"?**\nUnlike demand data in the ERP, sensor data does not exist until you install, wire and stream it. The cost is sunk before any model is trained, so step 3 of the Twin test (data readiness) often decides the timing.\n"}, {"slug": "ch10-logistics-routing-optimisation", "label": "Logistics & Optimisation", "tag": "10", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 10 — Logistics, Routing & Optimisation: The Mandate Is the Lesson (Sessions 14–15)", "md": "# Ch 10 — Logistics, Routing & Optimisation: The Mandate Is the Lesson (Sessions 14–15)\n**Syllabus unit 6 📘:** *AI in Logistics: Route Optimization, Vehicle Scheduling — tools: Google OR-Tools, OptaPlanner, Q-learning-based routing.*\n**Session 14 📘:** \"Routing, Rerouting, Reality: Why a Network Needs a Brain.\" It led into **Round 4: The Network Opens**.\n**Session 15 📘:** \"Optimization in Practice: The Mandate Is the Lesson.\" It covered min-cost flow and the AI–OR symbiosis, and led into **Round 5: Setting the Mandate**.\n\n> **Exam use:** this is the heart of the **Kaveri Motors** paper and of the Chain Reaction emphasis. Four habits answer almost every logistics question.\n> (1) The decision object is the **mode mix under capacity**, not \"the best route\".\n> (2) Under a shock, **split the shipment** (urgent part fast, bulk cheap).\n> (3) The solver does exactly what you tell it: **strategy lives in the objective function** (service floors, cost weights).\n> (4) **Agents operate optimisers, they don't replace them**: write the **Mandate Card**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The network in the game (and in Kaveri) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **3 plants → 4 RDCs → 7 dealer zones**, with three products (HATCH, SUV, EV) and three transport modes.\n- There are more feasible flow patterns than any planner could compare by hand in time, so **nobody hands the network a lane-by-lane plan**. You set **coverage, safety stock, front-loading and rail eligibility**, and the engine builds the flows.\n\n| Mode | Cost | Speed | Constraint | Use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Rail** | cheapest (**≈25% below road** in the game) | slow (multi-day lead) | **slot-constrained; booked a month ahead; charged whether used or not** | stable, predictable base volume |\n| **Road** | mid | flexible | lane capacity (shocks cut it) | **the default workhorse; the lane the engine auto-pushes** |\n| **Trailer-express** | most expensive | fastest | capacity-limited | urgent, high-value, stockout-risk volume |\n\n**Distribution dials:**\n- **distCover:** cover target, i.e. how many days or weeks of demand to position at the RDCs and zones.\n- **distSafety:** the safety buffer on top of cover.\n- **distFront:** **SPREAD** distributes evenly; **FRONT** front-loads the stock early.\n- **distMode:** **ROAD** (all road), or **MIX** (rail in the mix). **MIX stays locked until tech maturity 3.**\n\n## 2. What AI adds to moving things ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Classical dispatch optimises a snapshot:** fixed lead times, average demand, one route plan. It is followed until it visibly breaks, with **no correction mid-transit**.\n- **AI-driven logistics:**\n  - **re-routes dynamically**;\n  - **predicts delays early** (ETA models on traffic, weather, port congestion);\n  - lets **fleets self-coordinate locally**.\n  - It changes the **speed and granularity** of the mode trade-off (every lane, every hour, not weekly), and **it matters most when something breaks**.\n- **Every shipment is still a mode choice under capacity and cost tension.**\n\n### Class Q 4.1 (S14) — the capacity shock ⭐⭐⭐\nThe situation:\n- road lane capacity is −40%;\n- West RDC has full HATCH stock;\n- the Mumbai zone is about to stock out;\n- trailer-express is open but costly;\n- rail has slots but a 2-day lead time you don't have.\n\n**Answer: B, split the shipment.** Send part via trailer-express now and the remainder via rail as backfill.\n- The urgent portion protects service where the stockout risk is highest.\n- The bulk of the cost shifts to the cheaper, slower mode.\n- *Under a capacity shock the right answer is rarely \"pick one mode\"; it is rebalancing the mix in real time.*\n\n**Numeric version (exam style).** Mumbai needs 300 cars. 120 are needed within 2 days (stockout risk) and the other 180 can wait 5 days.\n- Express costs ₹9,000 per car, road ₹4,000 and rail ₹3,000.\n- Road capacity has fallen to 60% of its normal 200, i.e. 120 cars.\n- All by express = 300 × 9,000 = **₹27 L**.\n- Split: 120 by express = ₹10.8 L, plus 180 by rail = ₹5.4 L, so **₹16.2 L**, saving **₹10.8 L (40%)** with no stockout.\n- Alternatively, 120 by road (if road can reach in 2 days) = ₹4.8 L, plus 180 by rail = ₹5.4 L, so **₹10.2 L**.\n- Always check timing feasibility against each mode's lead time.\n\n## 3. Agents operate the model, they don't replace it ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Core course idea:** agents don't think instead of you. They **operate optimisation models on your behalf**, at a speed and scale you can't match by hand.\n- The flow-optimisation model solves the plants → RDCs → zones allocation in seconds. It needs **two things only a human should set**:\n  - **service targets** (which zones can't stock out);\n  - **cost weights** (transport cost vs a rupee of lost sales).\n- **You set policy; the engine executes at scale.** By hand it is combinatorial, error-prone work that humans do badly under pressure.\n\n### Practice: JD.com's dual engine and Amazon DeepFleet ⭐⭐⭐\n- **JD.com:**\n  - An **AI sensing layer** continuously senses demand and network conditions.\n  - It sits on top of a **classical optimisation engine** that solves routing and inventory placement across the national network.\n  - **The AI decides when and how to re-trigger the optimiser** and translates shifting priorities into its objective; the optimiser does the constrained math.\n  - \"Keep the AI pointed at the right target, and make sure the AI itself has the right target.\"\n- **Amazon DeepFleet:** thousands of warehouse robots **re-route around each other in real time**, inside a physical and algorithmic **envelope**, not a fixed central schedule. This is self-coordination.\n\n## 4. What a min-cost-flow optimiser actually does ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Nodes** are plants, RDCs and zones. **Edges** are lanes, each with a **capacity** and a **cost per unit**.\n- The optimiser finds the **lowest-cost flow on every edge that meets demand, given the constraints**.\n- In LP form:\n  - **Minimise Σ cᵢⱼ xᵢⱼ**\n  - subject to flow balance at each node (supply out = flow in, RDC in = out + stock change, zone in ≥ demand or the service floor);\n  - capacities **xᵢⱼ ≤ uᵢⱼ**;\n  - **xᵢⱼ ≥ 0**.\n- Lost sales can be modelled as a costly \"dummy lane\" whose **cost weight = margin lost per car**. That is how the cost weight decides whether the solver protects a zone.\n- **The solver doesn't get tired or anchor, but it can't decide whether a zone deserves 98% or 90% service.** That call is yours.\n\n**Worked mini-example.** Plant P (supply 500) serves zones Z1 (demand 300, premium margin ₹1.2 L/car) and Z2 (demand 300, margin ₹0.4 L/car).\n- Lane costs: P→Z1 rail ₹3k (capacity 150) and road ₹4k (capacity 200); P→Z2 rail ₹3k (capacity 300).\n- **Pure cost-minimising** with zero shortage penalty ships the cheapest lanes first: rail 300 to Z2 and rail 150 to Z1, i.e. 450 cars. Z1 gets 150 of 300 (**50% fill**) and the 50 spare go wherever.\n- Add a **shortage cost weight = margin** (₹1.2 L for Z1, ₹0.4 L for Z2). The solver now fills Z1 fully (rail 150 + road 150) and sends the remaining 200 to Z2.\n- The extra transport cost is about ₹0.15 L for the road leg vs ₹1.2 L × 150 = **₹180 L** of premium margin protected.\n- **Strategy lives in the objective function.**\n\n### Class Q 5.1 (S15) — identical cost weights, the premium zone starved ⭐⭐⭐\nEvery zone was given the same cost weight. The solver routes nearly everything by rail, and fill slips in the zone that drives **40% of premium-margin sales**. The plan was approved without override.\n**Answer: B, set a higher cost weight (or a service floor) for the high-value zone before solving.**\n- The solver did exactly what it was told. The output is **technically optimal and strategically wrong**.\n- The fix belongs **upstream, in the objective and constraints**, not in lane-by-lane second-guessing (C).\n- *\"A perfectly solved LP with the wrong mandate produces a perfectly wrong plan.\"*\n\n## 5. The AI–OR symbiosis ⭐⭐⭐\n- **A classical solver is static.** Give it a network, objective and constraints, and it returns one answer for one snapshot.\n- **An agent wrapped around the solver adds the judgement layer:**\n  - it watches for **re-solve triggers**;\n  - it **translates goals** (\"protect service in our top-margin zone\") into **LP weights and constraints**;\n  - it decides what happens to the plan: the **L2 / L3 / L4** call between human review and autonomous execution.\n- **None of this replaces the solver's math.** What changes is *who decides when to invoke it, what to optimise for and how much autonomy it gets*.\n- **Hand-planning fails at scale because of combinatorics, not because humans can't do math.**\n\n## 6. Tools named in the syllabus 📘\n| Tool | What it is | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Google OR-Tools** | open-source optimisation suite (LP/MIP, CP-SAT constraint solver, **vehicle routing (VRP) solver**, network-flow algorithms including min-cost flow) | vehicle routing with time windows and capacities, min-cost-flow allocation, scheduling |\n| **OptaPlanner** (now Timefold) | open-source Java **constraint-satisfaction / planning engine** using metaheuristics (tabu search, simulated annealing, late acceptance) with hard and soft constraint scores | shift rostering, vehicle routing, job-shop scheduling, where **hard constraints must hold and soft ones are traded** |\n| **Q-learning for routing** | RL agent learns Q(state = current node and conditions, action = next hop) from the reward (−travel time, −cost); adapts as congestion changes | dynamic rerouting in changing networks (packet routing, fleet dispatch), warehouse robots |\n| **LP/MIP solvers** | exact optimisers behind min-cost flow | network allocation (Chain Reaction's engine) |\n\n**VRP in one line:** visit all customers with a fleet of capacity-limited vehicles, minimising distance or cost, subject to time windows. It is **NP-hard**, so solvers use heuristics plus local search. OR-Tools and OptaPlanner are the two names the syllabus wants you to recognise.\n\n**Q-learning update:**\n**Q(s,a) ← Q(s,a) + α [r + γ · maxₐ′ Q(s′,a′) − Q(s,a)]**\n- α is the learning rate and γ the discount.\n- Mini numeric: Q(s,a) = 10, α = 0.5, r = −4, γ = 0.9, max Q(s′) = 12 → new Q = 10 + 0.5 × (−4 + 10.8 − 10) = 10 + 0.5 × (−3.2) = **8.4**.\n\n## 7. The Mandate Card ⭐⭐⭐ (framework, S14)\nSet these before you hand flow to any agent:\n1. **Service floor:** name the zones and segments where a stockout is unacceptable and must be protected first.\n2. **Cost ceiling:** the maximum acceptable cost per unit delivered, by mode.\n3. **Mode priority:** the default cost-vs-speed preference, so the agent has a tiebreaker.\n4. **Shock trigger:** what capacity or demand shift forces a **full re-plan** versus a **local patch**.\n5. **Review cadence:** how often a human re-checks the agent's flow allocation against outcomes.\n\n## 8. The Optimizer Mandate Checklist ⭐⭐⭐ (framework, S15)\n1. **Service targets:** set **per zone**, not one network-wide number. Name which zones can't slip and by how much.\n2. **Cost weights:** reflect **strategic priority, not just unit economics**. A high-margin zone shouldn't get the same weight as a cheap-to-serve one.\n3. **Review rule:** decide up front between **human sign-off (L2)**, **exception-based escalation (L3)** and **fully autonomous (L4)**.\n4. **Re-solve trigger:** name the specific events (cost shock, capacity loss, demand spike) that force a re-plan.\n5. **Verify the fit:** check the returned plan against the service floor **before it executes**. Optimal is not automatically right.\n\n### Applying the mandate to Kaveri Motors (the PYQ) ⭐⭐⭐\nSee the [Kaveri Motors solution](#/pyq-kaveri) for the full answer. In short:\n- Kaveri's loss was **physics, not luck**. Road-only, uniformly weighted flows starved the premium zones under a lane shock, and the congestion charge was the visible cost of an invisible **₹11 cr contribution forgone**.\n- The fix is a **Distribution Planning Agent at L3** under a written mandate:\n  - service floors by zone (zone fill ≥ 60–75%);\n  - a cash constraint;\n  - a **rail floor (MIX)** once tech maturity allows;\n  - re-solve triggers (>20% lane capacity loss, zone fill < 60%, expedite > ₹20 L);\n  - a CSCO owner and a monthly review.\n\n## 9. Chain Reaction link — Rounds 4 & 5 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **R4, the network opens:** you do not fill a single flow cell. Distribution is set by **distCover, distSafety, distFront (SPREAD/FRONT) and distMode (ROAD/MIX; MIX locked until tech maturity 3)**, and the engine generates the plan. A basic network agent becomes deployable.\n- **R5, setting the mandate:** the four dials **are** the objective and the policy the solver executes. Set them, read the generated plan back, and see whether your mandate survives contact with the month.\n- **Winning moves:**\n  - raise tech maturity early so **MIX (rail)** unlocks and cost falls by about 25% on base volume;\n  - book rail for **predictable base load only**, because unused slots are still charged;\n  - keep road and express for the variable tail;\n  - use **FRONT** before known peaks and **SPREAD** in steady months;\n  - set **distSafety** by zone value, not uniformly.\n  - See the [Chain Reaction playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook).\n\n## Key takeaways (S14–S15 recaps 📘)\n1. AI in logistics wins by **re-evaluating mode and route trade-offs continuously**, not by picking one smart route.\n2. The real decision object is the **mode mix under capacity** (rail, road, trailer-express), not a single \"best path\".\n3. Agents **operate** optimisation models; they don't replace the human-set mandate of service targets and cost weights.\n4. A min-cost-flow LP finds the best flow **for the objective you gave it**, nothing more and nothing less.\n5. Hand-planning fails at scale because of **combinatorics**, not maths ability.\n6. **The mandate (service targets, cost weights, review rule, re-solve trigger) is the real decision.** AI operates OR: it decides when to re-solve and what to optimise for.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. \"The optimiser produced the lowest-cost plan, so it must be right.\" Respond.**\nLowest cost **for the objective given**. With identical cost weights and no service floors, the solver will happily starve a premium zone. Check the plan against service floors, and put zone priority into the cost weights or constraints before solving.\n\n**Q2. Write the five items of a Mandate Card for a 3-RDC FMCG network entering the festival season.**\n- Service floor: metro zones ≥ 97% fill.\n- Cost ceiling: ₹/case by mode.\n- Mode priority: road default, express only for stockout-risk SKUs.\n- Shock trigger: >20% lane capacity loss or a demand spike >30% → full re-plan; otherwise local patch.\n- Review: weekly human review of fill by zone vs cost.\n\n**Q3. Why is rail \"charged whether used or not\" a design consideration?**\nRail is a commitment booked a month ahead. Over-booking turns into fixed cost for empty slots, so book rail for the predictable base load and leave the variable tail on road or express.\n\n**Q4. Distinguish OR-Tools and OptaPlanner.**\nOR-Tools: Google's suite of LP/MIP, CP-SAT, VRP and network-flow solvers. OptaPlanner: a Java constraint-satisfaction engine using metaheuristics with hard and soft scores, strong in rostering and scheduling. Both are solvers that an agent can operate.\n\n**Q5. What does the agent add on top of the solver (the AI–OR symbiosis)?**\nSensing when to re-solve (triggers), translating business goals into weights and constraints, and governing what happens to the output (L2 review, L3 exceptions, L4 autonomous).\n\n**Q6. In a shock, why split rather than pick one mode?**\nDifferent parts of the order have different urgency and value. Express protects the stockout-risk slice, and rail/road carries the rest cheaply. One mode either overspends (all express) or loses sales (all rail).\n"}, {"slug": "ch11-nlp-procurement-token-economics", "label": "NLP Procurement & Tokens", "tag": "11", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 11 — NLP for Procurement & Token Economics (Sessions 16–17)", "md": "# Ch 11 — NLP for Procurement & Token Economics (Sessions 16–17)\n**Syllabus unit 7 📘:** *NLP for Procurement Automation and Contract Analytics — HuggingFace, OpenAI API for auto-RFx generation, compliance monitoring.*\n**Session 16 📘:** \"NLP for Procurement: Reading the Fine Print at Scale.\" It led into **Round 6: The Chip Contract Negotiation**.\n**Session 17 📘:** \"APIs & Token Economics: The Bill Behind the Brain.\" It led into **Round 7: The Token Economy**.\n\n> **Exam use:** two rules carry this unit.\n> (1) **The accuracy bar scales with what the clause decides.** Liability and penalty clauses get domain-tuned models and a human; contact fields get a general model.\n> (2) **Tune before you retire.** Compare value-per-decision with cost-per-decision, never the size of the bill alone, and enforce **spend caps in code, not in prompts**.\n> The **Trishul** paper's vendor-terms and procurement-agent questions sit here (see [Trishul solution](#/pyq-trishul)).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Three jobs NLP already does in procurement ⭐⭐⭐\n| Job | What it does | Payoff |\n|---|---|---|\n| **RFx automation** | reads incoming bids and requisitions, auto-populates comparison matrices, drafts RFQs/RFPs from requirements (e.g. an **OpenAI API** call generating a structured RFx from a requisition) | \"a week of bid-tabbing becomes an afternoon\" |\n| **Contract clause extraction** | pulls structured fields (price, term length, renewal notice, liability caps, penalty triggers) from unstructured PDFs | risk visibility and renewal alerts across thousands of contracts |\n| **Spend classification** | tags every transaction into a category taxonomy (e.g. UNSPSC) instead of guessing from GL codes | spend cubes, maverick-spend detection, sourcing waves |\n\nFurther uses in the syllabus:\n- **supplier risk monitoring** (news and filings scanned for distress signals);\n- **compliance monitoring** (sanctions lists, ESG clauses, invoices checked against contract terms);\n- **negotiation agents** (Pactum).\n\n**Hugging Face 📘** hosts open transformer models (BERT-family, legal-domain models). You **fine-tune** them on your labelled contract-risk examples (domain grounding) and run them in your own environment, which keeps confidential contracts in-house. **OpenAI API 📘** is a metered general-purpose LLM: fast to prototype auto-RFx drafting and summarisation, billed per token (see §5).\n\n### The 94.7% vs 79.3% gap ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Domain-tuned models reach about 94.7%** accuracy on contract-clause risk classification in published benchmarks.\n- **General-purpose LLMs without domain grounding land around 79.3%.**\n- The **15-point gap** is why \"just point ChatGPT at the contract\" is not a procurement strategy.\n- **Why the gap exists:** clause-risk classification is **a legal-reasoning task wearing language clothes**. A general LLM can summarise a clause fluently and still miss that **indemnification shifted from mutual to one-sided**, because it never trained on enough labelled contract-risk examples.\n\n**Numeric framing.** 10,000 clauses are reviewed, of which 5% (500) are truly risky.\n- At 94.7% accuracy, roughly 530 are misclassified.\n- At 79.3%, about 2,070 are.\n- Even if errors were spread evenly, the general model would miss about 4× more risky clauses.\n\n**Precision/recall framing for risky clauses.** A model flags 600, of which 450 are truly risky, out of 500 risky in total.\n- Recall = 450/500 = **90%**.\n- Precision = 450/600 = **75%**.\n- The 50 missed are the dangerous ones. For liability clauses, **optimise recall and route flags to a human**.\n\n## 2. Class Q 6.1 (S16) — which clause gets the tuning budget? ⭐⭐⭐\nThe options are payment terms, address formatting, **penalty/indemnification** and contact details.\n**Answer: C, penalty and indemnification.**\n- A misread payment term is a scheduling headache: annoying and recoverable.\n- A misread penalty or indemnification clause can put the company on the hook for **liability it never knowingly accepted**, discovered only when the supplier invokes it. That is expensive and sometimes irreversible.\n- *The accuracy bar isn't one number; it scales with what the clause decides.*\n\n## 3. Audit the claim anyway ⭐⭐\n- Domain-tuning a vendor's model means **asking sharper questions, not fewer**.\n- A vendor claiming \"**~40% fewer disruptions**\" is reporting its own number, not one independently audited. You don't know the baseline, the window or how \"disruption\" was defined.\n- Apply the **Four-Question Claim Audit** (Ch 1): what was the baseline, measured how, over what window, and compared with what control?\n\n## 4. Bounded autonomy in production — Walmart × Pactum ⭐⭐⭐\n- Walmart runs a **live negotiation agent built with Pactum**, negotiating directly with **small and mid-tier suppliers** (the long tail no buyer has time for).\n- It negotiates **price, payment terms and volume commitments**, only **inside boundaries a human category manager sets in advance**.\n- The agent can **propose, counter and close within its mandate; it cannot expand that mandate on its own**. That makes it an **L3/L4 bounded-autonomy** pattern.\n- **Manager takeaway:** agents execute thousands of bounded negotiations a human would never have time for, while humans retain control over where the boundaries sit.\n- **EY survey:** about **80% of global CPOs** plan to deploy GenAI within three years, **led by spend analytics and contract management**.\n\n### The Clause-Stakes Specification Checklist ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n1. **List the clauses:** payment terms, renewal windows, liability caps, penalty triggers, termination, indemnification.\n2. **Name the decision** each clause feeds downstream.\n3. **Set the bar** per clause from the **cost of being wrong on that decision**, not one blanket target.\n4. **Pick the tier:** general-purpose extraction is fine for logistics and contact fields; **domain-tuned models for anything touching liability**.\n5. **Name the exception:** flag the one clause type whose interpretation you will **never fully automate**.\n\n**Worked example (Round 6 chip contract).**\n| Clause | Decision fed | Bar | Tier | Human? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Price/volume tiers | cost model, production plan | high | domain-tuned | review |\n| Payment terms | cash plan | medium | general | spot-check |\n| **Allocation in shortage / force majeure** | supply continuity | very high | domain-tuned | **always (the exception)** |\n| **Liability, penalties, indemnity** | financial exposure | very high | domain-tuned | **always** |\n| Delivery address / contacts | logistics admin | low | general | no |\n\n## 5. APIs & token economics — the bill behind the brain ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Software used to be free to run twice.** You bought a licence and extra use cost almost nothing.\n- **Agentic AI is billed on consumption:** per API call, per token in and out, or tiered usage bands that reset monthly. It is **closer to a utility bill than a software purchase**.\n- Cost scales with how much agents **think, read and write**. A verbose agent, or one told to \"double-check its work\", is quietly a more expensive agent (more tokens, more calls).\n- **Prices are volatile.** Vendors cut per-token prices as compute gets cheaper but add premium tiers for stronger models. **A cost model is a snapshot, not a constant.** (Recall the **3.2× token overrun** in the S5 platform case.)\n\n**Token-cost numeric (straightforward).** An RFx agent processes 2,000 bids per month at 6,000 input tokens and 1,500 output tokens per bid. Prices are ₹250 per million input tokens and ₹1,000 per million output tokens.\n- Input: 2,000 × 6,000 = 12 M tokens × ₹250 = **₹3,000**.\n- Output: 2,000 × 1,500 = 3 M tokens × ₹1,000 = **₹3,000**.\n- The total is **₹6,000/month**, or ₹3 per bid.\n- If a \"double-check\" step re-reads each bid (+6,000 input, +500 output), cost rises to 24 M × 250 + 4 M × 1,000 = 6,000 + 4,000 = **₹10,000 (+67%)**.\n- If a premium tier costs 5×, the bill is about ₹50,000. Then compare it with **value per bid**.\n\n### Governing the agent bill ⭐⭐⭐\n- **A runaway agent bill is a governance failure, not a technology failure.**\n- Extend the S4 identity-and-permissions stack to cover **spend**:\n  - every agent gets a **per-agent budget cap enforced in code**, a hard ceiling on tokens or ₹ per run, **checked automatically before the call fires**;\n  - **not** a system-prompt line asking the agent to \"be economical\", which it will ignore under pressure. *Guardrails live outside the model.*\n- **Tune first:** shrink the context, **cache** repeated calls, **batch** calls, drop to a **cheaper model tier**, reduce call frequency. **Retire** only when a well-tuned version still can't clear its value bar.\n- **Design for optionality:** **model-agnostic runtimes and open protocols** (e.g. MCP-style tool interfaces) let you swap models when a vendor's price spikes. \"That optionality is priced insurance, not paranoia.\"\n\n### Class Q 7.1 (S17) — the run cost doubles, quality unchanged ⭐⭐⭐\n**Answer: C, check what the agent's decisions are actually worth, then tune or retire based on value-per-decision vs cost-per-decision.**\n- A doubled bill on a low-stakes, high-volume decision can wreck margin.\n- The same doubled bill on a decision worth 50× its cost barely matters.\n- *A price shock is a signal to re-run the math, not a verdict. Tune before you retire.*\n\n### The Tune-or-Retire Checklist ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n1. **Measure cost:** total run cost (tokens, calls, compute) ÷ the number of decisions the agent actually made.\n2. **Measure value:** the ₹ or margin impact of those decisions over the same window.\n3. **Compare the ratio:** if value per decision still comfortably exceeds cost per decision, no action is needed.\n4. **Tune first:** cut context, cache, batch, downgrade the tier, then re-measure.\n5. **Retire if it still fails:** fall back to the next-best option rather than running an agent at a loss on principle.\n\n**Worked numeric.** A negotiation agent closes 400 deals a month.\n- Its run cost was ₹2 L and is now ₹4 L after repricing, so cost per decision has gone from ₹500 to **₹1,000**.\n- The average saving per deal vs the manual baseline is ₹3,500, so value per decision is ₹3,500.\n- The ratio is 3.5× (it was 7×). The agent **still clears**, so tune it anyway: caching might cut cost by 30% to ₹700 per decision.\n- If the average saving were ₹800, the ratio would be 0.8×. Tune first; if tuned cost cannot fall below about ₹600 (a sensible margin below value), **retire** and fall back to the manual or rule-based process.\n\n## 6. The efficiency-capture gap ⭐⭐⭐\n- **McKinsey:** a chemicals firm piloting agentic procurement saw real **20–30% staff efficiency gains**, but only **1–3% of that translated into direct bottom-line value capture**.\n- **Efficiency is easy to generate; margin on it is thin.** Run cost must be managed as tightly as value created, or the project quietly becomes a wash.\n- **Walmart–Pactum** makes the same point from the other side: a live negotiating agent must continuously justify its run cost against the deal value it closes.\n- **Ask:** not \"did the agent help?\" but \"**did it help by more than it cost, this month, at this price?**\"\n\n**Numeric illustration.** A 50-person procurement team costs ₹10 cr. A 25% efficiency gain is \"worth\" ₹2.5 cr on paper. If only 2% of it is captured (headcount not redeployed, savings not banked), the realised value is about ₹5 L, which a ₹40 L/yr agent bill wipes out. **Capture plan first**: redeploy freed hours to strategic sourcing, and bank negotiated savings in the budget.\n\n## 7. Chain Reaction link — Rounds 6 & 7 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **R6, the chip contract:** your procurement agent negotiates a chip supply contract **under a mandate you set**, much like Walmart–Pactum. Specify the clauses that matter (allocation in shortage, penalties, volume flexibility) **before** it signs.\n- **R7, the token economy:**\n  - **24 decision tokens per month**;\n  - a cap of **4 transformation actions per round**;\n  - **2 tokens to generate an operating plan, 1 to regenerate it**;\n  - every agent charges **1–4 tokens per month in upkeep**; terminating an agent costs 2.\n- Make the tune-or-retire call on **cost-per-decision, not the size of the bill**. Retire low-value agents whose upkeep crowds out plan regeneration, and avoid building agents you can't afford to run. See the [Chain Reaction playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook).\n\n**Token-budget numeric.** You own 4 agents with upkeep 3 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 8 tokens/month, and you generate one plan (2) and regenerate twice (2). That totals 12, leaving **12 of 24** for builds.\n- Retiring the 1-upkeep agent costs 2 now but saves 1 per month, so it **breaks even after 2 months**.\n- Retire it only if its decisions are worth less than about 1 token per month and at least 2 months remain.\n\n## Key takeaways (S16–S17 recaps 📘)\n1. NLP already does three procurement jobs well: **RFx automation, clause extraction, spend classification**.\n2. Domain-tuned models beat general LLMs on contract risk by **about 15 points (94.7 vs 79.3)**. Grounding matters more than fluency.\n3. **Audit every vendor accuracy claim.** \"40% fewer disruptions\" is a claim, not a fact, until checked.\n4. **The accuracy bar scales with what the clause decides.** Penalty and indemnification always get the strictest bar and a human in the loop.\n5. **Consumption pricing** makes the agent bill a live operating cost. Prices swing quarterly, so a cost model is a snapshot.\n6. **Spend caps belong in code**, not prompts. **Tune before you retire.** Mind the **efficiency-capture gap (20–30% vs 1–3%)**.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. A CPO wants one 95% accuracy target for all contract-extraction fields. Critique it.**\nOne bar ignores stakes. Set the bar per clause from the cost of error. Liability, penalty and indemnity clauses need domain-tuned models, high recall and human review. Contact and address fields can run on a general model at a lower bar. Name the one clause type you never fully automate.\n\n**Q2. Why does a general LLM score 79.3% and a domain model 94.7% on clause risk?**\nClause risk is legal reasoning, not language fluency. The general model lacks labelled contract-risk training, so it misses subtle shifts (mutual → one-sided indemnity).\n\n**Q3. An agent's monthly bill rose from ₹1 L to ₹3 L. What do you do?**\nRun tune-or-retire: cost per decision vs value per decision. If value still exceeds cost comfortably, keep it and tune (cache, shorten context, cheaper tier). If not, tune then re-measure, and retire to the next-best option if it still fails.\n\n**Q4. Why must spend caps live in code, not in the system prompt?**\nPrompts are requests the model can ignore under pressure. A coded cap is checked before the call fires and cannot be argued with: guardrails live outside the model.\n\n**Q5. Explain the efficiency-capture gap and how a manager closes it.**\nEfficiency (20–30% time saved) doesn't automatically become margin (1–3% captured). Close it with a capture plan (redeploy freed capacity, bank savings in budgets), track realised ₹, and keep run costs below captured value.\n\n**Q6. What makes Walmart–Pactum \"bounded autonomy\"?**\nThe agent proposes, counters and closes autonomously, but only inside price, terms and volume boundaries set by a human category manager. It cannot expand its mandate.\n"}, {"slug": "ch12-genai-design-multi-agent", "label": "GenAI Design & Multi-Agent", "tag": "12", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 12 — GenAI in Supply-Chain Design & Multi-Agent Coordination (Sessions 18–19)", "md": "# Ch 12 — GenAI in Supply-Chain Design & Multi-Agent Coordination (Sessions 18–19)\n**Syllabus unit 8 📘:** *Generative AI in Supply Chain Design & Planning — scenario generation, design-space exploration, multi-agent simulators.*\n**Session 18 📘:** \"GenAI in Supply Chain Design: Building Scenarios Before the Storm Hits.\" It led into **Round 8: Scenarios and the Agents That Exist**.\n**Session 19 📘:** \"The Amplification We Made.\" It revisited multi-agent coordination through the class's own **Command Your Agent** finale chart, and led into **Round 9: Coordination Under Pressure**.\n\n> **Exam use:** two traps and two tools.\n> - **Trap 1:** a vivid, detailed scenario treated as likely (\"plausible-but-wrong\"). **Tool:** the **Scenario Portfolio Checklist** (generate wide, label honestly, pick two, specify survival, guard the label).\n> - **Trap 2:** smart agents reacting to each other's orders, which produces the bullwhip (the **collaboration paradox**). **Tools:** **shared demand signal, order smoothing and trained coordination**, checked with **ACAP**.\n\n---\n\n## Part A — GenAI in supply-chain design (S18)\n\n### 1. Beyond brainstorming: generative scenario engines ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Human planners cap at four or five scenarios.** Recency bias and groupthink limit the range.\n- **GenAI**, trained on historical demand, macro signals and disruption patterns, generates **dozens of structurally different scenarios in minutes**: a regional chip shortage, a port closure compounding a demand spike, a currency shock hitting a key supplier.\n- **The value isn't a smarter machine.** It is a **wider distribution of futures considered before capital is committed**.\n- **Unlabelled volume is a trap.** Treat every scenario as equally likely and you either **over-build for fantasies** or **dismiss the whole exercise as noise**.\n- **The fix:** label every scenario **plausible / stretch / implausible** before it touches a design decision, based on **mechanism and precedent, not detail**.\n\n### Class Q 8.1 (S18) — the celebrity paint-colour spike ⭐⭐⭐\nThe scenario: \"a celebrity's post triggers a 400% spike in one paint colour within 72 hours\". It is vivid and internally consistent, and someone wants to fund paint-shop flex capacity now.\n**Answer: C. Label it \"stretch\", check for a real mechanism and base rate, then look for a cheap, reversible mitigation.**\n- Examples of cheap, reversible mitigations: an option contract on outsourced painting, or a postponement strategy (paint late, keep white bodies).\n- **Detail and internal consistency are not evidence of grounding.**\n- *A convincing scenario is not the same as a well-evidenced one. Label before you act.*\n\n### 2. From scenarios to designs: GenAI + classical optimisation ⭐⭐⭐\n- A **2025 sustainable supply-chain design architecture** pairs a **variational autoencoder (VAE)** with a classical optimiser.\n  - The **VAE generates** a wide spread of plausible demand scenarios: it learns the distribution of past demand patterns and samples new, realistic ones.\n  - A **multi-objective optimiser** then searches the **cost-vs-CO₂ trade-off frontier (Pareto frontier)** across all of them at once.\n- **\"The generator proposes; the optimiser disposes.\"** GenAI doesn't pick the answer; it **expands the futures the optimiser has to survive**. The optimiser does the disciplined trade-off math. This is the same split seen all course: **GenAI for range, classical methods for rigour** (the AI–OR symbiosis again).\n- **Design-space exploration 📘:** GenAI also proposes alternative **network designs** (DC locations, sourcing splits, product variants). The optimiser and the digital twin evaluate them against the labelled scenarios.\n- **Antifragility as a design goal:** not \"can it survive?\" but \"**can it get stronger because the scenario happened?**\" (Taleb). Examples:\n  - flex capacity that captures competitors' stranded demand during a shortage;\n  - dual sourcing that wins allocation when a rival single-sources;\n  - postponement that turns volatility into option value.\n\n**Pareto-frontier numeric (exam style).** Three network designs, scored across the labelled scenarios:\n| Design | Expected cost (₹ cr) | CO₂ (kt) | Survives plausible? | Survives stretch? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A: single mega-DC, road | 100 | 60 | yes | no (port closure strands it) |\n| B: two DCs, rail + road | 108 | 45 | yes | yes |\n| C: four DCs, all road | 120 | 70 | yes | yes |\n\n- C is **dominated** by B: it is worse on both cost and CO₂.\n- A vs B is a genuine trade-off: +8% cost for −25% CO₂ and survival of the stretch scenario.\n- **Choose B** if the stretch scenario is labelled with a real mechanism. Note the premium: ₹8 cr/yr of insurance.\n\n### 3. Practice — Walmart's Trend-to-Product ⭐⭐\n- Walmart used generative AI to compress trend-spotting to shelf, cutting **roughly 18 weeks** out of the design-to-shelf cycle.\n- This wasn't generating disruption scenarios; it was **generating and iterating product concepts fast**. The design loop itself became a source of **competitive response speed**.\n- **So what:** the same generative capability that stress-tests a network can also **compress your reaction time** when a demand scenario lands.\n\n### 4. The Scenario Portfolio Checklist ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n1. **Generate wide:** use GenAI to produce a broad, structurally diverse set, deliberately including scenarios a planner wouldn't brainstorm alone.\n2. **Label honestly:** plausible, stretch or implausible, **based on mechanism and precedent, not detail**.\n3. **Select your two:** the two scenarios your design must survive, typically **one plausible and one high-impact stretch**.\n4. **Specify survival:** the concrete capability each requires (flex capacity, dual sourcing, buffer) **and what it costs**.\n5. **Guard the label:** before committing capital, **name the mechanism connecting the scenario to your business**. If you can't, it's a story.\n\n### Round 8 link — scenarios and the agents that exist ⭐⭐\n- The game catalogue has **no scenario or crisis agent**. The nearest real capabilities are both in the distribution family:\n  - **DIS_EXCP_2/3**: Dynamic Exception & Re-Routing.\n  - **DIS_NET_2/3**: Network Design & Digital Twin.\n- Generate the scenarios yourselves, label them honestly, and **buy the capability that survives more than one of them**.\n\n---\n\n## Part B — Multi-agent coordination (S19)\n\n### 5. The collaboration paradox, live in your own data ⭐⭐⭐\n- In **Command Your Agent** (July), the class's **20 teams' agents produced a textbook bullwhip blow-up** on the shared screen. Nobody planned it; it emerged from **independent, locally sensible decisions**.\n- **Order swings grew larger the further upstream you went.** This is the bullwhip effect.\n- **It is not a bug.** It is what happens when agents **react to each other's orders, not shared demand**.\n- **LLM-based beer-game experiments** name this exact pattern the **\"collaboration paradox\"**: **smarter individual agents plus no coordination make system-wide oscillation worse, not better**. Better local optimisation means **faster, more confident overreaction to noise**.\n\n### The bullwhip — quick refresher (SCM basics) ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Causes (Lee, Padmanabhan & Whang):**\n  - demand-signal processing (forecasting from orders);\n  - order batching;\n  - price fluctuations (forward buying);\n  - rationing and shortage gaming.\n- Add lead times and lack of visibility.\n- **Measure:** bullwhip ratio = Var(orders placed) / Var(demand received). A ratio > 1 means amplification, and it compounds tier by tier.\n\n**Numeric.** Retail demand variance = 100, retailer order variance = 180, distributor = 400, factory = 1,000.\n- Retailer ratio = 1.8, distributor = 400/180 ≈ 2.2, factory = 1,000/400 = 2.5.\n- **Total amplification = 10×.**\n- Class Q 9.1 trace: the team's agent orders +15% \"to be safe\" (×1.15), and two tiers up another agent doubles (×2). The upstream order is about **2.3× the real need** while real demand hasn't moved.\n\n### Class Q 9.1 (S19) — which single change prevents the amplification? ⭐⭐⭐\n**Answer: B. Give every agent visibility into the same real end-customer demand signal, not each other's orders.**\n- Noise was re-interpreted as trend at every tier.\n- A shared, undistorted signal **removes what the agents were amplifying**.\n- Faster processors, local negotiation or more watchers all still process the same distorted signal.\n- *Coordination beats speed. Agents need to see the same truth, not just react to each other faster.*\n\n### 6. What actually restores stability — three fixes ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Shared demand signals:** every agent sees real end-customer demand (POS sell-through), not a neighbour's order. **This alone removes most of the noise-amplification loop.** In classical SCM terms: information sharing, VMI, CPFR.\n2. **Rate limits and order smoothing:** cap or dampen how much an order can move per period, e.g. \"order change ≤ ±10% per week\" or exponential smoothing of orders.\n3. **Trained coordination:** agents **trained jointly against one shared objective**, not isolated optimisation. **Consensus-seeking orchestration** forces agents to reconcile before acting.\n\n*If your agents talk to each other, be explicit about which of these three your design builds in.*\n\n### 7. Coordination beats cleverness — with numbers ⭐⭐⭐\n- **2025 study, *Production and Operations Management*:** **jointly-trained agents beat both a central agent and uncoordinated agents**, on **cost AND variability simultaneously**, not one at the expense of the other.\n- **2025 study, *International Journal of Production Research*:** **consensus-seeking frameworks** let agents iterate to agreement before acting.\n- **For your project:** \"each agent does its job well\" is **not** sufficient evidence of good design. **Make the coordination mechanism explicit**; it is the evidence of good design.\n- Ch 7 link: multi-agent RL coordinating replenishment across a chain beats one smart agent alone, with lower cost and less bullwhip.\n\n### Multi-agent simulators 📘\n- Agent-based simulators let you **test a multi-agent design before deployment**. Each tier, carrier or supplier is an agent with its own rules, and you watch for emergent amplification.\n- Examples: the beer game, Command Your Agent, Chain Reaction itself, and AnyLogic-style supply-chain simulations.\n- **Use them** to stress the coordination mechanism against the labelled scenarios from Part A.\n\n### 8. The ACAP self-check (S19 version) ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n| Letter | Check | Question |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | **Accountability** | Is it clear who owns a decision the agent makes, and can it be traced back to a person? |\n| **C** | **Capability boundaries** | Does the design state explicitly what the agent is and is not authorised to decide alone? |\n| **A** | **Alignment** | Does the agent optimise toward the **same objective** as the humans and agents around it? |\n| **P** | **Process oversight** | Is there a checkpoint **before** high-impact actions execute, not just after? |\n| + | **Coordination mechanism** | If multi-agent: name the **specific mechanism** that prevents agents from amplifying each other's noise. |\n\n(Session 20 restates ACAP as a governance checklist for the post-incident review: Accountability, Capability limits, **Auditability, Proportionality, Post-incident learning**. See [Ch 13](#/ch13-responsible-ai-ethics).)\n\n### Round 9 link — coordination under pressure ⭐⭐\n- The coordination muscle gets tested: **a shared demand signal, a fast but not panicked reaction, and a clear owner of the call**.\n- Don't let the procurement, production and distribution agents each add their own \"to be safe\" buffer. **Set one shared demand view and one safety policy.** See the [Chain Reaction playbook](#/chain-reaction-playbook).\n\n## Key takeaways (S18–S19 recaps 📘)\n1. GenAI **widens the scenario set** beyond what a planner would brainstorm alone.\n2. **Plausible-but-wrong** is the core failure mode: label every scenario before you act on it.\n3. **GenAI generates scenarios; classical optimisation searches the trade-offs.** The pairing drives the decision (VAE + multi-objective optimiser).\n4. **Antifragile design** aims to gain from disruption, not just survive it.\n5. Decentralised, uncoordinated agents don't just fail to fix the bullwhip; **smarter local optimisation can make it worse** (the collaboration paradox).\n6. **Shared demand signals, smoothing rules and trained coordination** are the fixes, and they improve cost and stability together.\n7. Name your coordination mechanism explicitly, and run **ACAP** before you trust any agent design.\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. GenAI produces 40 scenarios for your network review. What do you do before any capital decision?**\nLabel each one plausible, stretch or implausible by mechanism and precedent. Pick two to survive (one plausible, one high-impact stretch). Specify and cost the capability each needs. Before funding, state the mechanism linking the scenario to the business.\n\n**Q2. Why pair a VAE with a multi-objective optimiser instead of letting GenAI choose the design?**\nThe VAE widens the range of plausible futures. The optimiser does the rigorous cost-vs-CO₂ trade-off across them and finds the Pareto frontier. Generators are not built for constrained trade-off math: GenAI for range, OR for rigour.\n\n**Q3. Define antifragility with a supply-chain example.**\nA design that gains from disruption. For example, dual-sourced chips plus flex capacity let the firm keep shipping and win share when a rival's single source fails.\n\n**Q4. Five companies' agents each add 10% \"safety\" to the orders they receive. True demand is flat at 1,000. What does the factory see?**\n1,000 × 1.1⁵ ≈ **1,611**, a 61% phantom increase. Fix: a shared POS signal and order smoothing.\n\n**Q5. Your team says, \"Each agent hits its KPI, so the design is good.\" Respond using S19.**\nLocal excellence can produce system-wide oscillation (the collaboration paradox). Show the coordination mechanism (shared signal, smoothing, joint training or consensus) and pass the ACAP check, including alignment to one shared objective.\n\n**Q6. Which single change most reduces amplification across tiers, and why not \"faster reactions\"?**\nA shared end-customer demand signal. Faster reactions just amplify the same distorted order signal sooner.\n"}, {"slug": "ch13-responsible-ai-ethics", "label": "Responsible AI & Debrief", "tag": "13", "group": "chapters", "title": "Ch 13 — Responsible AI: Closing the Loop (Session 20 + the course synthesis)", "md": "# Ch 13 — Responsible AI: Closing the Loop (Session 20 + the course synthesis)\n**Syllabus unit 9 📘:** *Ethical, Legal and Social Implications of AI in SCM — case law examples, AI bias in vendor selection, fairness audits.*\n**Session 20 📘:** \"Closing the Loop: Responsible AI as the Last Mile of Operations.\" It covers the course synthesis, a governance core, your season's post-incident review, and **how all twenty teams actually played Chain Reaction** (the season debrief).\n\n> **Exam use:** the last part of almost every case answer. After you recommend an agent, show **who answers for it**: a named owner, an autonomy level, an audit log, proportional oversight, and a post-incident loop (**ACAP**).\n> The ethics question in a case is usually **bias in the data foundation** (an under-represented market, region or supplier group), the **EU AI Act duty split** (provider vs deployer), or **\"disclose now vs after the board meeting\"**. The answer is always to **use the guardrail when it costs you something**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Ten rounds, one system — what the course was actually teaching ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Autonomy ladder (L1–L5):** how many decision rights you hand an agent.\n   - L1: inform. L2: recommend, a human approves. L3: act within bounds and escalate exceptions. L4: autonomous with monitoring. L5: fully autonomous.\n2. **Guardrails live outside the model:** control sits in process and organisation design, not in the weights.\n3. **Claim-audit discipline:** check turn by turn whether outputs deserved the autonomy given.\n4. **AI–OR symbiosis:** agentic AI augments optimisation and human judgement and replaces neither.\n5. **Data foundations before intelligence:** the constraint underneath everything else.\n\n**Put together:** autonomy is **a dial you set deliberately**, and guardrails and audits keep it honest. The closing question is **who answers for it, and can you prove it?**\n\n## 2. Class Q 10.1 (S20) — the bias found two days before the board ⭐⭐⭐\nThe situation:\n- the forecasting agent has run at **L4** all season and outperformed manual;\n- the training data **under-represents the fastest-growing regional market**;\n- allocation has likely been skewed away from that market for months;\n- the board review is in two days.\n\n**Answer: C.** Drop the agent to **L2**, **disclose the bias and its likely impact in the incident log**, and bring both to the board **as a governance finding**.\n- (A) \"say nothing\" and (D) \"wait for the audit\" protect the meeting, not the operation.\n- (B) \"quietly patch\" hides the prior harm and breaks auditability.\n- *Guardrails only count as guardrails if you use them when it costs you something.*\n\n**Model write-up (assumptions format).**\n1. *Assume* the skew lowered allocation to Region R by roughly x% for n months.\n2. *Assume* lost sales can be estimated as the gap between R's demand trend and its allocation.\n\nThen:\n- **Act now:** step down to L2 (planner approves allocations).\n- **Quantify:** estimate the lost sales and the margin forgone.\n- **Fix the foundation:** re-weight or re-sample the training data, and add a fairness/representation check by region.\n- **Disclose:** an incident log entry plus a board paper.\n- **Re-promote** only after a holdout shows parity across regions.\n\n## 3. Bias, sustainability, governance — the trio you answer for ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Bias = an unrepresentative data foundation.**\n  - Under-represented regions, suppliers, customer segments or time periods.\n  - It shows up as **skewed allocation, unfair supplier scoring or vendor selection** (e.g. a supplier-risk model penalising small or minority-owned suppliers because they have thin histories), and as **service inequity** across zones.\n- **Sustainability = the resource cost at scale.** The **energy and water footprint** of agentic platforms (flagged in S5) still applies: **scale amplifies both value and cost**. Count model compute alongside logistics CO₂ in design trade-offs (Ch 12's cost-vs-CO₂ frontier).\n- **Governance = the structure that catches both before they become incidents.**\n- **Legal floor: the EU AI Act.**\n  - **High-risk obligations**: conformity assessment, **CE marking**, registration, and a duty split across **providers, importers, distributors and deployers**.\n  - \"As of this month\" (the session date) **those obligations are now enforceable**.\n  - The **provider** (who builds the system) owns conformity and technical documentation. The **deployer** (who uses it, e.g. Trishul) owns **human oversight**, use according to instructions, input-data relevance, monitoring and logging.\n- **The maturity gap:** only **~20% of firms have governance mature enough for autonomous agents**, though most plan to deploy within two years. **That gap is where incidents live.**\n\n### Bias-check numeric (exam style) 📗\nA supplier-scoring agent shortlists suppliers.\n- Large suppliers: 120 of 200 shortlisted (60%).\n- Small suppliers: 30 of 100 (30%).\n- **Selection-rate ratio = 30/60 = 0.5.** That is below the common **four-fifths (0.8) rule of thumb**, so it is a red flag.\n- Check whether the gap is explained by legitimate performance (on-time %, quality) or by thin-history artefacts. If it is artefacts, adjust (e.g. Bayesian shrinkage for thin histories) and keep a human approver (L2) for small-supplier exclusions.\n\n### Accountability in practice — a named precedent 📗\n**Air Canada (2024):** a tribunal held the airline liable for its website chatbot's wrong refund-policy answer and rejected the argument that the bot was \"a separate entity\". **The deployer answers for what its agent says and does.** For operations, a procurement or customer-service agent's commitments bind the firm, so bound its authority (mandates) and log its actions.\n\n## 4. The ACAP Governance Checklist — one last time (S20 version) ⭐⭐⭐ (framework)\n| Check | Question |\n|---|---|\n| **Accountability** | Is there a **named human owner** for this agent's decisions, not just a team? |\n| **Capability limits** | Is the agent's decision scope and autonomy level **explicitly bounded**, matching the ladder rung it operates at? |\n| **Auditability** | Is there a **log detailed enough to reconstruct what the agent did and why**, after the fact? |\n| **Proportionality** | Does the level of human oversight **scale with the real-world stakes** of the decision? |\n| **Post-incident learning** | Does an incident **actually change the next deployment**, or just get filed and forgotten? |\n\n(S19's design-time version reads Accountability, Capability boundaries, Alignment, Process oversight + Coordination mechanism; see [Ch 12](#/ch12-genai-design-multi-agent). In an exam, using either version is fine. Name the five checks and apply each one to the case.)\n\n### Post-incident review template (Round 10 practicum) ⭐⭐\n1. **What happened:** facts, timeline, the agent and its autonomy level.\n2. **Impact:** ₹, service, trust/reputation.\n3. **Root cause:** data, mandate, guardrail, coordination or human factors.\n4. **Which guardrail fired, or should have.**\n5. **Changes:** autonomy step-down or up, a new bound, a new trigger, a data fix.\n6. **Owner and date for each change; verify at the next review.**\n\nWrite it honestly and check it against a real framework, every time.\n\n## 5. The Chain Reaction season debrief — how 20 teams actually played ⭐⭐⭐\n**Two axes decided the season:** how much AI capability a team **built** (live agents), and how well it **kept operating**. Four archetypes emerged.\n\n| Archetype | Pattern | Examples (rank, value) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Foundations without operations** (4 teams) | laid deep ground and never ran on it; \"bought the platform, didn't operate the business\" | A4-Sapiens (#20, 46: fill 92 → 69, **trust 61, last**); B19-Utkarshan (#19, 63; rail near zero); B16-nEYtra (#14, 118); B13-Kolirt (#11, 125; only 5 live agents, ran out of tokens in R10 and shelved builds) |\n| **2. Transformation theatre** (7 teams) | **built the most on the least foundation; activity mistaken for outcome** | A1-Tankers (#18, 82; 12 agents, fill 65 → 77); B11-Dream11 (#17, 101; **third orchestrator build FAILED at 20% odds**, below gate); A5-Tokenizer (#16, 107; reputation 0.76); A6-Smooth Ops (#15, 111); B18-Promptplay (#13, 119; a **scheduling agent breached guardrails** in R10, reputation 0.75, the lowest); A10-Decepticons (#10, 132; five below-gate commissions, three build failures); B14-Six Sigma (#8, 153; clean ops but its **R10 orchestrator FAILED at 12% odds, below its D3·P3·T3 gate**) |\n| **3. Lean operators** (3 teams) | minimal transformation, deep foundations, excellent execution; **capital-efficient but a lower ceiling and thin cover** | B20-Andromeda (#12, 122; **best reputation 0.92 on a single agent**, but zero governance estate, so trust 78); A9-Skynet (#5, 170); **A8-The Odyssey (#3, 191; 6 agents, all foundations at L5, trust >100, ₹1,181 cr cash)** |\n| **4. Compounders** (6 teams) — **WON** | built capability **on foundations they had actually laid** and kept the business running | A3-OpsManiacs (#9, 148); B17-BLTB (#7, 159); B15-Neural 15 (#6, 166); A2-Doomsday (#4, 186; late miss of 338 cars auto-pushed to the nearest RDC); **A7-Kimi K3 (#2, 212; zero below-gate builds all season, rail every round, best cash ₹1,212 cr, highest trust 111; lost by 1.1 points)**; **B12-Titans (#1, 213, CHAMPION; widest estate, 21 agents, rail every round, trust held at 110 through every shock)** |\n\n### What the season taught — the four lessons ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Sequence beats speed.** The top teams built **ON** foundations they had laid; the rest built agents faster than the **D·P·T ground** could support. **A below-gate agent runs at full cost and a fraction of the effect.**\n2. **Building is not operating.** Size never predicted rank on its own: #3 ran 6 agents, #12 ran one, and four 12–13-agent estates finished 15th–18th. **Operating** the estate did predict rank: booking rail, filing a distribution plan, sizing the chip book to real demand.\n3. **Governance is the multiplier you feel late.** **Company value = profit × reputation × trust.** The champion held trust at 110 through every shock. Teams that skipped the resilience and governance estate watched it collapse (B20 to 78, A4 to 61), and the multiplication did the rest.\n4. **Efficiency is a real strategy.** A8 finished #3 on six agents, and B20 held top-tier service on one. Deep foundations amplify a tiny estate. **Lean works, provided you still buy the insurance.**\n\n**Multiplier numeric.** Team X has profit index 200, reputation 0.80 and trust 0.90, so value = **144**. Team Y has a lower profit of 180 but reputation 0.92 and trust 1.10, so value = **182**. Y wins with 10% less profit, because **governance multiplies**.\n\n## Key takeaways (S20 recap 📘)\n1. **Autonomy is a dial you set on purpose**, not a default you inherit.\n2. Guardrails and audits only matter **the moment they're inconvenient**. That is the whole test.\n3. Governance gaps don't close themselves. The **20%-mature** number is a gap you'll be closing in your own career.\n4. The habit that outlasts the course: **write the incident down honestly, and check it against a real framework, every time.**\n\n---\n\n## Self-test\n**Q1. An allocation agent at L4 is found to under-serve rural zones because training data was urban-heavy. Give the five ACAP checks as they apply.**\n- Accountability: the named owner (head of distribution).\n- Capability limits: step down to L2 until the fix is made.\n- Auditability: reconstruct the allocations from the logs and quantify the shortfall.\n- Proportionality: rural allocation changes need human sign-off.\n- Post-incident learning: add a representation test to the retraining pipeline before re-promotion.\n\n**Q2. Under the EU AI Act, who owns human oversight when a firm uses a vendor's high-risk AI system?**\nThe **deployer** (the firm). It must use the system per instructions, assign competent human oversight, monitor it and keep logs. The **provider** owns conformity assessment, CE marking, registration and technical documentation.\n\n**Q3. Why did \"transformation theatre\" teams score poorly despite building the most agents?**\nThey built below the foundation gates (full cost, a fraction of the effect, higher failure odds) and didn't operate the estate (rail, distribution plans, demand-sized chip book). Value = profit × reputation × trust, so failures and breaches hit the multipliers.\n\n**Q4. What does \"guardrails only count if you use them when it costs you something\" mean in practice?**\nWhen a guardrail fires at an inconvenient time (before a board meeting or quarter-end), you still step the agent down and disclose. Skipping it once turns governance into theatre.\n\n**Q5. A supplier-selection agent shortlists 45% of large suppliers and 18% of small ones. Is there a bias concern?**\nRatio = 18/45 = 0.40, well below 0.8, so investigate. Check whether the gap comes from performance or from thin-history artefacts, fix it, and keep a human approver for exclusions.\n"}, {"slug": "practice-cases", "label": "Mock Papers (3)", "tag": "MOCK", "group": "practice", "title": "Practice Cases — three full mock papers with model answers", "md": "# Practice Cases — three full mock papers with model answers\nThese are **new cases written in the exact style of the two past papers**: a company, exhibits with deliberate noise and defects, Section A diagnostics and Section B design. Each one recombines the course frameworks the way the real exam will. Attempt each paper for **30–35 minutes** before reading the model answers. Every answer follows the mandatory format: **Assumptions: A1. … A2. …**, then units and arithmetic, then the framework named, then the verdict.\n\n> All numbers below are checked. Where a model answer depends on a reading of the exhibits, that reading is stated as an assumption, exactly as you should do in the exam.\n\n---\n\n# Mock Paper 1 — Meru Pharma Distributors\n*(Tests: Claim audit, Q vs S, fill-rate weighting, data triage, Twin-Now test, autonomy and mandate, pilot ranking.)*\n\n### The case\nMeru Pharma Distributors moves branded and generic medicines from **6 distribution centres (DCs)** to about **9,000 pharmacies and 420 hospitals** across western and central India. Revenue is **₹2,400 crore**. A readiness self-assessment scored **[Data quality 3, Master data 2, Architecture 3, Security 4, Compute 3]**. The board approved **₹5 crore per year** for \"AI in operations\". Three proposals compete:\n\n- **Proposal 1: AI demand-forecasting suite.** ₹2.2 crore/yr subscription plus ₹1.0 crore one-time integration. Vendor claim: \"**35% lower inventory and 91% network fill rate within a year**.\"\n- **Proposal 2: Predictive maintenance for cold rooms.** IoT vibration and temperature sensors plus an RUL model on the **40 cold-room compressors** (vaccines, insulin, biologics). ₹45 lakh one-time plus ₹15 lakh/yr.\n- **Proposal 3: Emergency-delivery routing agent.** It decides, **without human sign-off (L4)**, whether hospital emergency orders go by courier-express or the next scheduled road run, and which DC ships. ₹0.9 crore/yr.\n\n### Exhibit 1 — Operations & finance (annual)\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Average cycle stock (Q/2) | ₹48 crore |\n| Average safety stock | ₹16 crore |\n| Inventory carrying rate | 18% |\n| Order quantities | supplier MOQ / full-truck multiples |\n| Corporate brand campaign | ₹22 crore |\n| Head-office renovation (one-time) | ₹6 crore |\n| Hospital emergency orders | ~3,100 per year |\n| Cold-room compressor failures | 20 per year (0.5 per compressor) |\n| Average stock spoiled per failure | ₹6 lakh |\n| Emergency repair per failure | ₹1 lakh |\n| Current maintenance | **quarterly** preventive service |\n\n### Exhibit 2 — DC fill rates (last quarter)\n| DC | Order lines ('000) | Fill rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pune | 5,200 | 95% |\n| Nagpur | 2,100 | 93% |\n| Hyderabad | 4,800 | 81% |\n| Indore | 1,500 | 96% |\n| Raipur | 900 | 97% |\n| Surat | 3,500 | 84% |\n| **Total (as reported)** | **18,500** | **\"Network fill 91%\"** |\n\n### Exhibit 3 — Supplementary\n- The vendor's reference customer is a European OTC retailer with weekly replenishment and **no cold chain**.\n- The PdM vendor's pilot elsewhere caught **70%** of compressor failures **at least 5 days ahead**. A planned repair then costs **₹1.2 lakh** with no spoilage.\n- The ESG team notes a **14% reduction in fleet diesel use** since EV vans were introduced.\n\n### Questions\n**Section A (5 marks)**\n- **A1.** Test Proposal 1's inventory claim with ONE calculation. Name your base and open with numbered assumptions.\n- **A2 (2 marks).** Data triage:\n  - (a) name three items that are irrelevant or unreliable for the decisions;\n  - (b) find the defective figure(s) in Exhibit 2 and show the arithmetic;\n  - (c) say what the correction does to the vendor's fill claim.\n- **A3.** Size the annual value of Proposal 2 and its payback, and run the Twin-Now / Twin-Later test.\n- **A4.** What is wrong with Proposal 3's autonomy level? State the governance design it needs.\n\n**Section B (5 marks)**\n- **B1.** Rank the three proposals for a 6-month go-live. For the top-ranked proposal give: the gate metrics (success + counter-metric), the owner, and the demotion trigger. For Proposal 3 give a Mandate Card.\n\n---\n\n## Mock 1 — Model answers\n\n### A1 (1 mark)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. Inventory = cycle stock + safety stock = ₹48 + ₹16 = **₹64 cr**, which is the base for \"35% lower inventory\".\n- A2. Order quantities are set by supplier MOQs and full trucks (Exhibit 1), so forecasting cannot change Q.\n- A3. A realistic forecast gain cuts forecast-error σ by about 25%.\n\n**Calculation (Q vs S):**\n- Claim = 35% × 64 = **₹22.4 cr**. But all safety stock is only ₹16 cr. **Even a perfect forecast (S → 0) removes at most 16/64 = 25%.**\n- The claim is **impossible** through forecasting alone.\n- Realistic: S falls 25% → ₹4 cr = **6.25%** of inventory. Carrying saving = 4 × 18% = **₹0.72 cr/yr**, against a **₹2.2 cr/yr** fee (plus ₹1 cr one-time).\n\n**Verdict:** the claim fails the Four-Question Claim Audit:\n- the baseline is a different business (a European OTC retailer, no cold chain, weekly replenishment);\n- it attacks the wrong stock (Q is MOQ-driven).\n\nFund it only if stockout or service benefits close the ₹1.5 cr/yr gap. Reshape it as a distribution-based safety-stock policy on volatile SKUs.\n\n### A2 (2 marks)\n**(a) Irrelevant or unreliable items:**\n1. **Brand campaign ₹22 cr:** a marketing spend that no proposal affects.\n2. **Head-office renovation ₹6 cr:** one-time and unrelated.\n3. **ESG note on EV vans (−14% diesel):** true, perhaps, but it doesn't bear on any of the three proposals.\n4. **The vendor's European reference:** unreliable as a baseline (different channel, no cold chain).\n\n**(b) Defects in Exhibit 2:**\n- **The total is wrong:** 5,200 + 2,100 + 4,800 + 1,500 + 900 + 3,500 = **18,000**, not 18,500.\n- **\"Network fill 91%\" is a simple average** of six DC fill rates ((95 + 93 + 81 + 96 + 97 + 84)/6 = 91.0%).\n- The **volume-weighted** fill = Σ(lines × fill) / Σ lines = 16,034 / 18,000 = **89.1%**.\n- Unfilled lines = **1,966 thousand**, concentrated in **Hyderabad (912k) and Surat (560k)**, the two biggest DCs after Pune.\n\n**(c) Effect of the correction:** the real network fill is 89.1%. The vendor's \"91% within a year\" is roughly the **current simple average**, i.e. **no improvement at all** on its own metric. Measure success on weighted fill and on the Hyderabad and Surat DCs.\n\n### A3 (1 mark)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. Failures stay at 20 per year.\n- A2. PdM catches 70% (14) at least 5 days ahead; the other 30% (6) fail as today.\n- A3. Quarterly preventive service doesn't catch these failures (they occur between services).\n\n**Today:** 20 × (₹6 L spoilage + ₹1 L repair) = **₹140 L/yr**.\n**With PdM:**\n- caught: 14 × ₹1.2 L = ₹16.8 L;\n- missed: 6 × ₹7 L = ₹42 L;\n- total **₹58.8 L**.\n\n**Saving** = ₹81.2 L/yr. Net of ₹15 L running cost = **₹66.2 L/yr**. **Payback = 45 / 66.2 ≈ 0.68 years (about 8 months).**\n\n**Twin-Now test:**\n1. Decision-changing: **yes**, because the quarterly service can't see a failure developing in week 6.\n2. Failure cost: **high**, given spoiled biologics and patient-safety risk.\n3. Data readiness: sensors must be installed (an infrastructure bet), but it is a small fleet of 40.\n4. Fidelity: an asset-level RUL model is enough, with no full twin.\n5. **Verdict: twin-now (simulation-lite, asset level).** Keep the quarterly service as the fallback.\n\n### A4 (1 mark)\n**What's wrong:** L4 (no sign-off) on a decision whose error can harm patients is **disproportionate**. Hospital emergencies have **asymmetric error costs**: a late critical drug is far worse than an unnecessary courier.\n\n**Design (ACAP):**\n- **Accountability:** a named owner (Head of Hospital Supply).\n- **Capability limits:** **L3**. The agent routes routine emergencies within bounds, and any order flagged *critical-to-life* defaults to the fastest mode, with no cost optimisation.\n- **Auditability:** log every routing decision with its reason.\n- **Proportionality:** a human approves diversions to a non-home DC above a ₹ threshold.\n- **Post-incident learning:** every late critical order triggers a review.\n\nGuardrails (service floor, cost ceiling) **live outside the model**.\n\n### B1 (5 marks)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. The 6-month deadline means a live pilot, not full scale.\n- A2. Sensor installation takes about 8 weeks.\n- A3. Hospital emergency orders are about 3,100 per year, i.e. enough volume for a 3-month L3 pilot.\n\n**Pilot-selection matrix (value, feasibility, blast radius):**\n| Proposal | Value | Feasibility (6 months) | Blast radius | Rank |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P2 PdM cold rooms | ₹66 L/yr net, payback about 8 months, plus safety | high (40 assets, simple model) | low (fallback = quarterly service) | **1** |\n| P3 emergency routing | moderate (courier cost and service) | medium | **high at L4**, low at L3 | **2 (at L3 only)** |\n| P1 forecasting suite | **₹0.72 cr/yr realistic vs ₹2.2 cr fee** | medium (MDM score 2) | medium | **3, reshape** |\n\n**Gate for P2 (Phase 1 → 2):**\n- **Success:** at least 60% of failures caught ≥ 3 days ahead **and** spoilage down at least 50% vs the same quarter last year (frozen baseline).\n- **Counter-metric:** false-alarm swaps no more than 2 per month (technician trust).\n- **Owner:** Head of Engineering, with Quality.\n- **Demote** (back to time-based plus monthly checks) if the catch rate stays below 40% after two quarters.\n\n**Mandate Card for P3 (L3):**\n1. **Service floor:** critical-to-life SKUs are never routed by the slower mode, and hospital emergency on-time ≥ 98%.\n2. **Cost ceiling:** courier ≤ ₹X per order for non-critical items; above it, escalate.\n3. **Mode priority:** for non-critical orders the default is the next scheduled road run if it arrives within the SLA; otherwise courier.\n4. **Shock trigger:** a DC outage or road disruption over 24 hours triggers a full re-plan with human approval.\n5. **Review cadence:** weekly review of late orders and courier spend.\n\n**P1 reshaped:** don't buy the suite. Replace weeks-of-cover rules with **quantile-based safety stock** on the volatile SKUs at Hyderabad and Surat (the 89.1% problem). Revisit a platform once master data reaches 3+.\n\n---\n\n# Mock Paper 2 — Brahmaputra Electronics\n*(Tests: average-hides-trend, contribution forgone vs visible charge, split shipment, bullwhip ratio, LP mandate, clause stakes, tune-or-retire, bias and ACAP.)*\n\n### The case\nBrahmaputra Electronics assembles **LED TVs** at **2 plants** (Noida, Sriperumbudur) and ships through **3 regional DCs** to **3 dealer zones**:\n- **Z1 metro premium**: contribution **₹2,400/unit**;\n- **Z2 urban**: **₹1,100/unit**;\n- **Z3 tier-2/3**: **₹900/unit**.\n\nLast quarter the operations team deployed a **flow optimiser** with **identical cost weights for all zones**, a **procurement agent** for display-panel and chip contracts, and a **supplier-news monitoring agent**. This month's review deck claims success.\n\n### Exhibit 1 — Zone performance (this month)\n| Zone | Demand (units) | Fill rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| Z1 metro premium | 18,000 | 72% |\n| Z2 urban | 12,000 | 90% |\n| Z3 tier-2/3 | 9,000 | 93% |\n| **Deck headline** | | **\"Network fill 85%, in line with target\"** |\n\nOther figures:\n- Express (air/expedite) charge this month: **₹22 lakh**, presented as \"the cost of the monsoon\".\n- Freight cost per unit is down 9% vs last month: \"optimiser success\".\n\n### Exhibit 2 — Modes (per unit to Z1)\n| Mode | Cost | Lead time | Constraint |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Road | ₹180 | 2 days | capacity cut to **1,500 units / 3 days** (flooded corridor) |\n| Rail | ₹135 | 6 days | slots available; booked monthly, charged whether used or not |\n| Express | ₹420 | 1 day | available |\n\nZ1 needs **6,000 units in the next 10 days**, of which **2,400 must arrive within 3 days** (festival launch).\n\n### Exhibit 3 — Order variance up the chain (weekly, units²)\n| Tier | Variance |\n|---|---|\n| Dealer sell-out (consumer demand) | 400 |\n| Dealer orders to DCs | 900 |\n| DC orders to plants | 2,500 |\n| Plant orders for panels/chips | 7,200 |\n\n### Exhibit 4 — Agent run costs (after the vendor repriced)\n| Agent | Run cost/month | Decisions/month | Avg value per decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Procurement agent | ₹6.0 L (was ₹2.5 L) | 1,500 POs | ₹1,100 saved per PO |\n| Supplier-news agent | ₹4.0 L | 30 alerts acted on | ₹8,000 per acted alert |\n\n### Exhibit 5 — Incident note\nAn analyst found that the **credit-limit agent**, which sets dealer credit at L4, used two years of history in which tier-2/3 dealers had **no digital payment records**. It approves tier-2/3 dealers at **half the rate** of metro dealers with similar sales. The board meets in three days.\n\n### Questions\n**Section A (5 marks)**\n- **A1.** Audit the deck's two success claims (\"network fill 85%\", \"freight per unit −9%\").\n- **A2 (2 marks).** Size the contribution forgone this month. Compare it with the ₹22 L \"cost of the monsoon\", and explain *why* the optimiser produced this plan.\n- **A3.** Design the shipment for Z1's next 10 days, with costs.\n- **A4.** Compute the bullwhip amplification and name the single highest-leverage fix.\n\n**Section B (5 marks)**\n- **B1.** Rewrite the optimiser's mandate (Optimizer Mandate Checklist).\n- **B2.** Tune-or-retire each agent in Exhibit 4.\n- **B3.** For the coming panel contract, list the clause stakes and the one clause you'll never fully automate.\n- **B4.** What do you do about Exhibit 5 before the board meeting?\n\n---\n\n## Mock 2 — Model answers\n\n### A1 (1 mark)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. Fill = units delivered / units demanded per zone.\n- A2. The deck averaged the three zone fill rates.\n\n**Calculation:**\n- The simple average is (72 + 90 + 93)/3 = **85.0%**.\n- **Volume-weighted** fill is (18,000 × 0.72 + 12,000 × 0.90 + 9,000 × 0.93) / 39,000 = 32,130 / 39,000 = **82.4%**.\n- The average **hides the problem**: the **biggest and most profitable** zone is the worst served.\n\n**Freight −9%:** this is **activity, not outcome**. Freight fell because the optimiser chose cheap lanes and **didn't ship** to Z1. A lower cost per unit shipped, combined with lost premium sales, is a worse business.\n\n**Verdict:** both claims fail the claim audit (an average hiding the mix; an activity metric presented as an outcome).\n\n### A2 (2 marks)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. Unfilled units are lost sales (no backorders in a festival month).\n- A2. Contribution per unit is as given.\n\n**Lost units:**\n- Z1: 18,000 × 28% = **5,040**.\n- Z2: 12,000 × 10% = 1,200.\n- Z3: 9,000 × 7% = 630.\n\n**Contribution forgone:**\n- Z1: 5,040 × ₹2,400 = **₹120.96 L**.\n- Z2: 1,200 × ₹1,100 = ₹13.2 L.\n- Z3: 630 × ₹900 = ₹5.67 L.\n- **Total ≈ ₹139.8 L (₹1.40 cr)**.\n\nThat is **6.4× the ₹22 L \"monsoon\" charge**. Z1 alone is 87% of the loss.\n\n**Why:** the optimiser **did exactly what it was told**. With **identical cost weights** and **no service floor**, minimising cost means starving the costliest-to-serve lane (Z1 via the flooded road). The plan is **technically optimal and strategically wrong**. Strategy lives in the objective function. The monsoon was the trigger; the **mandate** was the cause.\n\n### A3 (1 mark)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. The road capacity cap (1,500 units per 3 days) holds.\n- A2. Rail's 6-day lead time fits the 7 days left after the urgent window.\n- A3. Express capacity is sufficient.\n\n**Split shipment:**\n- **Urgent 2,400 in 3 days:** road 1,500 × ₹180 = ₹2.70 L, plus express 900 × ₹420 = ₹3.78 L.\n- **Remaining 3,600 within 10 days:** rail 3,600 × ₹135 = ₹4.86 L. Book it now (it is charged whether used or not, so book only the 3,600 you need).\n- **Total ₹11.34 L.**\n- All by express would be 6,000 × ₹420 = **₹25.2 L**, so the split saves **₹13.86 L (55%)** with no festival stockout.\n- Hold back one buffer: if road slips, convert part of the rail portion to express (the trigger).\n\n### A4 (1 mark)\n**Calculation:**\n- Ratios: dealer 900/400 = 2.25; DC 2,500/900 = 2.78; plant 7,200/2,500 = 2.88.\n- **Total = 7,200/400 = 18× amplification.**\n\n**Fix:** give every tier, and every agent, the **same real end-customer sell-out signal (dealer POS)** instead of the next tier's orders. Add order smoothing (e.g. weekly change ≤ ±10%), and name one owner of the shared plan.\n\nThis is the **collaboration paradox**: smarter local agents reacting to orders amplify noise faster.\n\n### B1 — the optimiser mandate (Optimizer Mandate Checklist)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. Z1's premium margin and brand matter strategically.\n- A2. Rail (MIX) is available.\n\n1. **Service targets per zone:** Z1 ≥ 95% in festival months; Z2 ≥ 90%; Z3 ≥ 88%.\n2. **Cost weights:** put a lost-sales penalty equal to contribution into the objective (₹2,400 / ₹1,100 / ₹900 per unit short), so the solver \"sees\" the premium.\n3. **Review rule:** **L3**. The plan executes automatically within the floors; exceptions (a floor breach forecast, or express > ₹10 L/week) escalate to the CSCO.\n4. **Re-solve triggers:** lane capacity loss > 20%; any zone's forecast fill < floor − 5 points; demand spike > 25%.\n5. **Verify the fit:** before execution, check the plan's projected fill by zone against the floors. Optimal ≠ right.\n- Also: book rail only for base load, keep road and express for the variable tail, and front-load (FRONT) before the festival.\n\n### B2 — tune-or-retire\n**Procurement agent:**\n- Cost per decision = ₹6.0 L / 1,500 = **₹400**; value = ₹1,100. Ratio **2.75×**, so **keep**.\n- Tune anyway: cache supplier histories, batch the low-value POs, and use a cheaper model tier for routine re-orders.\n- (Before repricing it was ₹167/decision, 6.6×. The shock is a signal to re-run the math, not a verdict.)\n\n**Supplier-news agent:**\n- Cost per acted decision = ₹4.0 L / 30 = **₹13,333**; value ₹8,000. Ratio **0.6×**, so it is **under water. Tune first:**\n  - scan daily instead of hourly;\n  - restrict to the top 50 suppliers;\n  - use a cheaper tier.\n- If cost halves to ₹6,667 (ratio 1.2×), keep it on watch.\n- If it can't get below about ₹6,000, **retire** it and fall back to a weekly analyst review plus the free alerts.\n- **Spend caps in code** for both.\n\n### B3 — clause stakes for the panel contract\n| Clause | Decision it feeds | Bar | Tier | Human |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Allocation priority in shortage / force majeure** | supply continuity in a shortage | very high | domain-tuned | **always (never fully automated)** |\n| Liability cap, penalties, indemnity | financial exposure | very high | domain-tuned | always |\n| Volume commitment and take-or-pay | inventory and cash (fees on the full book) | high | domain-tuned | review |\n| Price index / escalation | cost model | high | domain-tuned | review |\n| Payment terms | cash plan | medium | general | spot-check |\n| Delivery address, contacts | logistics admin | low | general | no |\n\nSize the volume commitment to **real demand** (the Chain Reaction lesson: fees are charged on the full book).\n\n### B4 — the credit-limit bias (ACAP)\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. The approval-rate gap is driven by missing digital records, not by repayment performance.\n- A2. The decisions are reversible (credit limits can be re-set).\n\n**Action now:**\n- **Step the agent down from L4 to L2**: a credit manager approves tier-2/3 decisions.\n- Log the incident.\n- Quantify the impact: the selection-rate ratio is about **0.5, below the 0.8 rule of thumb**, and estimate the lost Z3 sales.\n- **Disclose it to the board as a governance finding.** Do not wait, and do not patch quietly.\n\n**Fix:**\n- Add alternative data (sales history, GST filings) for thin-file dealers.\n- Run a parity test by region before re-promotion.\n- Name an owner.\n\n*Guardrails count only when you use them when it costs you something.*\n\n---\n\n# Mock Paper 3 — Chain Reaction mid-season (the simulation as an exam case)\n*(Tests: foundation gates, token economics, build speed, distribution dials, operating vs building, the value multiplier.)*\n\n### The case\nYour team runs **Ganga Motors** in Chain Reaction. It is the **start of Round 6**.\n- **Foundations:** **Data 2 · Process 3 · Tech 2**.\n- **Owned agents (monthly upkeep):** Forecasting agent L2 (2 tokens), Distribution Planning Agent (2), Predictive Maintenance L2 (1), Pricing agent (3).\n- **Last month:**\n  - you regenerated the operating plan **twice**;\n  - fill in the 3 premium zones was 71%;\n  - 410 cars auto-pushed to the home RDC of Plant 1;\n  - your rail booking was **zero** (distMode = ROAD);\n  - the chip book was contracted at **130%** of the demand plan.\n- **Company value** = profit 150 × reputation 0.84 × trust 0.95.\n- Your CTO wants to build the **Network-Intelligence Orchestrator** (gate **D3·P3·T3**) this round \"to catch up with the leaders\".\n\n### Questions\n1. Compute this month's token budget if you keep everything and generate one plan plus one regeneration. How many tokens remain for builds and foundations?\n2. Should you build the orchestrator this round? Use the season lessons.\n3. Which three operating moves fix last month's results, and which dial does each touch?\n4. A rival has profit 170, reputation 0.78 and trust 0.85. Who is worth more, and what does that teach?\n5. The pricing agent's upkeep is 3 tokens/month and it changed prices twice last month for about ₹0.8 cr of extra contribution. Tune or retire?\n\n## Mock 3 — Model answers\n**Assumptions:**\n- A1. 24 tokens per month; plan = 2 tokens, regeneration = 1; termination = 2 tokens.\n- A2. Below-gate builds have cut odds and deliver a fraction of the effect.\n- A3. 5 rounds remain.\n\n1. **Tokens:** upkeep 2 + 2 + 1 + 3 = **8**, plus plan 2 + regeneration 1 = **11**. **13 tokens remain.** (Last month's two regenerations cost an extra token. Regenerate only on a real trigger.)\n2. **Don't build the orchestrator yet. It is below gate on D (2 < 3) and T (2 < 3).**\n   - A below-gate agent runs **at full cost for a fraction of the effect**, with cut odds. B14-Six Sigma's and B11-Dream11's orchestrators **failed at 12% and 20% odds**.\n   - **Sequence beats speed.** Spend the 13 tokens raising **Tech to 3** first: it also **unlocks MIX (rail)**. Then raise Data to 3, and build the orchestrator once at gate (A7-Kimi K3: zero below-gate builds all season, #2).\n3. **Operating moves (building is not operating):**\n   - (a) **Raise Tech to 3, then set distMode = MIX** and book rail for the predictable base volume: about 25% cheaper per car, booked a month ahead and charged whether used or not, so book base load only.\n   - (b) **Stop the auto-push.** File a distribution plan with **distFront = FRONT** before peak months and **distSafety weighted to the premium zones**. The 410 cars piled at Plant 1's RDC are A2-Doomsday's 338-car mistake.\n   - (c) **Resize the chip book to about 100–105% of the demand plan** in the Round 6 negotiation. Fees are charged on the full book, so 130% pays for 25–30% idle volume.\n4. **Value:**\n   - You: 150 × 0.84 × 0.95 = **119.7**.\n   - Rival: 170 × 0.78 × 0.85 = **112.7**.\n   - **You are worth more despite 12% less profit.** Governance (reputation and trust) is the multiplier you feel late. Protect it by avoiding guardrail breaches and filing honest incident reviews.\n5. **Pricing agent:** about ₹0.8 cr extra contribution per month for 3 tokens. Compare value per token with the next-best use of a token (raising Tech to unlock rail saves far more).\n   - **Tune first:** limit it to the two products with real price elasticity, or drop to a lower tier if the catalogue allows.\n   - **Retire** only if tokens are binding on gate-raising this round and its value per token is the lowest in the estate.\n   - Retiring costs 2 tokens and saves 3 per month, so it **pays back in 1 month**.\n   - Decide on **cost-per-decision vs value-per-decision**, not the size of the bill.\n"}, {"slug": "exam-questions-qna", "label": "Exam Q&A Bank", "tag": "Q&A", "group": "practice", "title": "Exam Q&A Bank — application questions with model answers", "md": "# Exam Q&A Bank — application questions with model answers\nThe exam has **no standalone theory**. Every question below is therefore phrased the way the paper phrases things: a situation, a number or a decision. Each answer is a compact model answer that uses the **Assumptions → Calculation → Framework → Verdict** pattern where a call is made. Turn on **Self-test** to hide the answers.\n\n> Grouped by syllabus unit. For full-length solved papers see [Trishul](#/pyq-trishul), [Kaveri Motors](#/pyq-kaveri) and the [mock papers](#/practice-cases).\n\n---\n\n## Unit 1 — AI in Operations & SCM: claims, value pools, vocabulary\n\n**Q1. A vendor claims its AI will cut your inventory 30%. Your inventory is ₹20 cr, of which ₹15 cr is cycle stock driven by full-truck ordering. Test the claim.**\n- **Assumptions:** A1. Inventory = Q/2 + S = 15 + 5. A2. Order sizes are fixed by truck economics, so forecasting can't change Q.\n- **Calculation:** the claim is 30% × 20 = ₹6 cr. But S = ₹5 cr, so even a perfect forecast removes at most **25%**. A realistic 25% σ cut removes about ₹1.25 cr (6.25%).\n- **Framework:** Q vs S; Four-Question Claim Audit.\n- **Verdict:** the claim fails arithmetically. Ask for the vendor's baseline business and holdout. Real inventory levers are order frequency and lead time.\n\n**Q2. Distinguish predictive, generative and agentic AI using one procurement example each.**\n- **Predictive:** a model scores supplier-delay risk for each PO.\n- **Generative:** an LLM drafts an RFQ from a requisition.\n- **Agentic:** an agent negotiates tail-spend terms and posts the PO within a manager-set mandate.\n\nThe tell is **goal ownership plus action on systems of record within an envelope**.\n\n**Q3. \"44% of firms already deploy supply-chain AI, so we are behind.\" How should a COO respond?**\nAsk which adopters show measurable value and by what mechanism. Only about 30% move beyond copilots, and about 95% of GenAI pilots show no P&L impact. Adoption measures purchasing, not value. Fund mechanism-level cases with a baseline.\n\n**Q4. Forecast error falls from ±40 to ±15, but you order in pallets of 240. What is the value?**\nNear zero unless the better forecast changes the number of pallets ordered. Map predictions to decision thresholds before buying accuracy: precision pays only when a decision changes.\n\n**Q5. Explain why Amazon's DeepFleet is \"agentic\" while the original Kiva robots were \"automation\".**\nKiva executes centrally assigned moves along floor markers. DeepFleet is a learned generative policy that coordinates a million robots' traffic toward a goal (−10% travel time), adapting continuously within a physical envelope.\n\n## Unit 2 — Readiness: data, technology, process, organisation\n\n**Q6. A firm scores [Data 2, Master data 1, Architecture 3, Security 2, Compute 5]. The CEO wants a purchasing agent with ERP write access in 3 months. Advise.**\n- **Assumptions:** A1. Write access requires per-agent identity, scopes and logs. A2. Master data at 1 means duplicate vendors and items.\n- **Verdict:** not yet.\n  - Masters at 1 poison every downstream decision (duplicate vendors mean wrong concentration figures and payments to duplicates).\n  - Security at 2 forbids write access.\n  - Compute 5 is a stranded asset.\n- **Plan:** a predictive pilot on the cleanest domain now, plus vendor-master dedup with a named owner, plus an API inventory. Gate write access on security ≥ 3 and master data ≥ 3.\n\n**Q7. Which of the six data-quality dimensions binds for (a) real-time demand sensing, (b) spend concentration analysis, (c) a supplier-delay model?**\n- (a) **Timeliness** (overnight batches kill real-time).\n- (b) **Uniqueness** (duplicate vendor records split spend).\n- (c) **Completeness/accuracy** of receipt dates and lead times, plus the leakage test (the feature must be known at decision time).\n\n**Q8. Plants that ignored the AI tool avoided stockouts; plants that obeyed stocked out. What do you conclude and do?**\nThe model's data foundation is missing something (at Continental Parts, the 15% of demand arriving by phone). The rebels were right. Capture the missing channel, re-baseline, allow bounded overrides with reasons (±15%), and gate scaling on a frozen baseline. Don't punish overrides; mine them.\n\n**Q9. Rewrite \"25% inventory reduction in 6 months, company-wide\" into a gate-compatible target.**\n\"In the pilot region (2 DCs, top 300 SKUs), reduce safety stock ≥ 10% vs the frozen Q1 baseline **while keeping weighted fill ≥ 95%** (counter-metric), measured against a control region at month 4. Scale to Phase 2 only if both hold.\" It is scoped, baselined, has a counter-metric and is evidence-gated.\n\n**Q10. Why does adding \"recommendation adherence\" to planners' KPIs backfire?**\nA measure that becomes a target stops measuring: planners will follow the tool even when they know it's wrong, which hides the cases where human judgement mattered. Align incentives with outcomes (service, turns, forecast value added, quality of exception handling), not with compliance.\n\n## Unit 2b — Agentic systems and platforms\n\n**Q11. Design the autonomy level and guardrails for an agent that reorders MRO consumables under ₹50,000.**\n- **Level:** L3, acting within bounds and escalating exceptions. The decision is frequent, reversible and low-stakes, with fast feedback.\n- **Guardrails (outside the model):** per-order ₹ cap and monthly spend cap **in code**; approved-supplier list enforced by the system; anomaly triggers (price > +10% vs last, quantity > 2× average) escalate to the buyer; full action log; a named owner (category manager); monthly review with a demote trigger.\n\n**Q12. Score three use cases on the agentic suitability screen: strategic supplier negotiation, tail-spend reorders, warehouse robot routing.**\n- **Robot routing** scores highest (20+): very high frequency, reversible, rich feedback, clear rules, own premises.\n- **Tail-spend reorders** come next (12–19): frequent, moderate stakes, so L3.\n- **Strategic negotiation** is lowest (< 12): rare, high stakes, ambiguous rules, so human-led with AI input.\n\n**Q13. A feature-rich, cheaper platform can't enforce per-agent spend caps or full logs. Your roadmap has an L4 procurement agent. Decide.**\nDisqualify it. In the six-filter order, governance comes before features and price. At L4, a governance gap is unbounded financial exposure.\n\n**Q14. Copilot token spend is 3.2× forecast. Does that make the platform choice wrong?**\nNo. It is a usage-governance (FinOps) problem: per-team budgets, caps in code, caching, model tiering, quarterly review. Revisit the platform only if governed consumption still breaks the business case.\n\n**Q15. When should an operations firm build rather than buy its AI platform?**\nOnly where the capability is the firm's real differentiation and the firm has unified data and engineering depth. Otherwise **own the differentiating loop, rent the rest**, and check whether a commodity service already exists (OCR, classification).\n\n## Unit 3 — ML, forecasting, inventory, maintenance\n\n**Q16. \"Our demand model is 92% accurate.\" Naive scores 90.5%, and orders come in full-truck multiples. Verdict?**\nUnproven. It barely beats naive and may not change any truck-level order. Report accuracy against naive on an honest time-based holdout, at decision granularity, and price the decisions changed.\n\n**Q17. A model wins on staples but loses on promotions and new items. Deploy?**\nDeploy by segment: the model on staples, naive or incumbent on promotions and new items (champion–challenger). Revisit the map quarterly.\n\n**Q18. SKU A: mean 1,000, σ 50. SKU B: mean 1,000, σ 400. L = 1 week, 95% service. Safety stock for each, and what is wrong with a \"2 weeks of supply\" rule?**\n- SS(A) = 1.645 × 50 ≈ **82**. SS(B) = 1.645 × 400 ≈ **658**.\n- \"2 weeks of supply\" gives both 2,000 units of buffer: it massively overstocks A and is still a blunt instrument for B.\n- Forecast the distribution; the quantile is the decision.\n\n**Q19. Nandini (monsoon drives 40% of variance) is offered weather data. Apply the signal audit.**\n1. **Lead time:** does the forecast horizon (e.g. 2–3 weeks) cover the bottling and dispatch lead time?\n2. **Mechanism:** heat and rain drive consumption, which is honest.\n3. **Decision test:** does it change production or dispatch in the monsoon-transition weeks where fill fails?\n4. **Cost vs value:** subscription plus integration vs stockouts avoided in those weeks.\n5. **Weight check:** after a season, keep it only if the model learned material weight.\n\n**Q20. A forecasting vendor says its agent \"adjusts in real time with zero planner effort\". What do you ask, and why?**\n\"Does it update forecasts, or execute orders?\" Updating is a feature to bake off. Executing is an autonomy grant needing the ladder, bounds, fallback, owner and readiness.\n\n**Q21. Trident: 8,000 SKU-locations weekly, two analysts (one knows some Python), readiness [3,2,3,3,3], ₹25 L. Which forecasting tier?**\n- **Tier 2 (Prophet-class), scripted and scheduled**, with the spreadsheet as benchmark and fallback.\n- Cloud AutoML assumes a governed platform (T ≥ 3.5), data engineering and FinOps. LSTM needs Data Maturity 3+ and long histories.\n- **Flip condition:** T ≥ 3.5, a data engineer, volumes in the tens of thousands, and a bake-off showing decision value that covers the cost.\n\n**Q22. A DRL vendor claims 20% lower spare-parts inventory vs your tuned basestock (89% fill) \"in our backtest\". Respond as COO.**\n- **Assumptions:** A1. A tuned basestock or myopic policy is within about 5% of optimal. A2. Demand is intermittent.\n- **Analysis:** 20% exceeds the headroom, so it is likely a weak baseline or overfitting. Four weeks of shadow mode is statistically empty.\n- **Decision:** fund a **guardrailed pilot**:\n  - scope: one parts family for 2 quarters;\n  - bounds: incumbent floors and ceilings plus a spend cap;\n  - fallback: auto-revert if fill < 87% for 2 weeks;\n  - evaluation: matched control, service per rupee;\n  - trust loop: overrides with reasons;\n  - owners: named.\n\n**Q23. Planners override 30% of the system's recommendations. Problem or information?**\nInformation. Log the reasons, test which overrides beat the system, feed them into retraining, and keep a bounded override band. Banning overrides breeds shadow spreadsheets (Continental Parts).\n\n**Q24. Why is improving forecast accuracy often worth less than replacing the weeks-of-supply rule?**\nThe value dies in the conversion step: multiple distorted forecasts get turned into stock by crude rules. A policy optimised on the demand distribution and the cost structure attacks the leak directly. Optimise the policy, not the forecast.\n\n**Q25. A compressor fails twice a year at ₹31 L per failure; PdM would catch 70% and cost ₹22 L plus ₹5 L/yr; a planned repair costs ₹7 L. Fund it?**\n- Today: 2 × 31 = ₹62 L/yr.\n- With PdM: 1.4 × 7 + 0.6 × 31 = 9.8 + 18.6 = ₹28.4 L.\n- Saving ₹33.6 L; net of running cost ₹28.6 L/yr; **payback ≈ 0.8 yr**.\n- Twin test: it changes the decision (yes), failure cost is high (yes), instrumentation must be built, and asset-level fidelity suffices. **Twin-now.**\n\n**Q26. When should you NOT act on a high failure-probability flag?**\nWhen the existing protocol already catches the failure in time (e.g. weekly inspect-and-replace). The prediction doesn't change the decision or its timing, so it is informationally idle.\n\n## Unit 4 — Logistics, optimisation, procurement NLP, token economics\n\n**Q27. A lane loses 40% capacity before a festival; the premium zone needs 2,000 cars, 800 within 3 days. Express ₹9,000/car, road ₹4,000, rail ₹3,000 with a 6-day lead; road can carry 500 in 3 days. Plan it.**\n- Urgent 800: road 500 = ₹20 L, plus express 300 = ₹27 L.\n- Remaining 1,200 by rail = ₹36 L.\n- **Total ₹83 L**, vs all express 2,000 × 9,000 = **₹180 L**, a saving of ₹97 L (54%).\n- **Framework:** split shipment; the mode mix under capacity.\n\n**Q28. Your optimiser used identical cost weights and starved the premium zone. Fix it with the Optimizer Mandate Checklist.**\n1. **Service targets per zone** (premium ≥ 95%).\n2. **Cost weights** = a lost-sales penalty equal to unit contribution.\n3. **Review rule:** L3 with exception escalation.\n4. **Re-solve triggers:** > 20% capacity loss, a forecast floor breach, demand spike > 25%.\n5. **Verify the plan against the floors before execution.**\n\nThe solver was right for the objective; the objective was wrong.\n\n**Q29. Write a Mandate Card for a distribution agent in a 3-plant, 4-RDC, 7-zone network.**\n- **Service floor:** zones 1–3 (premium) ≥ 95%; others ≥ 88%.\n- **Cost ceiling:** ₹/car by mode.\n- **Mode priority:** rail for base load (once MIX is unlocked), road default, express only for stockout risk.\n- **Shock trigger:** a lane capacity loss > 20% or a zone forecast below its floor forces a full re-plan; smaller deviations get a local patch.\n- **Review cadence:** weekly human review of zone fill vs cost; monthly mandate review.\n\n**Q30. Why is \"agents operate optimisers, they don't replace them\" the right design (AI–OR symbiosis)?**\nThe solver does the constrained math optimally for one snapshot. The agent senses when to re-solve, translates goals into weights and constraints, and governs execution (L2/L3/L4). JD.com's dual engine is the model. Neither layer works alone.\n\n**Q31. Your procurement NLP system extracts payment terms, addresses, penalty clauses and contacts. Where do you put the accuracy budget and the human?**\nOn penalty, liability and indemnity clauses: a domain-tuned model (94.7% vs 79.3% for general LLMs), high recall, and a human always reviewing. Interpretation of those clauses is never fully automated. Contacts and addresses can use a general model.\n\n**Q32. Walmart–Pactum negotiates with thousands of suppliers. What is the category manager's job now?**\nDesigning the envelope (tradable levers, walk-away limits, escalation triggers) and auditing outcomes. Not reviewing every deal, which would destroy the economics. The mandate is the management.\n\n**Q33. An agent costs ₹5 L/month for 2,500 decisions; each decision saves about ₹150. Tune or retire?**\n- Cost per decision = ₹200 vs value ₹150, a ratio of 0.75×.\n- **Tune first:** cache, shorten context, batch, use a cheaper tier, call less often.\n- If cost falls below about ₹120 per decision, keep it. If not, **retire** to the next-best process. Enforce the spend cap in code.\n\n**Q34. A procurement pilot shows 25% staff-efficiency gains, but the CFO sees no P&L change. Explain and fix.**\nThis is the efficiency-capture gap (McKinsey: 20–30% efficiency, only 1–3% captured). Freed time wasn't redeployed and savings weren't banked. Fix it with a capture plan: redeploy freed hours to strategic sourcing, bake negotiated savings into budgets, and track realised ₹ against run cost monthly.\n\n## Unit 5 — GenAI design, multi-agent, responsible AI\n\n**Q35. GenAI produced 30 disruption scenarios for your network redesign. How do you turn them into a design decision?**\nApply the Scenario Portfolio Checklist:\n1. **Generate wide.**\n2. **Label** each plausible, stretch or implausible by mechanism and precedent.\n3. **Select two to survive** (one plausible, one high-impact stretch).\n4. **Specify the capability and its cost** (flex capacity, dual sourcing, buffer).\n5. **Guard the label:** name the mechanism before committing capital.\n\nThen run a multi-objective optimiser (cost vs CO₂) across the scenarios: GenAI for range, OR for rigour.\n\n**Q36. What is the collaboration paradox, and which design fixes it?**\nSmarter individual agents with no coordination make system-wide oscillation (bullwhip) worse, because they overreact to each other's orders faster. The fixes:\n- a **shared end-customer demand signal**;\n- **rate limits and order smoothing**;\n- **trained coordination or consensus-seeking orchestration**.\n\nJointly trained agents beat both central and uncoordinated agents on cost and variability (POM 2025).\n\n**Q37. Retail demand variance is 250, wholesaler orders 600, factory orders 1,800. Compute the bullwhip and recommend.**\n- Ratios: 2.4 and 3.0, a total of **7.2×**.\n- Share POS data with every tier and agent, smooth orders (e.g. ±10% per week), reduce batching, and name one owner of the shared plan.\n\n**Q38. Apply ACAP to a credit-limit agent running at L4 for dealers.**\n- **Accountability:** a named owner (Head of Credit).\n- **Capability limits:** L4 only within ±20% of the prior limit; anything larger goes to L2 approval.\n- **Auditability:** every decision is logged with its features and reasons.\n- **Proportionality:** a human reviews limit cuts for dealers with more than ₹X outstanding.\n- **Post-incident learning:** each wrongful cut triggers a review and model update.\n\n**Q39. Under the EU AI Act, your vendor says \"compliance is handled by the platform\". What is still yours?**\nThe deployer duties: use the system per its instructions, assign competent **human oversight** (often meaning L2 for high-risk decisions), check input-data relevance, monitor it, keep logs and report incidents. The provider owns conformity assessment, CE marking, registration and documentation. Write the duty split into the contract.\n\n**Q40. Two days before the board, you discover your L4 forecasting agent's data under-represents your fastest-growing region. What do you do?**\n- Step it down to **L2**.\n- Log the incident.\n- Quantify the likely allocation skew and lost sales.\n- **Disclose it to the board as a governance finding.**\n- Fix the data (re-weighting, representation checks), test for parity on a holdout, then re-promote.\n\nGuardrails only count when you use them when it costs you something.\n\n## Chain Reaction — strategy questions\n\n**Q41. Your team wants to build an orchestrator with gate D3·P3·T3; you are at D2·P3·T2. Advise.**\nDon't build yet. A below-gate build runs at full cost for a fraction of the effect, with cut odds (B14's orchestrator failed at 12%, B11's at 20%). Raise Tech to 3 first, which also unlocks MIX (rail), then Data. **Sequence beats speed** (A7-Kimi K3: zero below-gate builds, #2).\n\n**Q42. Why did \"transformation theatre\" teams with 12–13 agents finish 15th–18th while A8 finished 3rd on six agents?**\n**Building is not operating.** Theatre teams built below gate and didn't run the estate (rail, distribution plans, a right-sized chip book), so failures and breaches hit reputation and trust. A8 had all foundations at L5, so a small estate was amplified. Value = profit × reputation × trust.\n\n**Q43. Kaveri lost money in a congestion month and blamed bad luck. Give the physics explanation.**\nUnrouted plant stock auto-pushes to the home RDC by road, and zones pull only from their own region's RDC. With no rail booked and no distribution plan, stock piled near the plants while distant premium zones stocked out. The congestion charge was the visible cost; the **contribution forgone** on unfilled premium demand (₹11 cr) was the real one. Fix: a Distribution Planning Agent under a mandate, with a rail floor. See [Kaveri](#/pyq-kaveri).\n\n**Q44. You have 24 tokens. Upkeep is 9; you generate one plan and regenerate twice. How many tokens remain, and what discipline follows?**\n9 + 2 + 2 = 13, so **11 remain**. Regenerate only on a real trigger. Retire agents whose value per token is lowest (termination costs 2 and saves their upkeep from the next month). Never leave a gate-raising action unfunded for a low-value agent.\n\n**Q45. What four lessons did the faculty draw from the season?**\n1. Sequence beats speed (build on the D·P·T foundations you have laid).\n2. Building is not operating (book rail, file distribution plans, size the chip book to real demand).\n3. Governance is the multiplier you feel late (value = profit × reputation × trust).\n4. Efficiency is a real strategy (lean works if you still buy the insurance).\n"}, {"slug": "mcq-bank", "label": "MCQ Bank", "tag": "MCQ", "group": "practice", "title": "MCQ Bank — every class question, plus exam-style extras", "md": "# MCQ Bank — every class question, plus exam-style extras\nYour faculty asked **\"one question at a time\"** in every session 📘, and those class questions are the best predictor of how the case questions will be judged. **Part A** reproduces **all class questions from Sessions 1–20** with the official answer and the one-line WHY. **Part B** adds **exam-style questions** on numericals, SCM basics and Chain Reaction. Turn on **Self-test** (top bar or press `T`) to hide the answers and reveal them one by one.\n\n> The exam itself is case-based, not MCQ. But each class question is a mini-case: the reasoning in the WHY lines is exactly what earns marks in a long answer.\n\n---\n\n## Part A — Class questions, Sessions 1–20 📘\n\n### Session 1 — AI in operations: claims, Q vs S, decisions\n\n**Q1. A vendor demo shows software that reads supplier emails, drafts replies and, once approved, posts confirmed delivery dates into your ERP. In precise course vocabulary it is: (a) A predictive AI model (b) A generative AI copilot (c) An agentic AI system (d) Robotic process automation**\n**Answer: (c) An agentic AI system** — it perceives, reasons and ACTS on systems of record. Approval-gated action puts it low on the autonomy scale. A copilot stops at drafting; RPA follows fixed rules. *Precise vocabulary is the first defence against agent-washing.*\n\n**Q2. A distributor holds ₹8 cr cycle stock (weekly truckloads) and ₹2 cr safety stock. A vendor claims its forecasting AI will cut total inventory 30%. Which single calculation kills or saves the claim? (a) Size the safety-stock share of total inventory (b) Compare the vendor's price to savings (c) Check the model's accuracy benchmark (d) Compute total logistics cost**\n**Answer: (a) Size the safety-stock share** — forecasting attacks S, not Q. S = 2/10 = 20% < 30%, so the claim fails arithmetic before any question of model quality arises. *Follow the cost pool, not the technology.*\n\n**Q3. Forecast error improves from ±40 to ±15 units, but orders must still be placed in full pallets of 240. Most likely business impact? (a) Large inventory savings (b) Better customer service (c) Little to no business impact (d) Lower transport cost**\n**Answer: (c) Little to no business impact** — decisions are discrete. Accuracy pays only until the decision stops changing. *Precision pays only when a decision changes.*\n\n**Q4. A CEO proposes replacing the planning department with \"a thousand small agents\" next quarter. Strongest reason it fails TODAY, even if each agent works? (a) Agents are too expensive (b) Regulators would prohibit it (c) Agents amplify each other's actions (d) Planners would strike**\n**Answer: (c) Agents amplify each other's actions** — decentralisation needs bounded scopes and coordination structure (shared signals, circuit breakers). *Stability is mechanism design, not intelligence.*\n\n**Q5. A consultant says you are \"behind\": 44% of companies deploy supply-chain AI and the agentic market will hit $53B by 2030. Correct executive response? (a) Approve, the market has spoken (b) Wait for the market to mature (c) Reject AI entirely (d) Ask which adopters show measurable value**\n**Answer: (d) Ask which adopters show measurable value** — only about 30% of adopters advance beyond copilots, and about 95% of GenAI pilots show no P&L impact. Adoption measures purchasing, not value.\n\n**Q6. A vendor's AI \"learns your business and answers any question\". The soundest first test is: (a) Which decision it changes, and its benchmark (b) Training-set size (c) Whether it uses deep learning (d) Vendor's customer count**\n**Answer: (a) Which decision it changes, and its benchmark** — capability is proven by the decision it moves and the naive benchmark it beats. *Judge mechanism and benchmark, not momentum.*\n\n### Session 2 — Readiness I: data and technology\n\n**Q7. A pilot promises \"real-time response to demand shifts\", but 70% of sales data arrives by overnight batch. Which data-quality dimension binds? (a) Accuracy (b) Validity (c) Timeliness (d) Uniqueness**\n**Answer: (c) Timeliness** — quality dimensions are per use case. 24-hour-old data is fine for weekly planning and fatal for real-time sensing.\n\n**Q8. Spend analytics shows the largest supplier at 4% of spend, but a master-data audit finds it duplicated across six vendor records. What did the analytics measure? (a) Nothing wrong (b) The supplier should be dropped (c) A minor finance issue (d) Record-keeping, not true concentration**\n**Answer: (d) Record-keeping, not true concentration** — humans merge duplicates mentally; models take records literally and agents transact on them (uniqueness). *Data defects compound at machine speed.*\n\n**Q9. Two firms buy the same demand-sensing model. Firm A has one owner per data domain and nightly DQ reports; Firm B has neither. Best predictor of success? (a) Firm A's data governance and accessibility (b) Vendor's benchmark accuracy (c) GPU capacity (d) Firm size**\n**Answer: (a) Firm A's data governance** — identical technology, opposite outcomes. *Foundations before intelligence; semantic before agentic.*\n\n**Q10. One vendor-facing API credential was phished last year, there is no inventory of which systems talk to which, and the CIO proposes purchasing agents with ERP write access. Verdict? (a) Approve (b) Approve with a \"be careful\" prompt (c) Block until API inventory and access controls exist (d) Outsource security review**\n**Answer: (c) Block until API inventory and access controls exist** — write-capable agents extend the attack surface, and prompts are not controls. *Autonomy raises every bar.*\n\n**Q11. One firm deployed an LLM planning assistant over unified data and existing optimisation models (success); another bolted it onto fragmented spreadsheets (pilot death). The difference? (a) Model quality (b) Prompt skill (c) Prior foundations in place (d) Budget**\n**Answer: (c) Prior foundations** — data preparation consumes 60–80% of successful projects.\n\n**Q12. Scorecard [Data 2, Masters 1, Architecture 3, Security 2, Compute 5]. Ambition ceiling and first move? (a) Deploy agents (b) Hire data scientists (c) Freeze AI for two years (d) Predictive pilot on the cleanest domain first**\n**Answer: (d) Predictive pilot on the cleanest domain** — masters at 1 poison everything and security at 2 forbids write access. Compute 5 is a stranded asset. *The scorecard sets the ceiling.*\n\n### Session 3 — Readiness II: process and organisation\n\n**Q13. Match to decision level and AI posture: (a) 5-year network redesign; (b) AI-assisted S&OP scenarios; (c) autonomous reorder of spares under ₹50,000. (a) a-operational/automate, b-strategic/augment, c-tactical/delegate (b) a-strategic/input-only, b-tactical/augment, c-operational/delegate (c) all strategic (d) a-tactical, b-operational, c-strategic**\n**Answer: (b)** — strategic = input only; tactical = augment; operational, frequent and reversible = the delegation frontier.\n\n**Q14. Before scoping any model to \"add AI to procurement\", a structured method insists you first: (a) Pick a cloud vendor (b) Map the process (c) Hire a data scientist (d) Estimate accuracy**\n**Answer: (b) Map the process first** — *standardise the process, preserve the judgement.*\n\n**Q15. Design A shows planners the back-tested record and allows ±15% adjustments with reasons; Design B mandates unmodified use. Behavioural evidence predicts: (a) B wins (b) A wins, bounded control keeps trust (c) No difference (d) Both fail**\n**Answer: (b) A wins** — algorithm aversion is reduced by slight modifiability. *Trust is designed, not demanded.*\n\n**Q16. Each planner will supervise 12 agent workstreams, \"checking anything odd\". Research says: (a) Fail, vigilance and skills decay (b) Works well (c) Only if salaries rise (d) Works if agents rarely err**\n**Answer: (a) Fail** — the ironies of automation (+14% mental effort, +19% overload for close monitors). Oversight is not a safety net; hard guardrails are.\n\n**Q17. Planners are paid on service and turns; corporate adds \"recommendation adherence\". Rational behaviour? (a) Full adoption (b) Resignations (c) Rebellion (d) Adherence tied to their metrics**\n**Answer: (d)** — when a measure becomes a target it stops measuring. *Incentives eat algorithms.*\n\n**Q18. A business case promises \"25% inventory reduction in 6 months, company-wide\", set before any diagnostic. Gate-compatible rewrite? (a) Keep it (b) Remove targets (c) Cut to 20% (d) Scoped, baselined and evidence-gated**\n**Answer: (d)** — *transform in survivable doses* (the Continental Parts lesson).\n\n### Session 4 — Agentic AI systems\n\n**Q19. \"Our AI agent reviews your inventory dashboard every morning and emails a prioritised action list.\" Is it an agent? (a) Yes, it acts autonomously (b) No, it only recommends; nothing is executed (c) Yes, if the emails are good (d) No, because it runs on a schedule**\n**Answer: (b)** — an agent needs goal ownership plus tool-mediated action within an envelope. This one is a recommender (agent-washing).\n\n**Q20. A negotiation agent closes deals with 2,400 tail suppliers inside category-manager mandates. The manager's job now is: (a) Gone (b) Reviewing every deal (c) Designing the envelope and auditing outcomes (d) Negotiating the big deals only**\n**Answer: (c)** — *the mandate IS the management.* Reviewing every deal would delete the economics.\n\n**Q21. Your replenishment agent and your distributor's ordering agent react to each other's orders. Prediction? (a) Order oscillation, unless signals are shared (b) Stable efficiency (c) Deadlock (d) Nothing**\n**Answer: (a)** — decentralised LLM agents mostly amplify bullwhip.\n\n**Q22. A system prompt says \"Never exceed ₹5 lakh per order; never order from unapproved suppliers\". Sufficient control? (a) Yes (b) Yes, if long enough (c) No, prompts are not enforceable controls (d) Agents can't be controlled**\n**Answer: (c)** — *guardrails live outside the model.* Hard limits belong in the surrounding system.\n\n**Q23. Rank for agentic suitability: (a) strategic partnership negotiation; (b) tail-spend reorders; (c) robot routing in your own warehouse. (a) a>b>c (b) equal (c) b>a>c (d) c>b>a**\n**Answer: (d) c > b > a** — the five-factor screen: frequency, reversibility, data/feedback, rule clarity, stakes.\n\n**Q24. Where should a human sit in an \"autonomous\" returns agent? (a) Nowhere (b) On every case (c) On low-confidence, high-impact cases only (d) Only after failure**\n**Answer: (c)** — an engineered escalation path.\n\n### Session 5 — AI platforms\n\n**Q25. A multi-month GPU waitlist in your region; two vendors blame each other. Response? (a) Pick whoever promises faster (b) Sequence around the shared constraint (c) Cancel (d) Build a data centre**\n**Answer: (b)** — upstream physics binds every hyperscaler. *Every platform is a supply chain.*\n\n**Q26. An average CPG firm wants to build its own planning platform \"like the famous 24-hour retailer\". Build vs buy is decided by: (a) Budget (b) Engineering pride (c) Real differentiation only (d) Vendor relationships**\n**Answer: (c)** — *own the differentiating loop; rent the rest.*\n\n**Q27. Copilot token spend is 3.2× forecast after six months. Was the platform wrong? (a) No, fix usage discipline (b) Yes, switch (c) Ban the copilot (d) Ignore it**\n**Answer: (a)** — FinOps for AI. TCO includes consumption governance.\n\n**Q28. Before building a custom OCR pipeline for invoices, ask: (a) Which DL framework (b) Does a good service already exist? (c) How many GPUs (d) Project name**\n**Answer: (b)** — commodity capabilities are mature APIs.\n\n**Q29. A cheaper, feature-rich platform can't enforce per-agent spend limits or produce complete action logs, and you plan an L4 procurement agent. Verdict? (a) Buy it (b) Buy and add governance later (c) Disqualified, no envelope enforcement (d) Negotiate a discount**\n**Answer: (c)** — *filters in order; governance before features.*\n\n**Q30. Supplier-risk scores feed EU-plant renewals; the vendor says compliance \"is handled by the platform\". You must verify: (a) Nothing (b) ISO certificates (c) Data residency only (d) The provider/deployer duty split, which is yours**\n**Answer: (d)** — oversight of your USE is yours by law (EU AI Act). This underpins Trishul A4.\n\n### Session 6 — ML foundations\n\n**Q31. You're asked to validate a k-means customer clustering's accuracy. What's wrong? (a) There's no ground truth to be accurate against (b) k-means is outdated (c) Too little data (d) Use supervised only**\n**Answer: (a)** — *the decision, not a metric, validates unsupervised work.*\n\n**Q32. You have three years of sales but no stockout-corrected true-demand field. Core difficulty? (a) Algorithm choice (b) Cloud vendor (c) GPUs (d) The label you need doesn't exist yet**\n**Answer: (d)** — frame the decision and construct the label first.\n\n**Q33. An 87%-accurate price model: Team 1 displays predictions; Team 2 auto-buys inventory. What decides whether 87% is enough? (a) Industry standard (b) Architecture (c) The decision's cost and reversibility (d) Training-set size**\n**Answer: (c)** — the Zillow lesson.\n\n**Q34. A delay model's backtest is stellar, but one feature is goods-receipt date. What happened? (a) Nothing (b) Weight it higher (c) More data (d) Data leakage, the feature isn't knowable in time**\n**Answer: (d)** — the most common cause of the demo-to-production cliff.\n\n**Q35. A model scores 92%, the naive rule 90.5%, and orders come in full-truck multiples. Verdict? (a) Excellent (b) Unproven, barely beats naive (c) Failure (d) Needs deep learning**\n**Answer: (b)** — *no model without a naive benchmark.*\n\n### Session 7 — Tools lab\n\n**Q36. A model beats naive by 6% overall, wins on staples, loses on promotions and new items. Deploy: (a) Everywhere (b) Nowhere (c) Where it wins; keep naive elsewhere (d) Retrain until it wins everywhere**\n**Answer: (c)** — champion–challenger by segment.\n\n**Q37. An analyst's model beats the incumbent by 8% but runs from manual exports on her laptop. What does the firm own? (a) Little durable, a one-person demo (b) A capability (c) An 8% cost reduction (d) A deployable API**\n**Answer: (a)** — *a model in a notebook is not a capability.*\n\n**Q38. A churn model shows 99% accuracy and only 3% churn. Most likely problem? (a) Excellent (b) Accuracy is misleading; check for leakage (c) Too few features (d) Wrong language**\n**Answer: (b)** — \"never churn\" already scores 97%.\n\n### Sessions 8 & 11 — Demand forecasting\n\n**Q39. Two SKUs both forecast 1,000; A swings ±50, B ±400; the rule stocks forecast + 2 weeks of supply for both. What is wrong? (a) The rule ignores demand uncertainty (b) Nothing (c) B needs a better point forecast (d) Discontinue A**\n**Answer: (a)** — *forecast the distribution, not the number; the quantile IS the decision.*\n\n**Q40. Pitch C: \"Our autonomous agent retrains and adjusts forecasts in real time with zero planner effort.\" Most clarifying question? (a) Which DL architecture? (b) Does it update forecasts, or execute orders? (c) Customer count? (d) Is it GenAI?**\n**Answer: (b)** — execution is an autonomy grant (S4 ladder), not a forecasting feature.\n\n**Q41. Model X has lower average error; Model Y is worse on average but far better at flagging pre-promotion spikes. For safety-stock and promo readiness choose: (a) X (b) Moving average (c) Whichever trains faster (d) Y**\n**Answer: (d) Y** — the metric must match the decision; the decision lives in the tail.\n\n**Q42. A politically important partner's forecasts mislead the model. What should the system do? (a) Use them anyway (b) Average them (c) Exclude all external data (d) Weight them by measured contribution**\n**Answer: (d)** — the zero-weight finding. *Signals earn their weights; politics are managed, not obeyed.*\n\n### Sessions 9 & 12 — Forecasting tiers\n\n**Q43. Cloud AutoML beats Prophet by 2% on Trident's data at 6× the cost, with skills Trident lacks. Verdict? (a) Prophet tier wins today (b) Cloud wins (c) Excel wins (d) Run both forever**\n**Answer: (a)** — the verdict is multi-column; name the flip condition.\n\n**Q44. A 12-SKU bakery asks whether to move from Excel to cloud ML \"to modernise\". Honest advice? (a) Yes (b) A spreadsheet already fits this (c) Only SageMaker (d) No forecasting needed**\n**Answer: (b)** — tiers are workload matches, not maturity badges.\n\n**Q45. A notebook must run nightly for 40,000 SKUs and feed planning by 6 a.m. The critical production question is: (a) Chart library (b) R-squared (c) Rewrite in a new language (d) Whether it runs reliably on schedule**\n**Answer: (d)** — the SLA is the product.\n\n**Q46. 60 SKUs, 4 dealerships, an Excel-only analyst, lenders want an auditable rationale; an LSTM demo shows bullwhip charts. Buy: (a) LSTM tier (b) AutoML regardless of skills (c) Spreadsheet/smoothing tier (d) Prophet always**\n**Answer: (c)** — the LSTM needs Data Maturity 3+ and gives the opaque output a lender won't accept.\n\n### Session 10 — Inventory optimisation\n\n**Q47. A vendor's backtest shows DRL beating your tuned basestock by 20%; a myopic rule is known to be within about 5% of optimal. The 20% most likely contains: (a) A breakthrough (b) Overfitting or a weak baseline (c) Proof to replace planners (d) Nothing verifiable**\n**Answer: (b)** — the maximum headroom is about 5%. *Benchmarks with a theory-informed ceiling.*\n\n**Q48. A spare part sells 0,0,0,4,0,0,2,0 units. Judging models by weekly MAPE is broken because: (a) MAPE is undefined on zero actuals (b) It's fine (c) Use daily MAPE (d) Use R²**\n**Answer: (a)** — use calibrated distributions and service per rupee of inventory.\n\n**Q49. Option (a): improve forecasts 15% and keep the weeks-of-supply rule. Option (b): keep forecasts and optimise the policy on the distribution and costs. More valuable? (a) a (b) Buy DRL (c) Equal (d) b, the conversion step is where value dies**\n**Answer: (d)** — *optimise the policy, not the forecast.*\n\n**Q50. Vendor plan: \"shadow mode 4 weeks, then full control of spare-parts ordering\". Missing? (a) Nothing (b) Bounds, fallback and a real evaluation (c) Longer shadow only (d) More data**\n**Answer: (b)** — four weeks of intermittent demand is statistically empty.\n\n**Q51. An RL agent beats the classical policy by 18% in simulation on two years of stable demand. Essential safeguard? (a) Deploy now (b) Cage it with a benchmark and guardrails (c) More layers (d) Switch cloud**\n**Answer: (b)** — *learned policies wear proven guardrails.*\n\n### Session 13 — Predictive maintenance\n\n**Q52. A model flags a bearing at 68% failure risk within 2 weeks, but every bearing is inspected weekly and replaced if worn. Act? (a) Yes, always above 50% (b) No, the weekly inspection already catches it; the prediction doesn't change the decision (c) Daily inspections until 90% (d) Ignore models entirely**\n**Answer: (b)** — a prediction earns its keep only when it changes what or when you decide.\n\n### Session 14 — Logistics\n\n**Q53. A road lane loses 40% capacity, the Mumbai zone is about to stock out, express is costly and rail is too slow. Do: (a) Hold and wait (b) Split: express now, rail as backfill (c) Reroute everything via another RDC (d) Cancel and reallocate**\n**Answer: (b)** — rebalance the mix in real time.\n\n### Session 15 — Optimisation\n\n**Q54. Identical cost weights for all zones; the solver routes nearly all by rail and fill slips in the zone with 40% of premium-margin sales. What went wrong? (a) Solver error (b) A higher cost weight or service floor for the high-value zone was needed before solving (c) Override lane by lane (d) Unavoidable**\n**Answer: (b)** — *a perfectly solved LP with the wrong mandate gives a perfectly wrong plan.*\n\n### Session 16 — NLP procurement\n\n**Q55. A fixed tuning budget can push one clause type to the top accuracy tier: (a) Payment terms (b) Address formatting (c) Penalty and indemnification (d) Contact details**\n**Answer: (c)** — misreads create uncapped exposure. *The accuracy bar scales with what the clause decides.*\n\n### Session 17 — Token economics\n\n**Q56. A negotiation agent's run cost doubles after repricing; quality unchanged. Do: (a) Keep as-is (b) Retire now (c) Compare value-per-decision with cost-per-decision, then tune or retire (d) Hope prices fall**\n**Answer: (c)** — tune before you retire.\n\n### Session 18 — GenAI scenarios\n\n**Q57. GenAI outputs \"a celebrity post triggers a 400% spike in one paint colour in 72 hours\"; someone wants to fund flex capacity now. Do: (a) Fund it (b) Discard all GenAI output (c) Label it stretch, check mechanism and base rate, seek a cheap reversible mitigation (d) Regenerate 100× and take the mode**\n**Answer: (c)** — vivid ≠ evidenced. Label before you act.\n\n### Session 19 — Multi-agent coordination\n\n**Q58. One agent orders +15% \"to be safe\"; two tiers up another doubles its order. Best single preventive change? (a) Faster processors (b) Every agent sees the same real end-customer demand (c) Local negotiation only (d) More watchers**\n**Answer: (b)** — *coordination beats speed.*\n\n### Session 20 — Responsible AI\n\n**Q59. An L4 forecasting agent outperformed all season; two days before the board, training data is found to under-represent your fastest-growing market. Do: (a) Say nothing until after (b) Patch quietly (c) Drop to L2, log and disclose, and bring it to the board as a governance finding (d) Wait for next quarter's audit**\n**Answer: (c)** — *guardrails only count if you use them when it costs you something.*\n\n---\n\n## Part B — Exam-style extras (numericals, SCM basics, Chain Reaction) 📗\n\n**Q60. Cycle stock ₹30 cr, safety stock ₹10 cr. A vendor claims 40% inventory reduction from forecasting. Maximum possible reduction from forecasting alone: (a) 40% (b) 33% (c) 25% (d) 10%**\n**Answer: (c) 25%** — S/(Q/2 + S) = 10/40. A perfect forecast removes only S.\n\n**Q61. D = 10,000/yr, S = ₹500 per order, H = ₹40 per unit per year. EOQ ≈ (a) 354 (b) 500 (c) 250 (d) 1,000**\n**Answer: (b) 500** — √(2 × 10,000 × 500 / 40) = √250,000 = 500.\n\n**Q62. σ weekly = 100, L = 4 weeks, z = 1.65. Safety stock ≈ (a) 165 (b) 330 (c) 660 (d) 400**\n**Answer: (b) 330** — 1.65 × 100 × √4.\n\n**Q63. Halving lead time (all else equal) cuts safety stock by about: (a) 50% (b) 29% (c) 71% (d) 0%**\n**Answer: (b) 29%** — SS ∝ √L, and 1 − 1/√2 ≈ 0.29.\n\n**Q64. Cu = ₹80, Co = ₹20. The newsvendor critical ratio and the stocking quantile: (a) 0.25 (b) 0.5 (c) 0.8 (d) 0.2**\n**Answer: (c) 0.8** — 80/(80 + 20). Stock the 80th percentile.\n\n**Q65. Zones: A 10,000 units at 70% fill, B 2,000 at 95%, C 2,000 at 95%. The simple average is 86.7%. The volume-weighted fill is: (a) 86.7% (b) 77.1% (c) 80% (d) 90%**\n**Answer: (b) 77.1%** — (7,000 + 1,900 + 1,900)/14,000. *An average can hide the mix.*\n\n**Q66. Variance of consumer demand 200, factory orders 3,000. Bullwhip ratio: (a) 1.5 (b) 15 (c) 6 (d) 0.07**\n**Answer: (b) 15.**\n\n**Q67. A spike detector flags 50 weeks, 35 correctly; there were 70 real spikes. Precision and recall: (a) 70%, 50% (b) 50%, 70% (c) 70%, 70% (d) 35%, 50%**\n**Answer: (a) Precision 35/50 = 70%; recall 35/70 = 50%.**\n\n**Q68. An agent costs ₹3 L/month for 600 decisions, each worth ₹400. Ratio and action: (a) 0.8×, tune first then retire if still below 1 (b) 1.25×, keep (c) 0.8×, keep (d) 2×, keep**\n**Answer: (a)** — cost per decision ₹500 vs value ₹400.\n\n**Q69. A missed failure costs ₹40 L and an unnecessary early repair ₹2 L. Act when failure probability exceeds about: (a) 5% (b) 20% (c) 50% (d) 95%**\n**Answer: (a) ≈4.8%** — 2/(2 + 40).\n\n**Q70. Express ₹500/unit, rail ₹150/unit. 1,000 units are needed, 300 urgently. Cost of the split vs all-express: (a) ₹2.55 L vs ₹5 L (b) ₹5 L vs ₹2.55 L (c) ₹1.5 L vs ₹5 L (d) equal**\n**Answer: (a)** — 300 × 500 + 700 × 150 = 1.5 L + 1.05 L = ₹2.55 L.\n\n**Q71. In Chain Reaction, company value is: (a) profit + reputation + trust (b) profit × reputation × trust (c) cash × trust (d) fill × profit**\n**Answer: (b)** — governance is the multiplier you feel late.\n\n**Q72. In Chain Reaction, the distMode setting MIX (rail in the mix) is: (a) Always available (b) Locked until tech maturity 3 (c) Only for EVs (d) Available from Round 1**\n**Answer: (b).**\n\n**Q73. Monthly decision tokens in Chain Reaction: (a) 12 (b) 24 (c) 48 (d) unlimited — with 2 tokens to generate a plan and 1 to regenerate**\n**Answer: (b) 24.**\n\n**Q74. A below-gate agent build in Chain Reaction: (a) is blocked (b) runs at full cost for a fraction of the effect, with cut build odds (c) is free (d) is automatically upgraded**\n**Answer: (b)** — sequence beats speed.\n\n**Q75. Which archetype won the Chain Reaction season? (a) Transformation theatre (b) Foundations, not run (c) Compounders (d) Lean operators**\n**Answer: (c) Compounders** — B12-Titans (#1) and A7-Kimi K3 (#2) built on laid foundations and kept operating.\n\n**Q76. Unrouted plant stock in Chain Reaction: (a) stays at the plant (b) auto-pushes to the plant's home RDC by road (c) is scrapped (d) goes by rail**\n**Answer: (b)** — this is why \"file a distribution plan\" is an operating must (A2-Doomsday's 338 cars; Kaveri).\n\n**Q77. Rail in Chain Reaction is: (a) ~25% cheaper than road, booked a month ahead, charged whether used or not (b) more expensive than road (c) unlimited (d) same-day**\n**Answer: (a).**\n\n**Q78. The FAST build option in Chain Reaction gives: (a) 0.85× cost at quality −18 (b) 1.25× cost at quality +6 (c) no change (d) a failure chance**\n**Answer: (a)** — CAREFUL is 1.25× over two rounds at +6; neither option carries a failure chance.\n\n**Q79. The four elements of the Q-learning update include all EXCEPT: (a) learning rate α (b) reward r (c) discount γ (d) the confusion matrix**\n**Answer: (d).**\n\n**Q80. Which tool is a Java constraint-satisfaction planning engine using metaheuristics with hard and soft scores? (a) Prophet (b) OptaPlanner (c) RapidMiner (d) SageMaker**\n**Answer: (b) OptaPlanner** — OR-Tools is Google's suite of LP/MIP, CP-SAT and VRP solvers.\n\n**Q81. The domain-tuned vs general LLM accuracy on contract-clause risk was: (a) 94.7% vs 79.3% (b) 99% vs 95% (c) 80% vs 60% (d) equal**\n**Answer: (a)** — grounding matters more than fluency.\n\n**Q82. McKinsey's agentic-procurement pilot: efficiency gain vs value captured: (a) 20–30% vs 1–3% (b) 5% vs 5% (c) 50% vs 30% (d) 1–3% vs 20–30%**\n**Answer: (a)** — the efficiency-capture gap.\n\n**Q83. Share of firms with governance mature enough for autonomous agents (S20): (a) ~20% (b) ~50% (c) ~80% (d) ~95%**\n**Answer: (a) ~20%** — \"that gap is where incidents live\".\n\n**Q84. In the spare-parts deployment, fill rose at constant inventory from: (a) 77% to 85% (b) 85% to 95% (c) 60% to 77% (d) 89% to 91%**\n**Answer: (a)** — or about $20M inventory released at constant service.\n\n**Q85. Which is NOT a bullwhip cause (Lee et al.)? (a) Demand-signal processing (b) Order batching (c) Price fluctuations (d) Sharing POS data with suppliers**\n**Answer: (d)** — sharing POS is a cure.\n"}, {"slug": "glossary", "label": "Glossary A–Z", "tag": "A–Z", "group": "practice", "title": "Glossary — Agentic AI in Ops & SCM, A–Z", "md": "# Glossary — Agentic AI in Ops & SCM, A–Z\nOne- or two-line, exam-ready definitions of the terms used across the 20 sessions, the Chain Reaction simulation and basic SCM. 📘 = the term comes from your sessions or syllabus; 📗 = a standard SCM/AI definition added for completeness. The **Flashcards** and **Quiz** views are built from this page.\n\n## A\n- **ACAP governance checklist** 📘: Five-check self-test for any agent. S19: Accountability, Capability boundaries, Alignment, Process oversight (+ coordination mechanism). S20: Accountability, Capability limits, Auditability, Proportionality, Post-incident learning.\n- **Adaptive forecasting loop** 📘: Continuous re-estimation as new data arrives (weights update, the plan refreshes) at a deliberate cadence that balances responsiveness against plan nervousness.\n- **Agent-washing** 📘: Marketing a recommender, copilot or script as an \"agent\". The test is goal ownership plus tool-mediated action within an envelope.\n- **Agentic AI** 📘: AI that perceives, reasons and ACTS on systems of record toward a goal, using tools within a bounded envelope (e.g. posts delivery dates into the ERP or closes a negotiation).\n- **Agentic suitability screen** 📘: A five-factor score (frequency, reversibility, data/feedback, rule clarity, stakes). 20+ = strong candidate; 12–19 = bounded pilot; < 12 = human-led.\n- **AI–OR symbiosis** 📘: Agents operate optimisation solvers (deciding when to re-solve and what to optimise) rather than replacing them. JD.com's dual engine is the paradigm.\n- **Algorithm aversion** 📘: People abandon algorithms faster than humans after the same errors. Slight modifiability (e.g. ±15% overrides) restores use and trust.\n- **Antifragility** 📘: A design goal of gaining from disruption, not just surviving it (e.g. flex capacity that captures a rival's stranded demand).\n- **Auditability** 📘: Logs detailed enough to reconstruct what an agent did and why, after the fact.\n- **Autonomy ladder (L1–L5)** 📘: L1 inform → L2 recommend (human approves) → L3 act within bounds and escalate exceptions → L4 autonomous with monitoring → L5 fully autonomous. Autonomy is granted per decision.\n- **AWS SageMaker** 📘: Amazon's cloud ML build/train/deploy service (with the no-code Canvas variant), the Tier-3 forecasting option. Amazon trained DeepFleet on it.\n- **Azure AI** 📘: Microsoft's AI platform (Azure OpenAI, AI Foundry, Copilot Studio). It fits Microsoft-centric estates by data gravity and skills.\n\n## B\n- **Bake-off ladder** 📘: Count it, audit readiness, name the skill, check governance, bake it off on your own data, all before buying a forecasting tier.\n- **Basestock policy** 📗: Order up to a target level set by the demand distribution over the lead time. Auditable and near-optimal when tuned.\n- **Below-gate build** 📘: A Chain Reaction agent built under its D·P·T foundation gate. It runs at full cost for a fraction of the effect, with cut build odds (e.g. 12%, 20%).\n- **Blue/green deployment** 📘: Run two environments and switch traffic to the new one after QA, with no downtime.\n- **Building is not operating** 📘: Season lesson 2. Estate size never predicted rank; operating it (booking rail, filing distribution plans, right-sizing the chip book) did.\n- **Bullwhip effect** 📗: Order variability amplifying upstream. Caused by demand-signal processing, batching, price swings and shortage gaming. Measured as Var(orders)/Var(demand).\n- **Bus factor** 📘: The number of people whose absence kills a capability. A notebook only one analyst can run has a bus factor of 1.\n\n## C\n- **Calibration** 📘: Whether stated probabilities match reality (e.g. 90% of actuals fall inside the 90% band). Used to score distribution forecasts.\n- **Capability checklist** 📘: Reproducible, monitored, owned, documented fallback, versioned data and model, permissions and logging. It separates a capability from a demo.\n- **Chain Reaction** 📘: The course's ten-round supply-chain simulation (3 plants, 4 RDCs, 7 zones; HATCH/SUV/EV), scored as value = profit × reputation × trust.\n- **Champion–challenger by segment** 📘: Deploy each method where it demonstrably wins (model on staples, naive on promotions) and revisit as data accrues.\n- **Claim audit (Four-Question)** 📘: Test every AI claim: what baseline, measured how, which stock/cost pool, and what decision changes? (S1)\n- **Clause-stakes checklist** 📘: List the clauses, name the decision each feeds, set the bar by error cost, pick the tier (general vs domain-tuned), name the never-automate exception.\n- **Collaboration paradox** 📘: Smarter individual agents with no coordination make system oscillation (bullwhip) worse, because they overreact to each other faster.\n- **Command Your Agent** 📘: The July class game (Session 10) where 20 teams' agents produced a live bullwhip blow-up.\n- **Company value (Chain Reaction)** 📘: Profit × reputation × trust. Governance is the multiplier you feel late.\n- **Compounders** 📘: The winning archetype. They built capability on foundations actually laid and kept operating (B12-Titans #1, A7-Kimi K3 #2).\n- **Confusion matrix** 📘: TP/FP/FN/TN counts behind every accuracy claim. Precision, recall and F1 derive from it.\n- **Critical ratio (newsvendor)** 📗: Cu/(Cu + Co). The optimal stocking quantile when under- and over-stocking costs differ.\n- **Cycle stock (Q/2)** 📘: Inventory created by order batch size. Forecasting does not reduce it; order frequency and MOQ changes do.\n\n## D\n- **D·P·T foundations** 📘: Data, Process and Tech maturity ladders (L1–L5) in Chain Reaction. Each agent has a gate (e.g. D3·P3·T3).\n- **Data leakage** 📘: Using a feature unknown at decision time (e.g. goods-receipt date). It flatters backtests and collapses in production.\n- **Data quality: six dimensions** 📘: Accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, uniqueness, validity. They are judged per use case.\n- **Decision-focused learning** 📘: Training models with the decision's cost as the loss, rather than symmetric prediction error.\n- **Decision levels** 📘: Strategic (AI as input only), tactical (augment), operational (delegate where frequent and reversible).\n- **DeepFleet** 📘: Amazon's 2025 generative foundation model coordinating 1M+ warehouse robots; robot travel time −10%.\n- **Demand sensing** 📘: Short-horizon forecasting from leading signals (POS, weather, promotions, IoT installed base) rather than history alone.\n- **Deployer (EU AI Act)** 📘: The organisation using an AI system. It owns human oversight, use per instructions, monitoring and logs.\n- **Digital twin** 📘: A live virtual replica, fed by real data, on which you test interventions before committing. Build it to fidelity-for-purpose.\n- **distCover / distSafety / distFront / distMode** 📘: Chain Reaction's four distribution dials: coverage, safety stock, SPREAD/FRONT loading, ROAD/MIX mode (MIX locked until tech maturity 3).\n- **DRL (deep reinforcement learning)** 📘: A neural policy learned by acting in a simulator (state → action → reward). Powerful but brittle off-regime.\n\n## E\n- **Efficiency-capture gap** 📘: Agentic procurement gave 20–30% staff efficiency but only 1–3% bottom-line capture (McKinsey). Efficiency is not margin.\n- **EOQ** 📗: Economic order quantity √(2DS/H), which balances ordering and holding cost (1913). Still right for stable demand.\n- **EU AI Act** 📘: EU regulation with high-risk obligations (conformity assessment, CE marking, registration) split across providers, importers, distributors and deployers. Now enforceable.\n\n## F\n- **F1 score** 📘: The harmonic mean of precision and recall. A single comparison number and the baseline for improvement.\n- **Fidelity-for-purpose** 📘: Build a twin only detailed enough to answer the one question asked.\n- **Fill rate** 📗: Units (or lines) delivered on time ÷ demanded. Weight by volume, never a simple average of locations.\n- **Foundations without operations** 📘: The losing archetype that laid deep ground and never ran on it (A4-Sapiens #20).\n- **Frozen baseline** 📘: A pre-pilot performance snapshot plus a control group, against which gate metrics are judged.\n\n## G\n- **Gate (roadmap)** 📘: An evidence checkpoint (success metric + counter-metric + owner + demotion trigger) between Crawl, Walk and Run phases.\n- **Generative AI** 📘: Models that create content (text, scenarios, designs). In SCM: RFx drafting, scenario engines, product concepts.\n- **Google OR-Tools** 📘: Google's open-source optimisation suite (LP/MIP, CP-SAT, vehicle routing, min-cost flow).\n- **Governance stack** 📘: Per-agent identity, scoped permissions, spend caps in code, action logs, escalation paths and kill switches. Guardrails live outside the model.\n- **Guardrailed pilot** 📘: Six design elements for any learned policy: scope, bounds, fallback, evaluation, trust loop, ownership.\n- **Guardrails live outside the model** 📘: Enforceable limits sit in the surrounding system, not in prompts or weights.\n\n## H\n- **Hugging Face** 📘: A hub of open transformer models that can be fine-tuned in-house (e.g. legal-domain models for clause risk).\n\n## I\n- **Installed-base signal** 📘: Machines in the field, their utilisation and maintenance schedules as leading indicators of spare-parts demand. It produced the 77 → 85% fill.\n- **Intermittent demand** 📘: Mostly zero sales with occasional lumps (spare parts). It breaks point forecasts and MAPE.\n- **Ironies of automation** 📘: The more reliable automation is, the worse humans get at monitoring it. Oversight is not a safety net.\n\n## J\n- **JD.com dual engine** 📘: An AI sensing layer on top of a classical solver for national routing and inventory placement. The AI decides when and what to re-solve.\n\n## K\n- **k-Means** 📘: Unsupervised clustering into k groups around centroids (SKU or customer segmentation). Validated by usefulness, not \"accuracy\".\n- **Kiva robots** 📘: Amazon's goods-to-person mobile robots (Kiva acquired in 2012), which carry shelving pods to pickers. Physical automation, the precursor of DeepFleet.\n\n## L\n- **Lean operators** 📘: An archetype with minimal transformation, deep foundations and excellent execution (A8-The Odyssey #3 on six agents).\n- **LSTM** 📘: A recurrent neural network with memory gates for long sequences. The Tier-4 forecaster; needs Data Maturity 3+ and long history.\n\n## M\n- **Mandate Card** 📘: Service floor, cost ceiling, mode priority, shock trigger, review cadence, set before handing flow to an agent.\n- **MAPE** 📗: Mean absolute percentage error. Undefined on zero actuals, so unsuitable for intermittent demand.\n- **Master data** 📘: Slow-changing reference data (item, vendor, customer, location, BOM). \"Where AI projects go to die.\"\n- **MEIO** 📗: Multi-echelon inventory optimisation under uncertainty (700+ Global 2000 deployments).\n- **Min-cost flow** 📘: An LP that finds the cheapest flow over a network of nodes and capacitated lanes meeting demand. It optimises exactly the objective it is given.\n- **MIX mode** 📘: Rail in the distribution mix in Chain Reaction (~25% cheaper than road), unlocked at tech maturity 3.\n- **Multi-agent coordination fixes** 📘: Shared demand signal, rate limits and order smoothing, trained or consensus coordination.\n- **M5 competition** 📘: The Walmart-data forecasting contest (42,840 series). ML won overall, but simple methods stayed competitive at SKU-store level.\n\n## N\n- **Naive benchmark** 📘: A simple rule (same as last week, seasonal naive) every model must beat on the same holdout.\n- **Newsvendor model** 📗: A single-period stocking model. Order the critical-ratio quantile of demand.\n- **Non-stationarity** 📘: Shifting demand regimes (promotions, 2020) that silently degrade history-trained models.\n\n## O\n- **One-step planning** 📘: Learning the decision function directly from features, constraints and KPIs, skipping the standalone forecast (Fortune 150: fill 77 → 85%).\n- **OpenAI API** 📘: A metered general-purpose LLM API, used for auto-RFx drafting and summarisation and billed per token.\n- **OptaPlanner** 📘: An open-source Java constraint-satisfaction planning engine (metaheuristics with hard and soft scores) for rostering, routing and scheduling.\n- **Optimizer Mandate Checklist** 📘: Service targets per zone, cost weights by strategic priority, review rule (L2/L3/L4), re-solve trigger, verify the fit.\n\n## P\n- **Pactum** 📘: The autonomous negotiation platform behind Walmart's tail-spend agent: ~3% savings, +35-day terms, 64–68% close rate, inside manager-set bounds.\n- **Pilot-selection matrix** 📘: Ranks pilots on value, feasibility and blast radius (reversibility). Used in Trishul B1.\n- **Pinball (quantile) loss** 📘: The error metric for quantile forecasts. It penalises under- and over-forecasts asymmetrically at the chosen quantile.\n- **Platform filters (six, in order)** 📘: Use-case fit, data gravity, skills/ecosystem, governance fit, TCO including consumption, exit/lock-in. Governance comes before features.\n- **Precision** 📘: TP/(TP + FP). When the model flags, how often is it right?\n- **Predictive maintenance** 📘: Using sensor data to predict failure or remaining useful life and schedule repair before breakdown.\n- **Prescriptive maintenance** 📘: Predictive maintenance plus the recommended action (when, which part, which crew, where to stock it).\n- **Probability forecast** 📘: A full demand distribution (mean, spread, tails) rather than a point. \"The quantile IS the decision.\"\n- **Prophet** 📘: Meta's open-source forecasting library (trend, seasonality, holidays by default). The Tier-2 forecaster.\n- **Provider (EU AI Act)** 📘: The developer of an AI system. It owns conformity assessment, CE marking, registration and technical documentation.\n\n## Q\n- **Q vs S** 📘: Cycle stock (Q/2) vs safety stock (S). Forecasting attacks S only; check the S share before believing an inventory claim.\n- **Q-learning** 📘: RL that learns Q(state, action) values from reward: Q ← Q + α[r + γ·max Q′ − Q]. Used for reordering and routing.\n\n## R\n- **RapidMiner** 📘: A visual, low-code data-science platform (drag-and-drop workflows) named in the syllabus ML tools.\n- **Recall** 📘: TP/(TP + FN). Of the real events, how many did the model catch?\n- **Regime change** 📘: A structural break (e.g. 2020 lockdowns) under which history-trained models fail. It requires monitoring and human override.\n- **Reinforcement learning** 📘: Learning a policy by trial and reward rather than labels.\n- **Re-solve trigger** 📘: A named event (capacity loss, cost shock, demand spike) that forces the optimiser to re-plan.\n- **Readiness scorecard (five pillars)** 📘: Data quality, master data, architecture/integration, security, compute, scored 1–5. It sets the ambition ceiling.\n- **RFx automation** 📘: NLP reading bids and requisitions, auto-filling comparison matrices and drafting RFQs/RFPs.\n- **RUL (remaining useful life)** 📘: The hours or days a machine has left. A better target than a binary failure alarm.\n\n## S\n- **Safety stock** 📗: z × σ × √L. A buffer against demand and lead-time uncertainty; the only stock forecasting reduces.\n- **Scenario Portfolio Checklist** 📘: Generate wide, label honestly (plausible/stretch/implausible), select two, specify survival, guard the label.\n- **Sequence beats speed** 📘: Season lesson 1. Build agents on foundations you have laid; below-gate builds waste tokens.\n- **Shared demand signal** 📘: Every tier and agent sees real end-customer demand. The single biggest bullwhip fix.\n- **Signal audit** 📘: Lead time, mechanism, decision test, cost vs value, weight check, before buying any data.\n- **Spend classification** 📘: NLP tagging every transaction into a category taxonomy instead of guessing from GL codes.\n- **Split shipment** 📘: Under a capacity shock, send the urgent part by fast mode and backfill the rest by the cheap mode.\n- **SVM** 📘: Support vector machine. A classifier that finds the widest-margin boundary between classes (e.g. late vs on-time supplier orders).\n\n## T\n- **Tier matching** 📘: Spreadsheet → open-source (Prophet) → cloud AutoML (SageMaker) → LSTM, chosen by volume, readiness, skills and governance.\n- **Token economics** 📘: Consumption billing per call or token. The agent bill is a live operating cost; the cost model is a snapshot.\n- **Tokens (Chain Reaction)** 📘: 24 decision tokens per month; plan = 2, regenerate = 1; upkeep 1–4 per agent; termination = 2; max 4 transformation actions per round.\n- **Trailer-express** 📘: The fast, expensive, capacity-limited mode for urgent top-ups.\n- **Transformation theatre** 📘: An archetype that built the most agents on the least foundation (\"activity mistaken for outcome\"), finishing 8th–18th.\n- **Trust design** 📘: Showing the track record, allowing bounded overrides with reason capture and explaining recommendations, so adoption is designed rather than demanded.\n- **Tune-or-retire** 📘: Measure cost per decision and value per decision, compare them, tune first (context, cache, batch, tier), and retire only if it still fails.\n- **Twin-now / Twin-later test** 📘: Decision-changing? Failure cost? Data readiness? Fidelity needed? Then a verdict.\n\n## U\n- **Update vs execute** 📘: The clarifying question for any \"autonomous forecasting\" claim. Executing orders is an autonomy grant.\n\n## V\n- **VAE + multi-objective optimiser** 📘: A 2025 design architecture. A variational autoencoder generates demand scenarios; an optimiser searches the cost-vs-CO₂ frontier across them.\n- **Value-per-decision** 📘: The ₹ impact of an agent's decisions ÷ the number of decisions. Compared with cost-per-decision in tune-or-retire.\n- **VRP** 📗: Vehicle routing problem. Serve customers with capacity-limited vehicles at minimum cost within time windows (NP-hard; OR-Tools, OptaPlanner).\n\n## W\n- **Walmart Trend-to-Product** 📘: A GenAI product-design loop that cut up to about 18 weeks from design-to-shelf.\n\n## Z\n- **Zero-weight finding** 📘: A Fortune 150 OEM's planning system gave retail partners' own forecasts zero weight. Signals earn their weights.\n- **Zillow Offers** 📘: A 2021 failure in which ML price forecasts auto-bought houses (>$500M write-down). The decision defines the model.\n"}];
